   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Read-aloud Text, General Description, Creature(s), Tactics, Trap(s), Treasure, Development, and Ad-Hoc XP Adjustment",
   "note": "Quote IS verbatim ('This section generally precedes the other entries of an encounter, although part of the general description might precede it if important to the encounter.'), and the header/EL and word-count halves are confirmed verbatim: 'Each encounter should be rated with an Encounter Level (EL #) in the main encounter header'; sample header '23. Vampire's Den (EL 13)'; 'Our adventures typically range from 5,000 to 15,000 words in length'; 'we automatically reject any adventure manuscript that contains fewer than 5,000 or more than 15,000 words. The ideal manuscript size is 10,000 words.' DOWNGRADED because the claim's section order is wrong: the source order is Treasure BEFORE Development, and the claim omits Trap(s) and Ad-Hoc XP Adjustment from a list it presents as the template. Also 'mandatory Read-Aloud Text' needs care - the heading is labelled (Mandatory) in the template, but the encounters paragraph says 'Each encounter can include any or all of the following sections... Do not include sections that are unnecessary for a given encounter.'",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the template's section order is wrong (the source puts Treasure before Development) and the list omits Trap(s) and Ad-Hoc XP Adjustment; 'mandatory Read-Aloud Text' is also qualified by 'Do not include sections that are unnecessary for a given encounter'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "14": {
   "index": 14,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Regardless of how cool your backstory is, an adventure is not a short story.",
   "note": "Quote verbatim in the query-writing tips. Continues: 'You need to condense or sacrifice background description in favor of telling us about PC involvement. It's how the adventure PLAYS that determines whether or not a proposal is something we want to pick up.' The anti-railroad half is verbatim elsewhere: 'Avoid excessively linear plots that force the story toward an inevitable conclusion or \"railroad\" the actions of the PCs. The adventure should be flexible enough for PCs to make choices and decisions that could affect the outcome of the story. Avoid rigid timelines.' The design guide adds 'The Adventure Background section should be no more than 5% of the total length of the adventure.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-chunk-00.json"
  },
