  "25": {
   "index": 25,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "encouraging players to adopt a certain mentality or approach",
   "note": "First half verified verbatim: 'the game's only guidance in portraying Chultans is to emphasize “tongue clicks” and their “heavy, characteristic accent.”' The quoted fragment 'correctly ascertain its original purpose' is also verbatim. But the claim's characterisation is the claimant's gloss, not Hines's: he presents it as the Archaeologist *background's mechanical feature* (an identity mechanic), arguing it caters to 'dungeons that combine race and place' and is 'meant to evoke images and stereotype in the minds of players' — he never calls it prose and never says ruins need outsider validation. Downgraded for that gap only.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the 'prose framing ruins as needing outsider validation' reading is the claimant's gloss: Hines presents 'correctly ascertain its original purpose' as the Archaeologist background's mechanical feature, never calls it prose and never says ruins need outsider validation",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "26": {
   "index": 26,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "gothic atmosphere clearly set through the statue and skeletal remains",
   "note": "The Death House passage is quoted verbatim on the page — 'This room is festooned with moldy skeletons that hang from rusty shackles against the walls... a gaunt, pale-faced man wearing a voluminous black cloak' — so all five adjectives are present, and Hewitt does say 'The gothic atmosphere is clearly set through the daunting presence of the statue, as well as the skeletal remains lurking in the corners of the room.' What the article does NOT do is what the claim credits it with: it introduces the passage as 'The suggested description', never uses the terms 'read-aloud' or 'boxed text' (zero occurrences), and makes no argument about decay adjectives, present tense, or third person. The adjective/tense/person analysis is the claimant's, not Hewitt's. Byline 'December 15, 2024 Meghan Hewitt' matches.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the read-aloud framing and the decay-adjective/present-tense/third-person analysis are the claimant's, not Hewitt's: the article calls the passage 'The suggested description', uses 'read-aloud' and 'boxed text' zero times, and makes no argument about adjectives, tense or person",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "27": {
   "index": 27,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "“double use” of framing devices was notoriously less prominent in early TRPGs",
   "note": "Quote verbatim in the full-text PDF (journals.uu.se/IJRP/article/download/228/252/576), in the passage 'by observing the simplicity of the original cover of the D&D game from 1974 (See fig. 3), it becomes evident that the aforementioned “double use” of framing devices was notoriously less prominent in early TRPGs'. The storytelling-game prologue point is also explicit: 'practically all of them begin with a prologue consisting of a short narrative text that provides an atmospheric introduction into the fictional setting'. The abstract names 'cover illustrations, prologues and epilogues' as the paratexts studied; section 3.3.2 covers 'Prologues, Epilogues and Framing/Embedded Narratives'. Note: the source's comparison is specifically the covers' double (typographic + pictorial) framing, not framing in general.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the source's comparison is specifically the covers' double (typographic + pictorial) framing, not framing in general as the '1974 booklets used such framing far less' limb states",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "28": {
   "index": 28,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "the use of framings in TRPGs to incite the production (and not only the reception) of text is what I have called genesic framing",
   "note": "Quote verbatim, in the conclusion and repeated as a pull-quote. Self-disclosure element confirmed ('of self-disclosure, as well as the use of framings...'), as is the persistence element: 'it is this feature that is likely to account for much of the complexity of the TRPG medium as well as for its persistence in time.' The source hedges with 'likely to account for much of', where the claim says flatly 'accounts for'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the source hedges with 'likely to account for much of', where the claim says flatly 'accounts for'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "29": {
   "index": 29,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "the first edition’s visual–textual dissonance produces interpretive ambiguity that might enhance embodied player engagement",
   "note": "ROUTE: journals.sagepub.com returned HTTP 403 to both WebFetch and a browser-UA curl, and the Wayback availability API reports no snapshot for this DOI, so the Sage page itself is unreachable. Verified instead against the publisher-deposited abstract for the same DOI via the Crossref API (HTTP 200): title 'Tomes and Tombs: Toward a Phenomenology of Interiors in Dungeons & Dragons', author Cameron Macdonell, published online 2025-09-03. That abstract carries the claim's quote word for word, and also 'Later editions increasingly removed visual assets'. Unconfirmed at abstract level (full text paywalled, as the claim itself concedes): the year 1978 and the specific 'illustration booklet'; the abstract says 'the first edition' and 'visual assets', and hedges with 'might enhance'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the year 1978, the 'illustration booklet' and the unhedged 'produces' are unconfirmed: the reachable abstract says 'the first edition', 'visual assets' and hedges with 'might enhance' (Sage 403, no Wayback snapshot)",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "30": {
   "index": 30,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "using the passive voice a lot, the occasional stream-of-consciousness digressions",
   "note": "Live fetch 200. Quote is word for word in Cook's own sentence, which continues 'an advanced vocabulary (I was a 14-year-old who used terms like \"weal or woe\" because of Gary), and a very authoritative tone.' The treatise/legitimacy element is also explicit: 'Reading Gary's AD&D Dungeon Master's Guide was like reading a treatise on some vital topic of complexity and depth' and 'it was meaningful to read someone treating the activity we loved as seriously as a scholar would' / 'We felt that it was both validating and compelling.' Claim fully supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i30-44.json"
  },
  "31": {
   "index": 31,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "use a platinum word when one worth a copper would do",
   "note": "COMPLETENESS CRITIC §1: 'Seven VERBATIM rows carry a limb the verifier says is not on the page: ... [31] three limbs (coinage, 1979 DMG peak, sentence length)'. Verifier's own note: 'THREE sub-claims NOT supported by this page: (a) that Maliszewski COINED \"High Gygaxian\"... (b) \"fullest in the 1979 DMG\" - the DMG appears only in a reader comment, never in the post body; (c) \"long multi-clause sentences\" - the post never characterises sentence length.' Plus: the quote 'use a platinum word when one worth a copper would do' is a reader's comment, not the post body.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "three limbs unsupported by the post body: that Maliszewski coined 'High Gygaxian' (the post uses it as already current), that it is fullest in the 1979 DMG (the DMG appears only in a reader comment), and 'long multi-clause sentences' (the post never characterises sentence length); the designated quote is also from the comment thread, not the post",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "32": {
   "index": 32,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "you had to be smarter than average to understand it",
