  "34": {
   "index": 34,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The Dungeon Master's Guide is strange and deeply idiosyncratic",
   "note": "Wikipedia wikitext via Special:Export. Quote word for word inside the Horvath quotation, cited to Monsters, Aliens, and Holes in the Ground (MIT Press, 2023, p. 58); continues 'Gygax expresses in it a singular vision that feels true'. Wieck element verbatim: 'it has become as much a guide of advice for Dungeon Masters as a manual of specific information' (White Wolf #17, 1989). Taylor element verbatim: 'The 5E DMG devotes the first 127 pages to teaching a novice player how to [be] a Dungeon Master in this system' (Black Gate). All three sub-claims supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i30-44.json"
  },
  "35": {
   "index": 35,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the spell section was an unrelenting wall of text",
   "note": "Wikipedia wikitext via Special:Export. Quote word for word (Benjamin Abbott, GamesRadar+, August 20 2024, about the 2014 edition). Turnbull element verbatim: 'whereas the original rules are ambiguous and muddled, the Handbook is a detailed and coherent game-system, and very sophisticated' - but the ref dates that review White Dwarf #10, December 1978 - January 1979, so '(1978)' is only approximately right. Schick element verbatim: spell descriptions 'have positively bloated to over 100 pages' (Heroic Worlds, 1991). Kelly element verbatim: 'the writing is much more polished' (GeekDad, August 18 2014). All four sub-claims supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i30-44.json"
  },
  "36": {
   "index": 36,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "modeled after medieval bestiaries, only with more precise figures",
   "note": "Wikipedia wikitext via Special:Export. Quote word for word: 'Games scholar Jaroslav Svelch saw the Monster Manual modeled after \"medieval bestiaries, only with more precise figures\"' (Monster Culture in the 21st Century, Bloomsbury, 2013, p. 197). Turnbull element verbatim: he 'praised the explanatory text, stating that it \"amplifies, where necessary, [the game statistics] and the result is the most comprehensive listing of D&D monsters you will find\"' (White Dwarf #8, Aug-Sep 1978). Both sub-claims supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i30-44.json"
  },
  "37": {
   "index": 37,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "there is no depth to their listings",
   "note": "COMPLETENESS CRITIC §1: 'Seven VERBATIM rows carry a limb the verifier says is not on the page: ... [37] one creature generalised to the Folio'. Verifier's own note: 'Greenwood element present but NARROWER than the claim... a remark about a subset of creatures, which the claim generalises to \"Fiend Folio entries\".'",
   "unsupportedLimb": "Greenwood's remark is about a subset of creatures (he considers 'the hook horror as one of the creatures with \"strange appearances and little else\"'), which the claim generalises to Fiend Folio entries",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "38": {
   "index": 38,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "It is the first D&D adventure to use boxed, read aloud text",
   "note": "COMPLETENESS CRITIC §1: 'two more carry provenance drift ([23] page 107 not 21 or 85; [38] surname from outside the page)'; and: \"McKenzie's endnote 31 for the Tamoachan sentence is Merric Blackman... the same post that is Wikipedia's only citation ([38]). So [20] and [38] are one source, not two... The live merricb.com URL now 301-redirects to an unrelated Spanish-language site, so the citation behind both is dead.\"",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the surname 'Blackman' comes from outside the page (the ref gives the author as 'Merric B'), and that single 2017 post is the sole citation behind both this sentence and McKenzie's endnote, its live URL now redirecting to an unrelated site",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "39": {
   "index": 39,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "sounds more like a cranky Redditor than a fantastical crime boss",
   "note": "COMPLETENESS CRITIC §1: 'Seven VERBATIM rows carry a limb the verifier says is not on the page: ... [39] one reviewer made plural'. Verifier's own note: 'Two wording drifts in the claim: it says \"Reviewers\" plural when the article cites only Hall for this point, and renders \"fantastical crime boss\" as \"fantasy crime lord\".'",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'Reviewers' is plural where the article cites only Charlie Hall for this point, and 'fantasy crime lord' renders the article's 'fantastical crime boss'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "40": {
   "index": 40,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "having Volo write the guide means that he can be wrong",
   "note": "Wikipedia wikitext via Special:Export. Quote word for word (the source has wiki italic markup around 'wrong', which renders identically). Kunzelman/Paste element supported: 'Volo is a device through which the designers of the Forgotten Realms can give Dungeon Masters a set of bounds ... Whatever Volo thinks or writes can be slightly off the mark'; the 'rumor and error' framing is explicit in his later review, which faults the book because it 'mostly tells the DM facts about a world, rather than opinions, rumors and suspicions that might be completely unfounded'. Furniss element verbatim: 'I love the marriage of narrative and mechanical information' (Destructoid, 16 November 2016).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i30-44.json"
  },
  "41": {
   "index": 41,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "like reading a choose-your-own-adventure book from front to back",
   "note": "Wikipedia wikitext via Special:Export. NOT verbatim: the claim's quote drops 'from'. The page reads 'it was a bit like reading a choose-your-own-adventure book from front to back, rather than following the breadcrumbs' (Rory Bristol, GeekDad, 2016-03-15). Substance otherwise supported, including the second element verbatim: Andrew Zimmerman Jones in Black Gate said Curse of Strahd 'is really a mix of setting manual and adventure module in one'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i30-44.json"
  },
  "42": {
   "index": 42,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "terminology and choices should be immediately intelligible to all",
   "note": "Wikipedia wikitext via Special:Export. Quote word for word inside Torner's sentence: 'D&D 3e and 3.5e bear the influence of Eurogame-style elegant design: that the terminology and choices in the game should be immediately intelligible to all who might play it', with the designers' aim given as to 'simplify and declutter the whole system'. Cited to Evan Torner, 'Combat In Dungeons & Dragons', Fifty Years of Dungeons & Dragons (MIT Press, 2024). Claim fully supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i30-44.json"
  },
  "43": {
   "index": 43,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "just being used in their normal sense",
   "note": "Live site refused with HTTP 403; verified through the Wayback Machine snapshot 20251219191134 of the same URL. Quote word for word under heading '8. Capitalising Game Mechanics In The Text': 'It really is a crime that the 2014 Player's Handbook left DMs to work out if certain words were just being used in their normal sense or if they referred to some mechanic in the game. Thankfully that mistake wasn't repeated in 2024.' Glossary element supported under '2. Handy Rules Glossary': 'a dedicated rules glossary covering basically everything you need in an a-z of mechanics at the back of the book'. All three sub-claims supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i30-44.json"
  },
  "44": {
   "index": 44,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Cast a Spell action -> Magic action",
   "note": "Live fetch 200. 'Magic action' word for word in the Updated Terminology table, which also carries every other rename in the claim: Race -> Species, Cast a Spell action -> Magic action, Inspiration -> Heroic Inspiration, Use an Object action -> Utilize action, and Shoving -> Unarmed Strike. The 'New Rules' list of the 2024 Rules Glossary includes Ally, Bloodied, Enemy and Passive Perception (the glossary spells the action entry 'Magic (action)'). All sub-claims supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i30-44.json"
  },
  "45": {
   "index": 45,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the lack of a dedicated section for Conditions",
   "note": "Quote appears word for word: 'Tossing so much into one glossary does come with certain drawbacks, like the need to reference the back of the book more frequently while learning the basic rules and the lack of a dedicated section for Conditions.' The art clause is also verbatim on the page: 'the Rules Glossary is also the one part of DnD's 2024 Player's Handbook that largely de-emphasizes artwork, squeezing monochrome scenes into banners at the top of some pages but devoting most of the real estate to the text.' Both halves of the claim confirmed. Fetched via WebFetch, HTTP 200.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i45-48.json"
  },
  "46": {
   "index": 46,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "three paragraphs and a table to roll on",
   "note": "Quote verbatim: 'Creatures like Aboleths, Beholders, and Mind Flayers from multi-page descriptions that show you their backstory and place in the world to, at best, three paragraphs and a table to roll on.' All three remaining sub-assertions confirmed on the page: subtitles — 'they also all now have Dark Souls boss subtitles' (article's word is 'subtitles', not 'epithet'); phylactery — 'Instead of the Greek word Phylactery ... the Monster Manual now uses \"spirit jar.\"'; utilitarian encyclopedia — 'It goes from an in-universe collection of monster lore to a glorified encyclopedia. It's stripped down to something purely utilitarian.' Byline and date in page JSON-LD match the citation exactly: author 'Dan Arndt', datePublished 2025-01-28. WebFetch returned HTTP 403; retrieved via curl with a browser user agent, HTTP 200, 289,672 bytes.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i45-48.json"
  },
  "47": {
   "index": 47,
   "verdict": "NOT_FOUND",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "The cited academia.edu page does not contain the quoted phrase or any supporting text. Direct fetch 403s (WebFetch and curl); the Wayback snapshot 20250914225534 fetched successfully (HTTP 200, 196,401 bytes) and is a login-walled listing page whose only body text is metadata ('Master's Thesis: The Narrative Structures of Dungeons and Dragons', '2011', 'Nathan Shank'), download prompts, related-paper links, and 'Sorry, preview is currently unavailable. You can download the paper by clicking the button above.' Zero occurrences of 'encyclopedic', 'geography', 'ancillary' or 'campaign setting' in the retrieved text. Not BLOCKED — the page was retrieved; it simply carries no abstract. An exact-phrase web search for the quotation surfaced no verbatim match anywhere. The thesis may well say this in its PDF body, but nothing at the cited URL supports it, so the citation as written is unverifiable and needs the PDF itself.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i45-48.json"
  },
  "48": {
   "index": 48,
   "verdict": "NOT_FOUND",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "The claim is a self-declared negative result; its 'source' field names the sweep's own searches, not the URL. The cited arXiv page (2210.07109, 'Dungeons and Dragons as a Dialog Challenge for Artificial Intelligence') was fetched successfully and does confirm the one clause that touches it — it is an actual-play dialogue NLP dataset ('a gameplay dataset consisting of nearly 900 games ... 800,000 dialogue turns ... 58 million words'), not stylometry or authorship attribution, so the citation is correctly characterised. But no single page can establish the load-bearing existential negative (that no stylometric/corpus study of official D&D prose exists, and that 'Gygaxian' is absent from three journal indexes), and the arXiv page asserts nothing about it. Two adversarial counter-searches (stylometry/corpus/authorship-attribution of D&D rulebook prose; 'Gygaxian' across AGS, Games and Culture, IJRP) surfaced no refuting study, so the negative is not contradicted — it is merely unestablished by the cited source. Verify this row by index searches, not by source fetch.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i45-48.json"
  },
  "49": {
   "index": 49,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "D&D Style Guide: Writing and Editing; Version 1.04a ©2013",
   "note": "Raw PDF fetched (HTTP 200, 9 pp., pypdf extraction). p.1 carries the title verbatim, 'Version 1.04a ©2013 Wizards of the Coast', and the two named foundations: 'The Chicago Manual of Style, 16th edition' and 'Merriam-Webster's Collegiate Dictionary, 11th edition'. Governance limbs all present: typography ('Typographic Treatment of Product Names'), numerals ('Numerals versus Words', 'Numerals in Rules Text'), verb collocations ('Verb Usage', p.6), capitalization ('Terms to Capitalize', p.5), tone (the 'High Fantasy' sidebar: humor/tragedy must not 'overtake the general tone'). Boxed/read-aloud limb confirmed by absence: case-insensitive grep for 'box' = 0 hits and 'aloud' = 0 hits across all 9 pages.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i49-63.json"
  },
  "50": {
   "index": 50,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Avoid overusing sidebars.",
   "note": "Raw PDF fetched (HTTP 200, 11 pp.). Quote appears verbatim as the opening of the new 'Sidebars' section (p.3). 'Version 1.08a ©2018 Wizards of the Coast' on every page; 'The Chicago Manual of Style, 17th edition' p.1. Additions confirmed against the v1.04a text: 'Headings' hierarchy section (Chapter Title / Heading 1-3 / Inline Subhead), 'Sidebars', 'Gender-Specific Suffixes', and 'Adventuring Parties' — which is the fellowship-vs-party rule ('The word “fellowship” has strong connections with The Lord of the Rings… use “group,” “band,” “company,” or, especially, “party.”'; 'fellowship' = 0 hits in v1.04a). Edition-naming rule is new in wording: 'In a departure from previous usage, we spell out and lowercase references to editions'. Boxed-text limb confirmed by absence: 'box' = 0 hits, 'aloud' = 0 hits. One nuance: 'updated January 2019' is the mirror's filename only — neither 'January' nor '2019' appears anywhere in the document — which matches the claim's own wording 'circulated as'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i49-63.json"
  },
  "51": {
   "index": 51,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that these PDFs 'had previously gone only to official-play writers' — the EN World thread says nothing about who received them before the DMs Guild posting",
   "trueWording": "Free on DMsGuild, 'only been on the site a month or so', four PDFs",
   "note": "Raw thread page 1 fetched (HTTP 200). Remathilis, Nov 25 2018: 'a rather interesting little gem that is free to download: A D&D Style-guide for publishing' and 'Its only been on the site a month or so' — which puts the posting around October 2018 — and 'Its divided into four PDFs: An IP Guide… an Adventure Guide… a Style Guide… and a Forgotten Realms Style Guide'. Those limbs are solid. Greps of the whole page for 'official play', 'organized play', 'Adventurers League', 'previously' and 'only to' return nothing. The missing limb is corroborated verbatim on this chunk's index-53 source (thekindgm.com: 'They were first available only to official play writers but now they're available to the broader public of the DMs Guild') — so the fact is sound but this citation does not carry it.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i49-63.json"
  },
  "52": {
   "index": 52,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "An updated #DnD House Style Guide is now on #DMsGuild",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). The embedded tweet reads verbatim: 'An updated #DnD House Style Guide is now on #DMsGuild, creators! Among other changes, this guide includes tips on achieving gender neutral language.' with the byline '— Dungeon Masters Guild (@dms_guild) February 22, 2019', matching the 2019-02-22 date and the DMs Guild account. The 'listing' limb is also on the page: 'Please note this is a totally new listing! We fixed an error when the original was set up.' (The sageadvice.eu post itself is dated April 1, 2019 — the tweet it archives is 2019-02-22, as the claim states.)",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i49-63.json"
  },
  "53": {
   "index": 53,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'so the public window was roughly 2018–2020' — the page never says availability ended; the author's reply is that he fixed the link",
   "trueWording": "'Apparently, this material is no longer available.' / 'I have updated the link'",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Post dated 'October 24, 2018 May 25, 2020'. Comment by mattwest2342, May 25 2020 5:43 am: 'Apparently, this material is no longer available.' Reply by The Kind GM, May 25 2020 12:33 pm: 'Thank you for bringing it to my attention. I have updated the link'. So the commenter report and the author's acknowledgement are exact. The inference limb is the problem: the author's response points to a working link rather than to a lapse, so this page does not establish that the public window closed around 2020. The page does support the window's opening: 'They were first available only to official play writers but now they're available to the broader public of the DMs Guild.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i49-63.json"
  },
  "54": {
   "index": 54,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "has not yet published the official style guide",
   "note": "All three sources the sentence names were fetched raw and each quote is word for word. Half-Cover (fetched HTTP 200; page footer '2 Sep 2017 / By: Michael'): 'unfortunately, to my knowledge Wizards of the Coast has not yet published the official style guide for D&D 5th Edition.' Old Dungeon Master (olddungeonmaster.wordpress.com/2015/08/03/5e-writers-guide/, fetched separately HTTP 200, 'Posted by Ronny on August 3, 2015'): 'I was looking for an official writer's guide, or style sheet, from Wizards of the Coast… Perhaps they have one and I just couldn't find it. So I took the copies I had from 3.5 and 4.0 and updated them for 5th edition' — both the quote and the 3.5/4e-compilation limb. Blog of Holding (this chunk's index-55 source, fetched HTTP 200, dated January 7 2025): 'it annoys me not to have an official style guide.' Note for the record that the claim's url field holds only the first of the three; the other two limbs are verified on the pages the sentence itself names, not on half-cover.com.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i49-63.json"
  },
  "55": {
   "index": 55,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "it annoys me not to have an official style guide",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Full sentence: 'When I'm writing rules for D&D 2024, it annoys me not to have an official style guide. In its absence, I made my own style guide so I can quickly answer my own questions' — quote verbatim, and it carries both the 'built an unofficial 2024 style guide' and the 'because no official one exists' limbs. Title: 'Unofficial D&D 2024 Style Guide (Your Capitalization Questions Answered!)', dated 'Tuesday, January 7th, 2025'. One attribution nuance: the post is first-person and unsigned — 'Hughes' appears nowhere in the page HTML or in the site's RSS feed (no dc:creator tags); the only byline evidence on the page is the sidebar's 'You can also get Paul's DM Notebook', i.e. the first name only.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the surname 'Hughes' appears nowhere on the page or in the site's RSS feed; the only byline evidence is the sidebar's 'Paul's DM Notebook', i.e. the first name alone",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "56": {
   "index": 56,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that the template is a Word template — the packet list on the page names only 'The 5e Template', never Word",
   "trueWording": "Receive an instructional packet containing: The D&D Wizards House Style Guide",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200; 'Graeme Barber / 23/03/2021'). Every other limb is exact. Stage 1 is 'Accept the offer, signing an NDA and providing payment information'; stage 2 is 'Receive an instructional packet containing: The D&D Wizards House Style Guide. The 5e Template. An example of a Pitch. Project Instructions. Project Appropriate Lore. An example of a pitch, draft, and extras.' — so style guide, template, pitch examples and lore documents at contract start, all confirmed, in a process the page frames as 'writing for Wizards as a work-for-hire freelance writer'. The formatting limb is verbatim too: 'Drafting Process: You will write a draft, formatted using the Style Guide and Template, and submit it.' Grep for the word 'Word' on the page returns nothing. The Word detail is corroborated elsewhere — the v1.04a style guide (this chunk's index-49 source) says 'If you are using the 5E Template in Word' — but POCGamer does not say it.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i49-63.json"
  },
  "57": {
   "index": 57,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "You should be familiar with the D&D Style Guide (also provided) as well.",
   "note": "Raw PDF fetched from media.wizards.com (HTTP 200, 10 pp.). p.1 'TECHNICAL INFORMATION': 'A template is provided for use, including the Community Created D&D Adventurers League logo. You must use this template, and follow the formatting standards as presented in the template. This logo must appear on your adventure… You should be familiar with the D&D Style Guide (also provided) as well.' Both quoted fragments are verbatim, and 'also provided' carries the claim's final limb. The document self-identifies as 'Dungeoncraft Adventures: The Wild Beyond the Witchlight' for 'D&D Adventurers League' with 'Effective Date: October 1, 2021. Version 1.2.' The same passage repeats for the second option later in the document.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i49-63.json"
  },
  "58": {
   "index": 58,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "more guiderails than your usual table does",
   "note": "Raw thread fetched (HTTP 200; thread starter Raddu, Nov 13 2023). Article body: 'The newly released Dungeoncraft Design Guide, effective from November 9th, 2023, provides comprehensive instructions and standards for crafting these adventures' and 'Creators must use a specific template provided for Dungeoncraft adventures, including the Community Created D&D Adventurers League logo. Familiarity with the D&D Style Guide and adherence to the DMsGuild Content Guidelines are also required.' The forum quote is verbatim in a reply defending the requirements: 'These guidelines above, however, are part of style of play that needs more guiderails than your usual table does', in a post that frames the contrast as Adventurers League / Living campaign play versus a home table — matching 'a poster frames this as organized play'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i49-63.json"
  },
  "59": {
   "index": 59,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "breaks down the official 5th-edition style guide",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200; 'By Hannah Rose / May 31, 2019 / Articles, Designing with Style'). Standfirst verbatim: 'The Designing with Style series breaks down the official 5th-edition style guide to help designers create content that's well-written, polished, and precise.' The Common Pitfalls limb is exact: 'Describing an ability check is so often botched that it falls under the “Common Pitfalls” section of the official D&D House Style Guide (emphasis mine):' followed by a block quote that I diffed against the v1.04a PDF — it reproduces the guide's 'Making a Roll vs. Succeeding on One' paragraph word for word. The trailing clause is the claim's own inference: the page demonstrates canonical treatment by a third-party 5e publisher directly, while 'the guide was public' is inferred from a rival publisher quoting it openly in 2019 rather than stated on the page.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'evidence the guide was public' is the claimant's inference from a rival publisher quoting it openly in 2019, not stated on the page",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "60": {
   "index": 60,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "In rules text, we often achieve gender neutrality by writing in the second person.",
   "note": "Raw PDF, p.2, under 'Gender' → 'Achieving Gender Neutrality'. The sentence appears exactly as the claim renders it, and it is the guide's only statement about second person, so 'the guide's stated reason for second person in rules text is gender neutrality' is precisely what the page says. (v1.08a softens the same rule to 'we prefer to achieve gender neutrality by writing in the second person whenever possible'.)",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i49-63.json"
  },
  "61": {
   "index": 61,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "but don't abuse this liberty",
   "note": "Both named versions fetched raw. v1.08a p.2 verbatim: 'In a departure from our past usage, we allow the use of “they” and “their” as singular pronouns, but don't abuse this liberty. Rely on this device only when you have no other reasonable or pleasing alternative.' — giving the quote, the 'in a departure from our past usage' wording and the no-pleasing-alternative limb. The v1.04a limb is verified on the v1.04a PDF (this chunk's index-49 source), p.2: 'Don't use “they” or “their” as singular pronouns, except in an informal context like dialogue.' Slight compression to flag: the older guide's exception is 'an informal context like dialogue', broader than the claim's 'except in dialogue'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "v1.04a's exception is 'an informal context like dialogue', broader than the claim's 'except in dialogue'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "62": {
   "index": 62,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "You make a saving throw, and it succeeds or fails.",
   "note": "Raw PDF p.6, 'Verb Usage' list. Every collocation in the claim is a line of that list, verbatim: 'You cast a spell.' / 'You deal damage.' / 'You make a saving throw, and it succeeds or fails.' / 'You make an attack (or you attack), and your attack hits or misses (it doesn't succeed or fail).' / 'You take damage.' / 'You regain hit points.' / 'You score a critical hit.' / 'You use a feature.' The list is indeed a fixed list of the game's verb idiom, so the framing limb holds as well.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i49-63.json"
  },
  "63": {
   "index": 63,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "You must succeed on a DC 15 Strength check to clamber up the wall.",
   "note": "Raw PDF p.6, 'Common Pitfalls' → 'Making a Roll vs. Succeeding on One', verbatim: 'Don't mistake making an ability check, a saving throw, or other roll with succeeding on it. The following sentence gets it right: “You must succeed on a DC 15 Strength check to clamber up the wall.” Don't write, “You must make a DC 15 Strength check to clamber up the wall,” unless that sentence is immediately followed by a description of what happens on a success or failure.' Both limbs — the prohibition-unless-consequences-follow and 'must succeed on' as the canonical form — are on the page, in the Common Pitfalls section the claim names.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i49-63.json"
  },
  "64": {
   "index": 64,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "skill checks are always written as [Ability Score] ([Skill])",
   "note": "Raw HTML fetched (200, 529KB). Quote verbatim. All three limbs on page: the [Ability Score] ([Skill]) rule; success/failure outcome language (Option 1/Option 2, 'On a success... On a failure...'); and 'If there are more than two possible skills... you probably want to pick at most two for the sake of brevity and elegance.' Third-party derivation limb confirmed: 'The Designing with Style series breaks down the official 5th-edition style guide.' Byline Hannah Rose, May 31, 2019, both on page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i64-78.json"
