  "71": {
   "index": 71,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'a deliberately conversational register for rules' — the guide gives no rationale for encouraging contractions; the phrase 'conversational' appears nowhere near this rule",
   "trueWording": "We encourage the use of contractions formed with \"not,\" such as \"don't,\" \"can't,\" and \"wasn't\"",
   "note": "The first two limbs are verbatim on p.3 'Contractions', including the ambiguity warning: 'Use other contractions with care, especially any contraction that can be misread. For example, \"who's\" can be read as \"who is\" or \"who has.\"' The register gloss is the compiler's inference, not the page's. Machine-checked: 'conversational register' is absent from all 9 pages; the guide's only two uses of 'tone'/'conversational' are the High Fantasy 'general tone' passage and a separate rule allowing 'Dungeon Master' as a verb 'in an informal context, such as in a blog or a web article that has a conversational tone' — a different rule, not this one.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i64-78.json"
  },
  "72": {
   "index": 72,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "there are moments of humor with the hobbits, but ring wraiths aren't used for gags",
