  "78": {
   "index": 78,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "a strict style guide that all D&D sections adhere to when explaining mechanics",
   "note": "Raw HTML fetched (200, 60KB). Quote verbatim. All limbs verbatim: 'This is why spells or saving throws have very specific wording'; 'the feature will always clarify what happens if you already have the cantrip because of another mechanic or feature in the game'; 'For example, starting equipment is always before class features in any class section.' The 'infers from the books' limb is supported: 'to my knowledge Wizards of the Coast has not yet published the official style guide for D&D 5th Edition' and 'the best way to draft your homebrew is to inspect the actual manuals'. Byline Michael, 2 Sep 2017, on page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i64-78.json"
  },
  "79": {
   "index": 79,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Write in the present tense. Wherever possible, avoid using the future tense",
   "note": "Raw page read via curl. Tense section verbatim: 'Write in the present tense. Wherever possible, avoid using the future tense \"will\" to describe NPC or monster actions. For example, do not say \"If the player characters open the door, the golem will attack.\" Instead say, \"If the player characters open the door, the golem attacks.\"' Every limb supported. Minor framing note: the guide is one blogger's (Ronny's) unofficial compilation updated from his 3.5/4.0 copies, so 'community-compiled' means fan-made rather than crowd-authored.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'community-compiled' overstates: the guide is one blogger's (Ronny's) unofficial compilation updated from his own 3.5/4.0 copies, i.e. fan-made rather than crowd-authored",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "80": {
