  "84": {
   "index": 84,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'the 48-page rules section plus 18-page glossary' - the 18 pages are inside the 48, not additional (29 rules pages + 18-page glossary = 'These 48 pages')",
   "trueWording": "29 pages of rules plus an 18-page glossary make 48",
   "note": "Raw page read via curl. Both quotations verbatim: 'The organisation and use of the rules glossary is absolutely stellar information design' and 'This is an incredibly confident book.' Influence limb verbatim: 'the Influence action isn't defined in the social interaction section for example'. But the page reads 'It summarises the lot of them in 29 pages... helped in great part by the 18 page rules glossary in Appendix C it depends on. These 48 pages aren't just rules', and later '(compared to 48)' as the 2024 core-rules total - so the claim double-counts the glossary. Second, smaller slippage: 'absolutely stellar information design' is applied specifically to the organisation and use of the rules glossary, not to the 48 pages as a whole.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i79-93.json"
  },
  "85": {
   "index": 85,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "D&D is a shared language. The rules serve to make it easier",
   "note": "Raw page read via curl; Tenkar's Tavern post of Sunday, March 3, 2013 blockquoting Mearls' column. Verbatim: 'Rapid Shot and Precise Shot are abstract things that aren't really clear', 'Describing your fighter as an archer, though, makes sense to anyone', 'D&D is a shared language.' Nuance: the page says D&D (not 'rules') is the shared language - the next sentence is 'The rules serve to make it easier to talk about the game'. 'Feat names' is contextual inference; Mearls does not call Rapid Shot/Precise Shot feats in the quoted passage.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the page says D&D (not 'the rules') is the shared language, and 'feat names' is the claimant's word - Mearls never calls Rapid Shot and Precise Shot feats in the quoted passage",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "86": {
   "index": 86,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "allows them to state things more plainly and use more natural language",
   "note": "Raw page read via curl. OP by Thaumaturge, Jun 25, 2014, thread 'Design Philosophy of 5e'. Quote verbatim: 'I think a benefit of this is it allows them to state things more plainly and use more natural language.' Rulings-over-exact-language limb verbatim: 'To let real people make real rulings that impact their own play instead of trying to nail down a specific style of play through exact language.' Community-inference framing correct - the natural-language line is the poster's own 'I think', hung off the Mearls Starter Set Unboxing video (one video, cited at ~46:15/48:00), not a designer statement about prose.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i79-93.json"
  },
  "87": {
   "index": 87,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'5e core-book editor' - the interview never says Gray edited the 5e core rulebooks; it names Secrets of Sarlona (3e Eberron) and Vault of the Dracolich, and 'freelance editing for Wizards'",
   "trueWording": "Prose editing requires attention to the emotional language of the game",
   "note": "Raw page read via curl. The substance of the three-layer answer is verbatim: 'Mechanics in RPGs need a specific amount of precision and clarity, not just within a single book but in terms of how that book fits in with the game as a whole. Narrative within a book needs attention to flow and pacing more than attention to words. Prose editing requires attention to the emotional language of the game and how a book can make the game feel real, so that a player reading an RPG doesn't just feel like a player reading rules - they feel on some level like a character in the world that the game creates.' He names the three layers 'narrative editing, rules editing, and prose editing'. What this page does NOT establish is the credential: no mention of the 5e Player's Handbook, DMG or Monster Manual anywhere in the interview. Cite the credential from a credits page, not from here.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i79-93.json"
  },
  "88": {
   "index": 88,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "James Wyatt has his own 'stupid little thing' that's been irking him",
   "note": "Raw page read via curl. Doors limb: 'Having to explain doors in every published adventure has apparently been irking him for the past decade' and the DMG's 'page on doors... a standardized set of information'. Wyatt limb verbatim: 'James Wyatt has his own \"stupid little thing\" that's been irking him - having to explain what boxed text is in every adventure.' Page 9 limb verbatim: 'explaining how it works on page 9 under a section telling DMs how to describe situations'. Editing-team limb verbatim: 'It's something that has to be hashed out with the editing team first'. Note the Perkins doors line is the interviewer's paraphrase of the interview; his only direct quote there is 'super, super important'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the Perkins doors statement is the interviewer's paraphrase of the interview; Perkins's only direct quote there is 'super, super important'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "89": {
   "index": 89,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'read-aloud scripts' - the page says the sample adventures are 'scripts for the DM to just to jog their memory', i.e. DM-facing, not read-aloud/boxed text for players",
   "trueWording": "scripts for the DM to just to jog their memory",
   "note": "Raw page read via curl (Screen Rant interview, Ben Brosofsky, Oct 25 2024). Perkins verbatim: 'everything in there better be cracking on topic and directed at DMs who are either new or who are looking for ways as experienced DMs to make their games more interesting.' And: 'I remember I was taught that an adventure was like a 32-page publishable thing. And so all of my home adventures I wrote as like 32-page things, fully written out... And so if I had the 2024 DMG, which has sample adventures in it, which are tight and short and really sort of scripts for the DM to just to jog their memory and make sure that they're sort of remembering the pace and flow of the adventure, I could have saved myself a lot of prep time.' 'tight and short' and the jog-the-memory and 32-page limbs are all verbatim; only 'read-aloud' is imported by the claim, and it inverts the audience of the scripts.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i79-93.json"
  },
  "90": {
   "index": 90,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "His follow-up, Storm King's Thunder, omits almost all read-aloud text",
   "note": "Raw page read via curl (post by David Hartlage, November 21, 2016, served on the tag archive URL). Verbatim: 'Curse of Strahd includes it for every location.'; 'DMs who dutifully present the box text for all the empty rooms in a place like the Argynvostholt dungeon will bore players.'; 'His follow-up, Storm King's Thunder, omits almost all read-aloud text. Find a happy middle, Chris.' The 'two extremes' framing is the claim's own wording but is carried by 'Find a happy middle' (and a commenter's 'On the other end of the spectrum, I find Storm King's Thunder barren').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i79-93.json"
  },
  "91": {
   "index": 91,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the Couatl is 'guardian manifestation of the divine' and the Roper 'tentacled subterranean trapper'",
   "note": "Raw page read via curl (EN World review by Beth Rimmels / brimmels, Jan 28 2025). Verbatim: 'every entry starts with a single phrase that gives the DM an efficient characterization of the creature. So the Couatl is \"guardian manifestation of the divine\" and the Roper is a \"tentacled subterranean trapper.\"' Lore limbs verbatim in two places: 'The lore/background material is compact - a little too compact for my taste' and 'Narrative/lore material is compact but effective.' Quote boxes limb verbatim: 'Quote boxes are another way the new MM adds a bit more flavor and insight. Not every entry gets them'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i79-93.json"
  },
  "92": {
   "index": 92,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The flavor has amped up narratively, and the mechanics have followed",
   "note": "Raw page read via curl (TheGamer, Jerel Levy, Published Jan 28 2025). Verbatim: 'The flavor has amped up narratively, and the mechanics have followed.' Angel example verbatim: 'where an angel might have been wielding a greatsword in the past... it now wields light itself in combat'. Kenku verbatim: 'The Kenku, known for being from the Shadowfell, now have a dagger with shadow magic.' No-lore-glossary limb verbatim: 'Though there is no lore glossary similar to what the Dungeon Master's Guide has'. Claim carried no quote field; the sentence it paraphrases is literal on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i79-93.json"
  },
  "93": {
   "index": 93,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "little notes that run as commentary throughout the book, usually a joke",
   "note": "Raw page read via curl (Skyland Games, Scott D, November 13, 2017). Whole claim verbatim in one sentence pair: 'As with Volo's Guide to Monsters, there are little notes that run as commentary throughout the book, usually a sort of joke or snipe about the subject matter. As the material is largely mechanics and game lore, it's less relevant than with Volo's but still fun.' Every limb - the device, the Volo's lineage, and the reduced relevance on a mechanics-heavy book - is on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i79-93.json"
  },
  "94": {
   "index": 94,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "If you're the DM, you get two sentences. Period.",
   "note": "Raw page via Wayback id_ capture 20210728094635 (live host 403s a bot UA). Page carries verbatim: 'If you're the DM, you get two sentences. Period. Beyond that, your players are stacking dice, talking to each other, or staring off into space' and 'missing the actual data in the boxed text - basic stuff, like room dimensions, how many doors exit the room, and number of monsters.' Dyson explicitly frames it as 'Back in 2005 when Jesse Decker and David Noonan still worked for Wizards of the Coast, they did some awesome hands-on research at the GenCon D&D tables' and links the WotC article. Post dated 29 Sep 2013. Every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i94-108.json"
  },
  "95": {
   "index": 95,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Ditch the boxed text and use your own words",
   "note": "Post #37 by 'touc', Apr 5, 2025, on page 4. Verbatim on page: 'There's a 2005 WOTC article (archived in criteria comments, Dave Noonan & Jesse Decker)'; the two-sentence block quote; 'DMs who didn't used boxed text had more engagement' (reproduced with the source's own grammatical error); 'DMs with box text are reading it \"cold,\" not having written it in their style nor practiced how it sounds'; and 'Ditch the boxed text and use your own words for the initial description of the room.' The claim's own self-flag stands: this is the forum re-citation, not the upstream WotC article, which I did not fetch.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i94-108.json"
  },
  "96": {
   "index": 96,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "3 to 5 sentences at most and then other details listed",
   "note": "Both attributions correct on page 4. el-remmen (Moderator Emeritus, #34, Apr 5 2025): 'I find it useful if written well and concisely. 3 to 5 sentences at most and then other details listed to be shared if the party looks closer/investigates/interacts.' - designated quote verbatim, and the 'listed details for closer investigation' limb verbatim too. GM Lent (#31): 'boxed text shouldn't be more than two sentences' - the claim renders this as 'no more than two sentences', a paraphrase of identical meaning, not a verbatim quotation; the limb itself is supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i94-108.json"
  },
  "97": {
   "index": 97,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Four sentences, and less than 100 words.",
   "note": "By Shawn Merwin, Nov 6 2019, on dndbeyond.com. Both quoted strings verbatim: under the heading 'A Boxed-Text Opening' the column presents a four-sentence in-media-res opening and comments 'Four sentences, and less than 100 words.'; Tip 2 has 'Nothing slows a game more than paragraph after paragraph of boxed text'. Nuance recorded, not a downgrade: the 100-word figure is stated as a descriptive count of the column's own model opening rather than as an explicit 'should', though the column plainly holds it up as the standard to emulate.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i94-108.json"
  },
  "98": {
   "index": 98,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "typically three lines, and never longer than five",
   "note": "Live host 403s a bare fetch and has no Wayback capture; reached raw on the www. host with full browser headers (HTTP 200). Page dated March 4, 2025. Every limb verbatim: 'the 5e work that I have done was longer form with bolded paragraph leaders and verbose descriptions'; 'So, I evolved and started to work toward the opposite side of the spectrum, focusing on short, bulleted phrases'; 'I introduced boxed text back into my typical formatting'; 'all my boxed text is precise... typically three lines, and never longer than five'; 'In practice, it has sped up play'; 'it allows me, as the designer, to help set a base-level tone for each room'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i94-108.json"
  },
  "99": {
   "index": 99,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "You have a tiny story to tell and less than 250 words",
   "note": "By Mike (Shea), 8 November 2010. All three fragments verbatim: 'writing good read-aloud text is like writing micro-fiction. You have a tiny story to tell and less than 250 words to tell it'; 'If you didn't edit your read-aloud text and remove 25% of what was there, you didn't try hard enough'; 'Keep it short and keep it powerful.' Every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i94-108.json"
  },
  "100": {
   "index": 100,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Aside from introductory passages... too long by half",
   "note": "Under the heading 'Don't Overdo It': 'Aside from introductory passages, it's rare that a boxed text that includes a paragraph break isn't already too long by half. This is especially the case with villain appearances... consider breaking it into statements that can be made in about six seconds - the average length of a round - and drop the phrases in during the fight.' Designated quote verbatim; both limbs, including the six-second/one-round equivalence, verbatim.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i94-108.json"
  },
  "101": {
   "index": 101,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "I agree that it must be mentioned that boxed text is often too long",
   "note": "The quotation IS on the page but it is not Justin Alexander's. It appears in the comment section, in the third of three responses, by a commenter named 'Andrew' dated August 2nd, 2022 - 7:13 pm, whose next sentence is 'I also tend to agree with Justin's critiques here', confirming Andrew is not Alexander. The essay body (everything above 'Filed under: Roleplaying Games Comments (3)') contains the string 'too long' zero times. The claim's other limbs ARE Alexander's own: 'what you should do is break it up into actionable chunks', 'What we've identified here is the reaction point', and the rule against letting 'the game world continue to move forward past the reaction point without letting the players react'. Related: the chunk's feature label for this claim, 'attention after three sentences', also traces to a comment - mellonbread, 11:58 am - recalling a lost WotC devblog piece, not to Alexander.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the attribution to Justin Alexander: 'boxed text is often too long' is a reader comment by 'Andrew', not the essay",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i94-108.json"
  },
  "102": {
   "index": 102,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Keys longer than five or six sentences can work, with a good editor",
   "note": "By Gus L., Monday December 19, 2022. The designated quote is verbatim (page: 'No one wants to read a three page key to a single room, written in the style of Undermountian or a 1993 Dungeon Magazine adventure'). But the page does not set five sentences as a maximum. It reads: 'Keys longer than five or six sentences can work, with a good editor, and an attention to ordering their contents, but that's a good basic limit. Longer keys are even needed at times for complex puzzles and npcs, but basic description shouldn't really get beyond a paragraph.' So the figure is five-or-six, framed as a soft 'good basic limit' that longer keys can exceed with editing - and puzzles/NPCs are given as cases where longer is 'even needed at times', not as the sole licensed exception.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'five sentences maximum' and 'longer only for puzzles and NPCs': page says five or six as a soft 'good basic limit' that longer keys can exceed with a good editor",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i94-108.json"
  },
  "103": {
   "index": 103,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the players are almost certainly going to be playing with their phones",
   "note": "Bryce Lynch, '(5e) Death in the Wood', posted February 9, 2019. Body text verbatim: 'There's long read-aloud at the beginning of each location... It's also pretty well written, which I don't think I've said more than three or four times? It does a good job of communicating the social vibe going on. It's still too long and the players are almost certainly going to be playing with their phones, but it's not the overwritten fantasy drivil that is present in most read aloud.' The 'even when, rarely, the prose is good' limb is carried by 'pretty well written, which I don't think I've said more than three or four times'. Sentence is in the review body, not a comment.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i94-108.json"
  },
  "104": {
   "index": 104,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Tip 1. Move from the second-person point of view to the third person.",
   "note": "Shawn Merwin, Critical Hits, August 27, 2010. Tip 1 heading verbatim. Both example strings verbatim and matching THIS 2010 version rather than the 2019 D&D Beyond rewrite: 'By avoiding the second-person (\"As you enter the clearing, you notice the bloated corpse of a headless dragon\") and using the third-person (\"The bloated corpse of a headless dragon dominates the center of this small clearing\")'. The 'actions or feelings' limb is supported in the essay body, not only by inference: 'boxed text can be rife with pitfalls when it assumes character feelings, movements, and actions.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i94-108.json"
  },
  "105": {
   "index": 105,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "avoid 'you' as subject; avoid game mechanical terms used non-mechanically",
   "note": "Same D&D Beyond column (Merwin, Nov 6 2019). Three of the claim's four quoted strings are verbatim on the page: the bad example carries 'As you approach the tower', Tip 1 rejects 'As you enter the clearing, you notice...', and the mock table dialogue skewers 'your heart pounds' and 'The palpable presence of evil is stunning' / 'You said we were stunned?'. Tip 6 is verbatim: 'Avoid game mechanical terms used in a non-mechanical way', listing 'reaction, action, incapacitated, stunned, frightened, magical'. Graded SUBSTANCE rather than VERBATIM for one word only: 'terrified' does not appear anywhere on the page - the page's words are 'bristle with terror' (in the bad example) and 'frightened' (in Tip 6's mechanical-term list). Tip 1's rationale covers the dictated-emotion limb: avoiding 'you' makes it hard to 'inadvertently include character actions, motivations, feelings, or movements'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'terrified' does not appear anywhere on the page - its words are 'bristle with terror' and, in Tip 6's mechanical-term list, 'frightened'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "106": {
   "index": 106,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "declaring the thoughts or feelings or (worse yet!) actions of the PCs",
   "note": "All of this is Alexander's own essay body (above the comment section), unlike claim 101. Section heading 'REMOTE-CONTROL BOXED TEXT'; 'Remote-control boxed text suffers from similar problems (preventing the players from participating), but insidiously goes one step further by declaring the thoughts or feelings or (worse yet!) actions of the PCs'; the two bad examples '\"You step forward and return the king's greeting with a deep bow.\"' and '\"As you return to Waterdeep, you smile, thinking fondly of the ale at Trollskull Manor.\"'; and the cure 'Focus on showing the players the scene and letting them react to it. Don't tell them how they're reacting to it.' Only cosmetic drift: the page's term is 'remote-control boxed text' and the second example's 'you smile' is lowercase mid-sentence.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i94-108.json"
  },
  "107": {
   "index": 107,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "predicts the future for the party (upon approaching...)",
   "note": "Same Wayback raw capture as claim 94. Verbatim: 'On top of which it predicts the future for the party (upon approaching...) instead of leaving that up to them. The only thing it doesn't do that some boxed text insists on doing is truly controlling the PCs with \"you stand in shock...\" or something similar at the beginning.' The cure limb is verbatim in the closing paragraph: 'reduce it down to one or two SIMPLE sentences. But really, replace it with a point-form list of things seen in the room.' Every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i94-108.json"
  },
  "108": {
   "index": 108,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "addressed to them instead of the empty air",
   "note": "Both quoted strings verbatim. Recommendation section: 'don't shy away from shifting text from third-person objective (\"A 200-foot waterfall cascades down...\") to second-person subjective (\"You see a majestic waterfall hundreds of feet in height...\") if your players tend to lose focus, as it will help them feel engaged.' Engagement rationale, from the Matthew Mercer passage earlier in the same essay: 'The players are drawn in because the description feels addressed to them instead of the empty air.' The direction of the shift is genuinely the reverse of Merwin's Tip 1 (verified against claims 104/105), though this page never names Merwin. Two nuances recorded, neither an unsupported limb: the two quoted phrases sit in different sections of the one essay, and the advice is conditional ('if your players tend to lose focus') and addressed to a DM adapting published boxed text rather than to a designer writing it.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i94-108.json"
  },
  "109": {
   "index": 109,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Rule 8: Write without assumption of perspective.",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200). Quote verbatim as the Rule 8 heading. Compass limb also verbatim: 'When I do describe a door, I describe it as being on the east or north wall, not the far or right one.' Byline 'Misha F', Tuesday, April 28, 2026, confirmed. Author's own retrospective adds that the GM will likely strip the directional information out again.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i109-123.json"
  },
  "110": {
   "index": 110,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Keep the descriptions short and punchy.",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), title 'Chapter 10: Formatting Room Descriptions | Designing Dungeons'. All four limbs found: quote verbatim; section heading 'Don't tell the players what to feel'; 'Writing down terms like \"the door to the right\" will become confusing... Instead, use cardinal directions'; and under 'Let the map work for you' — 'If something appears on the map, you don't have to waste space describing it... you can avoid repetitive descriptions of all the doors in a room.' The bylines Josh McCrowell / Warren D do not appear on this chapter page itself (site-level attribution, not a claim limb).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i109-123.json"
  },
  "111": {
   "index": 111,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "Don't give conclusions; don't use if/then; don't address the party",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200). Claim carries no quote, so no verbatim test applies; the substance is supported almost word for word by the comment of 'noisician', December 30, 2022 (matches the stated page field), offering '3 pieces of Bryce Advice': 'Don't give conclusions, just describe and let the players come to their own conclusions'; 'Don't use \"if/then\" constructs. I think this is mainly because it tends to be filler... \"if/then\" phrasing could be used to pad almost anything, but shouldn't'; 'Don't address the party directly in read-aloud, this typically takes decision making away from the players.' The framing 'Readers summarizing Lynch's standards' is exact — these are a commenter's additions, not the post body.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i109-123.json"
  },
  "112": {
   "index": 112,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "If the agents reach the safe house, Keletny burns the car.",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), 'Five Keys to Punchier Prose', 'By Robin D. Laws'. Quote verbatim in the corrected example under the section heading 'Deprecation of the \"will\"' ('In this construction, \"will\" is totally unnecessary'). Passive-voice limb verbatim: 'Avoid the passive voice'. The 'to be' + verb limb verbatim: 'look for instances of the verb \"to be\"... Most sentences pair these with an additional verb... reconfigure to drop the \"to be\"'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i109-123.json"
  },
  "113": {
   "index": 113,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'devotes a section to' — the page lists both only as topics the guide covers, never as sections",
   "trueWording": "It covers such topics as avoiding rampant capitalization, present tense",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200). Both quoted phrases appear verbatim inside a single sentence: 'It covers such topics as using gender-neutral language, ... avoiding rampant capitalization, ... using present tense instead of future tense, changing passive voice to active voice...'. The page is the book's marketing page for 'Writing With Style: An Editor's Advice for RPG Writers' (45 pages, by Ray Vallese, 20+ years editing games), so the editor-corrects-RPG-manuscripts limb holds. Nothing on the page states either topic gets its own section; the only mention of sections is inside a reader review ('Each section gives solid advice'). No table of contents is published on this page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i109-123.json"
  },
  "114": {
   "index": 114,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Describe everything the characters can sense, but not what they cannot.",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), 'Thinking Outside the Boxed Text', August 27, 2010 by Shawn Merwin. Quote verbatim as the heading of Tip 3. Both illustrative limbs are the page's own: the omission failure is 'the 50-foot-wide chasm between the doorway and the altar', and the over-telling failure is boxed text describing 'large crates full of mining equipment' when 'The characters couldn't know that without opening them'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i109-123.json"
  },
  "115": {
   "index": 115,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "If something 'appears to be' to the characters, it 'is' to the characters.",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), same essay. Quote verbatim; the claim's quote truncates the sentence, which on the page ends '...it \"is\" to the characters.' Heading is 'Tip 4. Avoid \"seems to be\" or \"appears to be.\"' Telegraphing limb verbatim: 'Those words generally mean that some trick or diversion is in the offing.' Perception limb verbatim: 'Let those Perception or Spot checks separate the \"seems\" from the \"is.\"'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i109-123.json"
  },
  "116": {
   "index": 116,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "unless you happen to be DMing for a party comprised solely of elves or florists",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), same essay. Quote verbatim, and the full sentence is the claim's second limb: 'The smell of flowers in the sunny glade can probably be left out, unless you happen to be DMing for a party comprised solely of elves or florists.' Relevance limb verbatim in the preceding sentence: 'leave in only the details about the environment that the PCs need to know about because they will have to interact with them.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i109-123.json"
  },
  "117": {
   "index": 117,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Tip 2. Avoid the novelist/dramatist urge.",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), same essay. Quote verbatim as the Tip 2 heading, with 'There are three main kinds of problem with this novelist's urge'. Soliloquy limb: subhead '2b. The villain's soliloquy' plus 'instead of having the evil guy do the soliloquy, let him talk with the characters interactively'. Coronation limb verbatim: 'The characters just saved the prince, and now they get to watch his coronation.' Short-and-act limb verbatim: 'find a way to keep the passages short, and give the PCs the chance to interact with the scene in some way.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i109-123.json"
  },
  "118": {
   "index": 118,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Tip 5. Read your boxed text aloud to check readability",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), 'Let's Design an Adventure: Boxed Text' by Shawn Merwin, Nov 6, 2019 — the article body rendered server-side, no summarizer used. Quote verbatim as the Tip 5 heading. Second limb verbatim: 'Trouble areas like tongue-twisters, funny-sounding phrases, unintended alliteration, and other pitfalls are hard to spot on the page but easy to hear when spoken.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i109-123.json"
  },
  "119": {
   "index": 119,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'Lynch's most-cited rule' — the page nowhere ranks this rule's prominence or citation frequency",
   "trueWording": "Go for the telltale sensory detail, rather than the generic abstract trait.",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200). Quote verbatim in item 21 of Jon Miller's summary, followed by the exact example the claim gives: 'Instead of stating \"One of the guards in the camp is a cruel bully,\" say \"The burly Manfred takes a leak on Tobias's bedroll, and then snatches Tobias's roasted chicken from his hand and quickly gobbles it down.\"' The 'specificity, not mere detail' limb IS on the page but NOT in the post the claim cites — it is a 2021 comment by 'Clever Munkey' ('Try for specificity not simply detail'), which Jon Miller endorses in reply ('Specific but not verbose'). The superlative 'most-cited' is the researcher's own framing, unverifiable from this page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i109-123.json"
  },
  "120": {
   "index": 120,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'as requirements', and the four-point Language scale is not Bryce Lynch's — it belongs to a second reviewer, 'The Pretty Girl'",
   "trueWording": "What I'm looking for in an adventure module; Language 4 - Succinct and evocative",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200). Third limb verbatim: 'Boxed text is usually not a good thing.' Both phrases verbatim as standalone bullets: 'Evocative atmosphere.' and 'Terse writing style'. But Bryce's list is prefaced 'What I'm looking for in an adventure module may not be what you are looking for. I try to avoid giving a module a rating' — and the same list explicitly grades requirement status elsewhere ('Wandering monster tables are generally required'; an overland portion is 'nice, but not required'), so neither of these two is labelled a requirement. Separately, the Language rubric (4 - Succinct and evocative / 2 - Conversational but clear / 1 - You should have hired an English Major to edit this / 0 - Very wordy/incomprehensible) sits in a later section headed 'The Pretty Girl', a second reviewer who introduces herself as 'not the gourmand that Bryce is' and calls it 'My ratings system'. It is on tenfootpole's standards page, so 'tenfootpole grades' holds, but the claim's source attribution to Bryce Lynch does not. Note also the rubric spans 0-4 with four defined rungs (no 3), so 'four-point scale' is loose.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i109-123.json"
  },
  "121": {
   "index": 121,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "it's not the overwritten fantasy drivil that is present in most read aloud",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), '(5e) Death in the Wood', Posted on February 9, 2019 by Bryce Lynch. Quote verbatim (including the misspelling 'drivil'). Praise limb: 'It's also pretty well written, which I don't think I've said more than three or four times?' Social-scene limb verbatim: 'The read-aloud tends to be describing a social interaction though, as if you just walked up on something happening... It does a good job of communicating the social vibe going on.' The praise is qualified in the same breath — 'It's still too long and the players are almost certainly going to be playing with their phones' — which the claim does not assert either way.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i109-123.json"
  },
  "122": {
   "index": 122,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Every sentence needs to fight for its life in a dungeon key.",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), 'OSR: Sharpening the Axe - How I Plan and Write RPG Books', dated 2019/09/05, Skerples. Quote verbatim, and the page continues 'Evoke. Make it visceral. Make it real.' Filler limb verbatim and with the claim's exact word list: 'Use find and replace to locate common \"filler\" words: little, big, grand, tall, dark, etc. Everyone has their own bugbears. Replace with evocative choices.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i109-123.json"
  },
  "123": {
   "index": 123,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "An idol shouldn't be described as 'evil'... rather it has a 'toothy glare' or is 'sharp edged obsidian'",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), 'Dungeon Design, Process and Keys', Monday, December 19, 2022, Gus L. Quote verbatim under 'a. Don't generalize: Describe specifics'; the page's negative example is 'evil' or 'forbidding' for an idol, matching the claim's 'evil idol'. Senses limb: 'there is advice to remember that senses other than sight exist: smells, sounds, and sensation etc.'  followed by 'This old advice to include other senses is still good' (with a caution against including everything). History/future limbs verbatim as headings under '7) Limit Your Description To The Room As It Is!': 'a. Don't describe the room's past.' and 'b. Don't describe the room's future.' Emotions limb is tip 10: 'Don't Tell the Characters How to Feel!' — 'You don't get to tell the players how their characters feel emotionally (barring creepy magic) or even physically.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i109-123.json"
  },
  "124": {
   "index": 124,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "keep it simple with a few powerful details",
   "note": "Kobold Press page (Celeste Conowitch, July 20, 2026) carries the quote word for word. Every limb holds: 'give the players the facts they need, and one thing to get them invested'; 'You step into a small stone room that reeks of garlic' -- 'the room has a story, a quirk'; and on naming monsters, 'describing the troll without ever using the word troll. It drives me bonkers when GMs use monster names.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i124-138.json"
  },
  "125": {
   "index": 125,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Try to include three of them in each description",
   "note": "The Alexandrian (Feb 8, 2022) verbatim. Room formula: 'List every notable thing in the room and its position'; 'Use the Three of Five rule' with the parenthetical 'Think about your five senses. Try to include three of them in each description.'; 'If appropriate, add a verb.' NPC formula reads 'An action. What are they wearing? One physical trait.' and closes 'you're not describing their headshot, you're establishing them in the scene.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i124-138.json"
  },
  "126": {
   "index": 126,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "five senses acting in dynamic concert; 'you detect orcs'",
   "note": "Claim carried an empty quote field, but its one embedded quotation, 'you detect orcs', is on the page word for word, and every limb is supported. Gaming Ballistic (July 19, 2017) reports: \"Listen! Do You Smell Something? By Anne K. Brown hits on some really good tips... available for consumption by the five senses acting in dynamic concert\"; 'show, don't tell'; 'a much more subtle way to convey information than \"you detect orcs.\"'; and 'She covers overdoing it too... if your players can't or won't use the information, it can be cut.' Caveat worth carrying forward: the 'you detect orcs' contrast is the reviewer's own illustration inside his report of Brown's essay, which the claim's '(as reported)' framing does cover.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i124-138.json"
  },
  "127": {
   "index": 127,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "GIANT SLAVERING OGRE is moving through broken furniture",
   "note": "The quotation is on the page verbatim, twice, and the ordering limbs all hold -- immediate threat first, 'the rest can be dealt with after the party has survived the immediate threat', 'use newspaper conventions - big details that are right in your face up front, and details later', and 'the more your party searches, the more paragraphs can be read.' What fails is the attribution. The reviewer introduces the whole newspaper-lead passage as HIS addition to Doyle: 'One thing that might be implied but could be made explicit' -- an explicit disclaimer that Doyle's essay does not say it -- and then credits the advice to someone else: 'In fact, Juan Ochoa gave me that advice about giving him art direction.' What the page does attribute to Doyle is the encounter checklist (read-aloud text, description and details, stats, how a critter fights, treasure, consequences and developments) and the balance line 'first, it goes without saying that encounters need to be balanced for the adventuring party.'",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that the newspaper-lead ordering is Chris Doyle's -- the page flags it as the reviewer's own addition ('might be implied but could be made explicit') and credits it to Juan Ochoa",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i124-138.json"
  },
  "128": {
   "index": 128,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "quit writing read-aloud text that sounds like it came from Ikea instructions",
   "note": "Sly Flourish (Mike, 8 November 2010) verbatim under the heading 'Keep it fantastic': 'We're playing a fantasy roleplaying game, quit writing read-aloud text that sounds like it came from Ikea instructions.' The second quoted fragment is verbatim too, under 'Keep it short': 'Ensure every word has a place in it.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i124-138.json"
  },
  "129": {
   "index": 129,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The descriptions themselves are very punchy and to the point",
   "note": "Gnome Stew, Jared Rascher, July 27th 2021. All three quoted fragments verbatim: 'The descriptions themselves are very punchy and to the point'; monster descriptions 'often convey emotional information, motivations, or default assumptions about the creature'; and 'Some descriptions are slanted towards \"selling\" the creature or location being described to trigger an emotional effect.' The claim says 'creature or place' where the page says 'creature or location' -- outside its quotation marks, so not a verbatim failure.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i124-138.json"
  },
  "130": {
   "index": 130,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "After: Its wormlike body is thin and scaly",
   "note": "tenfootpole.org, Bryce Lynch, August 13 2018, reviewing Ray Vallese. Stacked-modifier example verbatim: 'Before: Its body is thin, scaly, and wormlike. After: Its wormlike body is thin and scaly.' Expletive constructions verbatim: filler phrases 'there is' and 'there are'; 'There is an old wise man who watches over the children.' converted to 'An old wise man watches over the children.' The active-voice limb is supported more loosely -- the page lists 'Future vs Present Tense, my old friend passive voice, and a host of other examples' among what Vallese addresses, rather than stating an active-voice rule outright.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the active-voice rule is not stated outright: the page only lists 'my old friend passive voice' among the topics Vallese addresses",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "131": {
   "index": 131,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Just be sure you understand the rules before you break them",
   "note": "Gnome Stew, J.T. Evans, October 23rd 2017, quoting page four of Vallese: 'Do you have to follow all of these tips all the time? Nope. It's fine to break them to add variety to your writing. In fact, you should. Don't hammer all the flavor or voice out of your text to rigidly adhere to guidelines. Just be sure you understand the rules before you break them.' The claim's ellipsis elides exactly that flavor/voice sentence, which is also the limb it asserts.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i124-138.json"
  },
  "132": {
   "index": 132,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Characters can leave their pistols at the door, or leave them on the ship",
   "note": "Pelgrane Press, By Robin D. Laws, posted February 1, 2012. Verbatim: 'The character can leave his pistol at the door, or leave it on the ship. Becomes: Characters can leave their pistols at the door, or leave them on the ship.' The 'fifth key' limb checks out by counting the article's five sub-headings: Passive Voice, Weak Verbs, Paring 'of the', Deprecation of the 'will', and Plurals Are Pluralistic -- the plurals section is fifth and last. Rationale verbatim: 'turn the subject plural to avoiding assuming gender for your hypothetical subject.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i124-138.json"
  },
  "133": {
   "index": 133,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "check for 'to be' verbs and replace them with something more active",
   "note": "No quotation was supplied to check word for word, so this grades as substance, but the page supports every limb of Rule 7 directly. BAATAG (Misha F, April 2026), 'Rule 7: Information should be presented in the manner in which the players can discover it... This is \"show, don't tell\", but even more so... A rote way to help with this is to check for usage of \"to be\" verbs and replace them with something more active. This can be as simple as replacing \"there is a painting of the king\" with \"the painting reveals the form of the king\", but it's a good check to make sure that you're describing information that can actually be revealed in play.' The author's own retrospective later on the page reinforces it: he 'completely avoid[ed] statements of being in favor of statements of action (Rule 7)'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i124-138.json"
  },
  "134": {
   "index": 134,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Excellent boxed text does a lot of heavy lifting",
   "note": "sageadvice.eu reproduces two Shawn Merwin tweets. November 23, 2021: 'It's more than description. It's a series of prompts that can help your game flow more smoothly.' November 26, 2021: '(Perhaps that's why I prefer to call it \"narrative text\"). Excellent boxed text does a lot of heavy lifting, and atmosphere is definitely part of that burden.' Both quoted fragments verbatim, the preference for 'narrative text' explicit, and the atmosphere limb explicit. The host post itself is dated May 24, 2022; the tweets it archives are the 2021-11 ones the claim cites.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i124-138.json"
  },
  "135": {
   "index": 135,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "A majority of DMs out there WANT boxed text (well-written hopefully)",
   "note": "sageadvice.eu archives the Shawn Merwin tweet of April 14, 2019 verbatim: '...A majority of DMs out there WANT boxed text (well-written hopefully) to make their lives easier when they buy adventure content. So it is a risk not to provide what your audience and consumers want.' The streaming limb is in the same block: 'if boxed text worked well, then we would see it used on the streams as a go-to to start a scene. I don't think we ever see it.' Note that streaming sentence is the point Merwin is replying to ('A point I was trying to make was...' then 'While this is true...'), not a separate source. Host post dated May 5, 2019.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i124-138.json"
  },
  "136": {
   "index": 136,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "tends to encourage writers to 'fill the format'",
   "note": "The Alexandrian, May 19th 2014. The format named is confirmed as 3rd Edition's: 'When 3rd Edition arrived... First appearing in The Sunless Citadel, this format had the familiar boxed text and wall of text, but supplemented this with a specific set of bold-faced sections: Traps Creatures Tactics Development Treasure' -- exactly the five the claim lists, in order. Quote verbatim, and the ogre/goblin illustration verbatim: 'A room with an ogre standing in the middle of it should talk about the ogre first; a room with a goblin hiding behind a tapestry, on the other hand, shouldn't lead off with the goblin.' Plus 'the rigid adventure format forces information to be presented out-of-sequence.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i124-138.json"
  },
  "137": {
   "index": 137,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Complete sentences are overrated: Get the information across efficiently",
   "note": "The Alexandrian, Part 2, May 21st 2014. Every component is a section heading or explicit line on the page: 'TITLE OF THE ROOM'; 'BOXED TEXT... I also think of this section as seen in a glance'; 'REACTIVE SKILL CHECKS -- Directly after the boxed text are the reactive skill checks which should be made immediately'; 'SIGNIFICANT ELEMENTS... Notice that the bold title makes it easy to find the information you need'; 'DEVELOPING SIGNIFICANT ELEMENTS... move from the general to the specific'; 'GM BACKGROUND TAG'. Closing line verbatim: 'Complete sentences are overrated: Get the information across in the most efficient fashion possible.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i124-138.json"
  },
  "138": {
   "index": 138,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Write a paragraph that describes everything immediately observable",
   "note": "dungeons.hismajestytheworm.games Chapter 10: Formatting Room Descriptions. Quote verbatim, continuing 'by adventurers first entering the room... In this paragraph, highlight anything that's interactable in bold.' Ordering limb verbatim under 'First described, first keyed': 'Your bullet points should follow the same order as they were listed in the description.' Monster limb verbatim under 'Except monsters': 'I list obvious dangers like monsters last in my introductory paragraphs. However, because I know that players will want to interact with those elements first, I list those monsters first in subsequent keying.' One note: the page carries no byline, so the 'Josh McCrowell & Warren D' authorship in the source field is not confirmable from this page -- it is not a limb of the claim sentence, which the page supports in full.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i124-138.json"
  },
  "139": {
   "index": 139,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200). Quote verbatim: 'but only 3 or 4 bolded words maximum for a complex room that takes up a page or so'. 'Each bolded word refers to a follow up paragraph that explains it.' Rule 3: 'I start with the room's inhabitants... obvious environmental dangers (chasms, lava, etc) and visible valuables... Hidden things such as traps... I tend to write \"(trap)\"or \"(hidden)\" after them.' Every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i139-153.json"
  },
  "140": {
   "index": 140,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), dated Tuesday, April 28, 2026, author Misha F. Rule 2 heading: 'Everything defaults to unformatted paragraphs.' Body: 'No bullet points, no bolding.' Quote verbatim in Rule 2's second paragraph: 'Plain text paragraphs still need to serve as reference documents, and still need to follow patterns...'. Rule 4 verbatim: 'Later paragraphs describe one topic each, and that topic should be recognizable from the paragraph's first few words.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i139-153.json"
  },
  "141": {
   "index": 141,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), cygnus, November 30, 2023. Quote verbatim: 'focusing on what the characters can immediately perceive helps to both A) avoid rambling boxed text and, B) quickly identify key room elements'. Format block matches all limbs: 'Key # - Room Name / Short description (one to two sentences), highlighting the 5 senses. / Visible room element 1 ... | Additional detail provided upon party interaction. / || Nested additional detail ... / Creature stat lines'; plus 'I prefer having the stat lines for creature near the bottom.' ('long' vs the page's 'rambling' boxed text is the only wording slack.)",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i139-153.json"
  },
  "142": {
   "index": 142,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Sensory line is a commenter's suggestion, not Campbell's method",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'with sound/smell/breeze cues' — not part of Davis's account of Campbell's method",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), Tad Davis, December 5, 2013. Four limbs verbatim in the body: 'the immediately visible items are listed in bold-face'; 'Arrows to Non-boldfaced entries indicate items that are only described if the players investigate'; 'indents are used to group the items and these items are listed in the actual order they are stacked'; 'Words that are not essential are simply not used.' But 'sounds, smells, breezes, etc.' appears ONLY in a reader comment by 'Emily' (Dec 5, 2013, 7:43 am), proposed as something the format 'would work very well for' — not a described feature of Campbell's method and not Davis's text. Claim carries no quote field, so no verbatim grade available.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i139-153.json"
  },
  "143": {
   "index": 143,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Page: 'everything I need to run the encounter successfully in seven words' — unattributed blockquote",
   "unsupportedLimb": "attribution to Courtney Campbell — the page does not credit the seven-words target to him",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200). In 'How can I write even shorter, punchier room descriptions?' the seven-words sentence sits in an UNATTRIBUTED <blockquote> (no cite in the HTML), and the next, separate paragraph says 'On Set Design by Courtney Campbell lays out an alternate pattern for keying rooms that is very terse' — i.e. Campbell is offered as an alternative to the quoted approach, not as its author. Also the claim's inner quotation ('everything you need to run successfully in seven words') is not word-for-word: the page reads 'everything I need to run the encounter successfully in seven words'. The 'tick room' of the blockquote is not this chapter's worked example (that is a sludge/funeral-shrine room), confirming the block is imported from elsewhere.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i139-153.json"
  },
  "144": {
   "index": 144,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), Coins and Scrolls, 2019/09/05. Verbatim: 'But sprawl is the enemy of utility. Cut mercilessly. Turn pages to paragraphs, paragraphs sentences, sentences to tables, and tables to entries on better and more useful tables.' Order limb verbatim: 'Start with the most important information. What do the PCs see at a glance? What's moving? Then, smells. Details. Hidden information.' And: 'No \"This room is...\". It's a room description. We get it.' Every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i139-153.json"
  },
  "145": {
   "index": 145,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Rule heading: 'Listing Dimensions and Exits is a Waste!' — with stated exceptions",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'never' — the post explicitly allows exceptions for exits and for Dungeon23 dimensions",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200). Quote verbatim: 'Maps! Use them.' is the whole line under rule 2. But the same rule says 'However, listing exits can be helpful if things are confusing, the entrance is itself important, or it holds some evocative detail like a taxidermy crow nailed to a door. I mentioned before that there's no absolutes in these rules.' and 'I also suspect there's some odd specifics with Dungeon23 that make including dimensions and exits more helpful'. The absolute 'never spend key lines on dimensions and exits' overstates a rule the author himself qualifies twice.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i139-153.json"
  },
  "146": {
   "index": 146,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200). Rule 4 heading 'Plain Text is Better than Boxed Text!' and 'I dislike box text'. Quote verbatim: 'by sidelining the referee boxed text risks depreciating one big advantage RPGs have over computer RPGs.' Displacement limb verbatim in the next sentence: 'More practically, it discourages the referee from reading the adventure and thus understanding what's in the room before playing it.' Player-facing limb: 'since box text is player facing, the referee doesn't learn about the important hidden aspects of the room until they are already in play.' Only slack: the page hedges 'risks depreciating' where the claim asserts 'is'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the page hedges 'risks depreciating' where the claim asserts 'is'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "147": {
   "index": 147,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), David Hartlage, November 21, 2016. All three quotations verbatim: 'When D&D co-creator Gary Gygax created his first dungeon under Castle Greyhawk, he usually wrote a 1-line note for each room'; 'Gary started publishing adventures with G1 Steading of the Hill Giant Chief (1978). He included generous descriptions for every location'; 'Gary's longer descriptions set the pattern for virtually every adventure to follow.' Final limb is a section heading plus its first sentence: 'Matching description to a location's purpose / A location's purpose in the game should also figure into the length of its descriptions.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i139-153.json"
  },
  "148": {
   "index": 148,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), July 19, 2017. Verbatim: 'The first essay... is \"Adventures in Context,\" by Jobe Bittman. In it we are told that adventure writing is not fiction writing, that it's important to give that which is necessary for the GM to run the scenario and have it make sense, and no more.' Quote 'and no more' present word for word; the 'as reported' framing matches the page's 'we are told' (the reviewer paraphrasing Bittman's essay).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i139-153.json"
  },
  "149": {
   "index": 149,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), Monte Cook, RPG Design Theories, Dec 22, 2022. Solution-as-subtext limb verbatim: an adventure writer 'might be tempted to then explain: \"The way to get the idol out is to get a hover platform and follow the path to the airlock.\" This, I might argue, should be subtext. It's better unsaid.' Facts-plainly limb: 'Blatant \"expository\" writing is a good thing when it comes to telling them what's really going on' and 'Make it clear that the hover platforms will hold the weight of the idol... Don't hide that information'. Setting limb verbatim: 'Don't be coy about the important stuff.' Rules limb verbatim: 'The only place you shouldn't think about subtext is in straight-up rule writing.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i139-153.json"
  },
  "150": {
   "index": 150,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), Goblin Punch, Friday, January 27, 2017. Verbatim: 'Write snippets. You'll have time to sew them together later.'; 'running a dungeon for your players is the best way to (a) get ideas for your dungeon and (b) expose your dungeon's weaknesses'; 'Cut away all the parts that aren't awesome. It's better to have a small, excellent dungeon that it is to have a bloated, generic megadungeon.' Quote appears word for word. Every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i139-153.json"
  },
  "151": {
   "index": 151,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "'He does touch briefly, in an off-hand way, on those'",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'faults ... for saying nothing about white space, bullets or evocative writing' — the review says he does touch on them, and frames this as scope, not a fault",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), Bryce Lynch, August 13, 2018. Quote verbatim: 'A core part of being perfectly organized is scannability ... the ability of a DM to glance at the page and immediately find what they need'. But the white-space sentence reads: 'This isn't a book on evocative writing, or even adventure organization topics like the use of white space or bullets. He does touch briefly, in an off-hand way, on those but not to any real extent.' — an explicit denial of 'nothing'. The faults Lynch actually names are 'editor minutia' and the alphabetical organization ('WRONG!'); his verdict is positive: 'It's easy to recommend this' and 'the only thing in the How to Write Adventures category that I'd recommend.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i139-153.json"
  },
  "152": {
   "index": 152,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "'sample adventures ... which are tight and short and really sort of scripts ... to jog their memory'",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'read-aloud' and 'not to be recited' — Perkins describes the sample adventures themselves; the words read-aloud/aloud/recite appear nowhere in the article",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), Screen Rant, datePublished 2024-10-25, headline '\"Everything In There Better Be Cracking On Topic\": D&D Designers Talk The 2024 Dungeon Master's Guide'. Perkins verbatim: 'if I had the 2024 DMG, which has sample adventures in it, which are tight and short and really sort of scripts for the DM to just to jog their memory and make sure that they're sort of remembering the pace and flow of the adventure, I could have saved myself a lot of prep time.' The quoted phrase 'tight and short' modifies the sample adventures, not their read-aloud text; a case-insensitive grep for 'aloud', 'recit' and 'verbatim' over the whole article returns nothing. Boxed text is mentioned only by Wyatt, on a different point (adventures needlessly re-explaining what boxed text is).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i139-153.json"
  },
  "153": {
   "index": 153,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), David Hartlage, November 15, 2012. Verbatim: 'In 1979, Hidden Shrine of Tamoachan introduced boxed text descriptions for the DM to read when the players entered a location. The Hidden Shrine originated as the adventure used for the \"Official Advanced Dungeons & Dragons tournament at Origins in '79.\" The boxed text provided a way to offer a consistent play experience in a competitive environment.' The claim's inner quotation matches word for word; every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i139-153.json"
  },
  "154": {
   "index": 154,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "When most speakers read something aloud, their voice takes a different tone",
   "note": "Live page (dmdavid.com tag URL resolves to the 2012-11-15 post by David Hartlage). Exact sentence: 'When most speakers read something aloud, their voice takes a different tone and cadence than when they speak extemporaneously, and that monotony can cause players' attention to wander.' The sunset limb is also present: 'Perhaps too many boxed text descriptions have lingered on descriptions of the color of the sunset and other bits of obviously unimportant fluff.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i154-168.json"
  },
  "155": {
   "index": 155,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "it was considered a cardinal sin in my gaming circles",
   "note": "Live page. 'Back in the Reagan era, it was considered a cardinal sin in my gaming circles to read boxed text in published modules'; 'As players, we expected the GM to read the boxed text on her own and paraphrase it for us'; 'I found that I didn't mind boxed text so much. It helped with immersion'. The commenter line is in the page's comment markup as 'Basically, short box text is fine, long box text is a challenge.' — the claim drops 'Basically,' and capitalises. Scope note: the author locates the taboo 'in my gaming circles', not among GMs generally.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the author locates the taboo 'in my gaming circles', not among GMs generally",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "156": {
   "index": 156,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "match the pace of the text to your regular speech",
   "note": "Claim carries no quote field, so verbatim grading does not apply; the page supports every limb in near-identical words. 'Practice reading some of the text aloud between sessions if you can so that you can learn the pronunciation and how to match the pace of the text to the cadence of your regular speech.' Tool-not-script limb: 'The most important thing to understand about boxed text is that it's a tool... You need to take the boxed text and make it yours rather than submit to its authority' and 'Adjust it as much or as little as you like'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i154-168.json"
  },
  "157": {
   "index": 157,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "If you're the DM, you get two sentences. Period.",
   "note": "Wayback raw capture 20210713073031id_ of archive.wizards.com. Full context: 'If you're the DM, you get two sentences. Period. Beyond that, your players are stacking dice, talking to each other, or staring off into space.' Narrator of the undercover section is Noonan ('because Dave spent a lot of time wandering around the convention hall at Gen Con'), Gen Con 2005. Only difference from the claim's quote is the page's curly apostrophe.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i154-168.json"
  },
  "158": {
   "index": 158,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Boxed text replaces that conversation with oration.",
   "note": "Same 2005 capture. Verbatim. Conversation limb: 'At its heart, a D&D game is a conversation... You tell the DM what you want to do, and the DM replies with the consequences of the action.' Not-used-to-listening limb: DMs without boxed text 'are doing it in a style the players are used to', and 'oration is inherently harder to concentrate on than conversation.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i154-168.json"
  },
  "159": {
   "index": 159,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "basic stuff, like room dimensions, how many doors exit the room",
   "note": "Same 2005 capture. Full: 'players were missing the actual data in the boxed text - basic stuff, like room dimensions, how many doors exit the room, and number of monsters.' Corpse limb from the quoted player questions: 'Wait, the lady in the room is a corpse?' Repetition limb: DMs 'wound up repeating and paraphrasing all the information in it anyway - often in the middle of the action.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i154-168.json"
  },
  "160": {
   "index": 160,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Not a recipe for compelling delivery.",
   "note": "Same 2005 capture. 'DMs are, by and large, reading the boxed text cold... even a dynamic, dramatic DM is reading from a script that he or she has never uttered aloud before. Not a recipe for compelling delivery.' The claim's 'flatly' is the page's 'not a recipe for compelling delivery'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i154-168.json"
  },
  "161": {
   "index": 161,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Boxed text in adventures used to be a lot longer than it is today",
   "note": "Same 2005 capture. The Sons of Gruumsh excerpt quoted on the page is exactly three sentences about two 15-foot oak doors; it is contrasted with a single long paragraph, the cave-mouth entry from '1999's Return to the Keep on the Borderlands, pulled at random from my shelf'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i154-168.json"
  },
  "162": {
   "index": 162,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "ditch the boxed text and use your own words",
   "note": "Same 2005 capture. 'Or better yet, ditch the boxed text and use your own words for the initial description of the room. You'll engage your players in a conversation.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i154-168.json"
  },
  "163": {
   "index": 163,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "We're goofing around with some alternatives right now.",
   "note": "Same 2005 capture. 'But boxed text is something we're definitely watching closely. We're goofing around with some alternatives right now. If they play better at the table, you'll see them in products at some point.' Hedge worth carrying: the page says 'goofing around with', which is weaker than the claim's 'actively testing'; the table-play condition and the products limb are explicit.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the page says 'goofing around with' some alternatives, weaker than the claim's 'actively testing'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "164": {
   "index": 164,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Reading a roleplaying book just lets you make an educated guess",
   "note": "Wayback raw capture 20130531083417id_ of www.wizards.com. 'Roleplaying games are fundamentally different than most other media because the way you first experience the product (by reading it) isn't the way you experience it in the long term (by playing it)... Reading a roleplaying book just lets you make an educated guess about whether it's any good or not. You've got to play it to know for sure.' Byline is David Noonan alone.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i154-168.json"
  },
  "165": {
   "index": 165,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "In short, they read good, played bad.",
   "note": "Same 2006 capture. 'A lot of roleplaying games and especially campaign settings from the mid-1990s... an intertwined web of factions and organizations... Most had ongoing metaplots... It's difficult to slot your own PCs into a game with an important metaplot... either adhere to canon and relegate the PCs to bit players in the ongoing drama, or do your own thing and pitch the provided metaplot... In short, they read good, played bad.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i154-168.json"
  },
  "166": {
   "index": 166,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "They're no fun on a read-through.",
   "note": "Same 2006 capture. 'Another example of \"reads boring\" is the sets of pregenerated NPC stat blocks we put in books. They're no fun on a read-through. But they might save the busy DM's bacon at the table... (And for the record, we're working on ways to make that sort of thing easier for the DM to find and use quickly.)' The page hedges with 'might save'; the claim states it flatly.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the page hedges with 'might save the busy DM's bacon' where the claim states it flatly",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "167": {
   "index": 167,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Avoid the word \"you\" like the plague including derivations",
   "note": "Wayback raw capture 20161101184116id_ of archive.wizards.com; Living Greyhawk 'Intro to Writing' column, 04/13/2004, by Craig Heir. 'Avoid the word \"you\" like the plague including derivations like \"your\" or \"you're.\"' Reason limb: the rewritten example is glossed 'The text is straight forward but takes control of the characters.' Framing note: the column presents this as tips/guidelines, not a formal prohibition.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the column presents this as tips/guidelines, not the formal prohibition the claim's 'forbids' asserts",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "168": {
   "index": 168,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "read aloud text does not run the character, the player does",
   "note": "Same 2004 capture. 'some boxed text dictates character actions or assigns emotions to characters... After all, read aloud text does not run the character, the player does.' Both cited examples are on the page verbatim - 'The crying of the babies brings a tear to your eyes.' and 'Fearing for your safety, you grab the hilt of your sword.' - each followed by a neutral rewrite ('Inside the orphanage the babies cry for their lost parents.').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i154-168.json"
  },
  "169": {
   "index": 169,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "after all this is roleplaying not story time",
   "note": "Wayback raw capture 20161101184116id_ (live archive.wizards.com 302s to company.wizards.com/en). Quote verbatim. Both limbs on the page: 'Avoid read aloud text where the characters sit idly by as the NPCs carry on for an extended time' and 'If you have to have extended interactions between NPCs provide breaks for the players to react to what they are hearing.' Also 'Using lengthy box text ... while players sit idle by is a problem.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i169-183.json"
  },
  "170": {
   "index": 170,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Get a feel for what is bare-bones essential and cut the rest out",
   "note": "Same Wayback capture. Quote verbatim. Limbs supported: 'The best way to gauge the amount of text to include is to run adventures.'; 'When too much information is presented as read aloud text, many GMs feel constrained.'; 'with too little information some judges find it difficult to lead the party through the adventure.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i169-183.json"
  },
  "171": {
   "index": 171,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "having a consistent style for adventures allows judges to quickly find information they need",
   "note": "Wayback raw capture 20161101184117id_. Quote verbatim. Second limb verbatim too: 'A copy of both the content and style guidelines can be found in the Living Greyhawk Writer's Guidelines.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i169-183.json"
  },
  "172": {
   "index": 172,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The need to keep your writing PG-13 is quite obvious. Children play this game",
   "note": "Same Wayback capture. Quote verbatim; the causal limb is the next sentence, 'Children play this game...'. Context is explicitly RPGA adventure content ('The content of your adventure must also meet a set of guidelines for both content and style.').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i169-183.json"
  },
  "173": {
   "index": 173,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Read-aloud text provides a bare-bones description of the encounter area; it does not make any reference to the viewer",
   "note": "PDF fetched directly from paizo.com (HTTP 200, 171,743 bytes) and text-extracted with pypdf. Quote verbatim. Next sentence: 'Avoid phrases such as \"you see,\" \"as you enter the room,\" or other phrases that assume any action whatsoever on behalf of the players.' Note the guideline word is 'Avoid ... such as', slightly softer than the claim's 'banned', but it is an editorial prohibition in a submission guideline.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i169-183.json"
  },
  "174": {
   "index": 174,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "You should also avoid including descriptions of any creatures in the room, since their activities and positions ... often depend on multiple factors",
   "note": "Same PDF. Quote verbatim (it spans a line break in the original, so naive extraction shows 'avoidincluding'). The causal limb is verbatim too: 'such as if they hear the PCs coming, if it's night or day, and so on'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i169-183.json"
  },
  "175": {
   "index": 175,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Read-aloud text for an encounter should only rarely run more than a few sentences",
   "note": "Same PDF. Quote verbatim; the handout limb is the next sentence: 'Long sections of read-aloud text are better served as player handouts.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i169-183.json"
  },
  "176": {
   "index": 176,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Each encounter can include any or all of the following sections ... Do not include sections that are unnecessary",
   "note": "Quote verbatim: 'The editors greatly dislike printing the words \"This room is empty\" in DUNGEON.' The section names and the omit-don't-fill rule are fully supported ('Do not include sections that are unnecessary for a given encounter... the area is not an encounter and should not be assigned an encounter number'). But the page never prescribes an ORDER: it says each encounter 'can include any or all of the following sections', and read-aloud only 'generally precedes the other entries of an encounter'. The enumeration is also eight items, not seven - the claim drops 'Ad-Hoc XP Adjustment'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "fixed order - the guidelines say encounters 'can include any or all of the following sections' and never mandate an order (read-aloud merely 'generally precedes'); the list is also eight sections, the claim names seven",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i169-183.json"
  },
  "177": {
   "index": 177,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The Adventure Background section should be no more than 5% of the total length of the adventure",
   "note": "Same PDF. Quote verbatim (and restated as 'no more than 5% of the adventure's total length'). Word-count limbs verbatim: 'Our adventures typically range from 5,000 to 15,000 words in length' and 'The ideal manuscript size is 10,000 words'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i169-183.json"
  },
  "178": {
   "index": 178,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Outline surprises and plot twists here, not during the course of the adventure",
   "note": "Same PDF, under the header 'Adventure Synopsis (Mandatory)': 'This section provides a clear, concise summary of the adventure for the DM. Outline surprises and plot twists here, not during the course of the adventure.' Mandatory, DM-facing and start-of-document limbs all supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i169-183.json"
  },
  "179": {
   "index": 179,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Try to keep the number of sidebars in your adventure to a minimum, and to no more than 300 words in length",
   "note": "Same PDF. Quote verbatim; the reason limb precedes it verbatim: 'Remember that sidebars take up more space in the magazine than their word counts would indicate.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i169-183.json"
  },
  "180": {
   "index": 180,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Regardless of how cool your backstory is, an adventure is not a short story",
   "note": "Same PDF, in the query-writing tips. Quote verbatim. Limbs: 'You need to condense or sacrifice background description in favor of telling us about PC involvement. It's how the adventure PLAYS that determines whether or not a proposal is something we want to pick up.' The 'and adventures' half is carried by Adventure Background (Mandatory): 500-word/5% cap on backstory.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i169-183.json"
  },
  "181": {
   "index": 181,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Note that we will not publish an article written in the first person",
   "note": "PDF fetched directly from paizo.com (HTTP 200, 80,309 bytes), pypdf extraction. Quote verbatim; the second limb verbatim: 'you should not use the words \"I\" or \"my\" in your article unless some character other than yourself is saying them.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i169-183.json"
  },
  "182": {
   "index": 182,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "If your article begins with a narrative vignette or piece of example dialog, it should be no more than 200 words long",
   "note": "Same PDF. Quote verbatim; both 'narrative vignette' and 'example dialog' are named.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i169-183.json"
  },
  "183": {
   "index": 183,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Especially avoid passive writing, particularly “to be” verbs, and always spell check your work",
   "note": "Same PDF. Quote verbatim (the PDF uses curly quotes around 'to be'). A later FAQ entry reinforces it: 'Q: What is passive voice and why should I avoid it?'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i169-183.json"
  },
  "184": {
   "index": 184,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "names of magic items and spells should be italicized, and feat names should be capitalized",
   "note": "Paizo PDF extracted with pypdf. Full sentence: 'Article manuscripts should use the current style of D&D game products. For instance, the names of magic items and spells should be italicized, and feat names should be capitalized.' Quote verbatim; every limb (italic items/spells, capitalized feats, current D&D product style) supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i184-198.json"
  },
  "185": {
   "index": 185,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "A full page of text in DRAGON contains about 750 words",
   "note": "Page: 'A full page of text in DRAGON contains about 750 words. Submissions should not exceed 5,000 words or the specified word count for the Familiars article unless the editor specifically responds to a query that a longer article is desired.' Both limbs supported; the 5,000 cap carries an editor-exception the claim omits but does not misstate.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i184-198.json"
  },
  "186": {
   "index": 186,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "failure to present information in D&D styles",
   "note": "Page (Rejections section): 'Articles are most often rejected because of poor writing quality, imprecise game mechanics, failure to present information in D&D styles, poor formatting and organization, cliched approaches to topics, or differences between the query and the final article.' Quote verbatim; the adjacency to poor writing and imprecise mechanics is exact.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i184-198.json"
  },
  "187": {
   "index": 187,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "interesting enough to stand alone without their rules element",
   "note": "Page (New Rules): 'Stories, backgrounds, and inspiration for new rules should be interesting enough to stand alone without their rules element.' Quote verbatim. Page says 'should'; the claim says 'must' - a modal strengthening, not an added limb. A parallel sentence for Features rules articles says 'compelling enough to stand alone without the rules content.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i184-198.json"
  },
  "188": {
   "index": 188,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the language needs to be direct and clear",
   "note": "Grognardia interview, answer 2: 'journalists are trained to write for clarity and directness. Flowery phrases and clever wordplay are things to avoid. The same is true in games; the language needs to be direct and clear.' Winter's newsroom background (Iowa State journalism major, city-desk reporter, first TSR hire with professional editing experience) is stated in the same answer.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i184-198.json"
  },
  "189": {
   "index": 189,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "I don't believe it can do both.",
   "note": "Grognardia answer 5: 'A set of game rules needs to decide up front whether its job is teaching the material to newcomers or serving as a reference manual for people who already know the fundamentals. I don't believe it can do both.' Quote verbatim; every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i184-198.json"
  },
  "190": {
   "index": 190,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the writer as a documentarian of an imaginary world",
   "note": "SFWA page: 'The one element of game writing that is unique to the craft, in my opinion, is the idea of the writer as a documentarian of an imaginary world. Gaming writing focuses on the props of a story - locations, characters, creatures, past events - as opposed to characters and plot.' Words match verbatim; on the page the phrase is a clause ('the idea of the writer as...'), not a standalone sentence.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i184-198.json"
  },
  "191": {
   "index": 191,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the kind of exposition that can make a novel boring",
   "note": "SFWA page: '...but gaming dwells on the kind of exposition that can make a novel boring. Of course, little of that exposition appears as-is in a game. Instead, it's the invisible foundation on which the game stands.' Quote verbatim; both limbs supported. Page's subject noun is 'gaming' inside a paragraph defining game writing.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i184-198.json"
  },
  "192": {
   "index": 192,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "It's much more of a lawyerly approach to writing.",
   "note": "SFWA page: 'a world or character can tap into a gamer's imagination to fill in the details. You can't afford that kind of gap in mechanics. An unclear mechanic creates confusion and frustration. It's much more of a lawyerly approach to writing.' Quote verbatim. The gap limb is the page's own contrast; the page frames it as an affordance ('can tap into') rather than using the word 'deliberately'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'deliberately' is not the page's framing: it presents the gap as an affordance ('can tap into a gamer's imagination to fill in the details')",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "193": {
   "index": 193,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "D&D is a shared language.",
   "note": "LIVE URL DRIFT: enworld.org/threads/...334907/ now serves a different thread ('Chris Perkins and James Wyatt Answer Burning Questions About the 2024 DMG'); XenForo resolves by numeric id. Verified on the Wayback raw capture of the identical URL (web.archive.org/web/20200812035704id_/...). Post #1 (Mar 2, 2013) links wizards.com/dnd/Article.aspx?x=dnd/4ll/20130304 and block-quotes: 'D&D is a shared language. The rules serve to make it easier to talk about the game and make stuff happen. They take abstract concepts and give them clear meaning. When we say \"5th-level wizard,\" we know what you can do and how you do it.' Qualifier the claim compresses: the next sentences read 'We know that because we play D&D. Someone who never played the game would be utterly lost' - i.e. shared among players, not literally everyone. Column is titled 'Legend & Lore' (claim writes 'Legends & Lore').",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'tells everyone' overstates a source that restricts the shared language to players ('We know that because we play D&D. Someone who never played the game would be utterly lost'); the column is also titled 'Legend & Lore', not 'Legends & Lore'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "194": {
   "index": 194,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "They don't stand on their own in a meaningful way.",
   "note": "Same Wayback capture, same block quote: 'Rapid Shot and Precise Shot are abstract things that aren't really clear. You can only understand them by knowing what they are. They don't stand on their own in a meaningful way. Describing your fighter as an archer, though, makes sense to anyone.' Quote verbatim; every limb supported. The page calls them 'abstract things', not 'feats' - the feat categorization is externally true (3.5e) but is the claim's word, not Mearls'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'feat names' is the claimant's word: the page calls Rapid Shot and Precise Shot 'abstract things', never feats",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "195": {
   "index": 195,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "asked to trim the 28,000-word adventure to a more manageable 16,000 words",
   "note": "Quote 'I almost always overwrite' is verbatim ('Anyone who has edited my work knows that I almost always overwrite'), so the first limb holds. The second limb fails on the same page twice. (a) Outcome: 'I was asked to trim the 28,000-word adventure to a more manageable 16,000 words. I was not entirely successful; I think the final adventure came in at 21,000 words, still making it the longest module ever published in DUNGEON Adventures.' It was not cut to 16,000. (b) 'Umbra' (Issue #55) is called 'My first PLANESCAPE adventure', while the page names his first published Dungeon work as 'Wards of Witching Ways,' Issue #11. Cross-check: the Fraternity of Shadows interview in this same chunk has him in Dungeon #38 in 1992, well before #55.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "his first Dungeon adventure was cut from 28,000 to 16,000 words - the page says he was ASKED to cut to 16,000 but landed at 21,000, and names Issue #11 'Wards of Witching Ways' as his first Dungeon adventure, not #55 'Umbra'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i184-198.json"
  },
  "196": {
   "index": 196,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "What I miss are the pull-quotes; they made PLANESCAPE products even more fun to read!",
   "note": "Mimir.net interview: 'Warriors of Heaven is a core AD&D product and does not embrace the narrative style of most PLANESCAPE products. The cant is mostly absent, although I think I managed to slip the occasional \"basher\" and \"leatherhead\" past the editors!' followed by 'What I miss are the pull-quotes; they made PLANESCAPE products even more fun to read!' Quote verbatim; 'cant' is the Planescape slang the claim names.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i184-198.json"
  },
  "197": {
   "index": 197,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Somewhere in there is a cautionary tale about overwriting.",
   "note": "Fraternity of Shadows page (header line: 'This interview of Christopher Perkins was conducted in February, 2016 in anticipation of the March 15 release of Curse of Strahd'). Perkins: 'My first draft was 24,000 words, and Barbara asked me to cut it down to 15,000. A couple rewrites later, she was finally happy with it... Somewhere in there is a cautionary tale about overwriting.' The 1992 / Dungeon #38 dating comes from the interviewer's question. Register limb: asked where Curse of Strahd falls between Gothic Horror and Dark Fantasy, he answers 'There's a lot of Gothic horror with a dash of dark fantasy.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i184-198.json"
  },
  "198": {
   "index": 198,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "make it entertaining",
   "note": "D&D Beyond transcript (title 'Chris Perkins on Writing your own Adventures', page date 2018-04-19; Todd Kenreck is the interviewer): 'adventures that are published, aren't merely meant to be played, they are meant to be read and if you are going to make somebody read something for **** sakes', make it entertaining. Sometimes that means bringing a smile to the DM's face when they're reading it because the DM's thinking, \"Oh, I can't wait till the players get here.\"' Quote verbatim; every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i184-198.json"
  },
  "199": {
   "index": 199,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Put puns in your adventure. Put little laughs. Put little Easter eggs.",
   "note": "Quote verbatim in the D&D Beyond transcript. Perkins: 'Put puns in your adventure. Put little laughs. Put little Easter eggs in your adventure.' The 'embedded naturally rather than forced' limb is carried by 'you don't have to go out of your way to be funny because the people around the table are going to bring that' and 'my favorites have little gems of humor embedded in them'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i199-213.json"
  },
  "200": {
   "index": 200,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "write an adventure like a novel - you can't do that",
   "note": "Claim carries no quote, so verbatim is not testable; substance fully supported. Page: 'It's very easy for somebody who's accustomed to reading novels to write an adventure write a novel and you can't do that because A; you don't know who the protagonists are... and B; this is a game with dice rolls and random outcomes.' Dice limb: 'your villain will get critical hit four times in a row and die before he even gets through a monologue... The adventure has to be able to withstand that'. Dictated-resolution limb: 'if... everything they encounter has to be solved in a certain way or they just can't move forward, that also feels very unrealistic and unrewarding.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i199-213.json"
  },
  "201": {
   "index": 201,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "rich in such characterization, despite ... an 'economical' writing style",
   "note": "Wargamer page: 'The original Ravenloft was rich in such characterization, despite (as Perkins calls it) an \"economical\" writing style.' 1983 dating present ('The original 1983 Ravenloft module'). Expansion limb: 'If I was going to dare expand the setting around Castle Ravenloft...' and 'it was Perkins' goal to stay faithful to the module's villain-first nuance, even as he built up the world around the original module's castle.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i199-213.json"
  },
  "202": {
   "index": 202,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "the helpful tone to ease you in",
   "note": "CBR, James Wyatt verbatim: 'the Fourth Edition DMG was so good at introducing people to the game, and it's very sad that the 2014 DMG wasn't as good. So, part of what I did... was to try and bring in some of that, not even so much as the content, but the helpful tone to ease you in to the amazing and wonderful world of dungeon mastering.' Note the page's 'wasn't as good' is slightly softer than the claim's 'lacked', but the teaching/welcoming and 4e-model limbs are explicit.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the page's 'the 2014 DMG wasn't as good' is softer than the claim's 'lacked'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "203": {
   "index": 203,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "surfacing the information that's in the guide in a better way",
   "note": "CBR, Chris Perkins verbatim: 'I'll be describing something that was in the 2014 DMG, and they didn't even know it was there... one of the things we talked about early on was just making sure that we're surfacing the information that's in the guide in a better way, so that people actually know it's there and can find what they're looking for.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i199-213.json"
  },
  "204": {
   "index": 204,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "presenting the scripts of that unfolding in play",
   "note": "CBR, Wyatt verbatim: 'I worked on the examples of play in both the Player's Handbook and the Dungeon Master's Guide, which to me are really strong introductions to what this game is, and how you play it. As a narrative exercise, it's all dialogue... and presenting the scripts of that unfolding in play, I think, is the answer to my eight-year-old self saying, \"How is this a game?\"' The page says 'worked on' rather than 'wrote', in an interview about the 2024 books.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the page says Wyatt 'worked on' the examples of play, not that he wrote them",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "205": {
   "index": 205,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "front-loading all of the basic information about what DMing is",
   "note": "EN World page 2, Perkins verbatim: '...keeping in mind what we were doing with the Player's Handbook and putting in the more instructional first chapter on how the game works. And making this feel like the mirror image of that book by front-loading all of the basic information about what DMing is or ought to be and saving the more creative stuff, the create your own world for the middle of the book.' All three limbs (front-loading, world creation to the middle, PHB mirror) explicit.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i199-213.json"
  },
  "206": {
   "index": 206,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the organization of the 2014 DMG was its biggest drawback",
   "note": "EN World page 2, Wyatt verbatim: 'I think everybody recognized that the organization of the 2014 DMG was its biggest drawback... Well, it's not a Dungeon Master's Guide if it doesn't guide you on how to be a Dungeon Master.' Both limbs are in one Wyatt answer.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i199-213.json"
  },
  "207": {
   "index": 207,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "having to explain what boxed text is in every adventure",
   "note": "Raw page reached; the quoted string 'tells you what boxed text is' does NOT appear on the Screen Rant page (checked case- and whitespace-normalised over both HTML and extracted text). Substance holds in other words: 'James Wyatt has his own \"stupid little thing\" that's been irking him - having to explain what boxed text is in every adventure... the typical \"Running the Adventure\" section sticks an explanation inside an example'; p.9 limb: 'gets to boxed text just as quickly, explaining how it works on page 9'; wants-to-drop limb: 'Per Wyatt's preference, this explanation might serve as blanket coverage for future adventures, although he's not completely confident that the change will happen.'",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the designated quote 'tells you what boxed text is' appears nowhere on the Screen Rant page (checked case- and whitespace-normalised over both HTML and extracted text)",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "208": {
   "index": 208,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "super, super important",
   "note": "Screen Rant verbatim: 'Having to explain doors in every published adventure has apparently been irking him for the past decade... addressing something adventurers encounter so often seemed \"super, super important\"'. Doors-page limb: 'the inclusion of a page on doors'; stop-repeating limb: 'The obvious implication is that future published adventures can leave the door explanations behind.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i199-213.json"
  },
  "209": {
   "index": 209,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "the DM still needs some way to understand what to convey",
   "note": "Alphastream verbatim: 'If you don't have boxed text, then the DM still needs some way to understand what to convey to the PCs.' Reach-the-DM limb: 'Boxed text... is only part of the way an adventure communicates what is happening to the DM, and through the DM to the players' and 'how all parts of the adventure format can help or hinder the DM'. The cited page carries no byline naming Abadia and does not state an AL-admin role; authorship (Teos Abadia) and organized-play credentials are confirmed on the same site's About page (alphastream.org/index.php/about-and-contact/), which lists AL adventure authoring/development and 'Lead Administrator for Ashes of Athas' rather than literally 'AL admin'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the cited page carries no byline naming Abadia and never states an AL-admin role; the About page lists AL adventure authoring/development and 'Lead Administrator for Ashes of Athas' rather than 'AL admin'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "210": {
   "index": 210,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "You must read both places",
   "note": "Alphastream verbatim: 'We get a description of the door under \"Dimensions & Terrain,\" but then we get a clarification under \"Door\"... But we can't just look at \"Door.\" You must read both places.' Third section limb: 'the \"Creatures/NPCs\" section has information on when the door animates (within 5 feet) and what it says.' Season 8 / rigid template limb: 'Season 8 required that authors use the format'; obscuring limb: 'it actively obfuscates the content and makes the adventure harder to run well at the table.' (The worked example is a Moonshae CCC adventure written to the Season 8 format, not a WotC-authored AL module.)",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i199-213.json"
  },
  "211": {
   "index": 211,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "It exists sort of just to exist, and doesn't add a ton of value",
   "note": "Alphastream verbatim: 'The Playing the Pillars sidebar hits one of my pet peeves. It exists sort of just to exist, and doesn't add a ton of value.' The page discusses one instance of a format-mandated sidebar; the format generally is described earlier ('you might have \"Playing the Pillars\" to give DMs ideas').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i199-213.json"
  },
  "212": {
   "index": 212,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "far fewer words devoted to describing the monster",
   "note": "Alphastream verbatim: '2024 seems to forget this, providing far fewer words devoted to describing the monster. The words we do find have less useful or inspiring information, particularly when it comes to actionable information for creating encounters.' 2014 comparison limb: '2014 is often mentioned favorably by DMs who were inspired by its lore.' Written in Abadia's own voice under his 'Needs Work: Loss of Lore' heading (he opens the section citing designer friends, then states the finding himself); the book is called the '2024/2025 Monster Manual' on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i199-213.json"
  },
  "213": {
   "index": 213,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "a fair lack of clarity in monster stat blocks",
   "note": "Alphastream verbatim: 'there is still a fair lack of clarity in monster stat blocks.' Inconsistent-Multiattack limb: 'Some monsters state Multiattack like the Bandit Captain: \"The bandit makes two attacks, using Scimitar and Pistol in any combination.\" Other monsters are like the Behir... it appears the order is fixed, since it lacks the \"any combination\" wording seen in the Bandit Captain.' Cube limb: 'Gelatinous Cube has updated language that in attempt to codify what it means to be in the cube, ends up harder to understand.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i199-213.json"
  },
  "214": {
   "index": 214,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Beginners want all stats/contingencies; long-time players want bullet lists.",
   "note": "Raw EN World AMA thread (Jun 3 2015) fetched live with browser UA. Baur's answer: 'Beginners tend to want as much support as possible, all the stats, contingencies for everything, answers in the text for corner cases, map support, tokens, guidelines. Long-time players tend to want bullet lists, bare bones text that allows them to improvise in response to the players at the table, a bit more wiggle room, less wall of text.' Quote verbatim; every limb supported ('veterans' = his 'long-time players').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i214-228.json"
  },
  "215": {
   "index": 215,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Write write write. Turn out a lot of prose.",
   "note": "Raw EN World AMA. Answer to 'If you had three tips for unpublished fantasy and/or RPG authors': 'Three tips for unpublished authors and game designers? ... 1) Write write write (and don't quit your day job too early). Turn out a lot of prose. Speed keeps you fed; quality keeps you respected. Find a balance. Do the work.' Quote verbatim; advice is explicitly addressed to authors AND game designers. Context worth carrying: the volume advice is immediately balanced by 'Find a balance.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i214-228.json"
  },
  "216": {
   "index": 216,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Much time lavished on history and background is wasted energy.",
   "note": "Wayback raw capture 20130531110015id_ of the dead wizards.com column. Page: 'much of the time lavished on history and background is wasted energy ... Make sure your backstory is recent and relevant; avoid anything that starts \"Thousands of years ago...\"' Quote verbatim, both advice limbs verbatim. On the 'ex-Dungeon editor' descriptor the page says only 'When I worked on Dungeon magazine I saw them constantly' - the editorial role is entailed (he saw beginners' submission errors constantly) but the title itself is never printed here or in the AMA (index 214/215 source), which says only 'your time with Dungeon/Dragon magazine'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the 'ex-Dungeon editor' title is never printed on the page or in the AMA - the page says only 'When I worked on Dungeon magazine I saw them constantly'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "217": {
   "index": 217,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Four common errors, seen constantly at Dungeon magazine.",
   "note": "Wayback raw capture. Page: 'There are four fairly common errors in beginning adventure design. When I worked on Dungeon magazine I saw them constantly, and the errors haven't changed. 1) too much useless backstory 2) slow starts 3) random encounters 4) too many encounters'. Quote verbatim; the enumerated four match the claim exactly and in order.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i214-228.json"
  },
  "218": {
   "index": 218,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Skip involved history the PCs cannot learn; save it for sourcebooks.",
   "note": "Wayback raw capture. Page: 'Unless it connects directly to action in the current timeframe (and the PCs have a way of learning it), skip the involved history. Save that for sourcebooks.' Quote verbatim; the PC-cannot-learn condition and the sourcebook destination are both explicit, and 'not adventures' is the column's subject.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i214-228.json"
  },
  "219": {
   "index": 219,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Hooks should be specific and very hard to turn down.",
   "note": "Wayback raw capture 20130531075546id_. Section 'Make It Concrete and Tough to Refuse': 'You want your hooks to be specific, and you want them to be very hard to turn down. Vague, overused, or clichéd hooks don't interest players because they have heard them before.' The overused list includes 'Meeting a stranger in a tavern' and 'Requests from the mayor or head villager'. Quote verbatim; every limb, including both cited examples, is on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i214-228.json"
  },
  "220": {
   "index": 220,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Arrow with Elvish note beats the guy in a bar.",
   "note": "Wayback raw capture. Page opens by comparing '\"You meet a guy in a bar and he tells you about a dungeon outside town.\"' (called 'old news') with '\"During the midnight watch, someone shoots an arrow into your camp. There's a note attached, written in Elvish.\"', then: 'The adventure hook is what kicks off the action ... In fiction, it would be called the \"inciting incident.\"' Quote verbatim; both limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i214-228.json"
  },
  "221": {
   "index": 221,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Intentionally modern-sounding labels so roles stay out of game text.",
   "note": "Wayback raw capture 20140106064951id_. Page: 'And I intentionally used modern-sounding labels on some of the roles because I didn't want the roles to creep into game text. Only the designers and I would ever know what the monster's role is.' The role list on the page includes Mook, Decathlete and Artillery, as the claim's parenthetical names. Quote verbatim (page uses a curly apostrophe in 'didn't').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i214-228.json"
  },
  "222": {
   "index": 222,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Accepting that restriction limits how much you write and art size.",
   "note": "Wayback raw capture. Page: 'Laying out a monster manual is trickier than most other books, once you accept the restriction that monsters don't break out of page spreads. If you want a monster manual where you don't have to flip pages during your time at the table, then you have to accept some significant limitations on how much you can write and how big your illustrations can be.' Quote verbatim; both consequences (text length, illustration size) explicit. Framing nuance: the page presents it as a restriction one accepts to get a no-flip book, not as a house rule imposed from outside.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the page frames the no-break rule as a restriction one accepts to get a no-flip book, not as a prohibition imposed from outside ('are not allowed to')",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "223": {
   "index": 223,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "VERY fond of boxed text back in the day.",
   "note": "Live blogspot page fetched raw. Reviewing his 1992 TSR Dark Sun super-adventure Dragon's Crown among his 28 published adventures: 'I also seem to have been VERY fond of boxed text back in the day, although that was kind of a 2e style thing.' Quote verbatim; the self-review framing and the TSR-era adventure are both on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i214-228.json"
  },
  "224": {
   "index": 224,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "MU was the scheduling unit used at TSR, not WotC.",
   "note": "Live blogspot page. Page: 'A \"MU\" was the scheduling unit used at TSR for RPG work. It stood for \"module unit\" and represented 32 pages of writing/design, or 20,000 to 22,000 words. When I first started at TSR, the standard was that a designer like me would be scheduled for 1 month per MU.' Quote verbatim and the 32 pages / 20,000-22,000 words / one designer-month limbs are all exact - but the page attributes the unit to TSR (Baker joined TSR in Oct 1991; this is the 1992 Dark Sun project), five years before WotC acquired TSR. The claim's 'WotC' is the page's 'TSR'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'WotC scheduled' - the page says the MU was TSR's scheduling unit",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i214-228.json"
  },
  "225": {
   "index": 225,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Chicago Manual of Style is your main bible for WotC work.",
   "note": "Live Critical Hits page fetched raw. 'Lessons Learned 2: Yes the Chicago Manual of Style is a HUGE and expensive document. If you want to write for WotC, that's going to be your main bible.' Templates limb: 'While you are given templates to follow and a series of well-written guidelines...', 'Lessons Learned 3: If you're serious about being a freelancer, get MS-Word', and Lesson 4 on 'the WotC templates'. Editing limb verbatim: 'deliver top grade, engaging manuscripts that will require no or minimal editing in terms of grammar, syntax and spelling.' Date limb: post is 19 Dec 2012 describing assignments from early 2011 to early 2012.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i214-228.json"
  },
  "226": {
   "index": 226,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "D&D House Style Guide - WotC docs given to freelancers.",
   "note": "Live slyflourish.com page fetched raw. The bullet 'Focus on the here and now. Omit verbose backstories.' appears verbatim, but under the heading 'D&D House Style Guide', described as 'This free set of documents from Wizards of the Coast includes the house styles the D&D team gives to freelancers. It includes a short paper on adventure writing.' The WotC origin, the freelancer audience and the adventure-writing paper are all supported; the document name 'Adventure Guide' never appears anywhere on the page.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the source document's name - the page calls it the D&D House Style Guide, never 'Adventure Guide'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i214-228.json"
  },
  "227": {
   "index": 227,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Small beats large. Keep it brief. Be specific. Read aloud.",
   "note": "Live slyflourish.com page. Under 'Kobold's Guide to Game Design: Adventures' the bullets include 'Small beats large. Keep it brief.', 'Read your work aloud.' and 'Be specific.' Quote verbatim; all three precepts named in the claim are on the page and correctly attributed to the Kobold guide.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i214-228.json"
  },
  "228": {
   "index": 228,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Daunting task of fitting in, not contradicting existing lore.",
   "note": "Live koboldpress.com page fetched raw (Jeremy L.C. Jones interview, 26 May 2009). Greenwood's answer: 'Yet the shared world writer faces many constraints. Character descriptions, backstories, aims, and mannerisms are usually well-established, and the writer faces the daunting task of \"fitting in\" (perhaps even to a particular prose style) and not contradicting what may be a sizable body of existing lore.' Quote verbatim, speaker attribution explicit ('Greenwood:'), every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i214-228.json"
  },
  "229": {
   "index": 229,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "I'm smelling what the characters are smelling",
   "note": "Kobold Press page (Jeremy L.C. Jones, May 26, 2009) fetched raw, HTTP 200. Full passage: \"I plunge into the story and imagine that I'm there. I'm smelling what the characters are smelling, tasting what they're—well, you know the rest. What I then have to do is write down enough of what I can see, in my mind's eye, so that any reader will feel they too can see the moldering crypt ... or the glittering court of Zayang the Unimpeachable.\" Every limb — imagining presence, smelling, tasting, writing enough for the reader to see crypt and court — is on the page. Quote matches word for word (page uses a curly apostrophe).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i229-243.json"
  },
  "230": {
   "index": 230,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "I write about what publishers ask me to",
   "note": "Same Kobold Press page. \"I get the chance to get published because I write about what publishers ask me to ... I write about what those who set the assignment want me to. And do so in a way that is entertaining and leaving the reader wanting more.\" The 'in the way they want it' limb rests on the exam analogy plus the same page's 'constrained by editors, publisher word counts and formats' and 'fitting in (perhaps even to a particular prose style)'; 'entertainingly' is explicit.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i229-243.json"
  },
  "231": {
   "index": 231,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "never think your words are perfect",
   "note": "Paizo blog, James L. Sutter, Jun 20, 2013, fetched raw, HTTP 200. Advice-for-writers answer: \"rewrite anything that seems weak and awkward ... always work on clearly dated copies, but never think your words are perfect. Don't be afraid to rewrite.\" Quote is word for word; the page has lowercase 'never' mid-sentence where the claim capitalizes it. Both limbs present.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i229-243.json"
  },
  "232": {
   "index": 232,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The text is forced to stand on its own.",
   "note": "SA-Compendium.pdf fetched (23pp, 'Version 2.7 ©2021 Wizards of the Coast'), text extracted with pypdf. Page 1-2, RAW section: \"When I dwell on the RAW interpretation of a rule, I'm studying what the text says in context, without regard to the designers' intent. The text is forced to stand on its own. Whenever I consider a rule, I start with this perspective.\" Reads-first, in-context, intent-disregarded and stands-on-its-own limbs all present.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i229-243.json"
  },
  "233": {
   "index": 233,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "lay a foundation of rules that a DM could build on",
   "note": "Same PDF, 'The Role of Rules': \"no set of rules could reasonably account for every contingency. If the rules tried to do so, the game would become unplayable ... The direction we chose for the current edition was to lay a foundation of rules that a DM could build on, and we embraced the DM's role as the bridge between the things the rules address and the things they don't.\" Deliberateness ('the direction we chose') is explicit.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i229-243.json"
  },
  "234": {
   "index": 234,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "are not official rulings; they are advice",
   "note": "Same PDF, 'Official Rulings': \"Official rulings on how to interpret rules are made here in the Sage Advice Compendium ... The public statements of the D&D team, or anyone else at Wizards of the Coast, are not official rulings; they are advice. The tweets of Jeremy Crawford ... are sometimes a preview of rulings that appear here.\" Both limbs (compendium-only official; tweets/public statements are advice) present verbatim.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i229-243.json"
  },
  "235": {
   "index": 235,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "appendices summarizes all of the main rules terminology",
   "note": "D&D Beyond post 1745 fetched raw, HTTP 200; byline 'by Michael Galvis / Sep 17, 2024' matches. \"The 2024 Player's Handbook lowers the barrier to entry ... with an expansive play guide. In its early chapters, you'll learn key terms of the game, like what a D20 Test and Magic action are, and find examples of play, such as in combat, with sidebars that break down the game mechanics.\" The appendix limb is supported by \"One of the included appendices summarizes all of the main rules terminology\" — substance matches, though the article body never uses the word 'glossary' (that word appears only in site navigation).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i229-243.json"
  },
  "236": {
   "index": 236,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "uses some new terms, as shown in the Updated Terminology Table",
   "note": "D&D Beyond post 1810 fetched raw, HTTP 200; byline 'by DND Staff / Sep 3, 2024' matches. Under 'New Terminology' the Updated Terminology table lists all three named pairs: Race > Species, Cast a Spell action > Magic action, Ki > Monk's Focus (also Ki Points > Focus Points).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i229-243.json"
  },
  "237": {
   "index": 237,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Use bullet points and formatting to draw the DM's eye",
   "note": "D&D Beyond post 753 fetched raw, HTTP 200; byline 'by Shawn Merwin / Mar 25, 2020' matches. \"Use headers and subheaders to call out breaks in information. Use bullet points and formatting to draw the DM's eye to the right information at the right time.\" All three devices and the DM's-eye purpose are on the page; the page splits the purposes across two sentences (headers/subheaders 'call out breaks in information'; bullets and formatting 'draw the DM's eye'), which the claim merges into one.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the page splits the purposes the claim merges: headers and subheaders 'call out breaks in information', while it is bullets and formatting that 'draw the DM's eye to the right information at the right time'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "238": {
   "index": 238,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "short, evocative, and helpful to the DM and players",
   "note": "EN World thread page 10 fetched raw, HTTP 200. Post #96 by 'Burnside', Apr 8, 2025: \"Boxed text needs to be short, evocative, and helpful to the DM and players.\" Same post quotes his own 'first published adventure (2018)' boxed text and judges it \"filled with excessive detail, irrelevancies, and the area in general has too many distractions/too many little things going on.\" The 'Goodman Games designer' label rests on the same post's \"Area description for something I wrote this year for Goodman Games\" plus \"something I had to learn as a designer\" — no formal title is stated.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "no formal 'Goodman Games designer' title is stated on the page - the label rests on the post's 'Area description for something I wrote this year for Goodman Games' plus 'something I had to learn as a designer'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "239": {
   "index": 239,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "don't want to assume that people have access to the old books",
   "note": "CBR interview fetched raw, HTTP 200; 'Published Feb 5, 2025'. Crawford: \"In all of the books we have coming up, including the Monster Manual, we've made sure to fill them with lore nuggets. Because ... this game's been around for half-a-century, and we love it, but we don't want to assume that people have access to the old books.\" The page says 'have access to' rather than 'own', and says 'all of the books we have coming up' — broader than, and inclusive of, the 2025 core books (the adjacent paragraph does discuss 'the new Core books').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i229-243.json"
  },
  "240": {
   "index": 240,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that no D&D house style guide is published — the page says nothing about whether the style is published or unpublished",
   "trueWording": "making sure that everything matched the D&D house style",
   "note": "Grognardia fetched raw, HTTP 200; 'Wednesday, August 27, 2025', 'Interview: Mike Mearls'. Answer 8: \"Ian Pace developed the rules and nailed down the technical end of the manuscript, making sure that everything matched the D&D house style and that our rules synched with the D&D rulebooks.\" Quote verbatim; the collaborator, the manuscript (Cthulhu by Torchlight, a Chaosium/non-WotC 5e product per the introduction) and the existence of a D&D house style are all supported. The trailing inference 'even where none is published' is the researcher's gloss — the page makes no statement about publication of the style, and items 241-243 of this same chunk cite a circulating WotC style guide.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i229-243.json"
  },
  "241": {
   "index": 241,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "takes both of those works as its foundation",
   "note": "Style guide PDF fetched (11pp) and extracted with pypdf. p.1: \"We have two primary resources for the house style of DUNGEONS & DRAGONS products: The Chicago Manual of Style, 17th edition / Merriam-Webster's Collegiate Dictionary, 11th edition. This guide takes both of those works as its foundation, so it focuses on matters of style and spelling that are particular to D&D products, where we depart from or expand on the recommendations of those works.\" Note on dating: the running header reads 'Version 1.08a ©2018 Wizards of the Coast' and the document text contains no 2019 or January date — the 'updated January 2019' label comes only from the filename, as the source line itself flags. Version number and both style foundations verified.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i229-243.json"
  },
  "242": {
   "index": 242,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "apply to all D&D products, whether RPG books, board games, novels",
   "note": "Same PDF, p.1, section heading 'General Rules': \"The rules in this section apply to all D&D products, whether RPG books, board games, novels, or something else.\" Quote verbatim; the 'not just rulebooks' limb is exactly the page's enumeration.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i229-243.json"
  },
  "243": {
   "index": 243,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "we spell out and lowercase references to editions",
   "note": "Same PDF, rule spans p.1-p.2: \"Editions of the RPG. In a departure from previous usage, we spell out and lowercase references to editions of the roleplaying game. We write “fifth edition,” for instance, not “5th Edition.” ... Our internal documentation occasionally uses edition abbreviations like 4E and 5E. In other contexts, such abbreviations should appear only in a table, a conversion document, or in an informal text.\" Quote verbatim on p.2 as cited; both limbs supported. The page adds one carve-out the claim omits: internal documentation itself does use 4E/5E.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the claim's 'confined to tables, conversion documents, or informal text' omits the guide's own carve-out that internal documentation does use 4E/5E",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "244": {
   "index": 244,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Don't rely on Internet searches; beware prior-edition products",
   "note": "p.1 sidebar 'Texts about D&D Settings': quote verbatim. Wikis limb explicit ('Both Wikipedia and the Forgotten Realms Wiki ... contain some unreliable information'); prior-edition limb worded 'beware of relying solely on D&D products from previous editions'; positive rule is that main references are WotC guides or official fifth edition products.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i244-258.json"
  },
  "245": {
   "index": 245,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Think Lord of the Rings or Game of Thrones",
   "note": "p.2 'High Fantasy' sidebar: 'Elements of humor or tragedy are fine, so long as they are true to the facts of the brand and don't overtake the general tone. When in doubt, think about properties like the Lord of the Rings or Game of Thrones. If a situation would seem out of place there, it probably doesn't belong in D&D. For instance, there are moments of humor with the hobbits, but ring wraiths aren't used for gags.' Guide adds a second condition ('true to the facts of the brand') the claim omits, which does not weaken it.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i244-258.json"
  },
  "246": {
   "index": 246,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Lone knight less characteristic than an adventuring team",
   "note": "p.2 'High Fantasy': 'D&D is about small bands of characters embarking on adventures together. Teamwork and friendship are a huge part of what makes D&D distinct as a brand. The lone knight fighting a dragon is less characteristic of D&D than an adventuring team fighting a dragon.' Both limbs verbatim.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i244-258.json"
  },
  "247": {
   "index": 247,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Expect gender neutrality; readers' gender identity not assumed",
   "note": "p.2 'Gender': 'We expect gender neutrality whenever the gender of a person is unknown or irrelevant. We don't assume that our readers are of a particular gender identity.' Quote verbatim; both limbs on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i244-258.json"
  },
  "248": {
   "index": 248,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "In rules text, prefer second person for neutrality",
   "note": "p.2 'Achieving Gender Neutrality': 'In rules text, we prefer to achieve gender neutrality by writing in the second person whenever possible.' Quote verbatim; 'chiefly' matches the guide's 'we prefer ... whenever possible'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i244-258.json"
  },
  "249": {
   "index": 249,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Rely on it only lacking a reasonable or pleasing alternative",
   "note": "p.2: 'In a departure from our past usage, we allow the use of \"they\" and \"their\" as singular pronouns, but don't abuse this liberty. Rely on this device only when you have no other reasonable or pleasing alternative.' Quote verbatim. The 'newly' limb rests on the page's own 'In a departure from our past usage' in a document stamped 'Version 1.08a'; the page never says the change was introduced at 1.08a specifically.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the page never says the singular-'they' permission was introduced at v1.08a specifically; it says only 'In a departure from our past usage'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "250": {
   "index": 250,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Use 'he or she' sparingly; don't alternate he and she",
   "note": "p.2: 'Use \"he or she\" sparingly, and avoid \"he/she\" and \"s/he\" altogether. Also, don't alternate between \"he\" and \"she\" in an attempt to be inclusive.' All three limbs verbatim on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i244-258.json"
  },
  "251": {
   "index": 251,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Man and woman reserved for describing humans only",
   "note": "p.2 'Gender and Fantasy Races': '\"Man,\" \"woman,\" and their plurals are reserved for describing humans only. For example, we write \"male dwarf,\" not \"dwarf man\" or \"man dwarf.\"' Rule and quote verbatim. The '2019' dating is not on the page — every footer reads 'Version 1.08a (c)2018 Wizards of the Coast'; the January 2019 update date comes from the hosting file name, as the claim's own source field records.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the '2019' dating is not on the page: every footer reads 'Version 1.08a ©2018 Wizards of the Coast', and the January-2019 date comes only from the hosting filename",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "252": {
   "index": 252,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "A character doesn't necessarily follow our editorial style",
   "note": "p.3 opening line, continuing the p.2 'Gender and Fantasy Races' section: 'This rule can be broken in dialogue and first-person narrative, since a character doesn't necessarily follow our editorial style!' 'This rule' is the man/woman restriction, so the antecedent limb holds.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i244-258.json"
  },
  "253": {
   "index": 253,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Priestess only if part of a person's or in-world title",
   "note": "p.3 'Gender-Specific Suffixes': 'Avoid gender-specific suffixes whenever possible. For example, we write \"priest\" or \"actor\" whether the person is male or female. Use a word like \"priestess\" or \"actress\" only if it's part of a person's title or an in-world organizations' titles.' Quote verbatim; the claim's 'in-world title or organization name' compresses 'a person's title or an in-world organizations' titles'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'in-world title or organization name' compresses the guide's condition, which is 'only if it's part of a person's title or an in-world organizations' titles'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "254": {
   "index": 254,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "In-world uses of 'goddess' are fine",
   "note": "p.3: 'Another example: use \"god\" whether the deity is male or female. Again, in-world uses of \"goddess\" are fine.' Quote verbatim; both limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i244-258.json"
  },
  "255": {
   "index": 255,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Work down the hierarchy without skipping steps",
   "note": "p.3 'Headings': hierarchy printed as Chapter Title / Heading 1 / Heading 2 / Heading 3 / Inline Subhead [aka Heading 4], introduced by 'Work your way down the heading hierarchy without skipping steps'; then 'Use a heading for a structural reason, not an aesthetic one.' Quote verbatim; both limbs on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i244-258.json"
  },
  "256": {
   "index": 256,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Use a period, an exclamation point, or a question mark",
   "note": "p.3 'Inline Subheads': 'The lowest level of our heading hierarchy is the inline subhead. It functions as our heading 4. Contrary to past D&D usage, this subhead should be followed by terminal punctuation, not a colon. Use a period, an exclamation point, or a question mark.' Bold-italics limb: 'If you don't have access to the style, set the subhead in bold italics to distinguish it from text that is simply bold' (p.3), confirmed on p.4 ('the entire inline subhead is bold italics').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i244-258.json"
  },
  "257": {
   "index": 257,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that bold is the ONLY emphasis device - the page prescribes bold for emphasis in place of an inline subhead, and nowhere states or implies that it is the sole permitted emphasis device",
   "trueWording": "Use bold for such emphasis, unless it's in the heading",
   "note": "p.3: 'Finally, don't use an inline subhead when you simply want to emphasize a word or phrase. Use bold for such emphasis, unless the emphasized term or phrase appears in the section's heading. Then, no emphasis is required. For example, if a Heading 3 says \"Ki Points,\" don't bold \"ki points\" in the associated running text.' Quote verbatim and the heading-term limb fully supported; the exclusivity quantifier 'only' is the claim's, not the guide's - the guide's italics rules elsewhere (pp.4, 6-7) govern titles, spells, words-as-words and unspoken discourse, and it never rules italics out for emphasis in so many words.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i244-258.json"
  },
  "258": {
   "index": 258,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "If it must follow a section to be intelligible, probably not a sidebar",
   "note": "p.3 'Sidebars': 'Avoid overusing sidebars. If a sidebar must follow a particular section of text to be intelligible, it probably shouldn't be a sidebar. The best sidebars can appear anywhere on a page and still make sense.' Quote verbatim; both limbs supported, with the guide hedging 'probably shouldn't be a sidebar' and leaving the remedy (body text) implicit rather than naming it.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the body-text remedy is the claimant's: the guide hedges 'it probably shouldn't be a sidebar' and leaves the remedy unnamed",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "259": {
   "index": 259,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "we use “toward,” not “towards,”",
   "note": "Quote verbatim p.4 'Variant Spellings'. All limbs on page: 'We use American style for spelling and punctuation'; 'If a word has variants in Webster's, we use the one printed first (regardless of alphabetical order), except as noted in the D&D word list'; both examples toward/backward present. Merriam-Webster's Collegiate 11th named as a primary house resource on p.1.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i259-273.json"
  },
  "260": {
   "index": 260,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "We require the serial comma",
   "note": "Quote verbatim p.4 under heading 'The Serial Comma'. 'Require' = mandatory; the Mystra/Elminster example shows the ambiguity rationale. '(Oxford)' in the claim is only a synonym for 'serial comma', not a separate assertion the page must carry.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i259-273.json"
  },
  "261": {
   "index": 261,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "We encourage the use of contractions formed with “not,”",
   "note": "Quote verbatim p.4. Page continues 'such as \"don't,\" \"can't,\" and \"wasn't\" (see Chicago 5.105). Use other contractions with care, especially any contraction that can be misread.' Both limbs — negatives encouraged, others used with care where misreadable — are explicit.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i259-273.json"
  },
  "262": {
   "index": 262,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "non-elf, non-dwarf",
   "note": "Quote verbatim p.4. Page: 'The prefix is hyphenated in front of many of our fantasy races: non-elf, non-dwarf, non-orc, non-drow (but nonhuman, as in Webster's).' Numeral limb also explicit: 'we don't hyphenate a compound composed of a numeral and an abbreviation. So, it's a \"100 gp treasure,\" not a \"100-gp treasure.\"' The page's hedge 'many of our fantasy races' is exactly the nonhuman exception the claim itself carries.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i259-273.json"
  },
  "263": {
   "index": 263,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "We follow the approach in the seventeenth edition",
   "note": "Quote verbatim p.4 under 'Possessives', followed on-page by '(see Chicago 7.16–29)'. The unpronounced-s limb is explicit: 'particularly those that involve words that end with an unpronounced s, as in \"Descartes.\"'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i259-273.json"
  },
  "264": {
   "index": 264,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "is italicized when used as a word",
   "note": "Quote verbatim p.4. English limb explicit: 'Our style for words used as words is to enclose them in quotes if the words are English (see Chicago 7.63).' 'real or invented' matches 'A non-English word, whether from the real world or a D&D world, is italicized when used as a word.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i259-273.json"
  },
  "265": {
   "index": 265,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "introduce it with a complete sentence ending with a colon",
   "note": "Quote verbatim p.4. Page scopes it to 'a bulleted list—or any other vertical list, such as a numbered one', and adds 'We begin each list item with a capital letter. Use closing punctuation in an item only if the list is composed of complete sentences.' All three limbs present.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i259-273.json"
  },
  "266": {
   "index": 266,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "we spell out whole numbers from zero to one hundred",
   "note": "Quote verbatim p.5, preceded by 'We follow the general rule for numerals that is presented in Chicago 9.2.' All four named exceptions appear in the page's bulleted exception list: units of measure {6'3\", 5 gp, 3 lb.}, parts of a book {chapter 2, page 34, table 9}, dates, and times. 'In prose' is the page's own contrast: 'Numerals in Rules. In rules writing, we use numerals more often.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i259-273.json"
  },
  "267": {
   "index": 267,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Use the word “percent,” rather than the symbol %",
   "note": "Quote verbatim p.5. Exception limb explicit: 'You can break this rule in a table, as well as in a list where space is tight.' Dice limb explicit: 'Also, the abbreviation we use for percentile dice is d100, not d%.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i259-273.json"
  },
  "268": {
   "index": 268,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Use cross-references judiciously",
   "note": "Quote verbatim p.5 ('They are useful tools that can be easily overused'). Chapter limb p.5: 'When you refer to a chapter, use its number.' Subsection/sidebar and table limbs p.6: 'When you refer to a subsection, including a sidebar, use its title in quotes. A table title is not enclosed in quotes.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i259-273.json"
  },
  "269": {
   "index": 269,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "We use letters, not numbers, for appendices",
   "note": "Quote verbatim p.5. Cited-like-chapters limb explicit: 'References to appendices should follow the same principles that we follow for chapter references', with the on-page example '• See appendix A for definition of each condition.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i259-273.json"
  },
  "270": {
   "index": 270,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "We avoid page references as much as possible",
   "note": "Quote verbatim, though it sits on p.6 of the PDF, not the p.5 the claim record cites — a locator slip, not a substance error. Both reasons on page: '(they must be added by hand during galley review), and they invite error'; and 'Referencing a particular page in another product is especially ill-advised; that product's pagination could change in a reprint or a new edition.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i259-273.json"
  },
  "271": {
   "index": 271,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "a change from our previous policy",
   "note": "Quote 'Aesthetics and clarity should dictate appropriate usage' is verbatim on p.6, and the discretion limbs are fully supported: 'In D&D fiction, authors are free to use italics, quotation marks, or no distinguishing typography to present a character's thoughts or psionic communication (see Chicago 13.43).' But the page says only '(This is a change from our previous policy.)' — the word 'reversal' is absent, and the guide never states what the previous policy was, so the claim's characterization of the direction of the change is not established by the page.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'a reversal of the older policy' — page says only 'a change from our previous policy' and never states the prior rule",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i259-273.json"
  },
  "272": {
   "index": 272,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "“She is in Elysium.”",
   "note": "Quote verbatim p.6 under 'Planes of Existence'. Both limbs explicit: 'A character is on a plane of existence. For example, \"He was trapped on the Plane of Fire.\" But a character is in a plane when it is referred to by name without the word \"plane.\"'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i259-273.json"
  },
  "273": {
   "index": 273,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Action names {the Attack action, the Dash action}",
   "note": "Quote verbatim p.6 in the 'Terms to Capitalize' list. Every one of the eight categories the claim names is on that list: Ability scores {Strength, Intelligence}; Action names; Class features {Sneak Attack, Rage}; Feats {Loremaster}; Languages {Common, Dwarvish, Elvish}; Planes of existence {the Abyss, the Nine Hells}; Skills {Arcana, Perception}; Traits in races and monsters {Aquatic, Keen Senses}. Scoped on page to the RPG: 'In the RPG, certain terms receive special typographic treatment.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i259-273.json"
  },
  "274": {
   "index": 274,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Terms to Italicize: Artifacts, Magic items, Spells {magic missile}",
   "note": "Quote exact on p.6 under 'Terms to Italicize' (artifacts, magic items, spells all listed). The second limb sits one page later, p.7: 'The italicized terms are treated as titles... Don't confuse one of these titles with an effect it creates (see Chicago 8.174). For example, the wall of fire spell produces a wall of fire, not a wall of fire (the latter would imply an infinite loop).' Both limbs supported; only the page cite (p.6) is narrow for the second limb.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i274-288.json"
  },
  "275": {
   "index": 275,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Bold only first appearance; no text between number and stat block name",
   "note": "Quote exact on p.6. Both limbs explicit there: 'Set a monster's name in bold only the first time it appears in an encounter area or other subsection' and 'Make sure no text gets between the number of monsters appearing and the name of the stat block.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i274-288.json"
  },
  "276": {
   "index": 276,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Numerals for points, damage, game durations, tactical distances",
   "note": "Quote exact on p.7 under 'Numerals in Rules Text'. Every limb explicit: 'Use numerals for points, scores, damage expressions, and any game trait with a numerical value'; 'units of time that quantify the duration of a game effect'; 'units of distance that quantify the size or extent of game effects' plus 'dimensions and distances... on a tactical scale'. The narrative counterexamples are the guide's own: 'the characters slept for six hours' and 'The two cities are fifty miles apart.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i274-288.json"
  },
  "277": {
   "index": 277,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Verb Usage list: cast, take damage, make a saving throw, finish a rest",
   "note": "Quote exact on p.7. The 'Verb Usage' list carries all five pairings: 'You cast a spell.', 'You take damage.', 'You make a saving throw, and it succeeds or fails.', 'You finish a short or long rest.', 'You score a critical hit.' (guide's rest wording is 'a short or long rest').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i274-288.json"
  },
  "278": {
   "index": 278,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Succeed on a DC 15 Strength check, not make one, unless outcomes follow",
   "note": "Quote exact on p.7 under 'Making a Roll vs. Succeeding on One'. The unless-clause is the guide's own: \"Don't write, 'You must make a DC 15 Strength check...' unless that sentence is immediately followed by a description of what happens on a success or failure.\" Claim drops the ability name (Strength) but adds nothing.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i274-288.json"
  },
  "279": {
   "index": 279,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Sparingly, preferably only in a stat block; 4E sense abandoned",
   "note": "Quote exact on p.7 under 'Saving Throws'. Both limbs explicit: 'use it sparingly, preferably only in a stat block or when preceded by \"saving throw\"' and 'In 4E, we used \"save\" as a synonym for \"successful saving throw.\" We have abandoned that practice.' The claim omits the second permitted context (when preceded by 'saving throw'), which narrows rather than overstates.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i274-288.json"
  },
  "280": {
   "index": 280,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Die-number sense abandoned; result = roll plus modifiers; total preferred",
   "note": "Quote exact on p.8 under '\"Result\" and Die Rolls'. All three limbs: 'In 3E and 4E, the word \"result\" was sometimes used as a synonym for \"the number rolled on a die.\" We have abandoned that practice'; '\"Result\" can legitimately refer to the sum of a die roll and any modifiers'; 'We often prefer the word \"total\" in such a context.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i274-288.json"
  },
  "281": {
   "index": 281,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Magic for objects; magical used more often for other things; never magical item",
   "note": "Quote exact on p.8. Limbs 1 and 3 are verbatim rules ('a magic item, a magic sword, a magic trap'; 'should never be \"magical item\"'). Limb 2 is a preference, not an absolute, in the source: 'The adjective can be used for other things, but we use \"magical\" more often than \"magic\" for them' — the claim's clean split states the guide's default correctly but drops the 'more often' hedge.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the 'magical for other things' half is a preference, not the absolute the claim's clean split states: 'we use \"magical\" more often than \"magic\" for them'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "282": {
   "index": 282,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Avoid points of damage: write 10 fire damage, not 10 points of fire damage",
   "note": "Quote exact on p.8 under 'Points of Damage'. The guide's example is the claim verbatim: 'write, \"The spell deals 10 fire damage,\" not \"The spell deals 10 points of fire damage.\"' Claim's 'never' hardens the heading verb 'Avoid', but the worked example is a flat write-X-not-Y.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i274-288.json"
  },
  "283": {
   "index": 283,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "We like to keep the die expression with the damage type",
   "note": "Quote exact on p.8 under 'Extra Damage'. Rationale limb explicit: 'We write \"an extra 1d4 fire damage,\" not \"1d4 extra fire damage.\" We like to keep the die expression with the damage type.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i274-288.json"
  },
  "284": {
   "index": 284,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Proficient in a skill/language/activity; with a tool/weapon/armor",
   "note": "Quote exact on p.8 under 'Proficiency'. Both bullets match limb for limb: 'proficient... in a skill, language, or other activity that is learnable and repeatable' and 'proficient... with a tool, weapon, type of armor, or other object', with the example 'proficient in Elvish and Arcana... proficient with longswords and heavy armor.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i274-288.json"
  },
  "285": {
   "index": 285,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Noun not verb; breakable in an informal context such as a blog",
   "note": "Quote exact on p.8 under '\"Dungeon Master\" Isn't a Verb'. The exception in the source is broader than the claim's: 'This rule can be broken in an informal context, such as in a blog or a web article that has a conversational tone' — informal context generally, with blog/web article as examples. The claim is stricter than the guide, not unsupported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i274-288.json"
  },
  "286": {
   "index": 286,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Week is ten days in the Realms, a tenday; use days, years allowed",
   "note": "Quote exact on p.8 under 'Avoid \"Week\" and \"Month\"'. Every limb: 'The lengths of weeks and months vary in different D&D worlds. A week is ten days long in the Forgotten Realms, for instance, and is called a tenday'; \"don't express durations or other lengths of time in weeks or months when you're writing rules\"; 'The length of the year varies as well, but you may use years for durations and the like.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i274-288.json"
  },
  "287": {
   "index": 287,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Beware human for mortal; be cautious with humanoid, a creature type",
   "note": "Quote exact on p.8 under 'Misusing \"Human\" and \"Humanoid\"'. Second limb explicit: 'be cautious when using the word \"humanoid,\" given the fact that it refers to a creature type. For example, a giant has humanlike features, but it isn't a humanoid in the game.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i274-288.json"
  },
  "288": {
   "index": 288,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Word list: dwarves (not dwarfs), mithral (not mithril), staffs, war band",
   "note": "Quote exact on p.9. Every listed item found in the same word list on p.9: 'dwarves (not dwarfs)', 'mithral (not mithril)', 'staffs', 'war band (not warband)', 'drow (singular and plural)', 'kenku (singular and plural)', 'yuan-ti (singular and plural)'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i274-288.json"
  },
  "289": {
   "index": 289,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "goodsir (a form of address, not capped)",
   "note": "Raw PDF text extracted (pdfminer + pypdf). p.9 'Forgotten Realms Terms' list carries verbatim 'Goodman/Goodwoman (a title or form of address, usu. capped)', 'godsforsaken (established in the Realms...)', 'goodsir (a form of address, not capped)'; the list continues on p.10 with 'the Realms (should be preceded by “the Forgotten Realms”)'. All four limbs supported. Only hedge: source says 'usu. capped', claim says 'capped'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i289-303.json"
  },
  "290": {
   "index": 290,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "godsforsaken (established in the Realms, but not necessary in other polytheistic settings; “godforsaken” has no necessary connection to monotheism)",
   "note": "Quote fragment appears word for word on p.9 of the v1.08a PDF; every limb of the claim (established Realms form, not necessary elsewhere, godforsaken has no necessary monotheistic connection) is the source's own wording.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i289-303.json"
  },
  "291": {
   "index": 291,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "use “group,” “band,” “company,” or, especially, “party.”",
   "note": "p.9 'Adventuring Parties' sidebar, verbatim: 'The word “fellowship” has strong connections with The Lord of the Rings and doesn’t represent the typical D&D adventuring party well... D&D adventuring companies are often fractious or more mercenary than the word “fellowship” implies. In most in-world contexts and all other contexts, use “group,” “band,” “company,” or, especially, “party.”' All three limbs (Tolkien association, fractious/mercenary, four preferred words) supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i289-303.json"
  },
  "292": {
   "index": 292,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "NPC nonplayer character",
   "note": "pypdf page-10 reconstruction gives the table row verbatim: 'HD Hit Die/Dice \\n hp hit points \\n NPC nonplayer character'. Every abbreviation named in the claim is present in the p.10 Abbreviations tables: PH, DMG, MM (Brand/Product), gp (Coin), d100 (Die), AC, DC, DM, XP, HD, hp, NPC (Other Term).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i289-303.json"
  },
  "293": {
   "index": 293,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Don’t use “they” or “their” as singular pronouns, except in an informal context like dialogue.",
   "note": "v1.04a PDF confirmed (footer 'Version 1.04a ©2013 Wizards of the Coast'); quote is verbatim on p.2 under 'Achieving Gender Neutrality'. Both limbs (ban; informal-context/dialogue exception) supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i289-303.json"
  },
  "294": {
   "index": 294,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "We also assume that our readership includes both men and women.",
   "note": "Quote verbatim on p.2 of v1.04a. The second limb checks out against v1.08a: the 2019 guide has zero occurrences of 'readership' and its Gender section instead reads 'We don’t assume that our readers are of a particular gender identity.' Note the claim's inline rendering 'we don’t assume a gender identity' is a compression, not the 2019 wording.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i289-303.json"
  },
  "295": {
   "index": 295,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Five of the six are new headings; 'Bulleted Lists' renames v1.04a's 'Vertical Lists'.",
   "note": "Both raw texts extracted and grepped. Heading-string counts: Gender-Specific Suffixes 0/2, Headings 0/4, Sidebars 0/3, Bulleted Lists 0/1, Appendix References 0/1, Adventuring Parties 0/1 (2013/2019). v1.04a's full heading roster was enumerated and contains none of the six strings; its only 'sidebar'/'heading' mentions are passing references inside 'Subsection/Table References' and 'Inline Subheads', and it has zero occurrences of 'appendix', 'appendices', 'suffix' or 'fellowship'. So five limbs are genuinely new sections; the sixth is a rename, which the claim's word 'Sections' over-states even though its parenthetical scopes verification to heading presence. No quote field, so VERBATIM is inapplicable in any case.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "“Bulleted Lists” as a section absent from v1.04a — v1.04a has “Vertical Lists” covering the same ground (complete-sentence lead-in + colon, capitalized items, closing punctuation only for complete sentences); it is a rename, not a new section.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i289-303.json"
  },
  "296": {
   "index": 296,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "creator Kim Mohan; lastModifiedBy 'Bauer, Judy'; modified 2021-07-06; Company Wizards of the Coast.",
   "note": "Zip fetched (HTTP 200, 1,624,152 bytes) and expanded; 'Dungeoncraft Pack/DnD House Style Guide.docx' present, zip entry dated 12-04-2023 matching the pack URL. docProps/core.xml verbatim: <dc:creator>Kim Mohan</dc:creator>, <cp:lastModifiedBy>Bauer, Judy</cp:lastModifiedBy>, <dcterms:created>2016-03-07T20:43:00Z</dcterms:created>, <dcterms:modified>2021-07-06T18:09:00Z</dcterms:modified>; docProps/app.xml <Company>Wizards of the Coast</Company>. 2021-07-06 postdates the v1.08a PDF (©2018, 'updated January 2019'). Empty quote field, so graded on substance.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i289-303.json"
  },
  "297": {
   "index": 297,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "merriam-webster.com",
   "note": "docx body line 5, in the two-primary-resources list: 'The Chicago Manual of Style, 17th edition / merriam-webster.com'. The contrast limb holds: the docx has zero occurrences of 'Collegiate', while v1.08a p.1 reads 'Merriam-Webster’s Collegiate Dictionary, 11th edition'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i289-303.json"
  },
  "298": {
   "index": 298,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "we can write “dwarf man” or “gnome woman.”",
   "note": "docx 'Gender and Fantasy Races': '“Man,” “woman,” and their plurals are fine for describing the gender of people. For example, we can write “dwarf man” or “gnome woman.”' The reversal limb is confirmed against v1.08a p.2, which reads 'reserved for describing humans only. For example, we write “male dwarf,” not “dwarf man” or “man dwarf.”' — the exact phrase the docx now permits.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i289-303.json"
  },
  "299": {
   "index": 299,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "you must ensure that the pronoun’s antecedent is clear",
   "note": "docx: 'We allow the use of “they,” “their,” “them,” and “themself” when referring to a person whose gender is unknown. But you must ensure that the pronoun’s antecedent is clear if more than one person appears in the context.' All four pronouns present; docx has zero occurrences of 'abuse this liberty', which v1.08a carries ('but don’t abuse this liberty. Rely on this device only when you have no other reasonable or pleasing alternative').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i289-303.json"
  },
  "300": {
   "index": 300,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "In rules text, use “it.”",
   "note": "docx 'Pronoun Preference' — 'Short Version: In running text, use a person’s preferred pronoun: he, she, or they. Whatever pronoun you use, ensure that its antecedent is clear. In rules text, use “it.”' Both limbs verbatim.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i289-303.json"
  },
  "301": {
   "index": 301,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "A beholder never keeps its promises.",
   "note": "docx 'Pronouns for Generic Creatures', descriptive-text bullets: 'Use they/them pronouns for Humanoids and other sapient creatures that have gender identity, but when we don’t know their gender identity...'; 'Use “it” otherwise (animals, cosmic horrors, extraplanar creatures, etc.)'; and under '“They/them” in Descriptive Text' — 'But Also… vampires (Undead that retain their identity)'. The quote is the closing example under '“It” in Descriptive Text'. Every limb, vampires included, supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i289-303.json"
  },
  "302": {
   "index": 302,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "If using they/them pronouns would be ambiguous, reword to avoid using pronouns.",
   "note": "Quote verbatim in the docx. The plural limb is supported twice: the 'Avoid Ambiguity' example offers 'OR “Merfolk attack elves on sight.”', and 'Sidestep Edge Cases' gives 'Blorvents are friendly, but they rust easily. (switching to plural to avoid choosing between “they” and “it”)'. Worth noting the first-listed cure is repeating the noun ('the merfolk immediately attacks'), with the plural as the alternative.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i289-303.json"
  },
  "303": {
   "index": 303,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Such exceptions should be rare.",
   "note": "docx 'Limited Exceptions': 'On occasion, we might make exceptions to the guidelines above to achieve a specific effect. For example, characters might use gendered pronouns to talk about an animated doll, or we might use “it” to emphasize the inhumanity of a horrible monster (example). Such exceptions should be rare.' The word 'sapient' is not literally used but is entailed — an 'it' is an exception only for a creature the guidelines above would otherwise give they/them.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i289-303.json"
  },
  "304": {
   "index": 304,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "He/Him: Baphomet, Strahd; She/Her: Lolth, Tasha",
   "note": "Raw docx (DnD House Style Guide.docx, sha256 5bbfff1c…) extracted from the pack ZIP. Under 'Pronouns for Specific Individuals': 'For a character in canon (famous hero, demon lord, powerful celestial, etc.), use the established pronouns for their identity, regardless of their species or type.' followed verbatim by 'He/Him: Baphomet, Strahd' and 'She/Her: Lolth, Tasha'. Every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i304-318.json"
  },
  "305": {
   "index": 305,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Creature type (Humanoid, Fey, Undead)",
   "note": "Quote is verbatim in the 'Terms to Capitalize' inventory of the 2021 docx (docProps/core.xml: created Kim Mohan 2016-03-07, lastModifiedBy 'Bauer, Judy', modified 2021-07-06 — source attribution confirmed). But the docx makes no comparison to any 2019 PDF, and the ZIP contains no 2019 style-guide PDF (only DnD Standards.pdf, PDF title 'D&D Standards', CreationDate D:20200716). The 'adds … a convention the 2019 PDF lacks' limb is unverifiable from this URL.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that this is an addition the 2019 PDF lacks",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i304-318.json"
  },
  "306": {
   "index": 306,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Bold stat block names … this indicates the term is a game object",
   "note": "Verbatim: 'Bold stat block names in running text to indicate what stat block to use for an encounter—this indicates the term is a game object.' The facing examples list 'Roman' cases including 'Strahd likes to make vampire spawn in his free time.' Both limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i304-318.json"
  },
  "307": {
   "index": 307,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The word “attack” confuses many of our readers",
   "note": "Section 'Attack vs. Attack Roll': 'When an effect is triggered by an attack roll hitting or missing, use the term “attack roll” in the effect’s text, not just “attack.” The word “attack” confuses many of our readers, who often read it with its English meaning instead of as a rules term.' Fireball limb also explicit: 'there are readers who think fireball hits targets'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i304-318.json"
  },
  "308": {
   "index": 308,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "use one of our official areas of effect: cone, cube, cylinder, line, or sphere",
   "note": "Section '“Within X Feet”': 'that literally means the effect reaches anything within that area, even if there’s total cover in the way. If you want to limit the reach of such an effect, require that the target be able to see or hear the effect’s source or that the target can’t be behind total cover. Alternatively, use one of our official areas of effect…'. All limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i304-318.json"
  },
  "309": {
   "index": 309,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "When referring to a monster’s stat block, call it a stat block",
   "note": "Section '“Statistics” vs. “Stat Block”': 'Don’t call it the creature’s statistics. Writing “game statistics” is acceptable if you’re referring to the monster’s crunch, rather than its story material, but use “stat block” when you mean that box of game information about the monster.' Both limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i304-318.json"
  },
  "310": {
   "index": 310,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "We use letter-by-letter alphabetization. When comparing, ignore spaces",
   "note": "Section 'Alphabetical Organization': 'lists, spells, stat blocks in a bestiary, etc. should all be organized alphabetically. We use letter-by-letter alphabetization. When comparing, ignore spaces…' plus 'Microsoft Word Warning. Microsoft Word’s handy alphabetization tool (Table < Sort) sorts paragraphs word-by-word, NOT letter-by-letter!' All three limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i304-318.json"
  },
  "311": {
   "index": 311,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "insectile (not insectoid); blood red (contra Merriam-Websters)",
   "note": "All eight items are verbatim in the docx word list: 'aasimar (singular and plural)', 'blood red (contra Merriam-Websters; we’re matching CMOS 7.89)', 'cyclopes (plural of cyclops)', 'gold (made of gold)' / 'golden (the color of gold)', 'insectile (not insectoid)', 'parlay (exploit or transform)' / 'parley (confer or negotiate)', 'tabaxi (singular and plural)', 'tenant' / 'tenet'. The 'adds' limb — that the 2019 PDF lacked them — is nowhere in this document and no 2019 style-guide PDF is in the pack.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that these entries are 2021 additions relative to the 2019 PDF",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i304-318.json"
  },
  "312": {
   "index": 312,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Anyone who touches the orb regains 3 hit points.",
   "note": "Quote is verbatim under 'Numerals in Rules Text', lowercase 'hit points'; the abbreviations table likewise reads 'hp / hit points' lowercase, so the first limb is well supported. The comparison to the 2024 rules glossary — and therefore the conclusion that the 2021 guide 'predates 2024 house style' — is not on this page; the document is silent on 2024.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that the 2024 rules glossary capitalizes “Hit Points”",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i304-318.json"
  },
  "313": {
   "index": 313,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Boxed text should be used very sparingly—only when a particularly important event truly calls for it",
   "note": "Raw docx (DC Adventure Format v1.3.docx, sha256 73965593…; core.xml: creator Claire Hoffman, lastModifiedBy Ma'at Crook, created 2023-11-07, modified 2023-11-16 — attribution confirmed). Boxed-text instruction continues 'Don’t use it to describe rooms or normalcy.' The document is the D&D Adventurers League (Dungeoncraft, code '$$-DC-$$$-$$') adventure template, so the 'AL adventure format' framing holds.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i304-318.json"
  },
  "314": {
   "index": 314,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "no more than one or two brief and descriptive sentences",
   "note": "Same boxed-text instruction: 'Additionally, it should be short and to the point, no more than one or two brief and descriptive sentences.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i304-318.json"
  },
  "315": {
   "index": 315,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the boxed text should only provide a narrative for exciting events",
   "note": "'The body of the counter [sic — encounter] should be found in the headings below; the boxed text should only provide a narrative for exciting events.' Both limbs supported; note the source's own typo 'counter' for 'encounter'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i304-318.json"
  },
  "316": {
   "index": 316,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "“Go away!” the puppet snaps.",
   "note": "The only narrative boxed text in the template (area 'A. The Hidden Room') reads: 'A greasy, irate-looking dwarf in a stained leather apron with a bulging pocket stands between the contraptions. He clutches a handaxe in one hand and has a sock puppet on the other. “Go away!” the puppet snaps.' — exactly two descriptive sentences plus the one-line spoken tag, with concrete nouns (apron, pocket, handaxe, sock puppet).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i304-318.json"
  },
  "317": {
   "index": 317,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Keywords should be CAPITALIZED and BOLDED the FIRST time they appear in the Background",
   "note": "In the 'Background' section: 'Keywords should be CAPITALIZED and BOLDED the FIRST time they appear in the Background. This alerts the DM to the adventure’s more important aspects.' All limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i304-318.json"
  },
  "318": {
   "index": 318,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "doesn’t bloat the background with backstory that isn’t ultimately important",
   "note": "'There’s a fine line between two little [sic] background and too much exposition. Try to stay on the side of the line that doesn’t bloat the background with backstory that isn’t ultimately important.' Both limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i304-318.json"
  },
  "319": {
   "index": 319,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Each part gets one-to-two sentences; \"This is Story Objective A.\"",
   "note": "Overview template, DC Adventure Format v1.3 p.'Adventure Primer': Parts 1-4 each read \"Each part of the adventure should have its own entry of one to two sentences describing the part. This is Story Objective A/B/C/D.\" A margin comment adds \"Each part should have a Story Objective entry that describes the objective as a single sentence.\" Quote verbatim; both limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i319-333.json"
  },
  "320": {
   "index": 320,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "NPC Snapshot \"is titled accordingly\"; examples \"Stoic Guardian\", \"Ruthless Conqueror\"",
   "note": "Quote verbatim. The NPC block is exactly as claimed: an opening paragraph giving \"a brief glimpse into the NPC's backstory and physical appearance,\" then \"What They Want. This single-sentence paragraph...\" and \"NPC Snapshot. This single-sentence paragraph... and is titled accordingly.\" The document states only that the Snapshot is retitled to suit the NPC; it never prescribes a length. Both worked examples happen to be two words, so \"two-word\" is induced from the examples, not a stated rule.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the epithet is specifically two words - the page says only \"titled accordingly\" and gives two examples that happen to be two words",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i319-333.json"
  },
  "321": {
   "index": 321,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Area Information entries: Dimensions and Terrain, Lighting, Sounds and Smells",
   "note": "Quote \"Dimensions and Terrain.\" appears twice - in the worked example under an Area Information heading (followed by Lighting) and in the margin comment \"Area Information breaks down the area itself. There are usually three standard entries here: Dimensions and Terrain... Lighting... Sounds and Smells.\" Inline-subhead limb supported: \"If under a heading 3, use inline subheads without the Area/Creature Information headings.\"",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i319-333.json"
  },
  "322": {
   "index": 322,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Creature names and keyed objects such as traps or secret doors are bold",
   "note": "Quote \"Use boldface judiciously.\" verbatim, in: \"names of creatures or other significant game objects such as traps or secret doors are in bold. Use boldface judiciously. With the exception of creature names, it usually applies to important objects that are keyed on the map.\" Every limb supported, including the keyed-object restriction.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i319-333.json"
  },
  "323": {
   "index": 323,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Sidebar gives background to help run the location; Playing the Pillars excepted",
   "note": "Quote verbatim: \"It should provide the DM with background information that may help run the location or event. With the exception of Playing the Pillars (see example, below), it shouldn't provide directly applicable information.\" The page's prohibition is on directly applicable *information*, which is broader than the claim's \"rules content\" and so entails it.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i319-333.json"
  },
  "324": {
   "index": 324,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"Playing the Pillars. Suggestions for this encounter:\" then Combat, Exploration, Social",
   "note": "Quote verbatim in the worked encounter, followed by Combat / Exploration / Social entries. The optionality in \"can carry\" is supported by the margin comment \"If you include this entry, each pillar of play should be represented\" and \"99% of the time, these entries should be under a Creature Information heading.\"",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i319-333.json"
  },
  "325": {
   "index": 325,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Cover: \"CONTENT WARNING: $\"; credits: \"Sensitivity Lead: TBD\"",
   "note": "Quote verbatim. The cover block runs Adventure Title / Subtitle / description / \"A Two/Four -Hour Adventure for Tier $ Characters.\" / \"CONTENT WARNING: $\", then the Credits list containing \"Sensitivity Lead: TBD\" among Writer, Editor, Graphic Design. File metadata confirms the 2023 dating: created 2023-11-07 by Claire Hoffman, last modified 2023-11-16 by Ma'at Crook.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i319-333.json"
  },
  "326": {
   "index": 326,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "Consult Chicago 17th; preferred dictionary Merriam-Webster's Collegiate 11th",
   "note": "No quote was supplied with the claim, so the verbatim grade cannot apply; the substance is stated almost word for word on the first page: \"Consult The Chicago Manual of Style (17th edition) if you have questions about punctuation, grammar, and other matters of writing style. Our preferred dictionary is Merriam-Webster's Collegiate Dictionary (11th edition).\"",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i319-333.json"
  },
  "327": {
   "index": 327,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"This Boxed Text style is used only for text that a DM is meant to read to the players.\"",
   "note": "Quote verbatim in the style-demonstration line of the .dotx. \"Solely\" matches the page's \"only\". Metadata confirms the source: dc:creator \"Crawford, Jeremy\", created 2019-09-30, modified 2019-10-08.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i319-333.json"
  },
  "328": {
   "index": 328,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Unless excerpting a book where the quotes appear in the original",
   "note": "Quote verbatim: \"An epigraph shouldn't be enclosed in quotation marks, unless you're excerpting a passage from a book and the quotes appear in the original.\" Both limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i319-333.json"
  },
  "329": {
   "index": 329,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"If it must immediately follow something, don't make it a sidebar; integrate it into the body copy.\"",
   "note": "Quote verbatim under Sidebar Body: \"ask yourself whether the sidebar must immediately follow a section of text or whether it can go anywhere on a spread. If it must immediately follow something, don't make it a sidebar; integrate it into the body copy.\" Both limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i319-333.json"
  },
  "330": {
   "index": 330,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"Profanity. Don't use profanity.\" plus \"not without approval\"",
   "note": "Quote verbatim in the standards list. The approval carve-out limb is supported by the RATING section: \"D&D products can violate certain standards, but not without approval,\" and \"PG-rated content allows things we normally don't, such as nudity and profanity.\"",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i319-333.json"
  },
  "331": {
   "index": 331,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "G-rated, slides toward PG; Rick & Morty PG-13 needed approval",
   "note": "Quote verbatim. Every limb on the page: \"When it deals with mature themes or features scenes of graphic violence, it slides toward being a PG-rated experience\" and \"The Rick & Morty boxed set, which contains profanity and glamorizes substance abuse, is an example of just such a product. It is rated PG-13,\" following \"can violate certain standards, but not without approval.\"",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i319-333.json"
  },
  "332": {
   "index": 332,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"...to all members of a particular people, culture, religion, or sexuality.\"",
   "note": "Quote verbatim under the Stereotypes standard, which opens \"Avoid stereotypes.\" All four categories in the claim appear on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i319-333.json"
  },
  "333": {
   "index": 333,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"Villains can be sympathetic, but their evil should be called out as awful and wrong. Avoid depicting evil heroes.\"",
   "note": "Quote verbatim under Glamorization of Evil. Both limbs on the page, including the evil-heroes limb.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i319-333.json"
  },
  "334": {
   "index": 334,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"Don't use real-world religions as analogs for evil religions in D&D.\"",
   "note": "Raw PDF reached: zip downloaded from media.dndbeyond.com (200, application/zip, 1,624,152 bytes), unzipped, 'Dungeoncraft Pack/DnD Standards.pdf' text-extracted with pypdf. Quote appears word for word under the 'Religion.' bullet; header line reads '(c)2020 Wizards of the Coast LLC. Confidential information.' Source attribution (2020 Standards inside the 2023-12-04 Dungeoncraft pack) is exact.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i334-348.json"
  },
  "335": {
   "index": 335,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Principle 2: \"An adventure needs a credible threat\"",
   "note": "Raw PDF fetched by curl (200, application/pdf, 745,871 bytes) and extracted with pypdf. Byline reads 'By Chris Perkins & Greg Bilsland'; numbered principle 2 carries the quote verbatim, and the same paragraph adds that antagonists 'should have logical goals that the heroes can uncover and thwart in the course of the adventure.' Every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i334-348.json"
  },
  "336": {
   "index": 336,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Principle 4: \"An adventure is about the here and now.\"",
   "note": "Quote is word for word at principle 4, p.1. The backstory limb is supported by the next sentence: 'A little bit of history might be needed to set things up, but the less of it, the better.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i334-348.json"
  },
  "337": {
   "index": 337,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Principle 7: concise text \"to ease the burden on the DM\"",
   "note": "Principle 7 reads 'An adventure should strive to present information as concisely as possible, to ease the burden on the DM,' then names the exceptions deserving special attention: 'NPCs, dungeon dressings, and treasure hoards.' Both limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i334-348.json"
  },
  "338": {
   "index": 338,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Principle 6: \"present information in a logical order\"",
   "note": "Principle 6 carries the quoted fragment verbatim, plus 'so that a DM reading it for the first time isn't confused,' 'make sure that an NPC is properly introduced before casually mentioning the NPC by name,' and 'shouldn't conceal or bury important information.' All three limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i334-348.json"
  },
  "339": {
   "index": 339,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"features, mannerisms, and quirks\" the DM uses to roleplay",
   "note": "The sentence straddles the page break: p.1 ends 'describing his or her distinctive physical' and p.2 opens 'features, mannerisms, and quirks (which the DM can then use to roleplay the NPC effectively).' The cited quote and page (p.2) are correct.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i334-348.json"
  },
  "340": {
   "index": 340,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"Cliches are okay, as long as the adventure can still surprise\"",
   "note": "Principle 3 carries the quote verbatim and names all three tropes: 'dragons, orcs, and insane wizards in towers' and beginning 'in a tavern,' closing with 'try to put your own clever “spin” on it.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i334-348.json"
  },
  "341": {
   "index": 341,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"The player characters (PCs) are the heroes, not mere spectators\"",
   "note": "Principle 1 carries the quote verbatim; the merchant limb is explicit: 'Gathering animal pelts for a lazy merchant might be a tolerable MMO quest, but it makes for an underwhelming D&D experience.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i334-348.json"
  },
  "342": {
   "index": 342,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"players will feel “railroaded” by the plot\"",
   "note": "Principle 5 carries the quote verbatim (curly quotes around railroaded, as cited). Both limbs supported: 'it needs to allow for more than one preset outcome' and 'the text needs to account for the possibility that the heroes might slay or capture the villain.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i334-348.json"
  },
  "343": {
   "index": 343,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Principle 9: \"An adventure needs to rise above the humdrum\"",
   "note": "Quote verbatim at principle 9, p.2. The list limb is supported item by item: 'odd monster pairings, unusual terrain, environmental hazards, traps, unexpected reinforcements, red herrings, atypical monster tactics, ruses and other deceptions, NPCs with complex motives, and the occasional plot twist.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i334-348.json"
  },
  "344": {
   "index": 344,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"The more good D&D adventures you read, the easier it will be\"",
   "note": "Quote verbatim on p.2. The novelist analogy is the preceding sentence: 'To write good novels, you need to read good novels... The same is true for adventures.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i334-348.json"
  },
  "345": {
   "index": 345,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"Don't decide in advance how the adventure should end.\"",
   "note": "Under 'Additional Principles for Organized Play' / 'When writing adventures for the D&D Adventurers League,' item 4 reads 'Just because an adventure is only 2 hours or 4 hours doesn't mean it should be linear or have a pre-established outcome. Don't decide in advance how the adventure should end.' All limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i334-348.json"
  },
  "346": {
   "index": 346,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "\"The Chicago Manual of Style (16th edition) and the D&D House Style Guide\"",
   "note": "The quotation is on the page verbatim, in the closing line. The comparative limb is not: the design guide never mentions a 17th edition or any other bundle document's edition, so the 'one edition behind' contrast and the 'not synchronized' inference are the verifier's, not the page's. Corroboration from a sibling file (not the cited page): 'DnD House Style Guide.docx' inside the same Dungeoncraft pack does open with 'Chicago Manual of Style, 17th edition', and 'DC Adventure Format v1.3.docx' also cites the 17th - so the substance is true, but it is sourced from other documents and the claim as written overreaches its cited page.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that 16th is one edition behind the House Style Guide's 17th, and that this shows the bundle's documents were not synchronized - neither statement appears on the cited page",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i334-348.json"
  },
  "347": {
   "index": 347,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "\"For adventure format, refer to the Adventure Format Template.\"",
   "note": "The claim carries no quote field. The page's final two sentences support the first two limbs: 'For rules on grammar and punctuation, please see The Chicago Manual of Style (16th edition) and the D&D House Style Guide. For adventure format, refer to the Adventure Format Template.' Nothing on the page says what the template contains. Corroboration from a sibling file (not the cited page): 'DC Adventure Format v1.3.docx' in the same pack does carry boxed-text rules ('Boxed text should be used very sparingly'), so the limb is true in fact but unsupported by the cited source. Note also that the copy of the design guide bundled in the Dungeoncraft pack ends at the House Style Guide sentence and omits the Adventure Format Template sentence entirely; only the cyborgsandmages URL version verified here contains it.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that the Adventure Format Template is where boxed-text rules live - the page never describes the template's contents",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i334-348.json"
  },
  "348": {
   "index": 348,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"hopeful setting. The good guys will eventually win.\"",
   "note": "Raw PDF fetched by curl (200, application/pdf, 965,749 bytes), 29 pages, extracted with pypdf. Page 1, under 'Tone': 'The Forgotten Realms is a hopeful setting. The good guys will eventually win. This hopeful tone sets the Forgotten Realms apart from settings like Greyhawk (which is cynical) and Dragonlance (which is a setting of romance and tragedy).' Quote verbatim; every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i334-348.json"
  },
  "349": {
   "index": 349,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Stories should leave the Realms as they found them.",
   "note": "p.1 'Replace Your Divots': quote exact. All limbs on page — 'should not definitively destroy or irrevocably change locations, people, gods, planes, races as a whole, or other established elements of the setting or brand without special dispensation.' Only shading: guide says 'should', claim says 'must'; guide also allows high stakes provided 'there must always be the option of things being restored to order.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i349-363.json"
  },
  "350": {
   "index": 350,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Art must represent the Realms' real range of skin tones.",
   "note": "p.1-2 header exact. Section enumerates skin tones (sun elves bronze, wild elves dark brown, gold dwarves brown, strongheart halflings dusky) and earth analogs (Chult/Turmish black, Kara-Tur Asian, Calimshan Arab, Amn/Tethyr Spaniard), and the cosmopolitan-city limb is explicit: 'When you set something in Waterdeep, the most cosmopolitan city in the world, there absolutely should be humans from all over the Realms.' Artists limb supported by 'art has failed to consistently and appropriately represent the people of the Forgotten Realms.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i349-363.json"
  },
  "351": {
   "index": 351,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Males and females depicted in equal measure; two exceptions.",
   "note": "p.2 'Gender Roles': quote exact. 'males and females should be depicted in equal measure and doing all the same jobs in equal status'; cliche list names 'the damsel in distress, the sexy barmaid'; 'There are two exceptions: orcs and Northlanders.' Guide word is 'Avoid', not 'banned'; the two exceptions as elaborated concern gendered division of labour (orc females stay behind, Northlander women stay home), not licence to use the cliches.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the orc and Northlander exceptions concern a gendered division of labour, not licence to use the sexist cliches the claim frames them as excepting; the guide's word is also 'Avoid', not 'banned'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "352": {
   "index": 352,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Keep it PG for both sex and gore.",
   "note": "'Keep it PG.' appears twice, exact: p.2 'Sex and Sexuality' bullet (romance covered — 'Depictions of sex and romance between characters should be rare') and p.2 'Violence and Gore' ('As a rule of thumb, keep it PG'). Gore carries a stated exception: 'Occasionally more gore will be called for... but ask before you take gore into the R-rated range.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i349-363.json"
  },
  "353": {
   "index": 353,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "People use loose times; clock hours are called bells.",
   "note": "p.11 'Keeping Time': quote exact — 'People are accustomed to loose times like “mid morning” or “nigh sunset.”' Bells limb explicit: 'If a local temple or civic structure has a clock, people refer to hours as “bells,” as in “I’ll meet you at seven bells.”' The not-o'clock limb is confirmed by the p.25 Banned Words entry 'o’clock (should be “bells” but most people don’t reference time so specifically)'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i349-363.json"
  },
  "354": {
   "index": 354,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Tenday days are counted; no concept of the weekend.",
   "note": "p.11 'Time and the Calendar': quote exact. 'days of the tenday are denoted by counting from the beginning of the tenday. For example, “first day,” “second day,” “third day,” and so on (or alternatively “one-day,” “two-day” etc.). There is no concept of the weekend as a special time when businesses close or people stop work.' Both limbs exact.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i349-363.json"
  },
  "355": {
   "index": 355,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Banned Words list carries every term the claim names.",
   "note": "p.25 'Banned Words' list verbatim: a.m.; coffee (should be “kaeth” or “kaethae”); money (should be “coin”); restaurant (should be “feasting hall,” or more formally as “skaethar”); week (should be “tenday”); high elf (refer to the Forgotten Realms subraces); hill dwarf (should be “shield dwarf”); hourglass (should be “sandglass”); mountain dwarf (should be “gold dwarf”); o’clock (should be “bells”...); p.m. The generic-subrace limb = the high elf / hill dwarf / mountain dwarf entries. Provenance note only: the cited quote 'tenday (never week)' is verbatim but sits in the alphabetical Terms list (T section, p.27), not in the p.25 Banned Words block, which reads 'week (should be “tenday”)'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i349-363.json"
  },
  "356": {
   "index": 356,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "godsforsaken (replaces Godforsaken)",
   "note": "Forgotten Realms Terms, G section: 'godsforsaken (replaces Godforsaken)' — exact, capital G on the replaced word. Adjacent entry 'gold (not money)' corroborates claim 355's money/coin limb.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i349-363.json"
  },
  "357": {
   "index": 357,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "goodsir not capped; Goodman/Goodwoman usually capped.",
   "note": "Forgotten Realms Terms, G section, two consecutive entries: 'Goodman/Goodwoman (a title or form of address, usu. capped)' and 'goodsir (a form of address, not capped)'. Both limbs exact, including the 'usu.' hedge the claim renders as 'usually'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i349-363.json"
  },
  "358": {
   "index": 358,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Common is a pidgin-derived trade tongue, simpler and less expressive.",
   "note": "p.14 'The Common Tongue': quote exact. 'Common grew from a kind of pidgin Chondathan... but it is far simpler and less expressive. Nuances of speech, naming, and phrasing are better conveyed in the older, more mature languages, since Common is little more than a trade language.' Accent limb tracks the regional tongues, not Common: 'Other languages might be used to inform an accent or provide a necessary language barrier' — stated for electronic games and film, where Common is assumed to be English.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i349-363.json"
  },
  "359": {
   "index": 359,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "'Forgotten Realms Phrases' lists greetings and farewells.",
   "note": "p.14 section 'Forgotten Realms Phrases' — 'Below are some phrases common to speah [sic] in the Forgotten Realms.' Quote exact: 'Well met. The most often used greeting, particularly between strangers of a different race or those of whom one is unsure.' Farewell limb exact: 'Until swords part. Warriors’ farewell throughout the Realms.' Also lists Well again, Olore, Aluve, Amarast, Alavairthae, and racial farewells.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i349-363.json"
  },
  "360": {
   "index": 360,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Don't explain everything by exposition, but do inform players.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'not use exposition to explain the world' — the guide's ban is on explaining EVERYTHING, and in the same breath it requires the opposite of the claim's blanket: 'but do inform the players about the world. Don’t assume they come to the IP with complete knowledge.'",
   "note": "p.1, guideline 3: 'Stay experiential. Don’t attempt to use exposition to explain everything in the IP, but do inform the players about the world.' The two-word quote is exact and the experiential limb holds, but the claim drops both the 'everything' quantifier and the mandatory counter-clause, converting a calibrated instruction into a blanket prohibition the page contradicts.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i349-363.json"
  },
  "361": {
   "index": 361,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Mostly, monsters are for killing, and their stuff is for taking.",
   "note": "p.2 'Monsters': quote exact. Exception limb explicit: 'They can also occasionally play non-combat roles opposing or aiding the players (the helpful kobold, the hobgoblin informant, the doppelganger spy), but such instances are out of character for D&D and require explanation.' Also 'Monsters should be the bulk of combat foes players face.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i349-363.json"
  },
  "362": {
   "index": 362,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Must use the template; should know the D&D Style Guide.",
   "note": "p.1, 'TECHNICAL INFORMATION': quote exact. Version limb exact — 'Effective Date: May 2025. Version 1.9c (updated June 23, 2025)'. Template limb is the strongest — 'You must use this template and follow the formatting standards as presented in the template.' Style guide limb is 'You should be familiar with the D&D Style Guide (also provided) as well'; the DMsGuild limb is worded as review, not familiarity — 'Please review the Wizards and the DMsGuild Content Guidelines.'",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the DMsGuild content guidelines are to be reviewed ('Please review the Wizards and the DMsGuild Content Guidelines'), not a familiarity requirement as the claim's conjunction states",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "363": {
   "index": 363,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Adventures must use the template provided.",
   "note": "Quote exact, in 'THE FINE PRINT' (p.8), corroborated on p.1 by 'You must use this template' and 'Your adventure must follow the template provided for layout'. Accessible-version limb exact, p.1 'Accessibility': 'The template must be one of your downloads, adjusted to use tags, bookmarks, alt text... Additionally, you may create a version that does not use the template, using fonts, sizing, formatting, and layout as advised by accessibility experts.' — so the non-template version is permitted alongside, not instead of, the template.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i349-363.json"
  },
  "364": {
   "index": 364,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "adventures should not foster competition between the DM and players",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the prohibitive modality 'may not' — the guide says 'should not', and uses 'may not' two sentences earlier for a hard ban ('They may not cross over into other settings'), so the two registers are deliberately distinguished in the same paragraph",
   "note": "Raw PDF fetched (HTTP 200, 9pp, pypdf extract). Page 1: 'While characters suffering from poor choices is certainly reasonable, adventures should not foster an atmosphere of competition between the DM and the players.*' Quote 'atmosphere of competition between the DM and' is verbatim. The trailing asterisk is the document's change-marker (see the June 2025 changelog on the last page), not a hardness marker.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i364-378.json"
  },
  "365": {
   "index": 365,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Effective Date: May 2025. Version 1.9c (updated June 23, 2025)",
   "note": "Raw PDF, page 1 line 11 carries the string verbatim, including the May 2025 effective date. Both limbs (version 1.9c; updated June 23, 2025) supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i364-378.json"
  },
  "366": {
   "index": 366,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "You should be familiar with the D&D Style Guide (also provided)",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'so the requirement has been constant across versions' — a cross-version inference the 2021 document does not make; also 'required' overstates the page's 'You should be familiar'",
   "note": "Raw PDF fetched (HTTP 200, 10pp). The style-guide sentence appears twice (Option 1 and Option 2 sections); the doc also says 'you'll also want the adventure templates and style guides used for the Dungeoncraft program'. Version line: 'Effective Date: October 1, 2021. Version 1.2.' The claim carries no quote, so verbatim was not possible. Independent corroboration for the constancy limb: the v1.9c PDF fetched for claims 364/365 carries the same sentence verbatim ('You should be familiar with the D&D Style Guide (also provided) as well.') — but that is my cross-check, not this page's assertion, so the limb stays unsupported at its cited source.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i364-378.json"
  },
  "367": {
   "index": 367,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Tags in Brackets.",
   "note": "Raw HTML fetched (HTTP 200, 120KB; full glossary body present, not gated). Quote 'Tags in Brackets.' is verbatim in the Glossary Conventions section. Every capitalization limb is demonstrated on the page: conditions ('the Prone condition', 'the Blinded condition', 'the Charmed condition', '[Condition]' tags); actions ('Attack [Action]', 'take the Dash action', 'take the Disengage action', 'take the Dodge action', 'take the Help action'); 'Hit Points'; 'Armor Class (AC)'; 'Proficiency Bonus'; 'A Long Rest is a period of extended downtime'; 'spend Hit Dice during a Short Rest'. Page is the 2024 free rules glossary (posted Sep 1, 2024).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i364-378.json"
  },
  "368": {
   "index": 368,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "a monster that has a personal name and a distinct personality",
   "note": "All three limbs verbatim on the raw page. Creature: 'Any being in the game, including a player's character, is a creature.' Monster: 'A monster is a creature controlled by the DM, even if the creature is benevolent.' Nonplayer Character: 'A nonplayer character (NPC) is a monster that has a personal name and a distinct personality.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i364-378.json"
  },
  "369": {
   "index": 369,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Most player characters are of the Humanoid type.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'and species' — the word 'species' does not occur anywhere in the glossary text; its only two occurrences in the fetched page are D&D Beyond site-navigation links ('Rules Browse > Species'), which are chrome, not glossary prose",
   "note": "Quote verbatim under the 'Creature Type' entry: 'Every creature, including every player character, has a tag in the rules that identifies the type of creature it is. Most player characters are of the Humanoid type. These are the game's creature types:' followed by Aberration/Beast/Celestial/... The 'creature types' limb and the 'avoiding race' limb both hold — a case-insensitive grep for race/races/racial over the whole 70KB extraction returns zero hits. Only the 'species' limb fails at this source.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i364-378.json"
  },
  "370": {
   "index": 370,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "careful about avoiding the common pitfalls",
   "note": "Raw HTML fetched (HTTP 200). Embedded tweet, bylined 'Jeremy Crawford (@JeremyECrawford) December 21, 2018': 'The current version of the D&D style guide permits the use of they/them as a singular pronoun, as long as the writer is careful about avoiding the common pitfalls (\"Are you referring to this one person or this other group of people you also mentioned?\").' Every limb holds, including the antecedent-ambiguity gloss, which the parenthetical spells out. (The Sage Advice post itself is dated January 4, 2019; the claim's 2018-12-21 is the tweet date, which is what the page shows.)",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i364-378.json"
  },
  "371": {
   "index": 371,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "tips on achieving gender neutral language",
   "note": "Raw HTML fetched (HTTP 200) at the dated permalink. Embedded tweet bylined 'Dungeon Masters Guild (@dms_guild) February 22, 2019': 'An updated #DnD House Style Guide is now on #DMsGuild, creators! Among other changes, this guide includes tips on achieving gender neutral language.' 'Among other changes' is what makes 'singled out' and 'new' fair — it is the one change the tweet names. (Sage Advice post date is April 1, 2019; the claim's date is the tweet's.)",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i364-378.json"
  },
  "372": {
   "index": 372,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "boxed text (aka read-aloud text)",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'poll' — the page carries a plain question tweet with no poll options, no vote counts and no poll markup (a grep for 'poll' over the raw HTML returns zero hits); the embedded blockquote is only the question text plus #DnD #WOTCstaff",
   "note": "Raw HTML fetched (HTTP 200). Blockquote: 'DMs, how much boxed text (aka read-aloud text) do you like in an adventure you run? #DnD #WOTCstaff — Jeremy Crawford (@JeremyECrawford) November 17, 2016' (status 799355247135076354). Quote verbatim; Crawford and 2016 hold. A Twitter poll's options are stripped from oEmbed blockquotes, so this is not CONTRADICTED — the page simply does not support 'poll'. Second, weaker soft spot worth recording: 'the terminology WotC uses' is an inference — the page shows one WotC designer using the phrase (with a #WOTCstaff tag), not a house-style statement.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i364-378.json"
  },
  "373": {
   "index": 373,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Brevity is critically important",
   "note": "Raw HTML fetched (HTTP 200). Blockquote bylined 'SlyFlourish (@SlyFlourish) April 12, 2019': 'In my own #dnd adventure writing I use boxed text, and \"area aspects\" which are like bullet points. I also avoid talking about living things in boxed text to avoid the whole \"those orcs I mentioned aren't really there\". Brevity is critically important as well.' Quote verbatim; the living-things limb and its reason are both explicit. Recorded, not graded down: the page bylines the handle @SlyFlourish and never prints the name 'Mike Shea'. I treat handle-to-person resolution as naming the same account holder, not as an added assertion — unlike claim 374, where the claim adds a credential the page does not carry. Second minor: Shea states it as his own practice ('In my own adventure writing I... avoid'), which the claim generalizes to 'should be kept out'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "Shea states it as his own practice ('In my own #dnd adventure writing I... avoid talking about living things in boxed text'), which the claim generalises into a prescription; the page also prints only the handle @SlyFlourish, never the name Mike Shea",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "374": {
   "index": 374,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "too long (anything over a paragraph or so)",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'AL administrator' — the page gives Greg Marks no role, title or affiliation; it bylines him only as 'Greg Marks (@Skerrit7h3green)'. The credential is load-bearing here, because the surrounding claims lean on Adventurers League authority",
   "note": "Same raw page as 373/375. Blockquote bylined 'Greg Marks (@Skerrit7h3green) April 15, 2019': 'However, I also think lots of boxed text is unnecessary (an empty room doesn't need boxed text), too long (anything over a paragraph or so), written poorly burying important details, pretends to be a story to be listened to by the players, preventing them from being involved.' Quote 'a paragraph or so' verbatim; the length-ceiling criticism is fully supported. Only the AL-administrator credential fails.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i364-378.json"
  },
  "375": {
   "index": 375,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "A majority of DMs out there WANT boxed text",
   "note": "Same raw page. Blockquote bylined 'Shawn Merwin (or a poor clone) (@shawnmerwin) April 14, 2019' carries both halves: the streams point ('if boxed text worked well, then we would see it used on the streams as a go-to to start a scene. I don't think we ever see it. Instead, we see a back-and-forth between the DM & Players.') and Merwin's reply ('While this is true, different people (and roles) have different needs. A majority of DMs out there WANT boxed text (well-written hopefully) to make their lives easier when they buy adventure content. So it is a risk not to provide what your audience and consumers want.'). The streams half reads as quoted-tweet text that Merwin accepts with 'While this is true'; the page attributes the whole blockquote to him. 'Publishers keep providing it' is carried by 'it is a risk not to provide what your audience and consumers want' — prudential rather than descriptive, but the causal link is his.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'publishers keep providing it' is prudential in the source ('it is a risk not to provide what your audience and consumers want'), not the description the claim makes of it",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "376": {
   "index": 376,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Excellent boxed text does a lot of heavy lifting",
   "note": "Raw HTML fetched (HTTP 200). Two Merwin tweets on the page. Nov 23, 2021: 'This is exactly why boxed text is important. It's more than description. It's a series of prompts that can help your game flow more smoothly.' — quote-tweeting DM David's 'The quicker a description tempts players to act, the sooner DMs can stop talking and players can do something', which carries the push-players-toward-action limb. Nov 26, 2021: 'As a designer, I've found myself using boxed text to convey the atmosphere that I want to convey to the DM reading my adventure… it's over to them how they then convey that to their players. (Perhaps that's why I prefer to call it \"narrative text\"). Excellent boxed text does a lot of heavy lifting, and atmosphere is definitely part of that burden.' Quote matches word for word; only the sentence-initial E is capitalized on the page. (Sage Advice post date May 24, 2022; the claim's 2021-11 is the tweet dates.)",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i364-378.json"
  },
  "377": {
   "index": 377,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "break it up into actionable chunks",
   "note": "Raw HTML fetched direct (HTTP 200, no block, no Wayback needed); article dated August 1st, 2022, titled 'GM Don't List #13: Boxed Text Pitfalls', on Justin Alexander's site (byline block 'JUSTIN ALEXANDER'; a commenter writes 'I also tend to agree with Justin's critiques'). Naming limb: 'Freeze-frame boxed text is when the GM starts reading and then the PCs are frozen in place while a bunch of stuff happens.' Advance-past-reaction limb: 'what you should avoid at all costs is having the game world continue to move forward past the reaction point without letting the players react'. Cure limb, verbatim: 'what you should do is break it up into actionable chunks' and 'The other actionable chunks are:'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i364-378.json"
  },
  "378": {
   "index": 378,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "you have effectively removed from the game",
   "note": "Same raw page. 'Remote-control boxed text suffers from similar problems (preventing the players from participating), but insidiously goes one step further by declaring the thoughts or feelings or (worse yet!) actions of the PCs.' Agency limb: 'When you take the one thing they control away from them — even for a little bit — you have effectively removed from the game. They are, in fact, no longer a player, but merely a spectator.' The quote is verbatim including the source's own dropped word ('removed from the game', missing 'them'). Forbidden limb: 'So… don't do this.' in an entry of the 'GM Don't List'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i364-378.json"
  },
  "379": {
   "index": 379,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "should never be longer than three sentences",
   "note": "Live page fetched raw (HTTP 200). mellonbread's comment, dated August 2nd, 2022: 'There was a lovely article in the old Wizards devblog, from around the 3.5 or 4e days, which was lost when they nuked their archives. The basic thesis was that boxed text (or any descriptive text delivered by the GM) should never be longer than three sentences...' followed by 'I don't think Wizards actually abided by this rule in their own boxed text.' Every limb (3.5/4e era, source lost, WotC did not follow it) is on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i379-393.json"
  },
  "380": {
   "index": 380,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'originally distributed to organized-play writers' - no post in the three-page thread says where the guides came from",
   "trueWording": "divided into four PDFs: IP, Adventure, Style, Forgotten Realms guides",
   "note": "Post #1 (Remathilis, Nov 25 2018): 'Its divided into four PDFs: An IP Guide..., an Adventure Guide..., a Style Guide... and a Forgotten Realms Style Guide', and 'Its only been on the site a month or so' (supports the four components and the 2018 date). All three pages of the thread fetched and searched for 'organized', 'Adventurers League', 'RPGA', 'distributed', 'freelance' - the only adjacent remark is post #2's recollection of 'the old Dragon and Dungeon days', which is submission guidelines for the magazines, not organized play; post #9 speculates they are 'new internal guidelines'. Provenance limb unsupported here.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i379-393.json"
  },
  "381": {
   "index": 381,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "style guide bans 'week' for 'tenday'; week appears in FR products",
   "note": "Post #8 (pukunui): 'It's a real shame WotC doesn't bother to make sure their hardcovers follow the guidelines (e.g. The style guide says \"week\" is banned and one should use \"tenday\" instead, but \"week\" shows up in FR products all the time!)'. Post #9 (Parmandur) supplies the second limb: 'Could be these are new internal guidelines made for just that reason, that they decided to share.' Claim carried no quote string, so graded on substance.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i379-393.json"
  },
  "382": {
   "index": 382,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "attribution to the Realms style guide - the thread places the Sigil line in the D&D IP Guide",
   "trueWording": "That line is in the D&D IP Guide, not FR",
   "note": "Quote verbatim on page, post #4 (Quickleaf): 'Been looking it over the four documents and there was this odd line in the D&D IP Guide: \"Linking all planes together is the city of Sigil, characters should not be traveling there.\"' The reserving-Planescape reading is well supported - #5 'Suggests they want to keep Sigil clear for official stuff down the line'; #6 'they don't want that to backdoor open Planescape's IP as well yet'; #10 'The only reason I can think for them calling out Sigil specifically... is that they have plans to release a product dealing with Sigil.' Only the document attribution fails.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i379-393.json"
  },
  "383": {
   "index": 383,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "downloaded guide hasn't been updated since 2019; no reply posted",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200), full thread renders server-side. Single post #1, Jun 13 2025, Saracenus (Bryan Blumklotz), Dungeon Masters Guild forum: 'I recently downloaded the D&D Style Guide from the DMs Guild website and it hasn't been updated since 2019... culminating in the 2024 core rulebooks... are you still using the 17th Edition of the Chicago Manual of Style? There is now an 18th Edition.' No reply posts exist on the page (only 'To post a comment, please login'). Caveat on the causal wording: silence does not itself 'confirm' anything - the still-2019 fact rests on the asker's own report, not on the absence of a reply.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the absence of a reply confirms nothing: the still-2019 fact rests on the asker's own report, not on the thread's silence",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "384": {
   "index": 384,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'listing the Design Guide as coming soon' - the Upcoming Documents are the AL Organizer Guide and the D&D Session Log; 'Design Guide' appears nowhere on the page",
   "trueWording": "coming soon: Organizer Guide and Session Log, not Design Guide",
   "note": "Raw page fetched. 'Last edited by LaTiaJacquise: Aug 12, 2026' matches the 2026-08-12 update. Post #1: 'Adventurers League Dungeoncraft Creator Pack... Effective December 4, 2023', whose href is https://media.dndbeyond.com/compendium-images/ddal/reference-docs/03-2024/dungeoncraft-pack-2023-12dec04.zip - D&D Beyond CDN, 2023-12-04, both confirmed. But 'Upcoming Documents' reads: 'Adventurers League Organizer Guide - coming soon! D&D Session Log - coming soon!'. grep -ci 'design guide' over the page text returns 0. (The Adventure Design Guide is in fact already inside the linked pack.)",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i379-393.json"
  },
  "385": {
   "index": 385,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "template required; Style Guide familiarity, Content Guidelines adherence; removal for noncompliance",
   "note": "Raw article fetched. 'Technical Requirements: Creators must use a specific template provided for Dungeoncraft adventures, including the Community Created D&D Adventurers League logo. Familiarity with the D&D Style Guide and adherence to the DMsGuild Content Guidelines are also required.' 'Publication and Compliance: ...The adventures must adhere to specific coding and design standards... There are strict compliance rules, and adventures can be removed from the DMsGuild if they fail to meet these standards.' One wording gap worth flagging: the article requires 'familiarity with' the Style Guide (adherence is stated for the Content Guidelines), slightly weaker than the claim's 'comply with'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the article requires 'familiarity with' the Style Guide (adherence is stated only for the Content Guidelines), weaker than the claim's 'comply with'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "386": {
   "index": 386,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "That's just not right, and it's not something we believe in.",
   "note": "Quote verbatim in the WotC statement as quoted by Gizmodo (published June 18, 2020, James Whitbrook). Both limbs on the page: 'the use of sensitivity readers on upcoming and current Dungeons & Dragons sourcebooks as part of the creative process' and 'the D&D team will also go back through material as it is being prepared for reprints, and update them to modify and remove any racially insensitive material.' Metadata drift only: the article now carries the headline 'Dungeons & Dragons Team Announces New Plans to Address Race and Inclusivity in the Game', not the '...to Address Racism in the Game' recorded in the source field.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the article's headline is 'Dungeons & Dragons Team Announces New Plans to Address Race and Inclusivity in the Game', not the '...to Address Racism in the Game' recorded in the source field",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "387": {
   "index": 387,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "Wizards worked with a Romani consultant for Strahd's reprint",
   "note": "Same article: 'The adventures Tomb of Annihilation and The Curse of Strahd were cited as particular examples' (of reprint material to be updated), and 'In the editorial process for Strahd's reprint, as well as two upcoming products, Wizards worked with a Romani consultant to present the Vistani without using reductive tropes.' Both limbs supported; claim carried no quote string.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i379-393.json"
  },
  "388": {
   "index": 388,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "we changed text that was racially insensitive",
   "note": "Wikipedia 'Tomb of Annihilation', Publication history: 'On June 17, 2020, in the wake of the George Floyd protests, Wizards of the Coast announced: \"...In recent reprintings of Tomb of Annihilation and Curse of Strahd, for example, we changed text that was racially insensitive. Those reprints have already been printed and will be available in the months ahead. We will continue this process, reviewing each book as it comes up for a reprint and fixing such errors where they are present\".' Quote verbatim; both limbs (recent reprints already changed; review at each reprint) supported, and the 2020-06-17 date matches.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i379-393.json"
  },
  "389": {
   "index": 389,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "only text in Strahd revised; artwork and Tarokka cards unmodified",
   "note": "Raw article fetched. Update of 7/28/2020: 'According to Ray Winninger, the executive producer of Wizards' D&D studio, only text in Strahd has received revisions... and none of the original artwork in the module or the included Tarroka Cards has been modified as part of this update. For owners of the original release... will be released for players to download for free on the D&D website in the future, and also be made available for free to players who purchased Strahd digitally in the D&D Beyond app.' All three limbs supported. Two attribution flags: the quoted phrase 'surgical changes' is on the page but is Chris Perkins speaking to IGN ('More like surgical changes to the adventure than some sort of grand sweeping change'), NOT Winninger; and the article's live headline is 'D&D's Culturally Sensitive Strahd Revamp Is Here, But It'll Cost You [Updated]', not the '...But It Doesn't Go Far Enough' recorded in the source field.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the designated quote 'surgical changes' is Chris Perkins speaking to IGN, not Winninger; and the article's live headline is '...But It'll Cost You [Updated]', not the '...But It Doesn't Go Far Enough' in the source field",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "390": {
   "index": 390,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "fixes 'uncivilized' and heavy drinkers; curse and Evil Eye remain",
   "note": "Same article: 'amendments... sought to undo some of the harmful stereotyping of the Vistani people, along with revising the book's approach to a specific character's disability to avoid ableist stereotypes' and 'While modifications do fix things like the stereotyping of the Vistani people as \"uncivilized\" and heavy drinkers, the module still gives the Vistani abilities to curse and hypnotize players or cast spells like Evil Eye'. All three limbs supported; claim carried no quote string.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i379-393.json"
  },
  "391": {
   "index": 391,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "the adventure includes the latest errata and a revised depiction",
   "note": "Wikipedia 'Curse of Strahd', Revamped edition: 'Wizards of the Coast stated that \"the adventure includes the latest errata and a revised depiction of the Vistani\" who are based on stereotypes about the Romani people.' Quote verbatim, both limbs supported. Metadata note: the supporting footnote [9] is Macgregor, PC Gamer, dated 2020-08-03, not 2020-07 as the source field records.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the supporting footnote is Macgregor, PC Gamer, dated 2020-08-03, not the 2020-07 the source field records",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "392": {
   "index": 392,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the exhaustive 'WotC's only published boxed-text prose rules' - no page establishes that universal negative (WotC also published a 2005 boxed-text article at archive.wizards.com/default.asp?x=dnd/dd/20050916a, now Wayback-only)",
   "trueWording": "Style guide covers Chicago-based typography, terms, punctuation; no boxed text",
   "note": "Both named documents reached raw. The PDF (11 pages, footer 'Version 1.08a (c)2018 Wizards of the Coast') is built on Chicago 17th and Merriam-Webster 11th and its sections are Using Chicago, Titles and Trademarks, Gender, Headings, Sidebars, Spelling and Punctuation, Numbers and Math, Cross-References, Style Rules for the RPG, Formatting Game Terms, Word List, Forgotten Realms Terms, Abbreviations, Trademarks - a case-insensitive grep for 'boxed', 'read-aloud'/'read aloud' returns zero hits, so the first limb holds. I also pulled the Dungeoncraft Pack zip named in the source field (media.dndbeyond.com/.../dungeoncraft-pack-2023-12dec04.zip) and extracted DC Adventure Format v1.3.docx, which reads verbatim: 'Boxed text should be used very sparingly-only when a particularly important event truly calls for it. Don't use it to describe rooms or normalcy. Additionally, it should be short and to the point, no more than one or two brief and descriptive sentences.' So the sparing/one-or-two-sentence rule is confirmed at source. Only the word 'only' is unproven.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i379-393.json"
  },
  "393": {
   "index": 393,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Boxed text is usually not a good thing.",
   "note": "Raw page fetched. tenfootpole.org 'Review Standards', by-line 'Bryce Lynch', opening 'What I'm looking for in an adventure module...'. The bullet 'Boxed text is usually not a good thing.' appears word for word among the standards bullets (between the tricks-and-traps and empty-rooms bullets). Two shadings worth recording: the page is one mixed list of standards rather than a separate list of unwanted things, and Lynch hedges with 'usually', which the claim drops.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the page is one mixed list of review standards, not a separate list of unwanted things, and Lynch hedges 'Boxed text is usually not a good thing', which the claim drops",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "394": {
   "index": 394,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Bullet “Terse writing style” in Bryce Lynch’s list.",
   "note": "Page header: “Review Standards / Bryce Lynch / What I’m looking for in an adventure module…”; the bullet list that follows contains the standalone line “Terse writing style” (with adjacent bullets “Evocative atmosphere” and “I don’t care about your epic backstory”). Raw HTML fetched from tenfootpole.org, HTTP 200.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i394-408.json"
  },
  "395": {
   "index": 395,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "“I LOATHE the overly long backstory and fiction intro’s”",
   "note": "Comment on the same page: “Bryce Lynch says: April 19, 2016 at 8:36 am — I LOATHE the overly long backstory and fiction intro’s, but they are not on this list.” Author, date and wording all match; “in modules” is the page’s own subject matter (a review-standards page for adventure modules).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i394-408.json"
  },
  "396": {
   "index": 396,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Language scale: “4 – Succinct and evocative”, top of four listed levels.",
   "note": "In the section headed “The Pretty Girl”, written in her own first person (“When I built my rating scale…”, “My ratings system…”, “not the gourmand that Bryce is”), the Language scale reads 4 – Succinct and evocative / 2 – Conversational but clear / 1 – You should have hired an English Major to edit this / 0 – Very wordy/ incomprehensible. Four listed levels, top score 4, and the scale is explicitly hers and separate from Bryce Lynch’s bullet list above it.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i394-408.json"
  },
  "397": {
   "index": 397,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "“Boxed text … is a prewritten narration designed to be read to the players by the GM.”",
   "note": "Opening sentence of the article, verbatim including the quoted fragment. Raw page fetched from thealexandrian.net, HTTP 200.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i394-408.json"
  },
  "398": {
   "index": 398,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "“what we’ve identified here is the reaction point”; avoid moving past it.",
   "note": "Page: “What we’ve identified here is the reaction point” and “what you should avoid at all costs is having the game world continue to move forward past the reaction point without letting the players react.” The passage under discussion is the freeze-frame example labelled “(from Journeys Through the Radiant Citadel)”, quoted immediately above. All three limbs on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i394-408.json"
  },
  "399": {
   "index": 399,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "“should never be longer than three sentences, because … players stop paying attention”",
   "note": "Comment by mellonbread, timestamped “August 2nd, 2022 - 11:58 am”: “There was a lovely article in the old Wizards devblog … which was lost when they nuked their archives. The basic thesis was that boxed text (or any descriptive text delivered by the GM) should never be longer than three sentences…” No link is present in the comment, and it is explicitly the commenter’s recollection.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i394-408.json"
  },
  "400": {
   "index": 400,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "“If you’re the DM, you get two sentences. Period.”",
   "note": "Live archive.wizards.com URL now redirects to the Wizards SPA shell, so the raw page was recovered via the Wayback raw capture https://web.archive.org/web/20210713073031id_/http://archive.wizards.com/default.asp?x=dnd/dd/20050916a (HTTP 200, full article text). Byline “By Jesse Decker and David Noonan”; the first-person observer is identified as Dave (“because Dave spent a lot of time wandering around the convention hall at Gen Con”), the convention is named “Gen Con 2005”, and the quoted sentence appears verbatim under “Undercover Insight #1: Boxed Text = MEGO”.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i394-408.json"
  },
  "401": {
   "index": 401,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "“At its heart, a D&D game is a conversation … Boxed text replaces that conversation with oration.”",
   "note": "Both sentences appear verbatim in Undercover Insight #1, and the article later calls it “the conversation vs. oration factor”, ranking it above two lesser factors — so it is indeed the stated mechanism.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i394-408.json"
  },
  "402": {
   "index": 402,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "“Boxed text in adventures used to be a lot longer than it is today.”",
   "note": "In the “Tangent Alert!” inside Undercover Insight #1. The Sons of Gruumsh sample quoted is exactly three sentences (oak doors / iron bands / All-Seeing Eye) and is contrasted with a much longer sample from “1999’s Return to the Keep on the Borderlands”. The page calls Sons of Gruumsh “a recent Forgotten Realms adventure” rather than printing the year; the article’s own dateline is 09/16/2005, which is what makes it the 2005 side of the contrast.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i394-408.json"
  },
  "403": {
   "index": 403,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "“missing the actual data … basic stuff, like room dimensions, how many doors exit the room, and number of monsters”",
   "note": "Verbatim in Undercover Insight #1, immediately followed by the list of questions “from players who’d supposedly been paying attention” — supporting the limb that these were players who had heard the text.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i394-408.json"
  },
  "404": {
   "index": 404,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "“structuring the narrative like it’s a book … It doesn’t include things like ‘he says’”",
   "note": "The Out of the Abyss boxed text is quoted in full on the page and opens “‘I’ve told the allies ye met tonight what ye told me,’ he says.” The critique names that tag directly and the article elsewhere says such text seems “as though it belonged in a novel”. Raw page fetched from dmsworkshop.com, HTTP 200.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i394-408.json"
  },
  "405": {
   "index": 405,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "“The first words after this boxed text read ‘Paraphrase Bruenor’s speech as needed…’”",
   "note": "Full page sentence: “The first words after this boxed text read ‘Paraphrase Bruenor’s speech as needed to handle interjections by the characters’” — quotation verbatim and the “immediately after” limb is stated by the source itself.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i394-408.json"
  },
  "406": {
   "index": 406,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "“rare that a boxed text that includes a paragraph break isn’t already too long by half”",
   "note": "The quotation is on the page, under the heading “Don’t Overdo It”, and the “usually” and “containing a paragraph break” limbs hold (“it’s rare that … isn’t”). But the page states an idiom, not a ratio: it never says such a boxed text is twice as long as it should be, and it also exempts introductory passages (“Aside from introductory passages”), a carve-out the claim drops. The advice is also addressed to people writing their own adventures.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that the text is twice as long as it should be (the page says only “already too long by half”, and exempts introductory passages)",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i394-408.json"
  },
  "407": {
   "index": 407,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "“shifting text from third-person objective (‘A 200-foot waterfall cascades down…’) to second-person subjective”",
   "note": "Under the heading “Using Boxed Text Your Way”: “don’t shy away from shifting text from third-person objective (‘A 200-foot waterfall cascades down…’) to second-person subjective (‘You see a majestic waterfall hundreds of feet in height…’) if your players tend to lose focus”. Both registers are named and each is illustrated with a waterfall sentence.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i394-408.json"
  },
  "408": {
   "index": 408,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "“A bookstore receives a short paragraph description that is written to be more easily paraphrased”",
   "note": "Full page sentence continues: “; a great hall in a noble villa has multiple bullet points describing the furnishings, the sounds, and the bodies on the floor”, prefaced by “While most important areas in the adventure are given the boxed text treatment, minor areas are often described differently” — every limb of the claim, including the mixed-forms framing, is on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i394-408.json"
  },
  "409": {
   "index": 409,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "\"are Gygax at his mellifluent best: Jaroo Ashstaff, Calmert, Fernok of Ferd, Ostler Gundigoot, and more.\"",
   "note": "Quote verbatim in the post: 'The inhabitants of the village, from their names to their personalities, are Gygax at his mellifluent best: Jaroo Ashstaff, Calmert, Fernok of Ferd, Ostler Gundigoot, and more.' Confirmed against the raw HTML (grognardia.blogspot.com, 1 Oct 2008): exactly FOUR names are listed before 'and more', not five. The praise limb (names, as Gygax at his best) is supported; the count limb is not.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "\"lists five of them\" — the post names only four inhabitants",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i409-423.json"
  },
  "410": {
   "index": 410,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"his holy sayings presented in the module, such as:\" then three capitalised sayings",
   "note": "Raw page carries the quote word for word and then a blockquote of exactly three all-caps sayings: SQUARE CORNERS CAN BE POUNDED SMOOTH / THICK HEADS ARE NOT MADE OF GLASS / SALVATION IS BETTER THAN SMART ANSWERS. Capitalisation confirmed in the raw HTML blockquote. Every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i409-423.json"
  },
  "411": {
   "index": 411,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"Like Hemingway's iceberg... make the setting richer even if little of it is presented in-game.\"",
   "note": "j-nelson.net raw page: 'They have ambitions, suspicions, and secrets.' and 'it's possible, even likely, none of these inhabitants or their situations will be faced by the players. Like Hemingway's iceberg, Gygax's inclusion of these details make the setting richer even if little of it is presented in-game.' Quote verbatim; iceberg, the three nouns, and the never-met limb all supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i409-423.json"
  },
  "412": {
   "index": 412,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"They've stashed seventy-three silver coins in the hollow of a tree—there could be a story\"",
   "note": "Raw page, of the widower farmer and his spinster daughter: 'They've stashed seventy-three silver coins in the hollow of a tree—there could be a story within all these details, a story within the larger story.' Quote appears word for word; exact hidden sum and the implied-story limb both supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i409-423.json"
  },
  "413": {
   "index": 413,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "One reviewer notes Hommlet \"is a place with history and its history shapes and affects its layout, mood and inhabitants.\"",
   "note": "Raw page attributes it to an unnamed 'One reviewer' and the quoted words run on '...its layout, mood and inhabitants.' Quote verbatim; the reviewer limb and the three-noun limb both supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i409-423.json"
  },
  "414": {
   "index": 414,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Tip 1: second person → third; \"The bloated corpse of a headless dragon dominates the center of this small clearing\"",
   "note": "critical-hits.com (27 Aug 2010) Tip 1 heading: 'Move from the second-person point of view to the third person.' Same clearing given both ways: second person 'As you enter the clearing, you notice the bloated corpse of a headless dragon'; third person 'The bloated corpse of a headless dragon dominates the center of this small clearing.' Quote verbatim; the corpse is the grammatical subject of the third-person version.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i409-423.json"
  },
  "415": {
   "index": 415,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"My rule of thumb when writing or editing is to leave in only the details about the environment\"",
   "note": "critical-hits.com Tip 2a: '...to leave in only the details about the environment that the PCs need to know about because they will have to interact with them.' Quote verbatim; writer-and-editor limb ('when writing or editing') and the interaction limb both supported. (The later D&D Beyond rewrite reads 'when the PCs must interact with them on some level'; the quote as given matches the Critical Hits original cited.)",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i409-423.json"
  },
  "416": {
   "index": 416,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"Four sentences, and less than 100 words.\" then a first/second/third/fourth breakdown",
   "note": "dndbeyond.com/posts/625 section 'A Boxed-Text Opening' prints Merwin's own carrion-crow boxed text (four sentences, ~95 words), then: 'Four sentences, and less than 100 words. The first sentence... The second... The third... The fourth...'. Quote verbatim; every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i409-423.json"
  },
  "417": {
   "index": 417,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Tip 4 \"Avoid 'seems to be' or 'appears to be.' For the characters, perception is reality.\"",
   "note": "dndbeyond.com Tip 4 heading is exactly 'Avoid “seems to be” or “appears to be.”' and the body opens 'For the characters, perception is reality.' and adds 'nothing triggers meta-gaming like the words “seems” or “appears” in boxed text.' Quote verbatim; the metagaming limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i409-423.json"
  },
  "418": {
   "index": 418,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"tongue-twisters, funny-sounding phrases, unintended alliteration... hard to spot on the page\"",
   "note": "dndbeyond.com Tip 5: 'Read your boxed text aloud to check readability... it is required for boxed text. Trouble areas like tongue-twisters, funny-sounding phrases, unintended alliteration, and other pitfalls are hard to spot on the page but easy to hear when spoken.' Quote verbatim (hyphenated 'funny-sounding' matches the D&D Beyond text; the 2010 Critical Hits original reads 'funny sounding'). 'Required check' and 'invisible on the page' limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i409-423.json"
  },
  "419": {
   "index": 419,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Tip 6 \"Avoid game mechanical terms used in a non-mechanical way\"; list includes stunned, frightened",
   "note": "dndbeyond.com Tip 6 heading verbatim; body: 'Terms like reaction, action, incapacitated, stunned, frightened, magical, and many others have specific meanings in the game. Using their normal connotations in proximity to mechanical bits can lead to confusion. Avoid them whenever possible.' The tip sits in the article's list of tips for boxed (read-aloud) text; every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i409-423.json"
  },
  "420": {
   "index": 420,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"It requires the same precision that one might use for fiction or poetry... much like technical writing.\"",
   "note": "dndbeyond.com opening: 'Writing good boxed text is difficult. It requires the same precision that one might use for fiction or poetry, while also trying to perform the mechanical task of dealing with game elements, much like technical writing.' Quote verbatim; both limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i409-423.json"
  },
  "421": {
   "index": 421,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"takes a good, middle sentence and pads it like a school paper stretched to an assigned length\"",
   "note": "dmdavid.com: 'When Gamescience updated Tegel in 1989, they expanded the descriptions', then the B9 BEDROOM (taxidermist's bedroom) key, then 'This description takes a good, middle sentence and pads it like a school paper stretched to an assigned length.' Quote verbatim; 1989, Gamescience, Tegel Manor bedroom key and the one-good-sentence limb all supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i409-423.json"
  },
  "422": {
   "index": 422,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"The custom of longer description encourages authors to write something even when they have little to add.\"",
   "note": "dmdavid.com, same paragraph: '...encourages authors to write something even when they have little to add. The format makes authors feel obligated to describe the shelves and pegs in an empty closet.' Quote verbatim; the format-blaming limb is supported by the next sentence. Minor paraphrase drift: the page says 'little to add', the claim says 'nothing to add' — the empty-closet sentence carries the stronger sense, so not scored down.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i409-423.json"
  },
  "423": {
   "index": 423,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "\"If the player characters meet the sheriff about a wanted poster, no one needs an item-by-item inventory of her kitchen.\"",
   "note": "dmdavid.com section 'Matching description to a location's purpose': 'A location's purpose in the game should also figure into the length of its descriptions. If the player characters meet the sheriff about a wanted poster, no one needs an item-by-item inventory of her kitchen.' Quote verbatim; both limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i409-423.json"
  },
  "424": {
   "index": 424,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Quote verbatim on the raw dmdavid.com tag page (full article text present). Page also carries “author Chris Perkins fills them with creepy, moody details” and, for unreachability, “players might explore some of these rooms” + “they will never enter the Burgomaster’s scullery” + “Often then, I realized that nothing would bring players to the location.” The page’s quantifier is “often/many”; “most” follows from “some” being the explored minority. All limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i424-438.json"
  },
  "425": {
   "index": 425,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim: “His follow-up, Storm King’s Thunder, omits almost all read-aloud text.” The other limb is on the same page: “Curse of Strahd includes it for every location.” Page says “follow-up” (Perkins’s next adventure) where the claim says “successor” — same substance.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i424-438.json"
  },
  "426": {
   "index": 426,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim in the article’s third paragraph: “Which is why I find it fairly surprising that the location keys in published adventures are almost universally terrible.” Author/venue confirmed (The Alexandrian, ‘The Art of the Key’, May 19 2014).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i424-438.json"
  },
  "427": {
   "index": 427,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim including curly quotes. The orc limb is the very next sentence: “You probably don’t really need to tell a DM that the orcs attack people using their swords (since that’s the only weapon they have)… but the format says you should have a ‘Tactics’ section, so you might as well write two or three paragraphs about it.” Under the ADVENTURE FORMATTING GUIDES heading, as cited.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i424-438.json"
  },
  "428": {
   "index": 428,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim: “A room with an ogre standing in the middle of it should talk about the ogre first; a room with a goblin hiding behind a tapestry, on the other hand, shouldn’t lead off with the goblin.” The anti-template limb is supported by “This sort of one-size-fits-all formatting becomes both a straitjacket and an excess” and “a rigid adventure format forces information to be presented out-of-sequence.”",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i424-438.json"
  },
  "429": {
   "index": 429,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim under THE ERA OF BOXED TEXT. Page: boxed text “arrived for the first time in Lost Tamoachan: The Hidden Shrine of Lubaatum”; “In the original version of the module, the text was not yet boxed, instead appearing between quotation marks”; “But in 1980, TSR reissued the module as C1 The Hidden Shrine of Tamoachan, placing the prewritten descriptions in a box for the first time.” All three limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i424-438.json"
  },
  "430": {
   "index": 430,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim: “There were nouns and verbs but the adjectives and adverbs were not used very well.” The generic-feel limb is in the same paragraph: “Hence the kind of ‘generic’ vibe. Generic goblin. Generic cave. Generic wolves.” Review confirmed as Bryce Lynch on Lost Mine of Phandelver.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i424-438.json"
  },
  "431": {
   "index": 431,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim: “‘The farmers wife tells you … ’ instead of the ‘the prim farmers wife tells you …’”. Six alternatives counted on the page: “Granny, prim, biddy, lusty, stoic, proper”, of which the page says “They conjure a NON-generic image in your mind that you can build on” — the claim’s “each conjure a different woman” is that in other words.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i424-438.json"
  },
  "432": {
   "index": 432,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim inside “The whole town is quite light on descriptive and evocative language/scenes outside of the core locations”, in the Part 2 town section as cited. Lynch immediately widens it — “(and, I will assert later, IN the core locations as well.)” — so the claim is narrower than the page, not at odds with it.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i424-438.json"
  },
  "433": {
   "index": 433,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim: “I would have liked to see a web of personal relationships between the various people living in town.” Purpose limb verbatim too: “This will help make the town seem alive and to exist outside of the interactions of the party.” Page names residents by role (guild master, innkeeper, trading company) rather than by name.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the page names residents by role (guild master, innkeeper, trading company), not by name, so 'a town's named residents' is the claimant's word",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "434": {
   "index": 434,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim. Surrounding text carries every limb: “the adventure gives a brief summary of the major NPC’s, where they hang out, and what their objectives/quests are. That’s done in just a sentence or so each. I find these sorts of references INVALUABLE. … More adventures need to do this.”",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i424-438.json"
  },
  "435": {
   "index": 435,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim: “The monster descriptions are boring as hell, with almost no attention paid to what the DM actually needs: what the PC’s EXPERIENCE when they meet them.” The smell and the coat are the next two sentences: “Stink like the grave? Matted patchy fur? None of that is present.”",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i424-438.json"
  },
  "436": {
   "index": 436,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim, and inside the “On to the bad” section as cited (heading two paragraphs above). Page: “The read-aloud in this, in many places, is laughable”, then single-sentence examples — “You stumble upon an old grave.”, “You find a corpse.” — culminating in “Here’s a masterpiece of creative read-aloud writing!!! ‘You find something on the ground.’”, which names nothing.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i424-438.json"
  },
  "437": {
   "index": 437,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Three sentences are used when one will do. Six are used when it needed two, or three.",
   "note": "Quote is verbatim in the “On to the bad” section and the inflation charge is real (“the text is long/meaningless… the entries are so long that the important bits are lost… This is one of the major sins of the adventure”). But the page immediately supplies a second, different ratio, so Lynch offers illustrations of bloat, not a fixed ratio.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that the charge is a fixed inflation ratio — the page's very next sentence gives a different one (“Six are used when it needed two, or three”)",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i424-438.json"
  },
  "438": {
   "index": 438,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim: “The villagers and places, in particular, seem more alive than they have in many other WOTC/5E adventures…” Residents limb: “There are multiple people in each village who all have something going on with them. Each with their own personality.” Events limb: “This is then augmented by many of the locations having events… almost every location has one or two events… It brings the place to life. No longer static many of the villages now feel alive…”",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i424-438.json"
  },
  "439": {
   "index": 439,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "those words are actually our mortal enemies",
   "note": "Raw thread page fetched with a browser UA (HTTP 200). Under '1. Write A Good Description' the 'Try this:' procedure's fourth bullet is 'Try to cut out half the words... We may not be able to cut out half of them, but make it a goal', and the same bullet reads 'When we write, we include modifiers -- adjective and adverbs -- and those words are actually our mortal enemies.' Quote exact. Note the article's own top-level step 4 is 'Delivery: Don't Read - Perform'; the halving is the fourth step of the WRITING procedure (Dausuul labels it 'D' in reply #5), which is what the claim says.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i439-453.json"
  },
  "440": {
   "index": 440,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "a flap of skin flaps under an old woman's arm",
   "note": "Same page, same section: 'if I describe the way a flap of skin flaps under an old woman's arm, it paints a vivid image in your mind of the whole woman, very quickly.' Quote exact. Standing-for-the-whole limb also carried by 'we can say a lot about a scene with one or two well-chosen details' and 'What's the one thing you can describe about a person or a place that will carry with it enough detail of the whole?' The claim's 'loose skin' is a gloss of the page's 'flap of skin', which is description, not quotation.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i439-453.json"
  },
  "441": {
   "index": 441,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "instinctive DM pedantry... This is unnecessary. Players know about illusions.",
   "note": "Post #5 by Dausuul, dated Jul 15, 2013 on the page (matches the claim's attribution and date), rewriting boxed text from 'Bastion of Broken Souls'. Step D reads: '\"Appears as\" is just instinctive DM pedantry--hey, it could be an illusion, so I have to qualify that it \"appears\" this way. This is unnecessary. Players know about illusions.' The phrase is duly cut: the revision reads 'It's a python from the waist down.' Quote exact, every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i439-453.json"
  },
  "442": {
   "index": 442,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "When was the last time you met a snake that wasn't scaly?",
   "note": "Same post #5, same step D: 'Scaliness was pure fluff from the start. When was the last time you met a snake that wasn't scaly?' Quote exact. The 'noun already carries it' limb is the page's own reasoning — snakes are inherently scaly, and one sentence earlier 'by choosing \"python\" to replace \"snake,\" we have already implied bigness, color, and coils, so we can cut those.' The source description is the official one from Bastion of Broken Souls, quoted on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i439-453.json"
  },
  "443": {
   "index": 443,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "That's 23 words verses the originals 81.",
   "note": "Post #6 by Radiating Gnome, Jul 16, 2013: 'This is from Dungeon 213: The Dread Pirate Braxis, by Jeffrey Ludwig... (from page 40, encounter area 2, Approach)'; he calls it 'one of the descriptions of a location'. He then writes 'Here are the two -- the original and my revision -- side by side' followed by a two-cell table, and closes 'That's 23 words verses the originals 81.' (typo 'verses' is the page's). Quote exact; all limbs on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i439-453.json"
  },
  "444": {
   "index": 444,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the ramp needs to climb, not rise. Rise sounds like floating up effortlessly",
   "note": "Post #6: '\"rising like a ramp out of the island's bedrock\" ... \"rising\" isn't my first choice here. We want to give the sense of the cliff and fortress above, the ramp needs to climb, not rise. Rise sounds like you're floating up effortlessly.' The replacement is executed in the final version: 'A lone craggy causeway climbs from island bedrock...'. Quote exact; the causeway is the subject; the effortless-floating rationale is verbatim on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i439-453.json"
  },
  "445": {
   "index": 445,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the alliterative 'craggy causeway' which will resonate in the listener's ear",
   "note": "Post #6: 'Stone is probably not necessary... And that gives us the alliterative \"craggy causeway\" which will resonate in the listener's ear.' Quote exact. He keeps it deliberately — 'craggy causeway' survives into both revisions — and later reinforces the sound argument: 'it bring's the \"c\" sounds in from the beginning of the passage.' Strictly the alliteration is produced by cutting 'stone' as much as kept, but the deliberateness limb holds.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i439-453.json"
  },
  "446": {
   "index": 446,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "down (feet) to up (cliff)... That seems like a lot of redirection",
   "note": "Post #6 traces it explicitly: 'the reader's attention is going from down (feet) to up (cliff) to up (stronghold) to up (battlements) jump all the way down (causeway) to up (stronghold) to down (tower). That seems like a lot of redirection -- putting the causeway and the tower much earlier in the description also evens out that progression a bit.' Quote exact. The listener-loss limb is on the same page a few paragraphs up: the tower 'is buried in the description. Assuming you lose listeners after the first or second sentence of reading this...' — note the two rationales sit in separate paragraphs (redirection/evenness here, listener-loss there), but both are the page's.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i439-453.json"
  },
  "447": {
   "index": 447,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "better then strange names that imply a cliched world",
   "note": "Raw blogspot page fetched (HTTP 200). Names section: 'Simple names used in a way that imply a strange world are better then strange names that imply a cliched world.' Quote exact. Unmemorability limb: 'no need to add names that are impossible to remember', 'The dwarf merchant with the eye-patch is a lot easier to distinguish from other dwarves of business then \"Gundrun Rockseeker\"', and later 'nothing beyond an immediately forgotten fantasy name'. The claim's 'a familiar one' glosses the page's 'a cliched world'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i439-453.json"
  },
  "448": {
   "index": 448,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The locations of course have no evocative detail despite having a paragraph each.",
   "note": "Phandalin section, verbatim on the page: 'Following Lost Mine's tradition of almost good design philosophy in the shabbiest most cliched of dressing, the town has a few point-crawl style locations and a sidebar of NPCs. The locations of course have no evocative detail despite having a paragraph each.' Quote exact; both limbs (a paragraph each, no evocative detail) are the same sentence.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i439-453.json"
  },
  "449": {
   "index": 449,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "emblematic of Lost Mine's odd devotion to an antiquated house style",
   "note": "Phandalin section: 'Also a unique text box for the one line \"Let's find an Inn\" that the rescued NPC might say is a waste, and one emblematic of Lost Mine's odd devotion to an antiquated house style.' Quote exact. The 'rather than of that scene' limb is carried by the next sentences: 'the NPC's reactions to the town are worth noting if they are special, but finding an inn is not a clue or even a bit of color worth noting... this is a pointless waste of space.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i439-453.json"
  },
  "450": {
   "index": 450,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "a simple list of names with 'has a quest for party' behind them is useless",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'Bryce Lynch praises the same device' — nothing on this page has Bryce Lynch praising the NPC sidebar or any quest-list device",
   "note": "The Gus L. half is fully verbatim: 'A side bar lists the important NPCs about town, but provides nothing beyond an immediately forgotten fantasy name and the note that they have a quest for the party... but a simple list of names with \"has a quest for party\" behind them is useless.' Bryce Lynch does appear on the page twice, but neither instance praises this device: Gus L. praises HIM ('I have read and had my work reviewed by tenfootpole.org. Bryce is excellent when it comes to explaining what makes a good adventure'), and Lynch's own comment of April 20, 2016 attacks D&D as 'not only generic but aggressively generic' — no mention of NPC sidebars or quest lists. The praise limb needs a tenfootpole.org source it does not have here.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i439-453.json"
  },
  "451": {
   "index": 451,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "A 'goblin' is a vaguely defined monster... is a mystery to be unraveled",
   "note": "Description section: 'A \"goblin\" is a vaguely defined monster that low level adventurers are supposed to kill. A \"4' tall thing with mottled white and lavender skin, vague drooping features, four ropy, ill matched limbs and a musty fungal odor\" is a mystery to be unraveled.' Quote exact. Both halves describe the same creature (a goblin), and the surrounding text supplies the argument: 'monsters are reduced to ever more boring cliches if their only description is a name from a monster guide.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i439-453.json"
  },
  "452": {
   "index": 452,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the text mentioned the runes alongside other, less important details",
   "note": "Raw dmdavid.com tag page carries the full post 'Picturing the dungeon - boxed text' (David Hartlage, November 15, 2012). 'In 2012, the final challenge required you to close six arcane gates while demons poured out... the DM dutifully read the boxed text, which included a description of mysterious runes on the pillars in the room. Like any good boxed text description, the text mentioned the runes alongside other, less important details. No one on my team appreciated the runes significance, so we failed to discover that disabling the runes reduced the extremely difficult arcana checks required to close the gates.' Quote exact; year, tournament (D&D Championship at GEN CON), room, load-bearing runes and whole-team miss all supported. One nuance short of a downgrade: Hartlage frames the text as 'any good boxed text description' and adds 'We could not blame the DM's wording, because every team heard the same boxed text' — so it is a failure of the form's information order, not one he pins on that DM's phrasing.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i439-453.json"
  },
  "453": {
   "index": 453,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "their voice takes a different tone and cadence than when they speak extemporaneously",
   "note": "Same post: 'Boxed text does have a drawback. When most speakers read something aloud, their voice takes a different tone and cadence than when they speak extemporaneously, and that monotony can cause players' attention to wander.' Quote exact; delivery-as-cause and monotony-loses-the-listener are the same sentence. He offers a second, content-side cause immediately after ('too many boxed text descriptions have lingered on... obviously unimportant fluff'), which does not contradict the claim.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i439-453.json"
  },
  "454": {
   "index": 454,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Perhaps too many boxed text descriptions have lingered on descriptions of the color of the sunset and other bits of obviously unimportant fluff.",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200) — the tag URL serves the full 2012 post 'Picturing the dungeon - boxed text', bylined David Hartlage. Quote matches character for character; both limbs (sunset colour as the named example, 'too many' boxed descriptions dwelling on it) are on the page. The page hedges with 'Perhaps'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the page hedges with 'Perhaps too many boxed text descriptions have lingered on...'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "455": {
   "index": 455,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "No one has shown a better way to summarize what the players must learn when they enter a room.",
   "note": "Same raw page. Quote verbatim. The paraphrase limb is also there two sentences earlier: 'when I know the content well enough, I paraphrase boxed text. Still, even for DMs who never read boxed text, it helps.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i454-468.json"
  },
  "456": {
   "index": 456,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "In FR1 Waterdeep and the North, the Yawning Portal appears as Building #4: 4. The Yawning Portal (inn) - See Durnan, p. 17",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200). Quote verbatim including the en dash. 'First official entry' is supported: the page states the inn did not appear in the 1987 FRCS boxed set and that FR1 carried 'the first details of Durnan's tavern'. 'No description' is supported by the quoted line itself plus 'most of the original information ... is actually contained in the NPC write-up of its proprietor'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i454-468.json"
  },
  "457": {
   "index": 457,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Details of the inn itself have been omitted from these pages to allow DMs free rein in customizing this rambling, shady place.",
   "note": "Same raw page. Quote verbatim inside the block Alexander quotes from Ruins of Undermountain (1991). The implausibility limb is his next line: 'We've detailed this location in THREE different books, but we haven't included those details here so that you can have free rein in customizing it.' Yeah. Sure. Whatever you say, TSR.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i454-468.json"
  },
  "458": {
   "index": 458,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Durnan established the Yawning Portal in 1306 DR. / The Yawning Portal is a 3-story Class C building.",
   "note": "Same raw page. Quote verbatim as a City of Splendors (1994) new-detail bullet; the building-class limb is the adjacent bullet. 'Adding to the accumulated description' matches 'City of Splendors mostly compiles the known information ... but there are a few notable new details'. 'Registry-style' is the researcher's own characterisation, not the page's wording, but the facts quoted are of that kind (a founding year and a building class code).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i454-468.json"
  },
  "459": {
   "index": 459,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "what made those modules a popular success was the way they went beyond the thin backgrounds of earlier adventures ... memorable NPCs with hopes, dreams, and fears of their own",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), posted by James Maliszewski, 4 Dec 2008. Quote verbatim. 'Pre-1982' is a one-step inference the page licenses directly: the same paragraph opens 'Tracy Hickman made his TSR debut in 1982' and the contrast is with adventures earlier than his; the page's own word is 'earlier'. The NPC limb is verbatim, and 'introducing' matches 'what Hickman was doing was nothing short of revolutionary'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i454-468.json"
  },
  "460": {
   "index": 460,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "It is recommended that the DM read the module thoroughly before play starts, making notes in the margins where useful.",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200). Quote is an exact substring of the sentence Maliszewski quotes from C1's 'Notes for the Dungeon Master'. The page attributes it to that section by name. The module's register is 'It is recommended', slightly softer than the claim's 'instructing'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i454-468.json"
  },
  "461": {
   "index": 461,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The words on the page are only a memory cue to recall what you initially imagined.",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), 'The Book Introduction' on Bryce Lynch's tenfootpole.org. Quote verbatim. The transfer limb is the next paragraph: 'You have to transfer that vision from your head down to paper and in such a way that it can then get into someone else's head.' The private-note limb is the rope-bridge example that precedes the quote.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i454-468.json"
  },
  "462": {
   "index": 462,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "there are at least two translations between your head and the gamemaster ... leading to dry and tedious encounters",
   "note": "Same raw page. Quote verbatim, and the very next sentence carries the blame limb: 'Those translations seldom happen successfully, leading to dry and tedious encounters.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i454-468.json"
  },
  "463": {
   "index": 463,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "it should be clearly delineated from the rest of the key and contain all of the information that should be immediately conveyed when the PCs first enter the room",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), 'The Art of the Key - Part 2'. Quote verbatim; the page names the block 'seen in a glance'. Order limbs hold: 'Directly after the boxed text are the reactive skill checks', then 'At this point, each significant element ... is independently described'. One nuance: an optional 'TITLE OF THE ROOM' precedes the boxed text, so the glance block is first among the descriptive blocks, not literally the first line.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "an optional 'TITLE OF THE ROOM' precedes the boxed text, so the glance block is first among the descriptive blocks, not literally first",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "464": {
   "index": 464,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "move from the general to the specific while paying attention to how the players gain that information",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'tracked against how the reader will actually come by each fact' - the page ties the ordering to how the PLAYERS gain the information, not the reader (the GM) of the key",
   "note": "Raw page, section 'DEVELOPING SIGNIFICANT ELEMENTS'. Quote verbatim: 'What you want to do is to move from the general to the specific while paying particular attention to how the players are gaining that information.' The rationale paragraph is explicitly player-side: 'In interacting with a room, the players are most likely to start by asking questions about the things they've just been told about'. Alexander's separate GM-side point (bold titles making information easy to find) is about scanning, not about general-to-specific ordering. Swapping 'players' for 'reader' inverts whose acquisition of information governs the sequence, so the second limb is unsupported as written.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i454-468.json"
  },
  "465": {
   "index": 465,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Short, evocative writing communicates the feel of a place, allowing the GM to fill in the blanks with his imagination.",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), Age of Dusk 'Reviewing Standards', Writing section. Quote verbatim, both limbs in the one sentence. Worth flagging for the researcher: the same page's 'Shit I hate' list includes 'Blank spaces where The GM can fill in something of his own design' - a different sense of blank (unwritten content), not a contradiction of this sentence.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i454-468.json"
  },
  "466": {
   "index": 466,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Everyone dislikes long tedious backstories ..., room descriptions that go on for two pages, boxed read-aloud text, long exposition ...",
   "note": "Same raw page, same Writing paragraph. Quote verbatim and it sits inside the 'Everyone dislikes' list exactly as the claim describes.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i454-468.json"
  },
  "467": {
   "index": 467,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "When describing things, I only use the bolded words! The players receive no extra information unless they ask for it!",
   "note": "Raw page (curl, HTTP 200), 'On Set Design', 4 June 2012. Quote verbatim; the waiting-on-a-question limb is the following sentence. The post is signed '-C'; authorship as Courtney Campbell is corroborated on another page fetched in this same chunk (thealexandrian.net/wordpress/35201 names 'Courtney Campbell's Hack & Slash: On Set Design').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i454-468.json"
  },
  "468": {
   "index": 468,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Any word that is not important, is not used.",
   "note": "Same raw page, in the bullet list breaking down the notation. Quote verbatim, and it is stated as a rule of that same location notation, so both limbs hold.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i454-468.json"
  },
  "469": {
   "index": 469,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Dr Simon, post #8, names the Dragonlance boxed text",
   "note": "Quote verbatim on the page: 'I think my favourite worst offender is the very first piece of boxed text that you meet in the Dragonlance series; \"The air surges fierce and sweet...\"'. But it is post #8 by Dr Simon (Jul 18, 2013), not delericho. delericho's post #7 (Jul 16, 2013) is the bullet-point-instead-of-boxed-text advice; Dr Simon's post opens by addressing 'Delericho,' which is the likely source of the mix-up. Claim sentence itself is otherwise fully supported.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "attribution to forum member 'delericho'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i469-483.json"
  },
  "470": {
   "index": 470,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The Forest Oracle's 'men who are neither tarrying nor running'",
   "note": "Dausuul, post #5, Jul 15 2013: 'Now, this isn't awful boxed text--nothing like the infamous bit from \"The Forest Oracle\" with the men who are neither tarrying nor running--but it can certainly be tightened up considerably.' Author, post number and 'infamous' framing all match.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i469-483.json"
  },
  "471": {
   "index": 471,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "avoid having rooms that are just one thing",
   "note": "Alexandrian, 'Random GM Tip: Awesome Dungeon Rooms' (19 Jan 2022): 'our key tip today for making awesome dungeon rooms is to avoid having rooms that are just one thing*'. Quote verbatim; the rule is stated as the article's key tip for a keyed room.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i469-483.json"
  },
  "472": {
   "index": 472,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "include at least three interactive elements",
   "note": "Same article: 'A good rule of thumb here is to include at least three interactive elements (i.e., things that require or reward the players for doing more than just looking at them).' Both limbs -- the number three and 'more than looking' -- are on the page verbatim.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i469-483.json"
  },
  "473": {
   "index": 473,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "one flavorful thing without a significant interaction",
   "note": "Same article, the footnote marked by the asterisk: 'The exception are empty rooms. In my dungeons, empty rooms are rarely truly empty (in the sense of being barren and featureless). Instead, \"empty\" rooms commonly feature one flavorful thing which does not have a significant interaction. (\"Cool mural! Okay, let's go left.\")'. 'Barren and featureless' and 'footnote' both match.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i469-483.json"
  },
  "474": {
   "index": 474,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "note crucial details buried in the middle of a paragraph",
   "note": "'How to Prep a Module - Part 2' (28 Jul 2021), under the heading PREPPING PUBLISHED DUNGEONS: 'I usually won't waste a lot of time bringing them up to code... I find it more effective to occasionally make note of particularly crucial details that are essential to the adventure but buried in the middle of a paragraph, to make sure they don't get missed during play.' Quote verbatim and the section heading matches. Nuance: the page's own adverb is 'occasionally', set against 'usually won't' rewrite -- i.e. this is his standing light-touch repair rather than a per-room routine.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i469-483.json"
  },
  "475": {
   "index": 475,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "main text and glossary define the Influence action differently",
   "note": "'Review: Heroes of the Borderlands (D&D Starter Set 2025)' (16 Oct 2025): 'For example, in the main text the Influence action is defined as making a Charisma check to alter a creature's attitude. In the glossary, however, the Influence action is defined as making a monster do something you want and the monster's attitude is now a modifier on the check.' Quote verbatim; both definitions are located in the set's Play Guide, which the review has just called a glossary-based rulebook.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i469-483.json"
  },
  "476": {
   "index": 476,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "boxed text shouldn't be more than two sentences",
   "note": "GM Lent, post #31, Apr 5 2025: 'I remember reading a suggestion somewhere that boxed text shouldn't be more than two sentences, and should focus on what a character can physically sense when entering the room (what they see, feel, hear, etc). Any monsters or creatures in the room should get a mention too.' Author, post number, date, the recall framing, the sensing limb and the creatures limb all match.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i469-483.json"
  },
  "477": {
   "index": 477,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "3 to 5 sentences at most",
   "note": "el-remmen (listed as Moderator Emeritus), post #34, Apr 5 2025: 'I find it useful if written well and concisely. 3 to 5 sentences at most and then other details listed to be shared if the party looks closer/investigates/interacts.' Quote verbatim; the held-back-detail limb is the second half of the same sentence.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i469-483.json"
  },
  "478": {
   "index": 478,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "hates plain descriptors and boxes of unevocative keyterms",
   "note": "Shardstone, post #32, Apr 5 2025: 'I love read aloud text in my adventures... I really hate adventures that have very plain descriptors or that have a box of simple keyterms that aren't evocative.' Quote verbatim; the pro-prose limb and the bullet-keyword alternative are both explicit.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i469-483.json"
  },
  "479": {
   "index": 479,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "'off to the side' changes when you enter another door",
   "note": "Shiroiken, quoted in full inside Lanefan's post #36 (Apr 5 2025): 'I personally dislike the cardinal directions because the party may not know the cardinal direction... Personally I prefer vague descriptions when dealing with relative locations. \"Off to the side\" and \"nearby\" are typically adequate.' Lanefan replies: 'To me that's exactly the problem: \"off to the side\" when coming in one door might be \"across the room\" when coming in another or \"against one wall\" if coming up through a trap door in the floor.' Both halves of the claim, and the 'quoted post' page note, check out.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i469-483.json"
  },
  "480": {
   "index": 480,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "basic room description first, then monsters, minor details after the fight",
   "note": "DragonLancer, quoted inside MarkB's post #39 (Apr 5 2025): 'The author will describe the minutia in a room but never mention any monsters or at the very least, put that in last. They would rather have a basic description of a scene or room, then the monsters, and any relevant minor details after a fight or encounter.' Quote is verbatim and the deferred-minor-detail limb is exact. But the ordering limb inverts him: he wants a basic room description BEFORE the creatures, with only the minutia held back -- not the creatures named first.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "creatures named before the furnishings",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i469-483.json"
  },
  "481": {
   "index": 481,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "modules' language differs from his GMing voice; reading flags a clue",
   "note": "humble minion, post #4, Jul 12 2020: 'I try not to use it, because the way modules are written is very different to my normal use of language when I'm GMing. Reading out a chunk of boxed text is basically equivalent to waving a \"this location/NPC is A Clue to the plot, pay lots of attention to it\" flag in front of my players' eyes.' Quote verbatim; both limbs present.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i469-483.json"
  },
  "482": {
   "index": 482,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "it turns the DM into a TextToSpeech bot",
   "note": "oriaxx77, the thread's opening post #1, Jul 12 2020: 'I am from eastern Europe where not everyone speaks English. Maybe that is why. The read aloud seems weird and it turns the DM into a TextToSpeech bot.' Opening-poster, eastern-Europe and speech-synthesiser limbs all verbatim.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i469-483.json"
  },
  "483": {
   "index": 483,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "divided between common information and bold text players aren't meant to know",
   "note": "Age of Dusk, '[Review] T1 The Village of Hommlet (AD&D); Foreshadow', posted July 31 2021 by Prince: 'The village itself is helpfully divided between common information and bold text that the players are not meant to know and that can only be discovered by careful investigation.' It sits in the paragraph beginning 'There are the usual early TSR bugaboos with the module', exactly as the page field says.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i469-483.json"
  },
  "484": {
   "index": 484,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Room descriptions in thick, indigestible paragraphs",
   "note": "Raw HTML fetched (200, 51KB) and tag-stripped. Body: 'These are the usual early TSR bugaboos with the module; Room descriptions in thick, indigestible paragraphs and notes on monster placement in rooms other then the one where the monster is actually placed...' Byline PrinceofNothing, dated 2021/07/31.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i484-498.json"
  },
  "485": {
   "index": 485,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "There are also the odd Victorian room catalogs",
   "note": "Same paragraph: 'There are also the odd Victorian room catalogs (i.e. this room has 23 halbeards, 315 arrows and 5 casks of oil).' The parenthetical confirms the 'itemised room contents' limb.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i484-498.json"
  },
  "486": {
   "index": 486,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "A vast catalogue of seemingly mundane NPCs are presented",
   "note": "Verbatim in the village paragraph: 'A vast catalogue of seemingly mundane NPCs are presented, but underneath the facade of rural simplicity is a web of intrigue, espionage...'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i484-498.json"
  },
  "487": {
   "index": 487,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "creates the appearance of a world in flux, a continuous place, a living world",
   "note": "Paragraph opens 'Little details in the village. Unscrupulous merchants that sell broken down nags... Stores with prices that are a few percentage points higher or lower then standard, availability, a lengthy list of drinks and meals at the inn. It affects gameplay but it also creates the appearance of a world in flux, a continuous place, a living world.' Both the commercial-detail limb and the continuous/living-world limb are on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i484-498.json"
  },
  "488": {
   "index": 488,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "subsequent module design would be considerably sparser on their starting town details",
   "note": "Verbatim: '...up almost half the adventure, and subsequent module design would be considerably sparser on their starting town details, but I suspect this level of detail... will come'. 'after T1' = 'subsequent'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i484-498.json"
  },
  "489": {
   "index": 489,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Author Carl Sargent was a master at implying terrifying things with a few carefully chosen words",
   "note": "Raw HTML fetched (200, 74KB). Byline 'by John Roy', Oct 21, 2024, title 'Greyhawk Returns in the 2024 Dungeon Master's Guide'. Sentence follows three paragraphs on the Greyhawk supplement 'Iuz the Evil', so the 'Greyhawk author' limb is supported; full page wording adds 'that suggested more than they described.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i484-498.json"
  },
  "490": {
   "index": 490,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "descriptions of Oerth's nations in the chapter's Greyhawk Gazetteer, along with their history of conflict",
   "note": "Verbatim, in the 'A Battle-Ready World' section, whose preceding sentence names 'The Greyhawk campaign setting in the 2024 Dungeon Master's Guide', supporting the '2024 DMG's' limb; page continues 'and what strife they're currently facing.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i484-498.json"
  },
  "491": {
   "index": 491,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "He provided each nation's imports and exports and the trade routes they used to transport them",
   "note": "Verbatim in the 'Keeping It Real' section, subject 'Gygax' established two sentences earlier ('Gygax laid out not only the current nations of Oerik...'). Section heading is 'Keeping It Real', not the claim's page hint 'wargamer's setting', but the hint is not a limb of the claim.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i484-498.json"
  },
  "492": {
   "index": 492,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The first part is the gazetteer proper, covering the history, politics, and society of the Grand Duchy, including descriptions of its most important locales and NPCs",
   "note": "Raw HTML fetched (200, 23KB). Preceding sentence 'The module is divided into two sections' supports the 'half' limb; posted by James Maliszewski.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i484-498.json"
  },
  "493": {
   "index": 493,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "the module boasted of providing 'a complete historical, economical [sic], geographical, and sociological overview'",
   "note": "The quotation is verbatim on the page (with a bracketed [sic] after 'economical'), and the four adjectives plus 'complete... overview' all match. But the page attributes it only to 'the module boasted of providing' — it never says the wording is the cover/back-cover copy, so that attribution limb rests on outside knowledge, not this page.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that the quoted boast is GAZ1's cover copy",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i484-498.json"
  },
  "494": {
   "index": 494,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Scattered throughout the module are numerous maps for use in play, like typical taverns and manor houses",
   "note": "'typical taverns and manor houses' is verbatim. The page draws no contrast with named/specific taverns; the only contrast it makes is with the poster-sized map ('This is in addition to the large, poster-sized map of the Grand Duchy, its capital city of Specularum, and the frontier town of Threshold'), which is a different contrast from the one the claim asserts.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "rather than only named ones",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i484-498.json"
  },
  "495": {
   "index": 495,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the focus of Gaz 1 is not on all the exciting adventure locations... but rather, everything around those adventures, setting up the framework",
   "note": "Raw HTML fetched (200, 57KB), princeofnothingblogs 2020/03/30. Both limbs (not the adventure locations / everything around them) are in the same sentence.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i484-498.json"
  },
  "496": {
   "index": 496,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "In the hands of a lesser man, a boring encyclopedic recitation of meaningless and sometimes mutually contradictory drivel",
   "note": "Verbatim, immediately after the sentence verified for 484-index 495; 'In the hands of a lesser man' establishes it as the form's failure mode, contrasted with 'In the hands of Aaron Allston... the whole becomes compelling'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i484-498.json"
  },
  "497": {
   "index": 497,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Setting history is given in broad sweeping strokes",
   "note": "Verbatim at the start of its paragraph, continuing 'that serve the goal of hinting at prior ages from which the GM may readibly draw inspiration for a whole flock of dungeons.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i484-498.json"
  },
  "498": {
   "index": 498,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The capital city of Specularum gets an extensive treatment in terms of neighborhoods, main streets, ducal palace etc.",
   "note": "Verbatim; the paragraph does begin 'Contrary to what you might believe there's actual content in this damn Gazzeteer as well', matching the page hint.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i484-498.json"
  },
  "499": {
   "index": 499,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "every little settlement fits, is intelligently placed (trade routes considered)",
   "note": "Raw page (live URL, HTTP 200). Verbatim in the paragraph right after the Korizegy Keep sentences: 'What is probably better is that every little settlement fits, is intelligently placed (i.e. trade routes and bodies are considered), has distinguishing features and has a place within Karameikos.' Praise framing and the trade-route limb both explicit; page title '[Review] Gaz 1 The Grand Duchy of Karameikos (DnD Basic)' on Age of Dusk, byline Prince of Nothing.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i499-513.json"
  },
  "500": {
   "index": 500,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "boring ambassadors that add little: 'Just do a few sentences and ditch'",
   "note": "Raw page (live URL, HTTP 200). Verbatim in the Characters-section paragraph: 'The low point is the ambassadors, some of which are boring and add little... Just do a few sentences and ditch.' The claim's 'minor NPC' is a gloss on the page's 'boring and add little' ambassadors; the prescription and the adds-little limb are both on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i499-513.json"
  },
  "501": {
   "index": 501,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "locations have no evocative detail despite having a paragraph each",
   "note": "Raw page (live URL, HTTP 200), section THE TOWN AND BANDITS: 'the town has a few point-crawl style locations and a sidebar of NPCs. The locations of course have no evocative detail despite having a paragraph each.' Town is Phandalin (named throughout the section); the page's wording for 'keyed' is 'point-crawl style'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i499-513.json"
  },
  "502": {
   "index": 502,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The Inn for example is a 'large newly built roadhouse of field stone...'",
   "note": "Raw page (live URL, HTTP 200), same paragraph, and the review does present it as a quotation from the module (double-quoted, followed by 'exactly like every other boring nondescript fantasy inn'). Quote appears word for word.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i499-513.json"
  },
  "503": {
   "index": 503,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "none of the rumors add local color or an evocative detail",
   "note": "Raw page (live URL, HTTP 200), section THE TOWN AND BANDITS: 'Each rumor isn't a hook, so much as an explanation of who else in town might provide a quest, and none of the rumors add local color or an evocative detail.' Rumors are Phandalin's town rumors.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i499-513.json"
  },
  "504": {
   "index": 504,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "nothing beyond a forgotten fantasy name AND a 'has a quest' note",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'gives nothing beyond ... a fantasy name' - the page's sentence credits the sidebar with a second element the claim drops: 'provides nothing beyond an immediately forgotten fantasy name AND the note that they have a quest for the party', reinforced later by 'a simple list of names with \"has a quest for party\" behind them is useless'.",
   "note": "Raw page (live URL, HTTP 200). The quoted fragment is verbatim, and the sidebar/Phandalin limbs hold, but the claim truncates the reviewer's own 'nothing beyond X and Y' to 'nothing beyond X', overstating the emptiness of the sidebar.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i499-513.json"
  },
  "505": {
   "index": 505,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "tired of Lost Mines stupid fantasy cliches and its bloodless box text",
   "note": "Raw page (live URL, HTTP 200). Verbatim. Location differs from the claim's page hint: the sentence sits in the Redbrand-hideout discussion (after the nothic/secret-doors paragraph), not in the closing paragraph of THE TOWN AND BANDITS; the claim itself is unaffected.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i499-513.json"
  },
  "506": {
   "index": 506,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Making up towns is tricky ... a tightrope of sorts",
   "note": "Raw page (live URL, HTTP 200). Verbatim as the opening line of section THE TOWN AND BANDITS: 'Making up towns is tricky, they are instantly identifiable to players... but making them interesting without becoming the focus of the campaign is a tightrope of sorts.' 'for a designer' is implicit in a module review, not stated.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'for a designer' — the verifier's own note says that addressee limb is 'implicit in a module review, not stated' on the page",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r10.json"
  },
  "507": {
   "index": 507,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "being overwritten is a signature of 5th edition module writing",
   "note": "Raw page (live URL, HTTP 200), layout paragraph: 'while being overwritten is a signature of 5th edition module writing, it flags and highlights points of interest well'. Page is 'Bathtub Review: Lost Mine of Phandelver' on Playful Void (idlecartulary.com).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i499-513.json"
  },
  "508": {
   "index": 508,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "5e takes up a lot more space per key than they do",
   "note": "Raw page (live URL, HTTP 200), 'As for the module itself' paragraph: 'compared to the two shorter starter modules I've reviewed, because 5e takes up a lot more space per key than they do, but fits a lot more gameable content into its key' (Village of Hommlet named as one). The comparison class 'earlier starter modules' is supported; the page's balancing clause about more gameable content is not contradicted by the claim.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i499-513.json"
  },
  "509": {
   "index": 509,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Add at least one of these fantastic features and probably no more than three",
   "note": "Raw page (live URL, HTTP 200), section 'Identify a Fantastic Feature' of 'Running Towns in Fantasy RPGs', by Mike on 13 November 2023 at SlyFlourish.com. Full sentence adds 'enough to define the town in the eyes of the characters and your players', supporting the claim's 'defining a town'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i499-513.json"
  },
  "510": {
   "index": 510,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "it gave you guidelines on what to include: a town council, a merchant, a town map",
   "note": "LIVE URL IS DEAD CONTENT: merricb.com now serves an unrelated Indonesian slot-gambling parking page (HTTP 200, title 'SULTANSLOT...'), zero occurrences of 'Saltmarsh'. Recovered via Wayback raw capture https://web.archive.org/web/20230416034304id_/https://merricb.com/2019/05/22/ghosts-of-saltmarsh-looking-at-the-town/ (HTTP 200, title 'Ghosts of Saltmarsh - Looking at the Town - Merric's Musings'). Third paragraph: 'the original adventure didn't describe the town of Saltmarsh in much detail. Instead, it gave you guidelines on what to include: a town council, a merchant, a town map, a place for the PCs to rest between adventures, and the town's places of worship.' Both limbs verbatim.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i499-513.json"
  },
  "511": {
   "index": 511,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Saltmarsh's roughly five thousand residents are predominantly human",
   "note": "Same Wayback raw capture (live domain is a parked spam site). The post's block quote, attributed 'Chapter 1: Saltmarsh, \"Saltmarsh Overview\"', opens with exactly that sentence, then gives the dwarven mining contingent; population figure and predominant people are its first clause.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i499-513.json"
  },
  "512": {
   "index": 512,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "residents react to visitors, especially tieflings and dragonborn, with curiosity and fear",
   "note": "Same Wayback raw capture. Block quote's last sentence: 'The residents react to other visitors, especially tieflings and dragonborn, with a mixture of curiosity and fear.' Quote fragment verbatim; both named ancestries present.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i499-513.json"
  },
  "513": {
   "index": 513,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "I get a much better feel for the town than I did for Waterdeep in Dragon Heist",
   "note": "Same Wayback raw capture. In the paragraph beginning 'All-in-all', exactly as the claim states, including the Dragon Heist attribution.",
