  "544": {
   "index": 544,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "a booklet which offers in 96 pages what many books would have needed twice that to do",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Quote appears word for word under 'GAZ3: The Principalities of Glantri / OVERALL', preceded by 'That economy is admirable-'. Byline 'Posted by Lowell Francis', dated Wednesday, April 11, 2012. All limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i544-558.json"
  },
  "545": {
   "index": 545,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "about nine of the major buildings detailed, including the garrison, inn, jeweler, church and general store",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Quote verbatim in the paragraph beginning 'The village proper has...'. Hedge recorded: the page says 'about nine of the major buildings detailed', not a flat nine; the count itself is unchanged, so the limb stands. Posted October 7, 2011 by Bryce Lynch.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i544-558.json"
  },
  "546": {
   "index": 546,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "decent sized rumor table, as is proper for a home base environment",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Quote verbatim; the full sentence reads 'We also get a decent sized rumor table, as is proper for a home base environment.' The claim renders 'environment' as 'settlement', a faithful paraphrase of the same referent (the village of White Dragon Run as home base).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i544-558.json"
  },
  "547": {
   "index": 547,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The village is a bit ... bland. The occupants all seem to be fine upstanding members of the community",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Quote verbatim. The 'faults' limb is supported by the immediately adjacent 'The village is a bit ... bland' and 'The NPC's, major & minor, just don't seem to have much personality to them.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i544-558.json"
  },
  "548": {
   "index": 548,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Two sentences: purple stone and crystal, then 'It is ruled by Lord Zavere and Lady Rill.'",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that the key entry is a SINGLE sentence - the A2 CASTLE SHARD entry runs to two sentences, the second naming its rulers",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200), post dated January 14th, 2015. The quoted fragment 'A huge castle made from purple stone' is verbatim in THE KEY at entry A2. But the full entry reads: 'A huge castle made from purple stone, housing a massive crystal with strange magical properties. It is ruled by Lord Zavere and Lady Rill.' - two sentences, not one. Material (stone), colour (purple) and contents (the crystal) are all in the first sentence, so that limb holds; the 'single sentence' limb does not. Neighbouring entries (A1, A3, B5) are likewise one-to-three sentences, so brevity generally is supported - the specific count is not.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i544-558.json"
  },
  "549": {
   "index": 549,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Instead of writing the conclusion, you are writing the clue",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200), dated October 30th, 2020. Quote verbatim in the section headed WRITING THE BOOK; full sentence continues 'which will let the players figure out the conclusion.' All limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i544-558.json"
  },
  "550": {
   "index": 550,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "it is often useful if the key information is not what the book is primarily about",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Quote verbatim in WRITING THE BOOK, opening 'Along the same lines, it is often useful if...'. The claim's 'often' matches the page's 'often useful'. Page adds a qualifying parenthetical ('This is not universally true'), which the claim's 'often' already accommodates.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i544-558.json"
  },
  "551": {
   "index": 551,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Often I achieve this effect by presenting the information in an oblique or mythic manner",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Quote verbatim in WRITING THE BOOK. First person 'Often I achieve this effect by...' supports the claim's 'often presents'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i544-558.json"
  },
  "552": {
   "index": 552,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "these handouts more or less serve as an executive summary for a book that doesn't actually exist",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). The supplied quote fragment ends at the curly apostrophe in 'doesn't'; the full page sentence is 'In practice, these handouts more or less serve as an executive summary for a book that doesn't actually exist.' Found in the body paragraph on what lore books are, as cited. All limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i544-558.json"
  },
  "553": {
   "index": 553,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "describe the experience of reading the book ... the physicality of the book itself",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Quote verbatim in TIPS & TRICKS: 'As I mentioned before, describe the experience of reading the book.' The 'physical' limb is carried by the same passage ('This can be the physicality of the book itself') and by the earlier 'I will usually also discuss the actual physical interaction with the book.' Note the page's advice is broader than physicality alone - it also covers sequencing and revelation of knowledge - so the claim narrows rather than overstates.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i544-558.json"
  },
  "554": {
   "index": 554,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "it is rather difficult to separate what is meant to be scholarship from fancy",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Quote verbatim in the first block-indented example handout ('This slim, peculiar volume purports to be \"a dream woven from the true and factual accounts of many diverse peoples of the world,\" but it is rather difficult to separate what is meant to be scholarship from fancy.'), which Alexander presents as his own demonstration text. All limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i544-558.json"
  },
  "555": {
   "index": 555,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "most of the original information about the Yawning Portal is actually contained in the NPC write-up of its proprietor",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200), dated March 31st, 2022. Quote verbatim in THE EARLY DAYS, immediately after the FR1 key line '4. The Yawning Portal (inn) - See Durnan, p. 17', so the FR1 Waterdeep and the North limb and the proprietor (Durnan) limb are both anchored. Page says 'most of the original information', i.e. the earliest material, which is FR1's.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i544-558.json"
  },
  "556": {
   "index": 556,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "on the door itself, someone has chalked 'Come Ye Inn'",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Quote verbatim in the bulleted list of FRE3 Waterdeep details in THE EARLY DAYS: 'A signboard above the \"round door\" reads \"The Yawning Portal,\" and \"on the door itself, someone has chalked 'Come Ye Inn.'\" (sic)'. The 'welcoming visitors' limb is the plain sense of the chalked words themselves (a pun on 'come ye in'); the page quotes them without separately glossing them as a welcome.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i544-558.json"
  },
  "557": {
   "index": 557,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The Portal is a rambling, dingy, blue-tapestried building of smoothly carved pillars and paneling",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Quote verbatim in the bulleted list of new details Ruins of Undermountain (1991) adds, in THE EARLY DAYS. The claim drops 'smoothly' from 'smoothly carved pillars'; nothing else differs. All limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i544-558.json"
  },
  "558": {
   "index": 558,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Details of the inn itself have been omitted from these pages to allow DMs free rein in customizing this rambling, shady place",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Quote verbatim inside the block quotation from the Ruins of Undermountain boxed set in THE EARLY DAYS, which Alexander introduces as 'which rather hilariously notes:'. All limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i544-558.json"
  },
  "559": {
   "index": 559,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The Yawning Portal is a 3-story Class C building",
   "note": "Listed under City of Splendors (1994) 'notable new details'; quote appears word for word on the live Alexandrian page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i559-573.json"
  },
  "560": {
   "index": 560,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Durnan established the Yawning Portal in 1306 DR.",
   "note": "Same City of Splendors bullet list; quote verbatim, attribution to the boxed set explicit in the surrounding sentence.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i559-573.json"
  },
  "561": {
   "index": 561,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The well is 140 feet deep, dark below 50 feet",
   "note": "Under 'Ruins of Undermountain does add several new details'; the preceding bullet places the first well 'in the main taproom'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i559-573.json"
  },
  "562": {
   "index": 562,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Three blood-stained scrolls have been bound together",
   "note": "Quote verbatim, and the scrolls are named before the three contents sections; but the handout's first block is a set-off italic verse epigraph about the carach, so the naming is not the opening.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'opens' — an italic blockquote epigraph ('Like an arrow drawn from its quiver...') precedes the naming of the physical document",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i559-573.json"
  },
  "563": {
   "index": 563,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "achievements of the carach, which one can only hope are exaggerated",
   "note": "In UNTO THE DEATH OF WORLDS, hedging the scroll's escalating boasts (princes slain, kingdoms fall, worlds destroyed).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i559-573.json"
  },
  "564": {
   "index": 564,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "It appears that the carach... are methodical and relentless",
   "note": "Same section; the hedge marks an inference about behaviour the scroll only implies. One instance on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i559-573.json"
  },
  "565": {
   "index": 565,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "he is concerned that the text is 'obviously padded'",
   "note": "Wayback raw capture of the Medium article, Development Hell Level 7; Frank Mentzer's redline review of the 'Quagmire' rough draft.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i559-573.json"
  },
  "566": {
   "index": 566,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "'obviously padded' and 'extremely repetitive'",
   "note": "Same sentence as 565; both faults are the summary side-comments on the same rough draft.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i559-573.json"
  },
  "567": {
   "index": 567,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Constructions like 'Patriarch Cleric' are redundant, as in 'female girl'",
   "note": "Level 7, among Mentzer's system-point corrections; quote verbatim.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i559-573.json"
  },
  "568": {
   "index": 568,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "making sure modules exercised the system consistently",
   "note": "Page: Mentzer as shepherd of BECMI held responsibility that modules 'exercised the system consistently and met with quality expectations'; the article's examples are terminology fixes.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i559-573.json"
  },
  "569": {
   "index": 569,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Every TSR module had a product standards sheet which listed all of the styles, typesizes, margins",
   "note": "Development Hell Level 8: The Proof; 'and related layout decisions' follows.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i559-573.json"
  },
  "570": {
   "index": 570,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Much of the detail which troubled Frank... did not make the final cut",
   "note": "Level 7; the cut detail is enumerated as 'the saving throw notation and the flavor text'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i559-573.json"
  },
  "571": {
   "index": 571,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "measured both by page count and by line count",
   "note": "Level 5; lines 'reflect the total number of lines in the HP3000 EDIT2 program' and Merle's filled values 'are projections'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i559-573.json"
  },
  "572": {
   "index": 572,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "TSR removed the names of cities from the map",
   "note": "Level 6; 'Even the pointer to the Spiral City of Quagmire itself is withheld from the players.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i559-573.json"
  },
  "573": {
   "index": 573,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "only a few lines of text on the front: code, author, flavor text",
   "note": "Level 8; quote verbatim and the flavor text does sit below the cover illustration, but the page lists two further items on the front and never states the release year (it only puts release after the April 3, 1984 layoff).",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'only a few sentences of flavor text' — the front also carried the module's code and its author's name",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i559-573.json"
  },
  "574": {
   "index": 574,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "In total, I read and compared more than 5,500 pages of text across the versions of the game.",
   "note": "T&F returned 403 to a browser UA and its Wayback captures (abs and full) carry only the paywalled abstract; the raw full text was reached through the Internet Archive capture of an academia.edu copy of the same article (web.archive.org/web/20201004125159id_/https://www.academia.edu/39684237/...), which carries the journal running heads. Quote 'more than 5,500 pages' is verbatim. Pathfinder is one of the 12 editions in the study's Table 1 and its 575-page Core Rulebook is named in the same paragraph, so the 'D&D and Pathfinder' limb holds. One metadata correction: the sentence falls after the running head 'MIND, CULTURE, AND ACTIVITY 237', i.e. on p. 237, not p. 236 as the chunk's page field says; the claim sentence itself asserts no page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i574-588.json"
  },
  "575": {
   "index": 575,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Centuries of use have neutered the male pronoun. In written material it is clear, concise, and familiar. Nothing else is.",
   "note": "Same recovered full text. Garcia quotes Cook (1989) p. 9 in a block quote: 'The male pronoun (he, him, his) is used exclusively throughout the AD&D game rules... In written material it is clear, concise, and familiar.' Quote 'clear, concise, and familiar' verbatim; every limb (1989, 2nd edition, exclusive male pronoun, defence, written material) is on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i574-588.json"
  },
  "576": {
   "index": 576,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Explanations about pronouns would cease with later editions of D&D, starting with the third (Wizards of the Coast, 2000).",
   "note": "Same recovered full text; the sentence immediately follows the Cook block quote. Quote verbatim, all limbs supported including the 2000 date.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i574-588.json"
  },
  "577": {
   "index": 577,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "In the Forgotten Realms, nine human ethnic groups are widely recognized, though over a dozen others are found in more localized areas of Faerun. (PHB p. 30)",
   "note": "Same recovered full text. Garcia block-quotes the 5e PHB p. 30. Quote verbatim; 'nine', 'widely recognized' and 'Forgotten Realms' all present.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i574-588.json"
  },
  "578": {
   "index": 578,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The material culture and physical characteristics of humans can change wildly from region to region (p. 30).",
   "note": "Same recovered full text. This sentence is the lead-in Garcia quotes from PHB p. 30 and is followed by 'It continues,' then the nine-ethnic-groups block, so 'the same 5e passage' holds.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i574-588.json"
  },
  "579": {
   "index": 579,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Grudging Acceptance: Each half-orc finds a way to gain acceptance from those who hate orcs.",
   "note": "Same recovered full text. 'Grudging Acceptance' is verbatim and Garcia introduces the block with 'half-orcs have been largely despised by others' (PHB p. 41), so the named paragraph and the reception limb hold. The typographic limb 'bolded' is not attested: the recovered page carries no bold markup and no statement about how the heading is set (it may well be bold in the printed PHB, but the cited source does not say so).",
   "unsupportedLimb": "bolded — the article quotes the paragraph as plain running text and never describes its typography",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i574-588.json"
  },
  "580": {
   "index": 580,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the rewriting and editing necessary to extract a beginner's set of D&D from the basic set and its supplements",
   "note": "Live page fetched raw (HTTP 200). May 1978 entry, Dragon #14 Sorcerer's Scroll: Holmes 'was interested in undertaking the first stage of the project - the rewriting and editing necessary to extract a beginner's set of D&D from the basic set and its supplements. The result of his labors is the \"Basic Set\" of D&D'. Quote verbatim; the page's own preamble dates the Basic Set to 1977.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i574-588.json"
  },
  "581": {
   "index": 581,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "it was decided that a simplified, clarified, introductory piece was needed",
   "note": "Live page, March 1980 entry (Dragon #35, 'What's ahead for TSR?'): 'As we realized that \"Original\" D&D (the first three booklets and the supplements) wasn't anywhere near adequate for the needs of the readership it was attracting, it was decided that a simplified, clarified, introductory piece was needed.' Quote verbatim; 'wider readership' renders 'the readership it was attracting'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i574-588.json"
  },
  "582": {
   "index": 582,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "The version written by J. Eric Holmes was a little bit easier, and now the latest version is easily understandable by anyone willing just to spend the time to read it",
   "note": "Live page, Summer of 1981 entry (Polyhedron #1 p. 5, RPGA interview with Gygax, interviewer 'presumably Frank Mentzer'). Quote 'a little bit easier' verbatim, and the contrast with the first set 'for hard-core gamers' supports the easier-than-the-original limb. The page nowhere attributes the latest version to Mentzer.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "Mentzer's — the interviewer says only 'the latest version'; Mentzer is named merely as the presumed interviewer, and the latest Basic Set in summer 1981 was Moldvay's, not Mentzer's 1983 revision",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i574-588.json"
  },
  "583": {
   "index": 583,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "To cut to the chase, I thought that Eric did a fine bit of editing with the set.",
   "note": "Live page, 2/22/05 entry, EN World forum post archived at greyhawkonline. Quote verbatim; date (2005), forum reply, favourable judgment and the Basic Set referent all supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i574-588.json"
  },
  "584": {
   "index": 584,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The goal of orientalism is to produce encyclopedic knowledge about an imagined and exotic other.",
   "note": "Live page fetched raw (HTTP 200), Aaron Trammell, 11 January 2016, Vol. III No. I. Quote verbatim, in an image caption just before the 'Comeliness and Honor' heading. The claim's word 'register' stands in for the article's 'orientalism'; the essay elsewhere ties this to form and style ('written in a comprehensive and exhaustive style that mimics the form of an encyclopedia'), so the register framing is supported rather than added.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i574-588.json"
  },
  "585": {
   "index": 585,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "This mode of reductionism encourages players to imagine eastern culture as if it could be contained by a single text",
   "note": "Live page, Comeliness and Honor section. Quote verbatim. The 'one sourcebook' limb is carried by the next sentence: 'Oriental Adventures represents a complex and multifaceted set of cultural interests through a single and singular set of game mechanics.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i574-588.json"
  },
  "586": {
   "index": 586,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "the culture of the common people is barely distinguishable from that of the Western common folk",
   "note": "Live page, non-weapon proficiencies section. Quote verbatim, and 'the distinction of specificity between those proficiencies known by nobles and those by commoners' supports the elite-versus-commoner specificity limb (Calligraphy, Falconry, Flower Arranging, Noh, Tea Ceremony against Agriculture, Cooking, Fishing, Hunting). But the compared items are proficiencies, not occupations, and by count the common list is the longer of the two (17 entries against 12) - the article's point is specificity, not quantity.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "occupations — the page contrasts non-weapon proficiencies (skills), Court Proficiencies against Common Proficiencies, not occupations",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i574-588.json"
  },
  "587": {
   "index": 587,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Judging from the diction and style of the writing plus the similarities between the rules advanced here ... this sounds not only feasible but likely.",
   "note": "Live page, comment by 'Randy G', 8 September 2016, in the essay's comment thread. Quote verbatim. The commenter reports Cook's own statement that he wrote the bulk of the book and finds it likely on the evidence of diction and style plus rules similarities with 2e AD&D.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i574-588.json"
  },
  "588": {
   "index": 588,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Any information that could be of use to the DM, but is not meant to be seen by the players, can be included in an appendix.",
   "note": "Live page fetched raw (HTTP 200), byline 'by Shawn Merwin', dated Oct 9, 2019. Quote verbatim, in the 'Handouts, Maps, and Appendices' section rather than an outline checklist per se; the claim's substance is exact.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i574-588.json"
  },
  "589": {
   "index": 589,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Do any of your encounters contain elements better represented in a map rather than strictly described in the text?",
   "note": "Raw HTML fetched with a browser UA (HTTP 200). Byline 'by Shawn Merwin', 'Oct 9, 2019', title matches. Quote exact in the 'Handouts, Maps, and Appendices' section; posed as a checklist question to the writer, exactly as claimed.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i589-603.json"
  },
  "590": {
   "index": 590,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "give a less-detailed description that relies on place, and more on the resources",
   "note": "Raw page (HTTP 200), 'Let's Design an Adventure: Thinking Like a Designer', by Shawn Merwin, Mar 25, 2020. Sandbox limb exact ('Are you writing more of a sandbox-style adventure...'); kobold-ambush example says the DM can then set it 'in the mountain pass, the city streets, or the abandoned mines'. Caution for downstream use: the excerpted fragment stops mid-sentence and reads as if description relies ON place; the full sentence (apparently missing a 'less') puts the weight on the kobolds' resources, which is what the claim asserts.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i589-603.json"
  },
  "591": {
   "index": 591,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "make sure the layout you employ serves the DM who will be running your content",
   "note": "Raw page, 'Layout' section: 'In the end, though, make sure the layout you employ serves the DM who will be running your content. How much flipping between pages will they be forced to do?' The at-the-table limb is carried by the page-flipping/usability sentences that follow.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i589-603.json"
  },
  "592": {
   "index": 592,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "When D&D co-creator Gary Gygax created his first dungeon under Castle Greyhawk, he usually wrote a 1-line note for each room",
   "note": "Raw HTML fetched direct (HTTP 200); the /tag/ URL serves the full post 'How much description should a dungeon key include?', 21 Nov 2016, by David Hartlage. Quote exact in the opening paragraph. 'usually ... for each room' supports 'most rooms'; the post's frame is the dungeon key, and it adds that Gygax shared the notes with Rob Kuntz.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i589-603.json"
  },
  "593": {
   "index": 593,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "all they desired was a very skeletal framework with the more time-consuming level details worked out",
   "note": "Raw page, 'Tegel Manor and minimal descriptions': Bledsaw in Dragon magazine issue 27 - 'we had some bad feedback which indicated that judges felt that the actual description of dungeons was their domain and all they desired was a very skeletal framework'. Bledsaw is named a Judges Guild founder; 'judges' is the referee term, so 'referees told him' is supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i589-603.json"
  },
  "594": {
   "index": 594,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Gary's longer descriptions set the pattern for virtually every adventure to follow",
   "note": "Raw page, 'Gary Gygax sets the standard': 'Gary started publishing adventures with G1 Steading of the Hill Giant Chief (1978). He included generous descriptions for every location' then the quoted sentence. Title, year and credit limbs all on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i589-603.json"
  },
  "595": {
   "index": 595,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "This description takes a good, middle sentence and pads it like a school paper stretched to an assigned length",
   "note": "Raw page, 'Longer descriptions', on the 1989 Gamescience Tegel B9 bedroom; the next sentence supplies the convention limb: 'The custom of longer description encourages authors to write something even when they have little to add.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i589-603.json"
  },
  "596": {
   "index": 596,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "A location's purpose in the game should also figure into the length of its descriptions ... no one needs an item-by-item inventory of her kitchen",
   "note": "Raw page, 'Matching description to a location's purpose'. Both limbs exact: purpose governs length, and the sheriff example ('If the player characters meet the sheriff about a wanted poster').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i589-603.json"
  },
  "597": {
   "index": 597,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "An avalanche of description does more than squander page count. It buries many great details might actually enter play",
   "note": "Raw page, same section; the following sentence is the Victor Wachter example buried in a page-long workroom description inside a 5-page mansion. (The source sentence carries a typo, 'many great details might actually enter play', reproduced here verbatim.)",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i589-603.json"
  },
  "598": {
   "index": 598,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "boxed text consolidates and identifies features that require description",
   "note": "Raw page, 'Boxed, read-aloud text': 'Even though I seldom read-aloud text verbatim, boxed text consolidates and identifies features that require description. I like box text, but not every location needs it.' - a defence with a limit, as claimed.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i589-603.json"
  },
  "599": {
   "index": 599,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "His follow-up, Storm King's Thunder, omits almost all read-aloud text",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the parenthetical '(2016)' publication year for Storm King's Thunder - the page dates neither adventure",
   "note": "Quote exact and the contrast limb is exact too ('Curse of Strahd includes it for every location'), but the page gives no publication year for Storm King's Thunder; its only 2016 dates are the post's own date (November 21, 2016) and archive-sidebar labels. The year is independently correct but is not evidence from this page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i589-603.json"
  },
  "600": {
   "index": 600,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Curtis explains that the format provides 'the minimum amount of information needed to run the dungeon'",
   "note": "Raw page, 'The influence of one-page dungeons': 'Michael Curtis, author of the well-reviewed Stonehell megadungeon follows a style pioneered for one-page dungeons.' Quote continues '...allowing the referee to customize the adventure to his own (and his players') tastes.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i589-603.json"
  },
  "601": {
   "index": 601,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Descriptions should focus on telling details and plot-critical information-details I can use in play",
   "note": "Raw page, 'The ideal dungeon description'; stated as Hartlage's own prescription for a dungeon key, followed by 'Don't bury the evocative bits in lavish descriptions of sleeping pallets and rubbish.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i589-603.json"
  },
  "602": {
   "index": 602,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "A good DM details more than simply what PCs see. Don't forget noises ... smells ... and tactile senses",
   "note": "Raw HTML fetched direct (HTTP 200); the /tag/ URL serves the full post 'From B1 to Pinebrook...', 30 January 2024, by David Hartlage. Heading 'B11 King's Festival (1989) by Carl Sargent', subhead 'Description and the senses', and the post says TSR started the new set with it - year, author and publisher limbs all on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i589-603.json"
  },
  "603": {
   "index": 603,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "giving them an accent or favorite saying, showing them with a favorite thing like a lucky coin or pet cat, or adding a personality-related adjective",
   "note": "Raw page, section 'H1 Keep on the Shadowfell (2008) by Bruce Cordell and Mike Mearls'. The quoted adjective run is exact and continues 'sly, nervous, dumb, zealous, and so on'; Hartlage paraphrases with the list quoted, exactly as the claim's source line states.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i589-603.json"
  },
  "604": {
   "index": 604,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "visualize him as an animal, and picture that animal as you describe",
   "note": "Raw page (dmdavid.com tag archive carries the full 30 Jan 2024 post). Under heading 'DDA2 Legions of Thyatis (1990) by John Nephew' / 'Characterizing NPCs': 'One quick way to characterize an NPC is to visualize him as an animal, and picture that animal as you describe and play him.' Quote exact; adventure, year and author all match.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i604-618.json"
  },
  "605": {
   "index": 605,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "When you decide things aren't important, give the players a short summary instead",
   "note": "Raw page, section 'The Hidden Halls of Hazacor (2018) by Scott Fitzgerald Gray' / 'Summarize the boring parts': 'When you decide things aren't important, you can give the players a short summary instead. Traveling is often best summarized.' Quote exact; the page also says the adventure 'gives first-time DMs an adventure stocked with guidance', supporting 'new DMs'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i604-618.json"
  },
  "606": {
   "index": 606,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "The adventures even include scripted text for the DM to read to new players",
   "note": "Raw page, section 'Eye of the Wyvern (1999) by Jeff Grubb': 'Eye of the Wyvern and its twin adventure Wrath of the Minotaur both teach the game... The adventures even include scripted text for the DM to read to new players who apparently have no idea what activity awaits them.' Quote exact; post dated January 30, 2024 by David Hartlage. Minor: the page dates only Eye of the Wyvern to 1999, calling Wrath of the Minotaur its 'twin adventure' without a year, so the plural '1999 modules' rests on the pairing.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the page dates only Eye of the Wyvern to 1999, calling Wrath of the Minotaur its 'twin adventure' without a year, so the plural '1999 modules' rests on the pairing",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "607": {
   "index": 607,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Casual DMs could simply buy an adventure, read the boxed text",
   "note": "Raw page, post of February 14, 2023 by David Hartlage: 'Skill challenges satisfied three design goals... To make the DM's role undemanding. Casual DMs could simply buy an adventure, read the boxed text, and then run a sequence of skill challenges and combat encounters.' Skill challenges are the fourth-edition innovation the post is about, so 'fourth-edition design goal' holds.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i604-618.json"
  },
  "608": {
   "index": 608,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the 1980 adventure module The Hidden Shrine of Tamoachan marks its first appearance",
   "note": "Raw page, byline 'December 18, 2023 Brian McKenzie': 'Called read-aloud or boxed text (because it is typically set outside from the other text in a box), the 1980 adventure module The Hidden Shrine of Tamoachan marks its first appearance.' Quote exact; McKenzie cites Merric Blackman at note 31 for this.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i604-618.json"
  },
  "609": {
   "index": 609,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Read-aloud text is analogous to 'at rise' descriptions",
   "note": "Raw page: 'D&D adventure writers employ descriptive text in the same way as playwrights use “at rise” descriptions.' and 'Read-aloud text is analogous to “at rise” descriptions.' The page defines 'at rise' as text describing the stage when the curtain rises and treats it as a genre convention, supporting 'genre analogue'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i604-618.json"
  },
  "610": {
   "index": 610,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Hints of what may be done are given in this text",
   "note": "Raw page quotes the module: 'Hints of what may be done are given in this text and the DM should only provide vague information if questioned.' Introduced as 'The authors of The Hidden Shrine of Tamoachan instruct DMs...They explain its purpose'; endnote 34 is Johnson and Leason, The Hidden Shrine of Tamoachan (TSR, 1980), 2 - matching the claim's note and attribution.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i604-618.json"
  },
  "611": {
   "index": 611,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Read-aloud text is, in short, an aid to players",
   "note": "Raw page: 'Read-aloud text is, in short, an aid to players. It offers clues and the frames narrative boundaries.' Quote exact, including the source's own typo 'the frames'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i604-618.json"
  },
  "612": {
   "index": 612,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Cover offers one of the few - perhaps the only - academic discussions",
   "note": "Raw page: 'Cover offers one of the few—perhaps the only—academic discussions of read-aloud text in TTRPG adventures.' Quote exact with em dashes; the endnote identifies Cover as Jennifer Grouling Cover, The Creation of Narrative in Tabletop Role-Playing Games (McFarland, 2014).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i604-618.json"
  },
  "613": {
   "index": 613,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "if our D&D game is a Broadway show, our read-aloud text is part of our script",
   "note": "Raw page: 'The prolific D&D author Michael Shea...makes an explicit connection between read-aloud text and drama: “if our D&D game is a Broadway show, our read-aloud text is part of our script.”' Endnote 35 is Shea, 'Writing Awesome Read-Aloud Text', Sly Flourish, 2010 - matching the claim's note.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i604-618.json"
  },
  "614": {
   "index": 614,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "saw 2,248 replies; 59.5% occasional, 34.4% lots, 6% none",
   "note": "Raw page: 'A Twitter poll by D&D's current lead rules designer Jeremy Crawford saw 2,248 replies to the question “How much boxed text (aka read-aloud text) do you like in an adventure you run?” The results were 59.5% desired “occasional boxed text,” 34.4% “lots of boxed text,” and only 6% of respondents did not want any boxed text.' Every figure and the role match; endnote 38 is the Crawford tweet (17 Nov 2016).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i604-618.json"
  },
  "615": {
   "index": 615,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "It's as much a part of D&D's history and brand as saving throws",
   "note": "Raw page (dndbeyond.com, 'by Shawn Merwin', Nov 6, 2019), opening paragraph: 'It's as much a part of D&D's history and brand as saving throws, hit points, and armor class.' Quote exact (curly apostrophe in D&D's); subject is boxed text, 'also sometimes referred to as read-aloud text'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i604-618.json"
  },
  "616": {
   "index": 616,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "the very act of having to write it, to think harder about their encounters",
   "note": "Raw page, third paragraph: 'It can also force adventure designers, by the very act of having to write it, to think harder about their encounters.' Quote exact. Merwin's wording is the modal 'can also force' inside a 'when it's good' clause, where the claim states it flatly; the proposition itself is his.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the flat, unconditional 'forces designers to think harder' — the verifier's note records that Merwin's wording is the modal 'can also force' inside a 'when it's good' clause",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r10.json"
  },
  "617": {
   "index": 617,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "relies on five elements to embed the critical ideas",
   "note": "Raw PDF (19pp, text extracted), p. 145: 'The design of the adventure relies on five elements to embed the critical ideas: plot (including encounters), read-aloud text, player handouts, instructions to the Dungeon Master (DM), and NPC dialogue.' Quote exact; authors McKenzie, Zawilski and Beirne, IJRP Issue 17, article titled a 'Case Study of a Commercial Dungeons & Dragons Adventure'. The source's word is 'elements', not 'textual elements', and the fifth is plot.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the source's word is 'elements', not 'textual elements', and the fifth element is plot, not a textual one",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "618": {
   "index": 618,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "many early examples lack prose and narrative in favor of rules",
   "note": "Raw page, byline 'December 18, 2022 Stefan Huddleston', section 'Sidebar: A Note on Primary Sources': 'Presumably, TTRPG rule books have been ignored for their primary source value because many early examples lack prose and narrative in favor of rules.' Quote exact; the subject is TTRPG rule books, matching 'early tabletop rulebooks'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i604-618.json"
  },
  "619": {
   "index": 619,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "gothic atmosphere is clearly set through the daunting presence of the statue",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'rather than by naming the mood' - Hewitt draws no such contrast",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (Meghan Hewitt, 15 Dec 2024 byline confirmed). Quote is verbatim: 'The gothic atmosphere is clearly set through the daunting presence of the statue, as well as the skeletal remains lurking in the corners of the room.' The room is the Strahd shrine in the Death House module of Curse of Strahd, and the module description she quotes is all physical objects (moldy skeletons, rusty shackles, painted wooden statue). But Hewitt never contrasts object-description with mood-naming; that contrastive limb is the claimant's inference, not her argument.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i619-633.json"
  },
  "620": {
   "index": 620,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Strahd's suggested actions and descriptions do not change from one text to the other",
   "note": "Raw page. Full sentence: 'Despite this, Strahd's suggested actions and descriptions do not change from one text to the other to reflect this change in alignment, directly contradicting the role of alignment in D&D.' The alignment change (Chaotic Evil in Ravenloft to Lawful Evil in Curse of Strahd) is stated two sentences earlier. Every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i619-633.json"
  },
  "621": {
   "index": 621,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "this style of adventure has gradually fallen out of style in favor of fifth edition's style of heroism and clarity in description",
   "note": "Raw page (Mark Hines, 19 Mar 2023 byline confirmed). Preceding sentence: 'Many of the tricks and traps in Gygax's original adventure are indiscernible, devious, fatal, and intentionally counterintuitive.' The contrast the claim describes is explicit and adjacent. Quote verbatim within 'fifth edition's style of heroism and clarity in description and execution of narrative.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i619-633.json"
  },
  "622": {
   "index": 622,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Economics play a large role in the events described",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'dominate' - Peterson says only that economics 'play a large role'",
   "note": "Raw page. Quote verbatim, and the 1971 dating is on the page ('it seems safe to accept 1071 as 1971 ... late in the fall of that year'). But Peterson does not claim economics dominate: the same post gives comparable weight to the wargame component ('several thousand enemy troops') and to fantastic elements (dragons, 'evil creatures overrunning the region'). 'Dominate' overstates 'play a large role'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i619-633.json"
  },
  "623": {
   "index": 623,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The Wizard pays restitution for damages caused by his dragon's mischievous offspring",
   "note": "Raw page. All three listed items are on the page in one paragraph: 'Inspector General John of Snyder comes to collect taxes.' / 'The Wizard pays restitution for damages caused by his dragon's mischievous offspring.' / 'Baron Jenkins has \"all but ruined the tavern,\" and that too sounds like an expensive error on his part.' Quote is an exact substring. Peterson lists a fourth money item too (gypsy fortune telling), which does not contradict the claim's civic-and-financial characterisation.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i619-633.json"
  },
  "624": {
   "index": 624,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "does not tell us a great deal about the system of Blackmoor, but it does give significant insight into the setting and the state of the campaign",
   "note": "Raw page. Both limbs are in one sentence about the Blackmoor Gazette and Rumormonger. 'Earliest' is supported: the post is on issue #1 and refers forward to 'the next issue of the BMG&R (which seems to date from the following year).'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i619-633.json"
  },
  "625": {
   "index": 625,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The earliest explicit use I've found was that of Pieter Roos ... The Wild Hunt #15 (1977)",
   "note": "Raw page. Full sentence: 'The earliest explicit use I've found was that of Pieter Roos, as shown in this excerpt above from The Wild Hunt #15 (1977), where he identifies it as the overall goal of playing RPGs.' Subject of the paragraph is the term 'immersion'. Every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i619-633.json"
  },
  "626": {
   "index": 626,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Carr clearly connects 'playing your character's role' with his call to 'immerse yourself in the game setting'",
   "note": "Raw page. The exhibit immediately preceding that sentence is 'The tips for players in his module In Search of the Unknown (B1)' (the excerpt image filename is 1979-b1-carr-tips.jpg), with Boot Hill named as 'similar language.' Quote verbatim. Minor ambiguity only: Peterson attributes the connection to Carr generally rather than naming B1 in that sentence, but B1's player tips are the exhibit he has just presented.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i619-633.json"
  },
  "627": {
   "index": 627,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the location keys in published adventures are almost universally terrible",
   "note": "Raw page (Justin Alexander, The Alexandrian, May 19th, 2014, confirmed on page). Third sentence of the article: 'Which is why I find it fairly surprising that the location keys in published adventures are almost universally terrible.' Every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i619-633.json"
  },
  "628": {
   "index": 628,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The description of the key, however, was instructional rather than practical",
   "note": "Raw page, 'A Brief History of the Key' section: 'In 1974, the original edition of D&D included a \"Sample Map\" keyed with both numbers and letters. The description of the key, however, was instructional rather than practical.' Quote verbatim; the 1974 sample-map limb is the immediately preceding sentence.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i619-633.json"
  },
  "629": {
   "index": 629,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the Gygax 'wall of text' key which would become a staple at TSR for the better part of a decade",
   "note": "Raw page, same section, on The Lost Caverns of Tsojconth. Quote verbatim. 'Undivided' is a fair gloss: Alexander's own contrast is with Blake's 'DM Only' split and with B1, which 'effectively created the idea of separating the key into distinct sections.' The claim's definite article ('the TSR staple' for 'a staple') is a wording shift, not an added limb.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i619-633.json"
  },
  "630": {
   "index": 630,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "he introduced that organization by including a 'DM Only' section in each key entry ... effectively accomplishing the same thing on a structural level",
   "note": "Raw page. All limbs present: Bob Blake, Judges Guild, Gen Con IX Dungeons in the 1976 paragraph; the split entry; and 'Although not technically boxed text, this format was effectively accomplishing the same thing on a structural level.' Reinforced later by 'Like Blake's \"DM Only\" section from earlier in the decade, the boxed text clearly delineated...'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i619-633.json"
  },
  "631": {
   "index": 631,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the text was not yet boxed, instead appearing between quotation marks ... But in 1980, TSR reissued the module",
   "note": "Raw page, 'The Era of Boxed Text'. Both limbs: original Lost Tamoachan text 'not yet boxed, instead appearing between quotation marks', then 'But in 1980, TSR reissued the module as C1 The Hidden Shrine of Tamoachan, placing the prewritten descriptions in a box for the first time.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i619-633.json"
  },
  "632": {
   "index": 632,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the boxed text clearly delineated the elements ... that should be immediately shared with any PC entering the area",
   "note": "Raw page, same section. Quote verbatim. Alexander does add that boxed text's role 'would be expanded' later to any read-aloud text, but the definitional limb the claim states is exactly his.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i619-633.json"
  },
  "633": {
   "index": 633,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "if they did appear then they appeared in that order and no other",
   "note": "Raw page, 'Adventure Formatting Guides': 3rd Edition's new 'standard format', 'First appearing in The Sunless Citadel', with 'a specific set of bold-faced sections' (Traps, Creatures, Tactics, Development, Treasure). Note the page's qualifier, which the claim does not contradict: 'Not all of these appeared in every location, but if they did appear then they appeared in that order and no other.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i619-633.json"
  },
  "634": {
   "index": 634,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the format says you should have a “Tactics” section, so you might as well write two or three paragraphs about it",
   "note": "Raw page fetched by curl (browser UA, HTTP 200). Quote verbatim in the 'Adventure Formatting Guides' section: the format 'tends to encourage writers to “fill the format”' and the orcs/swords example supplies the 'nothing needed saying' limb.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i634-648.json"
  },
  "635": {
   "index": 635,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "A room with an ogre standing in the middle of it should talk about the ogre first",
   "note": "Verbatim on the raw page; the very next clause supplies the hidden-goblin limb: 'a room with a goblin hiding behind a tapestry, on the other hand, shouldn’t lead off with the goblin.' Alexander frames it as 'the problem… of sequencing information'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i634-648.json"
  },
  "636": {
   "index": 636,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "the culmination of this approach is the infamous “delve” format … the rigid adventure format forces information to be presented out-of-sequence",
   "note": "Quote verbatim and the 'culmination of rigid formatting' limb is supported ('This sort of one-size-fits-all formatting becomes both a straitjacket and an excess. And the culmination of this approach is the infamous “delve” format'). But the page never ties the delve format to 4th Edition: its only edition markers are '2nd Edition' (Apocalypse Stone) and 'When 3rd Edition arrived'; grep for '4th'/'fourth' on the page returns nothing, and the delve discussion is deferred to another article ('which I’ve talked about extensively elsewhere').",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the page never identifies the delve format as fourth edition's",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i634-648.json"
  },
  "637": {
   "index": 637,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Boxed text is usually not a good thing.",
   "note": "Verbatim bullet inside the list under the page's own heading 'Review Standards / Bryce Lynch / What I’m looking for in an adventure module may not be what you are looking for.' Attribution limb ('his own list') confirmed by that framing.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i634-648.json"
  },
  "638": {
   "index": 638,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Terse writing style",
   "note": "Verbatim bullet in the same Bryce Lynch 'what I’m looking for' list, two bullets after 'Evocative atmosphere.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i634-648.json"
  },
  "639": {
   "index": 639,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Evocative atmosphere.",
   "note": "Verbatim bullet (with the period) in Bryce Lynch's own list on the raw page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i634-648.json"
  },
  "640": {
   "index": 640,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "I don’t care about your epic backstory. Give me a little to work with to get things going.",
   "note": "Quote verbatim (curly apostrophe matches). The second sentence on the page supplies the 'only enough to start play' limb.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i634-648.json"
  },
  "641": {
   "index": 641,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Language / 4 – Succinct and evocative",
   "note": "Verbatim. It sits in the scale under the separate 'The Pretty Girl' section, whose author writes 'not the gourmand that Bryce is' and 'When I built my rating scale'; 4 is the top of that Language scale (4/2/1/0). The phrase is absent from Bryce's own bullet list, so the negative limb holds. Attribution is by section heading rather than a literal sign-off.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the attribution is by section heading, not the sign-off the claim's 'signed \"The Pretty Girl\"' asserts",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "642": {
   "index": 642,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "0 – Very wordy/ incomprehensible",
   "note": "Verbatim, including the space after the slash; it is the 0 rung, the lowest of the same 'The Pretty Girl' Language scale (4/2/1/0).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i634-648.json"
  },
  "643": {
   "index": 643,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "I LOATHE the overly long backstory and fiction intro’s, but they are not on this list.",
   "note": "Verbatim in the comments, attributed 'Bryce Lynch says: April 19, 2016 at 8:36 am' — date and 'fiction introductions' limb both confirmed.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i634-648.json"
  },
  "644": {
   "index": 644,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the location keys in published adventures are almost universally terrible",
   "note": "Verbatim in the article's third sentence. The cited /36903/ URL 301-redirects to /35180/roleplaying-games/the-art-of-the-key (verified with curl url_effective); both fetches returned the identical page, md5 191eaa61f9514f5b28703a6167ef75ee.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i634-648.json"
  },
  "645": {
   "index": 645,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "boxed text — prewritten text designed to be read aloud by the GM",
   "note": "Verbatim appositive in 'The Era of Boxed Text' section; it is Alexander's definitional gloss, exactly as claimed.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i634-648.json"
  },
  "646": {
   "index": 646,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "In either 1979 or 1980, depending on how persnickety you want to get with definitions",
   "note": "Verbatim; the same sentence names 'Lost Tamoachan: The Hidden Shrine of Lubaatum' as where boxed text 'arrived for the first time'. Note the page later hedges further, giving Quest of the Fazzlewood (1978) an 'honorable mention' that 'one could argue… is, in fact, the first example' — an extra caveat, not a contradiction of the claim's two limbs.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i634-648.json"
  },
  "647": {
   "index": 647,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "A room with an ogre standing in the middle of it should talk about the ogre first",
   "note": "Verbatim; Alexander introduces it as 'that of sequencing information', and the hidden-goblin counter-example confirms the sequencing-by-what-is-met-first limb. Same sentence as index 635.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i634-648.json"
  },
  "648": {
   "index": 648,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Instead of making the information easy to parse and reference, a rigid format ends up having the opposite effect.",
   "note": "Verbatim, and it is the closing sentence of Part 1 immediately before 'Go to Part 2'. The stated aim ('easy to parse and reference') is in the same sentence, so both limbs hold.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i634-648.json"
  },
  "649": {
   "index": 649,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "it tends to encourage writers to “fill the format”",
   "note": "Live page (HTTP 200, raw HTML fetched with a browser UA). Section 'ADVENTURE FORMATTING GUIDES' introduces the 3rd Edition 'standard format' (Traps/Creatures/Tactics/Development/Treasure, first in The Sunless Citadel), then: 'the problem with this specific approach to adventure writing is that it tends to encourage writers to “fill the format”. You probably don’t really need to tell a DM that the orcs attack people using their swords... but the format says you should have a “Tactics” section, so you might as well write two or three paragraphs about it.' Quote verbatim; the not-needed-text limb is the orcs/swords sentence.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i649-663.json"
  },
  "650": {
   "index": 650,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "featured the Gygax “wall of text” key which would become a staple at TSR for the better part of a decade",
   "note": "Section 'A BRIEF HISTORY OF THE KEY': 'The Lost Caverns of Tsojconth, on the other hand, featured the Gygax “wall of text” key which would become a staple at TSR for the better part of a decade:' Quote verbatim; 'most of a decade' = 'the better part of a decade'. The 'dense undifferentiated paragraph' limb is shown rather than asserted — the quoted Tsojconth exemplar is one unbroken paragraph, and the article's stated purpose of the later formats is 'To break up the wall of text'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i649-663.json"
  },
  "651": {
   "index": 651,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "We start with boxed text... I also think of this section as seen in a glance",
   "note": "Section 'BOXED TEXT': 'We start with boxed text which conveys the common information that anyone walking into the room would immediately perceive.' and 'it should be clearly delineated from the rest of the key and contain all of the information that should be immediately conveyed when the PCs first enter the room. I also think of this section as seen in a glance.' Quote verbatim. Minor: an optional room title ('Technically, this is optional') precedes the boxed text in his layout.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "an optional room title ('Technically, this is optional') precedes the boxed text in his layout, so the key does not literally open with the glance block",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "652": {
   "index": 652,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "focus on the sequencing of information: How the information will flow... at the actual gaming table",
   "note": "Opening of Part 2: 'We’ve already rejected the idea of a rigid or dogmatized format, but there has to be something better... The ultimate solution, in my experience, is to focus on the sequencing of information: How the information will flow (or is likely to flow) at the actual gaming table.' Quote verbatim; both limbs (alternative to a fixed format; ordering by table-use sequence) supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i649-663.json"
  },
  "653": {
   "index": 653,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "move from the general to the specific while paying particular attention to how the players are gaining that information",
   "note": "Section 'DEVELOPING SIGNIFICANT ELEMENTS': 'What you want to do is to move from the general to the specific while paying particular attention to how the players are gaining that information.' The investigation limb is explicit two paragraphs later: 'They’ll start with general questions and then proceed to detailed investigation (so put the information in that order).' Quote verbatim.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i649-663.json"
  },
  "654": {
   "index": 654,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Notice that the bold title makes it easy to find the information you need",
   "note": "Section 'SIGNIFICANT ELEMENTS' shows each element with a bold lead-in (Old Jars:, Clay Pots/Urns:, Vats:) then: 'Notice that the bold title makes it easy to find the information you need. It also makes it easy for the GM to quickly process what the room contains and how it “works” in play.' Quote verbatim; every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i649-663.json"
  },
  "655": {
   "index": 655,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "It should be brief, to the point, and infrequently needed",
   "note": "Section 'GM BACKGROUND TAG': 'The tag is particularly useful for information of the “this is what this ruined room used to be” and “this is what the NPCs use this room for” variety' and the caution 'It should be brief, to the point, and infrequently needed.' Quote verbatim. Nuance: he says the tag is 'particularly useful for' such history rather than issuing an absolute confinement rule, but his worked Ptolus example does exactly the confining the claim describes.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i649-663.json"
  },
  "656": {
   "index": 656,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the delve format does have one really good idea: Everything you need is on the page",
   "note": "Opening of Part 3: 'I’ve previously dismissed the dogmatization of the old delve adventure format... But the delve format does have one really good idea: Everything you need is on the page.' The GM-facing limb follows: 'you don’t have to open your Monster Manual... the GM can focus on running the encounter instead of flipping pages.' Quote verbatim, sentence-final period included.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i649-663.json"
  },
  "657": {
   "index": 657,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "putting it in boxed text will virtually guarantee that it’s not accidentally omitted in actual play",
   "note": "Advantages paragraph: 'If there’s essential information that needs to be conveyed to the players, putting it in boxed text will virtually guarantee that it’s not accidentally omitted in actual play.' Quote verbatim; 'essential information' and 'in play' both on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i649-663.json"
  },
  "658": {
   "index": 658,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The result is inherently less flexible... the boxed text needs to be generic enough to work for any potential entrance",
   "note": "Under 'So why wouldn’t you use boxed text?': 'The result is inherently less flexible. (For example, if a room has multiple entries the boxed text needs to be generic enough to work for any potential entrance. Add to this NPCs, lighting conditions, etc.)' 'Prewritten' is the article's own opening definition ('Boxed text in an RPG scenario is a prewritten narration'). Quote verbatim.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i649-663.json"
  },
  "659": {
   "index": 659,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Freeze-frame boxed text is when the GM starts reading and then the PCs are frozen in place while a bunch of stuff happens",
   "note": "Section 'SINS OF THE BOX', first paragraph of the freeze-frame discussion; the Radiant Citadel example follows, with 'everyone else in the scene – including the ox! – gets to react before they do.' Quote appears verbatim as a substring of that sentence.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i649-663.json"
  },
  "660": {
   "index": 660,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "What we’ve identified here is the reaction point",
   "note": "Section 'SINS OF THE BOX': 'What we’ve identified here is the reaction point. ... what you should avoid at all costs is having the game world continue to move forward past the reaction point without letting the players react; without letting the players play the game.' Quote verbatim; both limbs (naming, and the world-must-not-move-past rule) supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i649-663.json"
  },
  "661": {
   "index": 661,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "insidiously goes one step further by declaring the thoughts or feelings or (worse yet!) actions of the PCs",
   "note": "Section 'REMOTE-CONTROL BOXED TEXT': 'Remote-control boxed text suffers from similar problems (preventing the players from participating), but insidiously goes one step further by declaring the thoughts or feelings or (worse yet!) actions of the PCs.' The condemnation limb: 'So… don’t do this.' Quote verbatim.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i649-663.json"
  },
  "662": {
   "index": 662,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Focus on showing the players the scene and letting them react to it. Don’t tell them how they’re reacting to it.",
   "note": "Section 'REMOTE-CONTROL BOXED TEXT', closing rule. Quote verbatim including the final period; the show-the-scene limb is the immediately preceding sentence. The article's subject throughout is boxed text, i.e. prewritten read-aloud narration ('designed to be read to the players by the GM').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i649-663.json"
  },
  "663": {
   "index": 663,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "should never be longer than three sentences, because after that point the players stop paying attention",
   "note": "Comment #1976289, cited author 'mellonbread', timestamped 'August 2nd, 2022 - 11:58 am': 'There was a lovely article in the old Wizards devblog, from around the 3.5 or 4e days, which was lost when they nuked their archives. The basic thesis was that boxed text (or any descriptive text delivered by the GM) should never be longer than three sentences...' Every limb (reader comment, lost Wizards devblog, thesis) verbatim on the page; author and date confirmed in the raw comment markup.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i649-663.json"
  },
  "664": {
   "index": 664,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Raw dmdavid.com tag page (curl, browser UA, HTTP 200) carries the whole 15 Nov 2012 post by David Hartlage. Sentence appears in full: 'In 1979, Hidden Shrine of Tamoachan introduced boxed text descriptions for the DM to read when the players entered a location.' Every limb (1979, that module, DM reads on entry) supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i664-678.json"
  },
  "665": {
   "index": 665,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim: 'The boxed text provided a way to offer a consistent play experience in a competitive environment.' Tournament limb supported by the preceding sentence (Origins '79 Official AD&D tournament) and by 'its original purpose of leveling a competition'; 'every table the same' supported by 'every team heard the same boxed text'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i664-678.json"
  },
  "666": {
   "index": 666,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim: 'Like any good boxed text description, the text mentioned the runes alongside other, less important details.' Tournament limb = 2012 GEN CON D&D Championship final; missed limb = 'No one on my team appreciated the runes significance, so we failed to discover...'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i664-678.json"
  },
  "667": {
   "index": 667,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim in the full sentence: 'When most speakers read something aloud, their voice takes a different tone and cadence than when they speak extemporaneously, and that monotony can cause players' attention to wander.' Both limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i664-678.json"
  },
  "668": {
   "index": 668,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim: 'No one has shown a better way to summarize what the players must learn when they enter a room.' Preceded by 'Still, even for DMs who never read boxed text, it helps', which supports the 'defends ... still the best available' framing.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i664-678.json"
  },
  "669": {
   "index": 669,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim inside 'For this reason, when I know the content well enough, I paraphrase boxed text.' Knowledge-conditional limb supported by 'when I know the content well enough'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i664-678.json"
  },
  "670": {
   "index": 670,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Wayback capture 20161031220137 of archive.wizards.com fetched raw (HTTP 200). Verbatim: 'If you're the DM, you get two sentences. Period. Beyond that, your players are stacking dice, talking to each other, or staring off into space.' Gen Con 2005 limb supported ('went to Indianapolis for Gen Con 2005'); the first-person observer is Dave/David Noonan ('the \"Dave wanders around\" technique'; About the Authors: Design: David Noonan).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i664-678.json"
  },
  "671": {
   "index": 671,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim: 'this kind of \"research\" is pretty qualitative, which is science-speak for \"quite possibly bogus.\"' The 'not a controlled measurement' limb is supported by the next sentence: 'The \"Dave wanders around\" technique is no way to get a representative sample'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i664-678.json"
  },
  "672": {
   "index": 672,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim: 'Boxed text replaces that conversation with oration. Now the DM is doing the talking, and the players are doing the listening.' Framed by 'I started to develop a working theory for why listening comprehension takes a dive', so 'his explanation for the attention loss' is supported; the page itself ranks it above the two other factors ('the conversation vs. oration factor').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i664-678.json"
  },
  "673": {
   "index": 673,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim: 'At its heart, a D&D game is a conversation.' The DM/players limb is supported by the following example: 'You tell the DM what you want to do, and the DM replies with the consequences of the action.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i664-678.json"
  },
  "674": {
   "index": 674,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim: 'My hypothesis was that boxed text longer than a paragraph probably isn't worth reading, because players tend to have pretty bad listening comprehension.' The 'which the watching exceeded' limb is supported by the next paragraph's opener: 'What I actually saw was much more dramatic than my hypothesis.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i664-678.json"
  },
  "675": {
   "index": 675,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim: 'Or better yet, ditch the boxed text and use your own words for the initial description of the room.' Advice-to-DMs limb explicit ('if you're the DM'); dated 09/16/2005 in the Wizards Design & Development header.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i664-678.json"
  },
  "676": {
   "index": 676,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Verbatim in the Tangent Alert: 'Boxed text in adventures used to be a lot longer than it is today.' The 'Wizards adventures' limb is carried by the two examples contrasted - the short passage from the recent WotC Forgotten Realms adventure Sons of Gruumsh against the long one from 1999's Return to the Keep on the Borderlands; 'by 2005' = the article's 'today'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i664-678.json"
  },
  "677": {
   "index": 677,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "Raw grognardia.blogspot.com post (curl, HTTP 200), 19 Feb 2009. Verbatim quoted phrase inside: 'saying that it was mostly the result of his having read a lot of rather \"old fashioned\" books when he was a kid'. The 'rather than to Jack Vance' limb is supported by the two preceding sentences: 'I once told Gary my opinion on this and he demurred. He didn't think his own peculiar voice was anywhere as erudite and witty as that of Jack Vance.' Private-conversation framing supported by 'I once told Gary'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i664-678.json"
  },
  "678": {
   "index": 678,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "credited for his peculiar voice, not 'vocabulary' as such",
   "note": "Same sentence, same account: '...coupled with his lifelong love of dictionaries and thesauruses' - quoted phrase is word for word. Graded SUBSTANCE rather than VERBATIM because what Gygax is reported to have credited is 'his own peculiar voice' (his style/diction), not 'his vocabulary' - the page's only 'vocabulary' sentences are Maliszewski's own ('my vocabulary grew considerably') and commenters'. The reported cause is also joint (old-fashioned books plus the dictionary/thesaurus love), where the claim names only the second.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "what Gygax is reported to have credited is 'his own peculiar voice', not 'his vocabulary', and the reported cause is joint (old-fashioned books plus the dictionary/thesaurus love) where the claim names only the second",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "679": {
   "index": 679,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "I once told Gary my opinion on this and he demurred",
   "note": "Body para 4: 'High Gygaxian speech sounds to me like a local dialect of High Vancian. I once told Gary my opinion on this and he demurred. He didn't think his own peculiar voice was anywhere as erudite and witty as that of Jack Vance...' Byline 'Posted by James Maliszewski' present. Fetched raw HTML via curl (HTTP 200).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i679-693.json"
  },
  "680": {
   "index": 680,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Modern game books read like what they are: technical manuals.",
   "note": "Verbatim, para 3, immediately after the florid-Gygax contrast ('Reading Gygax at his florid best... In what game book can you find such prose nowadays?'). Both limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i679-693.json"
  },
  "681": {
   "index": 681,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Again, I say, you'll find few passages in contemporary adventures that are as evocative.",
   "note": "Verbatim, and it closes the block-quoted Vault of the Drow description he introduces as 'this bit from Vault of the Drow'. Source says 'passages' where the claim says 'place descriptions' — the quoted passage is itself a place description, so the claim is narrower than, and contained by, the source.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i679-693.json"
  },
  "682": {
   "index": 682,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "So, OSR adventure writers, get rid of the boxed text.",
   "note": "Verbatim in the concluding paragraph; post bylined 'Posted by Dyson Logos'. Raw page fetched (HTTP 200).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i679-693.json"
  },
  "683": {
   "index": 683,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "replace it with a point-form list of things seen in the room",
   "note": "Verbatim; the next sentence supplies the conversational limb: 'That makes it easy for the DM to present the salient information in a conversational manner without having to dig through all your \"beautiful\" prose.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i679-693.json"
  },
  "684": {
   "index": 684,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "I tend to blame this on the tournament usage",
   "note": "Verbatim, in full: 'During the 1e days, we saw the advent of \"boxed\" or \"read-aloud\" text. I tend to blame this on the tournament usage of the original published adventures.' Claim's 'early published adventures' = source's 'original published adventures'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i679-693.json"
  },
  "685": {
   "index": 685,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "The number one complaint about published adventures is that they are hard to run.",
   "note": "Verbatim; it is the opening sentence of the page. Attribution note: the page itself carries only 'Bryce' (About Bryce…, Bryce's ToDo List) — the surname Lynch appears nowhere in the HTML, so authorship rests on the site's identity, not this page's text.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the surname Lynch appears nowhere in the page HTML (it carries only 'Bryce'), so authorship rests on the site's identity rather than this page's text",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "686": {
   "index": 686,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The words on the page are only a memory cue",
   "note": "Verbatim (full sentence adds 'to recall what you initially imagined'), third body paragraph, opened by 'There's a key difference between personal use and having someone else use it.' Claim's 'stranger' = source's 'someone else'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i679-693.json"
  },
  "687": {
   "index": 687,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "leading to dry and tedious encounters",
   "note": "Verbatim: 'You have to transfer that vision from your head down to paper and in such a way that it can then get into someone else's head... Those translations seldom happen successfully, leading to dry and tedious encounters.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i679-693.json"
  },
  "688": {
   "index": 688,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "This book is advice on how to write an adventure for publication.",
   "note": "Verbatim, second body paragraph, preceded by 'This is not a book about how to design an adventure for your home game.' Both limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i679-693.json"
  },
  "689": {
   "index": 689,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Any word that is not important, is not used.",
   "note": "Verbatim in the 'Let's break down how this works' list. Attribution note: page byline is '-C'; the site's Biography page names the author 'Courtney' — the surname Campbell is not on either page.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the page byline is '-C' and the site's Biography page names only 'Courtney'; the surname Campbell is on neither page",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "690": {
   "index": 690,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "When describing things, I only use the bolded words!",
   "note": "Verbatim. The bolded-nouns limb checked against the raw HTML <b> spans: Tables, Wooden Cask, Refuse, Furniture, Bones, Ornate Iron Armchair, Blanket, Gem, Iron mail, Pouch, Scrolls, Sack, Barrels, Buckets, Stream, Pool of Water, Cylinder — all nouns, in a keyed entry rather than prose.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i679-693.json"
  },
  "691": {
   "index": 691,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "everything I need to run the encounter successfully in seven words",
   "note": "Verbatim: 'I look down at the tick room, and I see enough information to tell me everything I need to run the encounter successfully in seven words.' The tick-room limb is explicit.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i679-693.json"
  },
  "692": {
   "index": 692,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "After the bar are the immediately visible items!",
   "note": "Verbatim. The page states 'immediately visible' without the words 'on entering'; the worked example (the party enters the alcove and is told refuse, furniture, bones) supports that reading, so the qualifier is illustrated rather than merely inferred.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i679-693.json"
  },
  "693": {
   "index": 693,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "terse simplicity, expressive but functional language, play-oriented presentation",
   "note": "Verbatim under the 'Know your enemy' heading: 'the classics showed another way: of terse simplicity, expressive but functional language, play-oriented presentation', followed by classic-module examples (Glacial Rift, Tegel Manor, Keep on the Borderlands). Byline 'Melan' = Gabor Lux.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i679-693.json"
  },
  "694": {
   "index": 694,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "It doesn't just matter what gets told, it also matters how it gets told.",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200, browser UA). Quote exact in section 'The Keep on the Borderlands without The Keep on the Borderlands'. Byline 'Melan'; sidebar links a 'melan-gabor-lux-bibliography' page, confirming Melan = Gabor Lux. Context is adventures/game texts, so 'how an adventure is told' is supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i694-708.json"
  },
  "695": {
   "index": 695,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "In old-school gaming, Ultra-Minimalism is stripping games of their flavour, depth and inner complexity.",
   "note": "Quote exact. Page says 'stripping games of' rather than 'out of game texts'; the essay's subject throughout is modules and rulesets, so the limb holds. Locator drift only: the sentence sits in the 'Know your enemy' section, not the opening.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i694-708.json"
  },
  "696": {
   "index": 696,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "These things, even if technically 'superfluous' for specific, individual encounters, give game materials a voice.",
   "note": "Quote exact. 'These things' resolves in the preceding sentence to Gygaxian prose, Bob Bledsaw's quirky humour and M.A.R. Barker's dry wit, i.e. stylistic elements; 'technically superfluous for specific, individual encounters' and 'sticks in our imagination' both on the page. Locator section correct.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i694-708.json"
  },
  "697": {
   "index": 697,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Hommlet draws the portrait of an idyllic rural community by spending much of its time talking about random details.",
   "note": "Quote exact and every limb present verbatim: 'The Village of Hommlet draws the portrait of an idyllic rural community by spending much of its time talking about random details that don't really matter in an adventure.' Locator 'Know your enemy' is correct.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i694-708.json"
  },
  "698": {
   "index": 698,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Layout wizardry and graphical design ... vastly improve accessibility, but it doesn't replace style.",
   "note": "Quote exact (curly apostrophe matches). Full sentence: 'Layout wizardry and graphical design can make GMing flow better, and vastly improve accessibility, but it doesn't replace style.' Both limbs supported; locator section correct.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i694-708.json"
  },
  "699": {
   "index": 699,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the larger PF and 5e publishers ... a bloated, lumbering mess full of encounters which read well but play terribly",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Quote exact. Page writes 'larger PF and 5e publishers'; PF is Pathfinder. The read/play contrast is explicit ('may pass as interesting bog reading, but put them on the table').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i694-708.json"
  },
  "700": {
   "index": 700,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "a slogan pioneered by T. Foster - 'Creativity aid, not creativity replacement'",
   "note": "Quote exact. Page: 'I want to return to a slogan pioneered by T. Foster', which supports 'revives'. Applied to RPG books/supplements ('These mottoes articulate something about the flexibility of good game materials'), supporting the 'published game text' limb. Page also credits Bob Bledsaw with the original point.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i694-708.json"
  },
  "701": {
   "index": 701,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "What such a synthesis requires is a skillful application of the principle of economy.",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Quote exact, in the section headed 'A Via Media' (= middle way), which explicitly synthesises the maximalist and minimalist schools set out earlier. Byline on page: Tad Davis, December 5, 2013, ars phantasia.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i694-708.json"
  },
  "702": {
   "index": 702,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "these descriptions tend to take up too much time and bore players to tears",
   "note": "Quote exact. Reported, not asserted: 'Others have observed that the minimalist approach also has three advantages over its more ponderous rival ... Third, in particular the absence of boxed descriptions meant to be read aloud to players...'. Locator drift only: the sentence closes the section just before 'Assessing the Two Approaches'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i694-708.json"
  },
  "703": {
   "index": 703,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "I am also not convinced that we should completely eliminate the boxed text ... format.",
   "note": "Quote exact in 'Assessing the Two Approaches'. The valuing limb is explicit in the next sentence: 'they still strike me as a useful way of partitioning off information specifically to be given to the players.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i694-708.json"
  },
  "704": {
   "index": 704,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "As visually well laid out as exhibit A is, navigating the walls of text takes time.",
   "note": "Quote exact. The 'even when well laid out' limb is the same sentence's concessive clause. Same section as the locator states.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i694-708.json"
  },
  "705": {
   "index": 705,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Short description (one to two sentences), highlighting the 5 senses.",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Quote exact, and it is the first line of the key template under the heading 'The Key'. Byline: 'Published by cygnus on November 30, 2023', The Lady and Tiger. Note the running prose hedges slightly - 'tries to touch on a few of the senses' - while the template says 5.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i694-708.json"
  },
  "706": {
   "index": 706,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "focusing on what the characters can immediately perceive helps to ... avoid rambling boxed text",
   "note": "Quote exact. The page hedges where the claim does not: 'helps to both A) avoid rambling boxed text and, B) quickly identify key room elements', versus the claim's 'is what keeps'. Substance of the single asserted limb is supported.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the exclusive-causal 'is what keeps the description from rambling' — the verifier's note records that 'the page hedges where the claim does not' ('helps to both A) avoid rambling boxed text and, B) quickly identify key room elements')",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r10.json"
  },
  "707": {
   "index": 707,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "a mish-mash of Courtney's keying style in Hack & Slash: On Set Design and the Old-School Essentials house style for adventure writing",
   "note": "Quote exact, and the 'one of two models' limb is supported by the sentence naming exactly two sources for the rewritten key format.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i694-708.json"
  },
  "708": {
   "index": 708,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "there's one thing I really love about the delve format: Putting everything you need to run an encounter area in the description of the encounter area",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Quote exact, in the section headed 'ISOLATED ENCOUNTERS'. Page byline The Alexandrian, January 10th 2011, Justin Alexander. Page hedges with 'Conceptually' and goes on to name two problems with the format in practice; the claim's limb is only about what he loves, so it holds.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i694-708.json"
  },
  "709": {
   "index": 709,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "First, it artificially isolates the “encounter”.",
   "note": "Raw HTML fetched live (HTTP 200, browser UA), stripped to text. Section 'ISOLATED ENCOUNTERS': quote word for word; the 'defeats its own purpose' limb is carried by the next sentence, 'This tends to fatally sabotage the entire point of the delve format to begin with', and the context limb by 'Attempting to isolate a tactical encounter from the context in which that tactical encounter occurs'. Post dated January 10th, 2011.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i709-723.json"
  },
  "710": {
   "index": 710,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "I think publishers can put a little more trust in DMs.",
   "note": "Same raw page, section 'ENCOUNTERS ON THE FLY'. Quote verbatim inside 'I think publishers can put a little more trust in DMs (and, as DMs, we can put a little more trust in ourselves).' The pre-programming limb is supported throughout: the boardgame is 'forced to provide a “program” for each monster', the delve encounters have monsters 'pre-selected, pre-positioned, and pre-programmed', and the Helga example is offered against 'a pregenerated tactical map ... with accompanying text telling the DM' where to place minis.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i709-723.json"
  },
  "711": {
   "index": 711,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Areas are described in lengthy, proper english prose.",
   "note": "Raw page fetched live (HTTP 200). Quote verbatim. 'Dominant published format' limb supported by the same paragraph: 'The most common style in published modules (and also probably the oldest ...) is the dreaded wall of text' and 'Unfortunately, this has become the accepted format for modules.' Post is by Necropraxis/Brendan (he signs comments 'Brendan Post author').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i709-723.json"
  },
  "712": {
   "index": 712,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Modules in this format can be pleasurable to read (if well-written).",
   "note": "Quote verbatim as a substring of that sentence on the raw page; the concessive framing ('can be ... but are quite cumbersome') matches the claim's 'grants'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i709-723.json"
  },
  "713": {
   "index": 713,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "but are quite cumbersome to use in play",
   "note": "Quote verbatim on the raw page, in the same sentence as the concession verified at 712, and about the same wall-of-text prose modules ('They just don’t work very well in play').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i709-723.json"
  },
  "714": {
   "index": 714,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Two, it lacks poetry.",
   "note": "Quote verbatim. Usability limb: 'This method works admirably in terms of direct, in-game usability. However ... it has two flaws.' The page names the format only as 'Courtney’s “set design” hierarchical outline format', hyperlinked to hackslashmaster.blogspot.com/2012/06/on-set-design.html (Courtney Campbell's Hack & Slash); the surname itself is not printed on the page, so the expansion rests on that link, not on page text.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the page names the format only as 'Courtney's \"set design\" hierarchical outline format'; the surname Campbell is not printed and rests on the outgoing link",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "715": {
   "index": 715,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "When I am writing new area keys, I bold the immediately relevant features.",
   "note": "Quote verbatim. 'Keeps running prose' limb supported: the approach 'can be applied to existing modules without requiring rewriting', the worked Tower of Mouths example is bolded prose, and the saving is claimed 'without degrading the quality of the prose' — explicitly set against outline formats, which he says are unpleasant to read.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i709-723.json"
  },
  "716": {
   "index": 716,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Boxed text is also flawed because it separated the initial impression ... from elaboration",
   "note": "Quote verbatim including the curly quotes around “treasure chest”. 'Further down the page' limb supported by the parenthesis that follows: 'the contents are usually buried somewhere three paragraphs down the page, with no obvious connective element'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i709-723.json"
  },
  "717": {
   "index": 717,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "A referee can take in the area with a glance",
   "note": "Quote verbatim, in the paragraph reading the bolded Tower of Mouths example — 'the immediately important features are clearly offset' — so the 'aim of bolding' limb is the sentence's own subject.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i709-723.json"
  },
  "718": {
   "index": 718,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The amount of text that must be read ... has been cut down by more than half without degrading the quality of the prose.",
   "note": "Quote verbatim. Every limb present in that one sentence: text that must be read, cut by more than half, no degrading of prose quality; and it is stated of the worked Tower of Mouths (Matt Finch, Knockspell 3) example, with the caveat that this was 'already a rather short description, and the savings yielded are usually greater'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i709-723.json"
  },
  "719": {
   "index": 719,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "much boxed text and followup writing are expended to say relatively little",
   "note": "Raw page fetched live (HTTP 200). Quote is word for word once the source's mid-sentence line wrapping is normalized: 'It always feels like the encounters are overwritten – much boxed text and followup writing are expended to say relatively little'. Module identified on the page as 'DCC #35A: Halls of the Minotaur (2006) by Harley Stroh', review dated 20 March 2018. Byline reads 'Melan'; 'Gabor Lux' is not printed on the page.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the byline reads 'Melan'; 'Gabor Lux' is not printed on the page",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "720": {
   "index": 720,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "It really did play better than it reads",
   "note": "Quote verbatim on the same raw page. The table/page contrast is explicit: the review says it 'will also contrast how the module reads vs. how it was run by our GM', the play was a convention DCC game, and 'Fairly standard areas took on a character they didn’t have in the text I read later.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i709-723.json"
  },
  "721": {
   "index": 721,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Read-aloud should never be long and italics should never be long.",
   "note": "Raw page fetched live (HTTP 200). Quote verbatim as the first half of that sentence. Post 'The Swan Hollow Intrigue', posted September 5, 2026 by Bryce Lynch, reviewing Dylan Hyatt's Shadowdark adventure — date and reviewer as claimed.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i709-723.json"
  },
  "722": {
   "index": 722,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Long read-aloud causes players to loose focus and pull out their phones.",
   "note": "Quote verbatim, including the page's 'loose' for 'lose'. Both limbs — losing focus and reaching for phones — are in that one sentence, reinforced by 'They don’t pay attention.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i709-723.json"
  },
  "723": {
   "index": 723,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Long italics is hard to read, cognitively.",
   "note": "Quote verbatim on the same raw page, immediately after the read-aloud sentence; the claim's 'long passages set in italics' matches the antecedent 'There are long stretches of read-aloud, in italics.'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i709-723.json"
  },
  "724": {
   "index": 724,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "In this context detail is bad. It is trivia.",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200, browser UA). Post 'The Swan Hollow Intrigue', posted September 5, 2026 by Bryce Lynch. Quote verbatim in the paragraph opening on the confusion between details and specifics; the same paragraph sets specificity against detail, so every limb (distinguishes detail from specificity; calls it trivia; says detail is bad) is on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i724-795.json"
  },
