  "794": {
   "index": 794,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "unless you hook the reader in the first half-page",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). 'Let's Design an Adventure: Adventure Hooks and Opening Scenes', by Shawn Merwin, published 2019-10-23, section 'You May Ask Yourself, How Did I Get Here?'. Taught limb explicit: he frames it as one of the most sobering things he was told in his creative writing classes. One nuance, not a missing limb: the page hedges the consequence as readers being unlikely to finish, where the claim says 'go unfinished'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i724-795.json"
  },
  "795": {
   "index": 795,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "might not be necessary in adventure writing",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Same post and section, same paragraph as 794. The page attributes 'razor's edge audience-grabbing' to fiction writing and says the same sort of it might not be necessary in adventure writing, then adds that it is still important to start as strongly as you can.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i724-795.json"
  },
  "796": {
