  "839": {
   "index": 839,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "“conceptualizing TRPGs as, first and foremost, “literary gametexts””",
   "note": "PDF fetched (HTTP 200) and text-extracted with pypdf, 12 pages, International Journal of Role-Playing issue 18. Quote verbatim in section '1. INTRODUCTION', where Mizer attributes the framing to Carbonell (2019, 3) alongside Gagne and contrasts it with his own anthropological angle.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i796-842.json"
  },
  "840": {
   "index": 840,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "“emerges as a set of practices which are only later codified”",
   "note": "Same PDF, section '1. INTRODUCTION', two sentences after the index-839 quote. Quote verbatim; the page reads 'I will argue the role-playing game emerges as...', so it is Mizer's own argument, and the codification is 'in the formulation of a gametext', matching the claim's final limb.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i796-842.json"
  },
  "841": {
   "index": 841,
   "verdict": "SKIPPED_TRIAGE",
   "trueWording": "",
   "note": "S-BOUND: the feature already has three or more verified independent sources; not verified this round",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-triage-r6.json"
  },
  "842": {
   "index": 842,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "“centering the gametexts and gametools that create gameplay”",
   "note": "Raw page fetched (HTTP 200). Quote verbatim; the page's own frame is that Dread Trident 'claims to add to posthuman theorization' by this approach, i.e. the reviewer reporting the book's claim. Burton endorses the substance elsewhere in the review (the book expands posthuman discourse, shifting posthumanism from bodies to spaces), so the claim's 'theorises the posthuman by centering...' holds. Byline Adrianna Burton, Analog Game Studies, January 9 2022.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i796-842.json"
  },
  "843": {
   "index": 843,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "answers this question through a series of close readings",
   "note": "Live page (analoggamestudies.org, browser UA). Sentence sits in the paragraph beginning 'Dread Trident's audience appears to be literary studies scholars'; page reads 'this question' (the book's stated secondary-focus question about gametexts and tools), which the claim renders as 'his question'. Quoted fragment appears word for word, and 'of several TRPGs' follows it on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i843-870.json"
  },
  "844": {
   "index": 844,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Dread Trident's audience appears to be literary studies scholars",
   "note": "Live page. Opens the paragraph on audience; quoted fragment is word for word, reviewer Adrianna Burton, dated January 9, 2022, matching the citation.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i843-870.json"
  },
  "845": {
   "index": 845,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "an overlooked area of investigation for literary scholars",
   "note": "Live page (liverpooluniversitypress.blog). First answer, opening sentence, in Carbonell's own voice ('I was interested in how role-playing gametexts appeared to be...'). Note the source narrows the group with a following qualifier about fantasy and science fiction that the claim drops; no contradiction, but the claim is the broader statement.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the source narrows the group with a following qualifier about fantasy and science fiction that the claim drops, making the claim the broader statement",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "846": {
   "index": 846,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "I then began my close readings of the archives",
   "note": "Live page. Closes the first answer's second paragraph. 'The archives' is unqualified in that sentence; the same sentence ends by locating the finding in the play of these gametexts, and the second answer supplies the forty years of published material, so the claim's gloss 'of published role-playing material' is carried by the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i843-870.json"
  },
  "847": {
   "index": 847,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "because of its simplicity or lack of textual and psychological depth",
   "note": "Live page, second answer. The 'disputes' limb rests on the sentence's own structure: it is easy to say this, but that 'high-brow' critique holds less weight today when all manner of cultural activity is open to investigation.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i843-870.json"
  },
  "848": {
   "index": 848,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "forty years of published material was out there, waiting to be mined",
   "note": "Live page, second answer, word for word. Two limbs are contextual rather than stated: 'role-playing' comes from the question being about TRPGs, and 'by a literary scholar' from the same sentence's opening, where Carbonell says he returned to it as an adult and literary scholar when he realised this. The page does not name a mining agent explicitly.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the page names no mining agent explicitly: 'by a literary scholar' is contextual, drawn from the sentence's opening rather than stated",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r6.json"
  },
  "849": {
   "index": 849,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "where we see literary tropes push up against game mechanics",
   "note": "Live page, answer to the question on a standout TRPG. The sentence names D&D as exemplary for anyone interested in fantasy studies, then locates that 'middle ground' with the quoted words; both limbs on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i843-870.json"
  },
  "850": {
   "index": 850,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "an impulse which Carbonell argues is also reflected in early TRPGs",
   "note": "Live page (sfrareview.org), review signed Clare Wall, filed under 52.1, posted January 10, 2022. The preceding clause attributes to Lovecraft a materialism and impulse to categorize in rich description; the quoted fragment follows it word for word.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i843-870.json"
