   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i887-901.json"
  },
  "895": {
   "index": 895,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Each location is given details that include history, potential lore",
   "note": "Verbatim in the setting-content paragraph. History does head the list, which continues with lore, city or region maps, and firsthand accounts. Note the list is explicitly non-exhaustive ('include'), so 'first' is list order, not a claim of completeness.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i887-901.json"
  },
  "896": {
   "index": 896,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "firsthand accounts from the researchers and loremasters from whose",
   "note": "Verbatim; the sentence closes 'perspective the book is written', which supports the claim in full.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i887-901.json"
  },
  "897": {
   "index": 897,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "has 53 pages dedicated to the city of Baldur",
   "note": "Verbatim in the closing section, given as an example against which the reviewer measures SCAG, and paired with 24 pages for Waterdeep: Dragon Heist. The source spells the title 'Descent to Avernus'; the claim's 'Descent into Avernus' is the book's actual title.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i887-901.json"
  },
  "898": {
   "index": 898,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'each' - one italic read-aloud passage carries no read-or-paraphrase instruction",
   "note": "The quoted instruction is verbatim and recurs four times, before the departure, first-day, third-day and fourth-day passages. But the awakened dragon skull's italic speech near the end is introduced only by narration ending 'a raspy voice emerges', so the instruction is standing but not universal. The publisher limb does hold on the page as served: the footer reads (c) 2017-2024 Wizards of the Coast LLC, though the post itself dates to July 2020.",
   "trueWording": "Read or paraphrase the following to set the scene",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i887-901.json"
  },
  "899": {
   "index": 899,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the universal - one italic read-aloud passage is first-person NPC speech",
   "trueWording": "Just past midday, you come across a smashed cart",
   "note": "Verbatim, and the five scene-setting read-aloud passages are all second person and present tense (the departure, first day, third day and the two Bryn Shander paragraphs). The sixth italic passage, the dragon skull's line beginning 'Hey, what's the big idea', is first person, so the claim as a universal over the page's read-aloud passages has one counterexample.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i887-901.json"
  },
  "900": {
   "index": 900,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Just past midday, you come across a smashed cart",
   "note": "Verbatim, and the italic passage is exactly two sentences: the smashed cart at the roadside, then two sets of tracks leading into a fir grove. The next paragraph is DM-facing, so the passage does close there. The heading reads 'On the First Day, an Exploration Encounter: Following the Tracks'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i887-901.json"
  },
  "901": {
   "index": 901,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'each' - most DM-facing paragraphs carry no run-in label",
   "trueWording": "Investigating the Cart. This cart is a simple wooden carriage",
   "note": "Quote verbatim, and the 'bolded' limb holds: the label is marked up bold and italic together (strong wrapping em), as are Following the Tracks, End of the Line, Terrain and Tactics, Wrath and Retreat, Treasure, Speaking with Ogden, Raggamel the Renegade and Delivering to Raggamel. But twelve labelled paragraphs sit against roughly twenty-eight unlabelled DM-facing prose paragraphs, so the labels mark sub-topics within an encounter rather than opening every DM-facing paragraph.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i887-901.json"
  },
  "902": {
   "index": 902,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "you have a four-day journey ahead of you",
   "note": "Raw HTML, HTTP 200. Quote is word-for-word inside the italic read-aloud paragraph introduced by the 'Read or paraphrase the following to set the scene' line at the departure from Hundelstone; words, not digits.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i902-916.json"
  },
  "903": {
   "index": 903,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "making this a 4-day trip at best",
   "note": "Raw HTML, HTTP 200. Word-for-word in the DM-facing prose under the h2 'Trek to Ten-Towns' (12 miles per day over ~50 miles); the numeral form of the same four-day span as index 902, confirming the digits/words split by register.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i902-916.json"
  },
  "904": {
   "index": 904,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "better to simply describe the day's travel in a single sentence",
   "unsupportedLimb": "\"Wizards' own encounter\" - the page bylines James Haeck, a D&D Beyond columnist described there as a freelancer for WotC, not WotC editorial; in July 2020 D&D Beyond was Fandom-owned, and the only Wizards mark on the page is a boilerplate site-footer copyright.",
   "note": "Raw HTML, HTTP 200. The advice limb is fully supported word-for-word: where nothing special is around, one sentence beats a litany of uninspired descriptions. Only the authorship attribution overreaches; cite it as publisher-hosted, as indices 902/903/905 do.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i902-916.json"
  },
  "905": {
   "index": 905,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the largest of the ten settlements that make up Ten-Towns",
   "note": "Raw HTML, HTTP 200. Word-for-word under 'The Third Delivery'; the description sentence opens on rank before anything else, and the later read-aloud repeats the pattern, opening with the walls of the town named southernmost and largest of the Ten-Towns.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i902-916.json"
  },
  "906": {
   "index": 906,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Mystery. Barovia is mysterious, and adventurers should feel as",
   "note": "Binary PDF fetched from media.wizards.com (HTTP 200, 170785 bytes) and extracted with pypdf. Quote word-for-word. The bold limb was checked at the font level, not by eye: the visitor pass shows the run-in labels set in GoudyOldStyleT-Bold and the following sentence in GoudyOldStyleT-Regular. The Tone section carries three such one-word labels (Mystery, Horror, Threat), and Themes and Surviving Strahd's Domain repeat the form.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i902-916.json"
  },
  "907": {
   "index": 907,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Horror. Barovia is infused by evil",
   "note": "Same PDF, same Tone section. Word-for-word; present tense and the place name as grammatical subject both hold, and the sibling entries (Barovia is mysterious, Barovia is cursed under Themes) show it is the section's habit, not one sentence.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i902-916.json"
  },
  "908": {
   "index": 908,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "along with their history of conflict and what strife they",
   "note": "Raw HTML, HTTP 200. Word-for-word; the sentence closes with 're currently facing' and attributes the descriptions of Oerth's nations to the chapter's Greyhawk Gazetteer in the 2024 DMG. Vendor/promotional framing (the paragraph sits beside marketplace links), which the claim's own kind field already records.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i902-916.json"
  },
  "909": {
   "index": 909,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "rare that a boxed text that includes a paragraph break",
   "unsupportedLimb": "\"twice as long as it should be\" - the page names no factor, saying only that such a boxed text isn't already too long by half, an idiom that will not carry a numeric reading; it also exempts introductory passages, which the claim's 'usually' does not capture.",
   "note": "Raw HTML, HTTP 200. Quote word-for-word in the 'Don't Overdo It' subsection, which does sit under the claim's cited 'Using Boxed Text Your Way' heading. The advisory direction (limit boxed-text length; a paragraph break signals excess) is supported; only the quantification is invented.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i902-916.json"
  },
  "910": {
   "index": 910,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "shifting text from third-person objective",
   "note": "Raw HTML, HTTP 200. Word-for-word in 'Using Boxed Text Your Way'; the page names both registers, illustrates each with a waterfall example, and advises the shift. Note the advice is one-directional and conditional - third-person objective to second-person subjective, when players tend to lose focus - slightly narrower than the claim's 'between them'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the bidirectional 'shift between them' — the verifier's note records the page's advice as 'one-directional and conditional', third-person objective to second-person subjective only when players lose focus, and 'slightly narrower than the claim's between them'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r10.json"
  },
  "911": {
   "index": 911,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "most important areas in the adventure are given the boxed",
   "note": "Raw HTML, HTTP 200. Word-for-word; the sentence continues that minor areas are often described differently, in the passage on the limits of adventure design that cites designer James Introcasto and Waterdeep: Dragon Heist by name.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i902-916.json"
  },
  "912": {
   "index": 912,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "A bookstore receives a short paragraph description that is written",
   "note": "Raw HTML, HTTP 200. Word-for-word; the sentence ends 'to be more easily paraphrased', and it is the first of the three Dragon Heist minor-area examples in that sentence-chain, so the minor-location limb holds.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i902-916.json"
  },
  "913": {
   "index": 913,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "multiple bullet points describing the furnishings, the sounds, and the",
   "note": "Raw HTML, HTTP 200. Word-for-word; the clause is 'a great hall in a noble villa has multiple bullet points... and the bodies on the floor', set against the boxed-text prose treatment given to important areas, so the 'rather than prose' contrast is the passage's own.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i902-916.json"
  },
  "914": {
   "index": 914,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the text mentioned the runes alongside other, less important details",
   "note": "Raw HTML, HTTP 200. Word-for-word under the 'Boxed text' heading of the post (dated 15 Nov 2012, not 2016 as the claim's date field says). Every limb holds: the setting is the 2012 GEN CON D&D Championship, the runes were load-bearing (disabling them lowered the arcana DCs), and they went unmarked - the author's team missed their significance while every team heard the same text.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i902-916.json"
  },
  "915": {
   "index": 915,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Curse of Strahd includes it for every location",
   "note": "Raw HTML, HTTP 200; the tag archive at the given /tag/tag/ URL does carry the full post 'How much description should a dungeon key include?'. Word-for-word under the heading 'Boxed, read-aloud text'. The disputing polarity holds - the neighbouring sentences say not every location needs it and that reading it for empty rooms will bore players. Post date is 21 Nov 2016, not the claim's 2018-2020.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i902-916.json"
  },
  "916": {
   "index": 916,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The Curse of Strahd adventure lavishes detail on every location",
   "note": "Same page and post, word-for-word under the cited heading 'Matching description to a location's purpose'. Supported and hostile in the claimed way: notable NPCs' homes get pages of room descriptions the players will never enter. Same 2016 date correction as index 915.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i902-916.json"
  },
  "917": {
   "index": 917,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The homes of notable NPCs get pages of room descriptions",
   "note": "Raw page (live, browser UA). Verbatim in section 'Matching description to a location's purpose', of Curse of Strahd. Post footer dates it November 21, 2016, not the claim's 2018-2020.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i917-931.json"
  },
  "918": {
   "index": 918,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "page-long description of his workroom in the 5-page description",
   "note": "Quote verbatim; the measurement (page-long workroom inside a 5-page mansion) is exact. The page names only 'the players' ally Victor Wachter'; 'Vallaki' appears nowhere on it (0 hits). Post dated November 21, 2016.",
