  "923": {
   "index": 923,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "each town only gets 2 or 3 described locations",
   "note": "Raw page. Verbatim in section 'Ten-Towns Businesses & NPCs', explicitly of Rime's ten towns. Post dated November 2020.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i917-931.json"
  },
  "924": {
   "index": 924,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "each town write-up comes with at least one new NPC per location",
   "note": "Raw page, section 'NPCs', whose paragraph subject is Legacy of the Crystal Shard's campaign guide ('In this supplement we meet...'). The year 2013 is the claim's own editorial gloss; the page names the product but no year.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the '2013' dating of Legacy of the Crystal Shard — the verifier's note says it 'is the claim's own editorial gloss; the page names the product but no year'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r10.json"
  },
  "925": {
   "index": 925,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the Armory and Council Hall in Bryn Shander get 2-3 sentences each",
   "note": "Raw page, section 'Ten-Towns Businesses & NPCs'. Context puts both among the extra locations Legacy adds ('presents all of these plus 17 more'), and calls those write-ups 'not extensive', supporting 'minor'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i917-931.json"
  },
  "926": {
   "index": 926,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The DMG divides settlements just by population",
   "note": "Raw page, section 'Village'; the preceding sentence names 'The 5e DMG', and the quoted sentence continues 'and lumps together everything under 1,000 inhabitants as a village'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i917-931.json"
  },
  "927": {
   "index": 927,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "as described in the Commerce section (p19)",
   "note": "Raw page, section 'Village'. The article walks the DMG's settlement-generation section by heading (Purpose p15, Size with Village/Town/City sub-headings, Commerce p19), supporting both limbs.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i917-931.json"
  },
  "928": {
   "index": 928,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Baron Vargas Vallakovich, the Burgomaster of Vallaki, a ruthless cruel man",
   "note": "Live URL 403; recovered via Wayback raw capture 20260418221915id_. Verbatim under heading 'The powers at play'. Title, given name, surname, then office - all four limbs on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i917-931.json"
  },
  "929": {
   "index": 929,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "the \"guard\" consists of Izek and 24 guards",
   "note": "Wayback raw capture. Quote verbatim under heading 'The Bomb in Vallaki'. 'captain' and 'commander' return 0 hits on the page; Izek is named only as distinct from the 24 guards, and elsewhere as Ireena's brother.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'captain' - the page gives Izek no rank, title or command role",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i917-931.json"
  },
  "930": {
   "index": 930,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Reading out a chunk of boxed text is basically equivalent to waving",
   "note": "Raw page, thread 'Boxed texts'. The sentence continues to a flag reading that the location or NPC is a clue to the plot, supporting the claim. Post is by 'humble minion', dated Jul 12, 2020, not the claim's 2021.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i917-931.json"
  },
  "931": {
   "index": 931,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "about 32 pages of gazetteer information",
   "note": "Raw page, closing assessment, dated November 7th, 2018. Framed counterfactually ('could have easily been a 64 page module plus...'), but the next sentence treats it as the book's own usable 96 pages, so the gazetteer estimate does attach to Dragon Heist.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i917-931.json"
  },
  "932": {
   "index": 932,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The following tables allow you to flesh out details about a settlement.",
   "note": "Quote verbatim under 'Settlement Tables and Tracker'. All four headings appear exactly as given (Defining Traits 1d20, Claims to Fame 1d20, Current Calamities 1d12, Local Leaders 1d12), and a Settlements by Size table heads the section. The 'settlement-level' qualifier is doing real work and holds: the section carries two further roll tables, Tavern Names* and Random Shops, but each generates a sub-location inside the settlement, not an attribute of the settlement as a whole. Wizards' own Settlement Tracker corroborates the count by recording exactly those four rolled slots.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i932-946.json"
  },
  "933": {
   "index": 933,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Simply describing the settlement to your players is usually sufficient.",
   "note": "Quote verbatim in the sidebar headed 'Do I Need a Settlement Map?', whose opening sentence independently states a settlement does not always require a map. The page then adds that a map is helpful when it matters where buildings are - context the claim's own 'usually' accommodates rather than contradicts.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i932-946.json"
  },
  "934": {
   "index": 934,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Village Up to 500; Town 501-5,000; City 5,001 and higher",
   "note": "Quote verbatim in the sentence introducing the table. The three rows carry exactly the bands claimed. The same table also carries a Max. GP Value column (village 20 GP, town 2,000 GP, city 200,000 GP), which the claim does not mention but does not misstate.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i932-946.json"
  },
  "935": {
   "index": 935,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "(which costs 50 GP) is too expensive an item",
   "note": "Quote verbatim in the second paragraph, inside a worked example opening 'For example, a Potion of Healing...'. The numeral sits in running prose, not in a table, exactly as claimed.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i932-946.json"
  },
  "936": {
   "index": 936,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'in the present tense' for EVERY entry - row 2 of the 1d12 table opens in the past tense",
   "trueWording": "A key figure died; murder is suspected.",
   "note": "Quote verbatim as row 1. Measured all twelve rows: all twelve are complete sentences closed with a full stop, so those two limbs hold. The universal present-tense limb fails on row 2, whose main clause verb 'died' is past (the second clause 'murder is suspected' is present). Eleven of twelve are present tense; the claim should read 'almost every entry' or drop the tense limb.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i932-946.json"
  },
  "937": {
   "index": 937,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Respected, fair, and just leader or council",
   "note": "Quote verbatim as row 1 of the Local Leaders 1d12 table; rows 8, 11 and 12 also name a council ('Acrimonious council unable to make decisions', 'Iron-willed and respected leader or council', 'Religious leader or council'). The 'retitled' limb is corroborated from the 2014 source fetched for indices 944-946: the 2014 equivalent is headed 'Ruler's Status' and its row 1 reads 'Respected, fair, and just' with no council offered.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i932-946.json"
  },
  "938": {
   "index": 938,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'of the built place' - four of the eleven rows are natural or atmospheric, not built",
   "trueWording": "Lingering fog / Cliffs on one or more sides",
   "note": "Quote verbatim as the 19-20 row. The claim's central contrast holds completely: measured all eleven rows of the 1d20 table and not one is political or reputational (politics and reputation live in the separate Local Leaders and Claims to Fame tables). But 'built' over-specifies - Lingering fog, Cliffs on one or more sides, Lots of mud, filth, and litter, and Lots of gardens, parks, and greenery are natural or environmental. 'Noise and smoke from smithies and forges' is also audible rather than strictly visible. 'Physical, sensory features of the place' would be true as written.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i932-946.json"
  },
  "939": {
   "index": 939,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "2024 tables: Size, Traits, Fame, Calamities, Leaders, Taverns, Shops - none relations",
   "note": "Absence claim, so no quote is possible; graded on a full enumeration of the fetched section. The 2024 Settlements section carries exactly seven tables - Settlements by Size, Defining Traits, Claims to Fame, Current Calamities, Local Leaders, Tavern Names*, Random Shops - and none concerns relations between peoples. The second limb is independently confirmed in the 2014 OCR fetched for indices 944-946, where a d20 table headed 'Race Relations' is the first of the Random Settlements tables. Both limbs stand.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i932-946.json"
  },
  "940": {
   "index": 940,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "GP VALUE OF THE MOST EXPENSIVE ITEM FOR SALE:",
   "note": "Quote verbatim on the sheet's last line. PDF fetched as binary and read two ways - pdftotext -layout and a 90dpi page render inspected visually. The render confirms exactly the nine fields claimed and no tenth: settlement name, the three-way size band, four titled panels (Defining Trait, Claim to Fame, Current Calamity, Local Leader), the two ruled rosters, and the GP value line. Footer reads TM & (c) 2024 Wizards of the Coast LLC.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i932-946.json"
  },
  "941": {
   "index": 941,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "DEFINING TRAIT; CLAIM TO FAME; CURRENT CALAMITY; LOCAL LEADER",
   "note": "Quote verbatim. All four tracker slot labels are singular; the four corresponding DMG headings on the Roll20 capture are plural (Defining Traits, Claims to Fame, Current Calamities, Local Leaders). The claim's own stated weakness - that the plural side sits on a different page - is resolved here because both pages were fetched raw in this pass, so both limbs are directly verified rather than inferred.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i932-946.json"
  },
  "942": {
   "index": 942,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'that no table in the DMG's Settlements section generates' - the section's Tavern Names and Random Shops tables do generate places",
   "trueWording": "NOTEWORTHY PEOPLE / NOTEWORTHY PLACES, ruled blank lines",
   "note": "Quote verbatim; the render confirms both are free-text rosters of seven ruled lines each, unlike the four titled panels above them. The People half of the second limb holds - no table generates noteworthy people. The Places half does not: the same section carries a Tavern Names* table (1d20 x 1d20, two-part names) and a Random Shops table (1d20 shop types), both of which generate places a DM would enter on that roster. True as 'no table dedicated to them' or 'no table generates noteworthy people'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i932-946.json"
  },
  "943": {
   "index": 943,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'tick boxes' - the three marks are open circles (radio style), not boxes",
   "trueWording": "TOWN (POP. 501-5,000) preceded by an open circle",
   "note": "Quote verbatim including the en dash. The count and the numeral bands hold exactly: three mutually exclusive options reading VILLAGE (POP. UP TO 500), TOWN (POP. 501-5,000), CITY (POP. 5,001+). The shape limb fails on inspection - pdftotext renders the marks as 'l' from an embedded ZapfDingbatsITC subset, and a 90dpi page render shows three unfilled circles, radio-button style, not square tick boxes. The PDF has an AcroForm dictionary but no widget annotations, so these are printed ornaments on a photocopy sheet rather than interactive controls.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i932-946.json"
  },
  "944": {
   "index": 944,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "When creating a settlement for your campaign, focus",
   "note": "Quote verbatim in the full-book OCR; the sentence completes 'on the locations that are most relevant to the adventure.' Located in the Settlements section of ch.5, and the page foot two screens later reads '112 CHAPTER 5 I ADVENTURE ENVIRONMENTS', confirming the cited page and chapter.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i932-946.json"
  },
  "945": {
   "index": 945,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the inhabitants of every building; that way lies madness.",
   "note": "Quote verbatim; the full sentence opens 'Don't worry about naming every street and identifying...'. Both limbs of the claim - every street, every building's inhabitants - are present, as is the madness figure. Immediately follows the passage verified at index 944 on the same page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i932-946.json"
  },
  "946": {
   "index": 946,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "settlement. They assume that you've already determined",
   "note": "Quote verbatim, straight apostrophe in the OCR matching the claim exactly; the sentence completes 'its size and its basic form of government.' The five-table count is confirmed by enumeration: under the 'Random Settlements' heading the OCR carries Race Relations, Ruler's Status, Notable Traits, Known For Its..., and Current Calamity, after which a separate 'Random Buildings' subsection begins. Both limbs hold.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i932-946.json"
  },
  "947": {
   "index": 947,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "its size and its basic form of government.",
   "note": "Raw OCR of the archive.org _djvu.txt (1,207,785 bytes, HTTP 200, curl direct). Quote exact at the head of 'Random Settlements'. The section carries exactly five tables in this order before the p.112 footer and the next section ('Random Buildings'): Race Relations, Ruler's Status, Notable Traits, Known For Its ..., Current Calamity. All five headings match the claim.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i947-961.json"
  },
  "948": {
   "index": 948,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Known For Its ...",
   "note": "Quote exact, including the ellipsis, as the table heading. Its d20 column is headed 'Feature' and every result is a completing noun ('Delicious cuisine', 'Rude people', 'Piety', 'Gambling', 'Patriotism'), so the heading does read as a stem the rolled result finishes. Both limbs supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i947-961.json"
  },
  "949": {
   "index": 949,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Undead stirring in cemeteries",
   "note": "The quote is exact and is itself a noun phrase, and no entry carries terminal punctuation, so 'unpunctuated' holds. But the generalisation over the table fails: at least five of the twenty results are subject-verb clauses, not noun phrases - 'New cult seeks converts', 'Important figure died (murder suspected)', 'Powerful wizard has moved into town', 'Scandal threatens powerful families', 'Religious sects struggle for power'. Two more carry parenthetical punctuation ('Plague or famine (sparks riots)', 'Economic depression (trade disrupted)'). The table's register is 'unpunctuated fragment', which covers both shapes; 'noun phrase' does not.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the entries are noun phrases rather than sentences - several are unpunctuated finite clauses",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i947-961.json"
  },
  "950": {
   "index": 950,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "concentrate instead on the major features.",
   "note": "Quote exact. The sentence opens 'Mapping a Settlement' and reads, in full, that when you draw a settlement map you should not worry about the placement of every building and should concentrate instead on the major features. The claim's 'ignore' is a fair rendering of the book's 'don't worry about'; every limb is on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i947-961.json"
  },
  "951": {
   "index": 951,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the kinds of trades that dominate the neighborhood",
   "note": "Quote exact. The same paragraph in 'Mapping a Settlement' tells the DM to give wards names reflecting their personalities, which identify the trades, a geographical characteristic, or a dominant site - and all three of the claim's cited examples are the book's own, in its own parentheses: Tannery Square (with Temple Row), Riverside (with Hilltop), and the Lords' Quarter.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i947-961.json"
  },
  "952": {
   "index": 952,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "What does it look, smell, and sound like?",
   "note": "Quote exact, and the list does open the ch.1 'Settlements' guidance ('Consider the following questions as you create any settlement in your world'). But the position is wrong. The bullets run: purpose in your game; how big / who lives there; look, smell, sound; who governs; defenses; goods and services; temples and organizations; fantastic elements; why characters should care - nine in all. The sensory question is third, not second, and it is the list's only sensory question, so 'second sensory question' fails on either reading.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'second' - the sensory question is the third bullet in the list, and the only sensory one",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i947-961.json"
  },
  "953": {
   "index": 953,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Sensory details help bring a settlement to life",
   "note": "Quote exact under the 'Atmosphere' subhead; the sentence continues that they vividly communicate the settlement's personality to your players, so the 'for the players' limb is the book's own. One calibration for downstream use: the DMG hedges with 'help bring', where the claim says 'are what bring'. The assertion is the book's; the exclusivity is not, so do not quote the claim as though the DMG said sensory details alone do the work.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the exclusivity in 'sensory details are what bring a settlement to life' — the verifier's note states 'the DMG hedges with help bring... The assertion is the book's; the exclusivity is not'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r10.json"
  },
  "954": {
   "index": 954,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Settle on a single defining factor that sums up a",
   "note": "Quote exact; the sentence completes with the settlement's personality and an instruction to extrapolate from there, and the paragraph then demonstrates it - a canal city like Venice yields sights, sounds, smells and the feel of humidity; a fog-shrouded city yields cold mist, muffled hooves, the smell of rain. Both limbs, the single factor and the extrapolation, are supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i947-961.json"
  },
  "955": {
   "index": 955,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "of this sort needs no more than a brief description.",
   "note": "Quote exact, and the preceding sentence in 'Local Color' supplies the antecedent the claim depends on: a settlement that serves as a place where the characters stop to rest and to buy supplies. 'A settlement of this sort' is that settlement, so the rest-and-resupply limb is the book's own.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i947-961.json"
  },
  "956": {
   "index": 956,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Create only the features of a settlement",
   "note": "Quote exact (the claim's longer form, carrying the tail of the previous sentence, also matches the OCR byte for byte). Under 'Purpose'. The passage continues that you create the features you know you'll need, with notes on general features, then allow the place to grow organically as the adventurers interact with more of it - so the 'let the place grow later' limb is supported too.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i947-961.json"
  },
  "957": {
   "index": 957,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Disregard any advice here that runs counter to",
   "note": "Quote exact; the sentence completes with the DM's vision for a settlement, and the sentence before it frames the whole section as guidelines to help you build the settlement you want. The licence is explicit and it is scoped to this settlement section, exactly as claimed.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i947-961.json"
  },
  "958": {
   "index": 958,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Organizations: A village might contain one or two",
   "note": "Quote exact. Checked all three size entries, not just the quoted one: Village, Town and City each carry the same five labelled fields in the same order - Population, Government, Defense, Commerce, Organizations - before their running prose. The record shape holds across the set, so the 'each settlement size' limb is confirmed rather than inferred from one entry.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i947-961.json"
  },
  "959": {
   "index": 959,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Cities that hold more than twenty-five thousand people",
   "note": "Quote exact, in the running prose beneath the City record, in a sentence saying such cities are extremely rare before naming Waterdeep, Sharn and the Free City of Greyhawk. The threshold is spelled out in words in prose, as claimed.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i947-961.json"
  },
  "960": {
   "index": 960,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Population: Up to about 25,000",
   "note": "Quote exact, in the City entry's labelled Population field, and the numeral/word contrast the claim is really about is real: the same 25,000 figure appears as digits in the field and as words in the prose, on the same page and in the same entry. The proximity limb overstates it. Between them stand the Government, Defense, Commerce and Organizations fields plus two full paragraphs of prose - about thirty printed lines, not a few. Worth noting for the register work: the field states a ceiling ('Up to about 25,000') and the prose sentence a floor ('more than twenty-five thousand'), so they are the same figure doing opposite duty.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'a few lines above' - roughly thirty lines, four labelled fields and two paragraphs separate them",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i947-961.json"
  },
  "961": {
   "index": 961,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "worth 50 gp in Waterdeep and 30 gp",
   "note": "Quote exact. The sentence, about Waterdeep's harbor moon, completes with 'elsewhere', so both coin values sit as numerals inside one sentence of running prose under 'Example: The Forgotten Realms'. The surrounding paragraphs do the same repeatedly (the toal at 2 gp, Silverymoon's electrum moon at 1 gp / 1 ep, the eclipsed moon at 5 ep / 2 ep), so the pattern is the section's habit, not a one-off.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i947-961.json"
  },
  "962": {
   "index": 962,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "naturally divide the city into wards (neighborhoods defined by specific features)",
   "note": "archive.org _djvu.txt OCR of the 2014 DMG fetched raw by curl; the string appears at OCR line 2576 in the Settlements > Size > City passage. Both limbs hold: the preceding sentence has the stages of a city's growth identified by the expansion of the walls beyond the central core, and it is those internal walls that divide the city into wards. Section attribution ch.1 Settlements > Size > City is consistent (the Purpose section begins p.15 per a second source).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i962-976.json"
  },
  "963": {
   "index": 963,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "\"settlement\" appears twice: selling treasure, and hirelings from a distant settlement",
   "note": "Publisher PDF fetched raw by curl (3.1 MB), pdftotext -layout, 3.35 MB of text. Measured: grep -o -i settlement returns exactly 2 hits. Hit 1 is the treasure-selling clause; hit 2 is the hirelings passage. Zero hits for Race Relations, Ruler's Status, Notable Traits, Known for, Current Calamity, Metropolis, Forms of Government; no Settlements/Towns/Cities heading. Both limbs confirmed. No quote was supplied so this is graded SUBSTANCE, not VERBATIM.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i962-976.json"
  },
  "964": {
   "index": 964,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "four \"settlement\" hits, all spell text; no settlement section or tables",
   "note": "Publisher PDF fetched raw by curl (6.0 MB), pdftotext -layout, 2.15 MB of text. Four occurrences of settlement, every one inside spell or item text (commune with nature's Locations of settlements bullet, a druid-magic exclusion, a castles-and-settlements clause, a materials clause). Zero hits for Notable Traits, Current Calamity, Race Relations, Metropolis, Town Encounters, Settlement Tracker; no Settlements heading. Both limbs confirmed; no quote supplied, so SUBSTANCE.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i962-976.json"
  },
  "965": {
   "index": 965,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "doesn't include an index of all of the random charts",
   "note": "Live URL fetched raw with a browser user agent, HTML stripped locally. Quote appears verbatim in the second paragraph. The Random Table Index table then gives every figure the claim asserts: Race Relations 112, Ruler's Status 112, Notable Traits 112, Known For Its 112, Current Calamity 112, and Forms of Government 18 near the head of the index. Every limb supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i962-976.json"
  },
  "966": {
   "index": 966,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "is less about what settlements are like in the world",
   "note": "Live URL fetched raw with a browser user agent, HTML stripped locally. The sentence reads that the Purpose section, beginning on page 15 of the DMG, is less about what settlements are like in the world than about how much the GM should invent and record about them. Both halves of the claim's contrast are present. Authorship confirmed by the page's rel=author link to /author/ian/.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i962-976.json"
  },
  "967": {
   "index": 967,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The DMG divides settlements just by population",
   "note": "Same raw fetch. Quote verbatim, and it sits under the 'Village' sub-heading as the claim's page field says. The function limb is supported by the next sentences: historians draw a distinction in nature between villages and towns whose population ranges overlap, villages being agricultural units and towns commercial units. Disputes polarity is right.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i962-976.json"
  },
  "968": {
   "index": 968,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "more of a bullet-point list of suggestions than a guide",
   "note": "Live URL fetched raw with a browser user agent (2.1 MB of HTML), stripped locally. The quoted fragment is contiguous and verbatim; the full sentence continues 'to cooking up good ideas of your own'. The settlement limb is supported in the immediately preceding sentence, which names the toolbox's tables for dungeons and settlement creation. Complaint/disputes polarity is right.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i962-976.json"
  },
  "969": {
   "index": 969,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'the Settlement Tracker among them' - the page never names a sheet 'Settlement Tracker'; it says only that the sheets cover settlements among key NPCs, Bastions, magic items and backstories",
   "trueWording": "designed to help you keep track of important information",
   "note": "Live URL fetched raw with a browser user agent, HTML stripped locally. Perkins' quoted words appear verbatim ('these sheets are designed to help you keep track of important information in your game'), attributed to an official livestream from October 1, by-lined Mollie Russell. grep for 'settlement tracker' over the raw HTML returns 0. Downstream fix is one word: write 'settlements among them' rather than naming the sheet. Note also the article calls the guide upcoming, so this is pre-release reporting.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i962-976.json"
  },
  "970": {
   "index": 970,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "All that took about 30 seconds to create using the tables",
   "note": "Live Substack URL fetched raw with a browser user agent; the whole short post is in the HTML, not paywalled. Quote verbatim, continuing 'on pg. 112 (and the tavern name on 113)'. The demonstration is a single connected paragraph immediately above it, carrying page-112 slots visibly: harmony (Race Relations), a kind, just, and respected former war hero (Ruler's Status), sinister reputation (Notable Traits row 17), marauding monsters (Calamity row 8-9). Byline Maximilian Hart, dated Oct 27, 2022.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i962-976.json"
  },
  "971": {
   "index": 971,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'the full row lists' - the reproduction's Notable Traits stops at row 19, omitting the DMG's row 20 (built atop ancient ruins), and its Ruler's Status ranges are malformed (17-18 and 18-20 overlap where the book has 19-20)",
   "trueWording": "Dungeon Master's Guide 5e, p.112",
   "note": "Live URL fetched raw with a browser user agent, HTML stripped locally. The p.112 citation is verbatim as footnote 1 at the foot of the page, and all five named tables are reproduced with row lists. But the lists are not full: I cross-checked against the 2014 DMG OCR, where Notable Traits is a d20 with 20 rows ending 'Built atop ancient ruins', and Ruler's Status ends 19-20 Religious leader. The supplied low-confidence caveat is also confirmed on the page - it additionally prints Community Sizes (thorp/hamlet bands), Overall Alignment and a d100 Government table, none of which are 5e settlement tables.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i962-976.json"
  },
  "972": {
   "index": 972,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'opening on the city's physical situation' - the description's first paragraph is an adventure hook (would-be heroes drawn by promises of adventure); the river placement is the second paragraph",
   "trueWording": "The city stands on the eastern banks of the Selintan River.",
   "note": "roll20.net licensed compendium page fetched raw with a browser user agent; the body text is present in the HTML despite the purchase banner. Quote verbatim. The present-tense limb holds across the section (stands, flows, remains, is ruled, winds), with past tense reserved for the historical clause. Only the 'opening' limb misses by one paragraph, so the safe downstream wording is 'turns to the city's physical situation in its second paragraph'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i962-976.json"
  },
  "973": {
   "index": 973,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "proclaimed itself free and independent seventy-eight years ago",
   "note": "Same raw roll20 fetch. Quote verbatim in the third paragraph of the Free City of Greyhawk opening. The elapsed span of seventy-eight years is spelled in words, with no numeral form anywhere in that sentence, so both limbs hold.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i962-976.json"
  },
  "974": {
   "index": 974,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "made up of sixteen coequal rulers",
   "note": "Same raw roll20 fetch. Quote verbatim and it does sit under the 'City Government' heading as the page field says: the council is the Directing Oligarchy and its membership count is spelled in words, not given as a numeral.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i962-976.json"
  },
  "975": {
   "index": 975,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "a standing garrison of some eight hundred",
   "note": "Same raw roll20 fetch. Quote verbatim under the 'City Watch' heading; the sentence continues into linked creature names (Guards and Veteran Warriors). The garrison size is spelled in words. Both limbs hold.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i962-976.json"
  },
  "976": {
   "index": 976,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "A 30-foot-high stone wall winds like a snake around the city.",
   "note": "Same raw roll20 fetch. Quote verbatim as the first sentence under 'City Walls'. It is running prose, not a stat line, and the measurement is a numeral - and the same section keeps that habit (secret compartments at 300-foot intervals, a 30-foot-tall ladder, a sentry every 300 feet), so the contrast with the spelled-out counts in 973-975 is a real and consistent house rule: counts of people and years in words, physical measurements in numerals.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i962-976.json"
  },
  "977": {
   "index": 977,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The perpetrator pays a fine of 2d10 GP or works",
   "note": "Verbatim in the 'Crime' > 'Petty Crime' paragraph of the Roll20 licensed 2024 DMG page (HTTP 200, raw curl with browser UA, HTML stripped). Both limbs hold: the dice expression 2d10 and the coin abbreviation GP sit inside a prose sentence, and the same three-category Crime block also carries 50 GP, 250 GP, 100 GP, 1d6 years and 2d10 years in running prose.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i977-991.json"
  },
  "978": {
   "index": 978,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "the River Quarter, Clerkburg, the Artisans' Quarter, and the Foreign Quarter",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that this applies a ward-naming rule the 2014 DMG had set out - the page never mentions the 2014 DMG, any earlier edition, or a ward-naming rule; that link is the claimant's inference, not on this page",
   "note": "Quote verbatim in 'City Neighborhoods'. The naming limb holds on the page's own roster - Artisans' Quarter (a trade), River Quarter (the Selintan River, a physical feature), Foreign Quarter and Thieves'/Slum Quarters (resident classes). Only the cross-edition attribution is unsupported here.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i977-991.json"
  },
  "979": {
   "index": 979,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Clerkburg is the university district of Greyhawk, with dozens of schools",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that each entry runs two or three present-tense sentences - of the eight neighbourhood entries four (Foreign Quarter, Garden Quarter, Slum Quarter, Thieves' Quarter) are a single sentence and none reaches three; the range is one to two",
   "note": "Quote verbatim in 'City Neighborhoods'. The bolding limb is confirmed in the raw HTML, where each entry opens <strong>Clerkburg.</strong> etc. before the description, and every entry is present tense. Only the sentence-count limb fails.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i977-991.json"
  },
  "980": {
   "index": 980,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the year 576 in the Common Year",
   "note": "Quote verbatim in 'The Big Picture'. The dating limb holds: realm-level events are dated 356 CY (Nyrond joining the secession), 453 CY (accession of King Tavish IV) and 569 CY (Battle of Emridy Meadows). Caveat on form only - the present year is written out as the Common Year in the same parenthesis rather than abbreviated CY, though the abbreviation is used for the dated events.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i977-991.json"
  },
  "981": {
   "index": 981,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Seven years ago, knights and soldiers from Furyondy",
   "note": "Quote verbatim under 'Central Flanaess' > 'Battle of Emridy Meadows'; the numeral form is in 'The Big Picture' on the same page, which asks the reader to picture the Flanaess about 200 years ago. The no-single-rule limb is reinforced by a second mismatched pair on the page: the Eastern Flanaess culture paragraph writes almost 800 years ago as a numeral while the Western Flanaess paragraph spells out about a thousand years ago, so no consistent spell-out threshold is in force.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i977-991.json"
  },
  "982": {
   "index": 982,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "the only relevant details of this history are those that",
   "note": "Verbatim in the opening sentence of 'Living History', which completes with the clause that they feature in your adventures. The page states it of Greyhawk's own history of ancient empires and recent wars - the claim's generalisation to a setting's history is a fair reading of the worked setting, and the following sentence tells the DM to reveal significant details in the course of adventures.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i977-991.json"
  },
  "983": {
   "index": 983,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "provide concrete touchstones to past events",
   "note": "Verbatim in 'Living History' > 'Echoes of the Past', where all three of the claim's examples appear in one sentence: a crater in the side of a mountain, a defaced statue in the town square, a holiday celebrating a local hero. The page's own word is concrete touchstones rather than physical residue, and the third example (a holiday) is a custom rather than a physical object - a gloss, not a failed limb.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i977-991.json"
  },
  "984": {
   "index": 984,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Summarize what lengthy works say, and focus on the most",
   "note": "Verbatim as the closing sentence of 'Living History' > 'Historical Records', completing with plot-worthy parts. The page says to focus on the most plot-worthy parts rather than to give only those, so the claim's only is a slight strengthening of the instruction's emphasis.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the restrictive 'give only its plot-worthy parts' — the verifier's note says 'the page says to focus on the most plot-worthy parts rather than to give only those, so the claim's only is a slight strengthening'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r10.json"
  },
  "985": {
   "index": 985,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Move from the second-person point of view to the third person",
   "note": "Verbatim as the Tip 1 heading of the article, bylined Shawn Merwin and dated Nov 6, 2019 on dndbeyond.com (HTTP 200, raw curl with browser UA). It is the first of six numbered tips, and the article's opening paragraph equates boxed text with read-aloud text, so both limbs of the claim hold.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i977-991.json"
  },
  "986": {
   "index": 986,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The bloated corpse of a headless dragon dominates the center",
   "note": "Verbatim inside the Tip 1 body, given as the third-person rewrite (it completes with of a clearing) set against the second-person version he rejects, in which you notice the same corpse. In the preferred form the corpse - the object described - is the grammatical subject and the verb is dominates, exactly as the claim states.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i977-991.json"
  },
  "987": {
   "index": 987,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "leave in only the details about the environment when the PCs",
   "note": "Verbatim under Tip 2 (Avoid the novelist/dramatist urge), introduced as his rule of thumb when writing or editing and completing with the requirement that the PCs must interact with them on some level. The following sentences illustrate with a bog or a monster's stench that matters only as a terrain feature or monster ability.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i977-991.json"
  },
  "988": {
   "index": 988,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "boxed text is not the place to unleash that inner novelist",
   "note": "Verbatim in the first paragraph under the Tip 2 heading, which is itself Avoid the novelist/dramatist urge. The surrounding sentences warn that nothing slows a game more than paragraph after paragraph of boxed text and prose so purple it embarrasses a rainbow, so the claim's novelist's voice reading is exact.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i977-991.json"
  },
  "989": {
   "index": 989,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Describe everything the characters can sense, but not what they can't",
   "note": "Verbatim as the Tip 3 heading. The body supports the could-not-know limb directly: it faults boxed text that details things the characters couldn't know, giving locked crates whose contents are described as full of mining equipment, and tells the DM to let the mystery linger.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i977-991.json"
  },
  "990": {
   "index": 990,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "nothing triggers meta-gaming like the words “seems” or “appears”",
   "note": "Verbatim in the Tip 4 body (the heading reads Avoid seems to be or appears to be), and the metagaming ground is the page's own reason - those words generally mean some trick or diversion is in the offing. The page frames it as avoidance rather than a formal ban, and directs the DM to let Perception or Investigation checks separate the seems from the is after the boxed text ends.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i977-991.json"
  },
  "991": {
   "index": 991,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "If you're the DM, you get two sentences. Period.",
   "note": "Verbatim under 'Undercover Insight #1: Boxed Text = MEGO' on the Wayback raw capture (HTTP 200), which carries the Design & Development masthead, the date 09/16/2005 and the byline Jesse Decker and David Noonan. The first-person observer is Noonan: the piece describes ditching the Wizards polo shirt to watch games for twenty minutes at a stretch across the four days of Gen Con 2005, and later calls it the Dave wanders around technique. The next sentence supplies the consequence - beyond two sentences players are stacking dice, talking or staring off into space.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i977-991.json"
  },
  "992": {
   "index": 992,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "boxed text longer than a paragraph probably isn't worth reading",
   "note": "Wayback raw capture fetched 200, 13,874 bytes; quote is word-for-word under the heading 'Undercover Insight #1: Boxed Text = MEGO', introduced as 'My hypothesis was that...' and followed by 'What I actually saw was much more dramatic than my hypothesis' - so the prior-hypothesis limb is explicit. The first-person narrator of that section is Noonan: the column later calls the method 'Dave wanders around' and asks whether adventures change 'because Dave spent a lot of time wandering around'.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i992-1006.json"
  },
  "993": {
   "index": 993,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Boxed text replaces that conversation with oration.",
   "note": "Verbatim on the same page. The antecedent is supplied two paragraphs earlier ('At its heart, a D&D game is a conversation'), and the column ranks this above two other factors, naming it 'the conversation vs. oration factor'. Both limbs - that it is his explanation, and that the mechanism is conversation displaced by oration - are on the page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i992-1006.json"
  },
  "994": {
   "index": 994,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "ditch the boxed text and use your own words",
   "note": "Verbatim in the closing 'In the meantime' paragraph; the page continues 'for the initial description of the room', which carries the claim's 'describe the room' limb. Attribution limb: the column is co-bylined 'By Jesse Decker and David Noonan', both Wizards R&D staff per the About the Authors block, and the closing section speaks as 'we' - but the advice paragraph itself is in the singular undercover voice, so 'two Wizards designers' rests on the byline, not on a joint first person.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i992-1006.json"
  },
  "995": {
   "index": 995,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "That's pretty short. Contrast it with the first room",
   "note": "Verbatim in the 'Tangent Alert!' aside. The 2005 passage quoted (from Sons of Gruumsh, called 'a recent Forgotten Realms adventure' - the page does not date it) is indeed three sentences; the comparator is named as 1999's Return to the Keep on the Borderlands. The contraction limb is the column's own generalisation immediately above the pair - that boxed text used to be much longer than it is today - so it is asserted, not inferred; but it rests on two hand-picked passages, not a corpus measure, and 5th edition is nowhere on the page, so 'before 5th edition' is external chronology (2005 precedes 2014).",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'before 5th edition' — the verifier's note states that '5th edition is nowhere on the page' and that the contraction rests on two hand-picked passages rather than a corpus measure",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r10.json"
  },
  "996": {
   "index": 996,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'is an internal PDF ... rather than a published book' - the page never says internal, and states the documents 'are now available for all DMsGuild creators' as a free ($0.00) watermarked PDF download",
   "trueWording": "distributed by Wizards of the Coast to official play writers",
   "note": "Wayback raw capture fetched 200, 56,942 bytes. Quote verbatim. The January 2019 limb is verbatim too, on its own line above the description: the House Style Guide has been updated January 2019 (the package's File Last Updated is 22 February 2019). The package also bundles adventure-design tips from Chris Perkins and Greg Bilsand, D&D IP guidance and a Forgotten Realms guide, and the page says following them is not required to publish on DMsGuild. 'Internal' is fair as origin but wrong as present access - the cited page is itself a public free download of the guide.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i992-1006.json"
  },
  "997": {
   "index": 997,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'widely circulated' - the page carries no circulation evidence at all (a personal WordPress blog post with 8 comments); nothing on it speaks to reach",
   "trueWording": "I was looking for an official writer's guide, or style sheet",
   "note": "Live URL fetched 200 with a browser UA, 39,307 bytes. Quote verbatim in the opening under 'Style Guide for Dungeon Masters'. The two substantive limbs are verbatim: 'Perhaps they have one and I just couldn't find it' (no official 5e sheet found) and 'So I took the copies I had from 3.5 and 4.0 and updated them for 5th edition' (the back-fit). Author is 'Ronny', posted 3 August 2015, matching the claim's date.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i992-1006.json"
  },
  "998": {
   "index": 998,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Write in the present tense.",
   "note": "Verbatim as the first line of the 'Tense' section on the same page. The second limb is verbatim on the next line - avoid the future tense 'will' to describe NPC or monster actions - with a worked golem-attacks example. Note the claim's own confidence field is right: this is Ronny's compilation from the 3.5 and 4.0 guides, not a Wizards 5e document, so it is evidence of fan-codified house style, not of Wizards' own rule.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i992-1006.json"
  },
  "999": {
   "index": 999,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "reduce it down to one or two SIMPLE sentences",
   "note": "Live URL fetched 200 with a browser UA, 43,692 bytes; post dated 29 September 2013, byline Dyson Logos. Quote verbatim (SIMPLE capitalised in the original). The point-form limb is verbatim in the next sentence, as the preferred option ('But really, replace it with a point-form list of things seen in the room'), and the first option offered is to get rid of boxed text entirely. The relay limb holds: the post names Jesse Decker and David Noonan, block-quotes their Gen Con passage, and links the wizards.com article. One narrowing to flag: the page addresses 'OSR adventure writers', not adventure writers generally.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the generalised audience 'adventure writers' — the verifier's note flags that 'the page addresses OSR adventure writers, not adventure writers generally'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r10.json"
  },
  "1000": {
   "index": 1000,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'9 paragraphs' - re-measurement of the same PDF gives 21 indent-marked paragraphs (avg 73 words each); 9 is the count of heading-delimited sections. The 'only sustained place-describing prose in the document' limb is also a whole-document judgment over 403 pages that the cited pages cannot establish",
   "trueWording": "1,530 words, 72 sentences, 21 paragraphs (pp. 363-365)",
   "note": "CONFIRMED by re-measurement: PDF re-fetched from media.wizards.com (200, 3,158,713 bytes, sha256 2504d2a0abb0a4d491a939be4f17910a2dde0312570ab8d208080225ccf0a1f0), pdftotext -enc UTF-8, appendix isolated on PDF pages 363-365. Heading 'Appendix PH-C: / The Planes of / Existence' verbatim at the top of p. 363. Body prose (headings and the running head/folio furniture excluded) = 1,530 words excluding the two bold run-in heads 'Spells.' and 'Portals.' (1,532 including them) and 72 sentences - both exact. The 9 corresponds to the intro plus eight headings (The Material Plane, Beyond the Material, Planar Travel, Transitive Planes, Inner Planes, Outer Planes, Outer Planes, Demiplanes).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i992-1006.json"
  },
  "1001": {
   "index": 1001,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "mean 21.25 words; population SD 7.54 across 72 sentences",
   "note": "CONFIRMED by independent re-measurement on the byte-identical PDF: 1,530 body words / 72 sentences = 21.25 -> 21.2, population stdev 7.542 -> 7.5. Counting the two run-in heads instead gives 1,532 words, mean 21.28, population stdev 7.537 - both roundings land on the claim's 21.2 / 7.5 only under the 1,530-word tokenisation, which is the one the claim itself uses. Sample (n-1) stdev would be 7.59, so the claim's 'population' qualifier is load-bearing and correct.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i992-1006.json"
  },
  "1002": {
   "index": 1002,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "shortest 8, longest 41; p10 11, p50 21 confirmed",
   "note": "CONFIRMED for the headline and three of four bracket figures. Re-measured min 8 ('Such a journey is the stuff of legend.'), max 41 (the 'Some are vortices...' sentence on p. 363), p10 11, p50 21. One convention caveat, stated visibly rather than buried: p90 re-measures at 30.9 by linear interpolation and 31 by nearest rank, so the claim's p90 30 holds only under a lower/truncating percentile method. Nothing in the appendix falls below 8 words once the run-in heads 'Spells.' and 'Portals.' are attached to their sentences, which is how the 72-sentence count is reached.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the 'p90 30' bracket figure — the verifier's note confirms only 'three of four bracket figures' and records that p90 re-measures at 30.9 (linear interpolation) or 31 (nearest rank), so 30 holds 'only under a lower/truncating percentile method'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r10.json"
  },
  "1003": {
   "index": 1003,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "8 of 72 sentences exceed 30 words (11.11%)",
   "note": "CONFIRMED exactly by re-measurement: 8/72 = 11.1111%. The eight are the 33-, 32-, 34-, 35-, 41-, 32-, 31- and 34-word sentences; the 31-word one is the 'guided tour of the Nine Hells' sentence, so the count is sensitive to whether >30 or >=30 is used - >30 is what reproduces 8.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i992-1006.json"
  },
  "1004": {
   "index": 1004,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "zero digit characters in the appendix body prose",
   "note": "CONFIRMED: a regex scan for [0-9] over the 1,530-word body returns nothing. The only digits on pp. 363-365 are page furniture excluded from the body - the running head 'System Reference Document 5.1' and the folios 363, 364, 365 - so the claim is true of the prose and would be false of the raw page text.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i992-1006.json"
  },
  "1005": {
   "index": 1005,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "from the first layer to the ninth",
   "note": "Quote verbatim on p. 364 in the Outer Planes section, matching the claim's page cite; 'sixteen planes' is also verbatim on p. 364. CONFIRMED by the same zero-digit scan: every quantity in the appendix is spelled out - four Elemental Planes, two ways, eight alignments, sixteen, Nine Hells, first, ninth, single.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i992-1006.json"
  },
  "1006": {
   "index": 1006,
   "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
   "trueWording": "21 paragraphs; 3.43 sentences per paragraph, not 8.00",
   "note": "The 72-sentence numerator reproduces, but the 9-paragraph denominator does not. Re-measurement of the byte-identical PDF finds 21 paragraphs in PH-C, identified by the typographic first-line indents pdftotext preserves plus the first paragraph after each heading: 72/21 = 3.43 sentences and 73 words per paragraph. The 9 is the number of heading-delimited sections (intro plus The Material Plane, Beyond the Material, Planar Travel, Transitive Planes, Inner Planes, Outer Planes, Outer Planes, Demiplanes), so a section count has been reported as a paragraph count and the per-paragraph average is inflated roughly 2.3x. Downstream: do not carry 8.00 sentences per paragraph.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i992-1006.json"
  },
  "1007": {
   "index": 1007,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "second paragraph of the appendix opening, p. 363",
   "unsupportedLimb": "it stands in the opening paragraph",
   "note": "Fetched the PDF fresh with a browser UA (HTTP 200, 3,158,713 bytes, md5 af3bffd37916093b21325574febf1f72, 403 pages) and ran pdftotext -enc UTF-8. Appendix PH-C occupies PDF/printed pp. 363-365. Sentence count: 74 sentence-final periods, minus the two run-in bold labels 'Spells.' and 'Portals.', = 72 sentences exactly as claimed. Second-person tokens across the whole appendix: 3 (you x1, your x2), and all three sit in one and the same sentence, so exactly one of the 72 sentences uses the second person - CONFIRMED. Quote 'you might walk on streets made of solid fire' appears verbatim. The failing limb is location: that sentence opens 'As your character achieves greater power...' and stands in the SECOND paragraph of the appendix's unheaded introduction. The first paragraph runs 'The cosmos teems with a multitude of worlds...' through '...the dominions of the gods.' and contains no second-person token; the indented paragraph beginning 'Many spells and magic items...' is where the sentence lives.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1007-1021.json"
  },
  "1008": {
   "index": 1008,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "pp. 77-85 measure 42.92; 38.38 only including p. 86",
   "unsupportedLimb": "38.50 per 1,000 words in the SRD 5.1 rules pages 77-85",
   "note": "Appendix limb CONFIRMED exactly: 3 second-person tokens over the finder's 1,530-word denominator = 1.96 per 1,000. Comparator limb does not reproduce for the stated range. Measuring pp. 77-85 the same way (pdftotext, page-footer and page-number stripped) gives 245 second-person tokens in 5,708 words = 42.92 per 1,000. The claimed 38.50 requires a denominator of 6,364 words, which is 11% MORE text than pp. 77-85 contains, so no heading- or table-stripping can reach it; excluding headings pushes the rate UP, not down. The figure is reproducible only if p. 86 is included: pp. 77-86 give the same 245 tokens (p. 86 has zero) over 6,383 words = 38.38, and 6,363 words once headings are dropped = 38.50 exactly. So the denominator spans one page more than the label. The comparison's substance survives - the appendix is roughly 20x less second-person than the rules pages - but the specific figure is attached to the wrong range.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1007-1021.json"
  },
  "1009": {
   "index": 1009,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "7 em dashes, 4.58/1,000; rules pages measure 2.10",
   "note": "CONFIRMED for the appendix: exactly 7 U+2014 em dashes in Appendix PH-C (and zero en dashes), 7/1,530 = 4.575 = 4.58 per 1,000, matching the claim to the rounding. Comparator: measuring pp. 77-85 inclusive of headings and table cells gives 12 em dashes in 5,708 words = 2.10 per 1,000, against the claimed 2.23. The gap is 6% and is explained by denominator scope rather than by any error of range: 12 em dashes over a prose-only denominator of 5,381 words gives 2.230, and pp. 77-85 carry well over 300 words of headings and table cells (Ability Modifiers, Skills, Travel Pace). The finder's PH-C denominator of 1,530 against my raw 1,556 shows the same heading-excluding convention. Direction and magnitude - the appendix roughly doubles the rules pages - hold on either denominator.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1007-1021.json"
  },
  "1010": {
   "index": 1010,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "They're, doesn't, don't - the only three contractions",
   "note": "CONFIRMED. A scan of every apostrophe-bearing token in Appendix PH-C returns exactly five: They're, plane's, doesn't, plane's, don't. Two are possessives ('a plane's alignment', 'the plane's'); the three contractions are precisely the three the claim names, in that order. 3/1,530 = 1.9608 = 1.96 per 1,000, matching. My raw whitespace word count over the three pages is 1,556 including the appendix title and the nine section headings; net of headings this lands on the claimed 1,530, and the same 1,530 denominator reproduces the claim's other three rates (4.58 em dash, 24.84 is/are, 0.65 was/were) to the last digit, so the denominator is internally consistent.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1007-1021.json"
  },
  "1011": {
   "index": 1011,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "quote verbatim, Inner Planes paragraph, p. 364",
   "note": "CONFIRMED on every limb. is = 13 and are = 25, total 38; 38/1,530 = 24.837 = 24.84 per 1,000. was = 0 and were = 1, total 1; 1/1,530 = 0.654 = 0.65 per 1,000. The single 'were' is the quoted clause, which appears verbatim inside the Inner Planes paragraph on p. 364: the plane provides the raw elemental substance 'from which all the worlds were made'. Checked the 'single past clause' limb adversarially by listing every -ed form in the appendix (abandoned, abstracted, accomplished, called, created, defined, formed, generated, governed, pinched, required, resurrected, suspended and the rest): all are passives or participles inside present-tense or present-perfect constructions ('is called', 'are defined', 'have abandoned', 'has been pinched off'), and 'had' occurs zero times. 'were made' is the only finite past-tense clause in the appendix.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1007-1021.json"
  },
  "1012": {
   "index": 1012,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "zero semicolons, question marks, exclamation marks throughout",
   "note": "CONFIRMED. Character counts over the normalised text of Appendix PH-C (pp. 363-365, footers stripped): semicolon 0, question mark 0, exclamation mark 0. The 72-sentence denominator is the same one verified at index 1007 (74 sentence-final periods less the two run-in labels 'Spells.' and 'Portals.'), so every one of the appendix's sentence-final marks is a full stop. No quotation was supplied with the claim, so the verdict is by substance rather than verbatim.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1007-1021.json"
  },
  "1013": {
   "index": 1013,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "quote verbatim, opening of The Environment, p. 86",
   "note": "CONFIRMED on every limb, and the sentence count is exact. The quoted words open the section verbatim on p. 86: 'By its nature, adventuring involves delving into places that are dark, dangerous, and full of mysteries to be explored.' Second-person pronouns across the whole of pp. 86-87 (you, your, yours, yourself, yourselves): ZERO - a regex sweep returns an empty list, which also covers the Resting material that shares p. 87. Sentence count for the section proper - The Environment through the end of Interacting with Objects, i.e. stopping where 'Resting' begins - is 56, matching the claim exactly. The section is written entirely about 'a creature' and 'a character' with the GM in the third person.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1007-1021.json"
  },
  "1014": {
   "index": 1014,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "17 digit runs in 1,065 words; appendix has none",
   "note": "CONFIRMED, and the figure reproduces to the last digit once the counting convention is recovered. The Environment section (p. 86 to the end of Interacting with Objects on p. 87) contains these numeric strings: 1d6, 10, 20d6, 1, 30, 1, 0, 14, 3, 2, 0, 3, 1, 15, 0. Counted as digit runs rather than whole tokens, the dice expressions split (1d6 gives 1 and 6; 20d6 gives 20 and 6), yielding exactly 17 runs; 17/1,065 = 15.962 = 15.96 per 1,000. My raw word count for the section is 1,083 including its ten sub-headings, which lands on the claimed 1,065 net of headings - the same heading-excluding convention the finder used throughout. The contrast limb is likewise CONFIRMED: Appendix PH-C contains no numeral at all, the only digits on those pages being the page-number footers.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1007-1021.json"
  },
  "1015": {
   "index": 1015,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "pp. 5-18 measure 42.97; SRD 5.1 pp. 36-49 reach 88.91",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the highest second-person density of any segment measured in either SRD",
   "note": "Fetched SRD 5.2.1 fresh with a browser UA (HTTP 200, 6,031,375 bytes, md5 393692267005a40d5a194e1707eb9ebb, 364 pages). The density limb is close but not exact: pp. 5-18 measure 416 second-person tokens in 9,682 words = 42.97 per 1,000, against the claimed 44.12 over 8,024 words. The 1,658-word denominator gap points to table content (Ability Modifiers, Skills, Action Summary, Conditions) being stripped, which I cannot reconstruct; on any reading the segment is very heavily second-person, so that limb stands substantively. The superlative does NOT stand. Sweeping every 14-page window across both documents, the peak second-person density in SRD 5.1 is pp. 36-49 - the class descriptions - at 667 tokens in 7,502 words = 88.91 per 1,000, more than double the claimed figure; pp. 35-56 all sit above 83. SRD 5.2.1's own peak window is pp. 48-61 at 73.41. The claim's own comparator, SRD 5.1 pp. 77-85, measures 42.92, so pp. 5-18 lead it by 0.05 per 1,000 - a statistical tie, not a margin.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1007-1021.json"
  },
  "1016": {
   "index": 1016,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "quote verbatim, Magnificent Mansion spell, SRD 5.1 p. 162",
   "note": "CONFIRMED on every limb, with the numeral rate exact. The spell sits on SRD 5.1 p. 162; the quoted words appear verbatim in 'Beyond the portal is a magnificent foyer with numerous chambers beyond.' Second person: the description body carries 12 second-person tokens in 262 words (45.80 per 1,000) and every sentence of it addresses the caster - 'You conjure an extradimensional dwelling', 'You choose where its one entrance is located', 'You can create any floor plan you like'. Numerals: the body after the Duration line contains 7 numbers (5, 10, 30, 50, 10, 100, 100) in 262 words = 26.718 = 26.72 per 1,000, matching exactly. Checked the 'one dwelling described at length' limb against the SRD's other candidates: Tiny Hut is a 10-foot dome of force at roughly 180 words with no rooms, Rope Trick an extradimensional space with no description, and Mighty Fortress and Secure Shelter are absent from SRD 5.1 entirely.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1007-1021.json"
  },
  "1017": {
   "index": 1017,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "all five strings return zero in both documents",
   "note": "CONFIRMED. Case-insensitive substring searches over the full normalised pdftotext extraction of both PDFs - SRD 5.1 at 403 pages and 241,237 words, SRD 5.2.1 at 364 pages and 238,360 words, page counts matching the claim - return zero matches for all five strings in both documents: 'read aloud' 0/0, 'read-aloud' 0/0, 'read the following' 0/0, 'boxed text' 0/0, 'aloud to the players' 0/0. Adversarial control: the bare string 'aloud' does occur, 3 times in each document, so the searches were reaching live text rather than failing silently; none of those occurrences is a read-aloud instruction.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1007-1021.json"
  },
  "1018": {
   "index": 1018,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "singular 'settlement' twice and once; plural adds three",
   "note": "CONFIRMED on the counting convention the claim's own method implies. Word-boundary searches for the singular return exactly 2 in SRD 5.1 and exactly 1 in SRD 5.2.1, as claimed. Both SRD 5.1 instances are plain rules nouns - selling loot on returning 'to a town or other settlement', and delivering something 'to a distant settlement'; the SRD 5.2.1 instance is the crafting-materials rule, 'in any other settlement, that chance is 25 percent'. Flag for downstream use: SRD 5.2.1 additionally carries the plural 'settlements' 3 times (spellcasting services, an area-divination spell, and a druidic locate effect), so a reader who takes 'the word' to include inflected forms would find 4, not 1; SRD 5.1 has no plural at all. The no-settlement-table limb holds: neither document contains any settlement-size table, 'hamlet' and 'metropolis' occur zero times in both, and the only table with settlement categories is the SRD 5.2.1 Spellcasting Services availability column.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the count 'the word settlement occurs... once in SRD 5.2.1' — the verifier's own FLAG records that SRD 5.2.1 carries the plural 'settlements' three further times, 'so a reader who takes the word to include inflected forms would find 4, not 1'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r10.json"
  },
  "1019": {
   "index": 1019,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "three occurrences, all rows of that same table",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the only occurrence",
   "note": "The quoted phrase is verbatim and the table attribution is right, but the count is wrong. 'Village, town, or city' occurs THREE times in SRD 5.2.1, not once - it is the Availability entry on three consecutive rows of the Spellcasting Services table, against Cantrip (30 GP), spell level 1 (50 GP) and spell level 2 (200 GP); the rows above level 2 read 'Town or city only' and then 'City only'. The phrase appears nowhere outside that table, so the claim's point - that this is the SRD's only settlement-size vocabulary and it exists purely as a price-table row label - survives intact, and the table is in the Equipment chapter as stated. Graded PARTIAL solely because the singular 'the only occurrence' is not what the document shows.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1007-1021.json"
  },
  "1020": {
   "index": 1020,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "all five strings absent from both SRD texts",
   "note": "CONFIRMED, and deliberately over-inclusively. Case-insensitive substring searches (not word-boundary, so 'annal' would have caught 'annals' and 'archive' would have caught 'archives' or 'archivist') over the full text of both documents return zero for all five terms in both: gazetteer 0/0, chronicle 0/0, annal 0/0, archive 0/0, ledger 0/0. Denominators are 241,237 words for SRD 5.1 and 238,360 for SRD 5.2.1, so the absence is across the whole of both texts, not a sampled segment. The civic-record register is entirely absent from the SRD.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1007-1021.json"
  },
  "1021": {
   "index": 1021,
   "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
   "trueWording": "p. 379 Giant Spider: 'making their lairs on ceilings'",
   "note": "The specific limb is right and the generalisation is refuted by the same document. Aboleth CONFIRMED: PDF/printed p. 261 carries 'Monsters (A) / Aboleth / Large aberration, lawful evil' straight into Armor Class, Hit Points, Speed, the ability array, traits, actions and legendary actions, with no descriptive or habitat paragraph anywhere in the entry. But SRD 5.1 monster entries DO carry habitat and lair description elsewhere. The Giant Spider entry on p. 379 reads that giant spiders 'are most commonly found underground, making their lairs on ceilings or in dark, web-filled crevices' and that such lairs 'are often festooned with web cocoons holding past victims' - habitat and lair, explicitly. The same pattern recurs: giant fire beetles 'are most commonly found underground and in dark forests', mammoths 'inhabit a wide range of climes, from subarctic to subtropical', reef sharks 'inhabit shallow waters and coral reefs', and in the NPC entries druids 'dwell in forests and other secluded wilderness locations' while evil mages 'dwell in isolated sites'. The habitat-free pattern holds for the main Monsters chapter but not for SRD 5.1 monster entries as a class, which is what the claim asserts.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1007-1021.json"
  },
  "1022": {
   "index": 1022,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "Elementals are beings from the Elemental Planes",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that the phrase survives only in spell text, the Amulet of the Planes and the Elementals creature-type note",
   "note": "Quote verbatim, SRD 5.2.1 p. 254 creature-types list. First limb CONFIRMED: title-case 'Planes of Existence' occurs 0 times in the 364-page PDF, no such section or heading, and WotC's own SRD page records the appendix as removed. Second limb refuted by count: lower-case 'planes of existence' occurs 6 times, in the Arcana skill entry, the Rare Languages note, a divination spell's list of facts, Sending, the telepathic-bond spell and the GM's Areas of Knowledge table; singular 'plane of existence' occurs 43 more. The Amulet of the Planes reads 'another plane of existence' (singular), and the Elementals note says 'Elemental Planes', not 'planes of existence' - so two of the three named homes are wrong and the real homes are unlisted. Route: curl browser UA + pdftotext -enc UTF-8 (cross-checked -raw, -layout).",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1022-1036.json"
  },
  "1023": {
   "index": 1023,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "as those are not rules-bearing to play fifth edition",
   "note": "Raw page reached at www.dndbeyond.com/srd (dnd.wizards.com/resources/systems-reference-document 301s there, curl browser UA, 197 KB HTML). The Appendix row of the SRD 5.1-vs-5.2 differences table carries the sentence with both appendix names, verbatim. Page footer: last updated March 02, 2026, as cited.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1022-1036.json"
  },
  "1024": {
   "index": 1024,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "not to replicate every element of the D&D brand or setting",
   "note": "SRD 5.2 FAQ, answer to 'Why is [class, spell, monster, etc.] not in SRD 5.2?'; the sentence opens 'SRD 5.2 is designed to give creators a strong foundation for building original material,' then the quoted clause. Both limbs (foundation for original material; not reproducing the setting) supported.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1022-1036.json"
  },
  "1025": {
   "index": 1025,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "as that is not featured in the 5.5e core rules",
   "note": "The quoted fragment is verbatim in the Game Rules row of the SRD 5.2 differences table, but the page attributes the removal to the '5.5e core rules' and never says '2024'; the only '2024' on the page is the footer copyright line. 5.5e is the 2024 revision, so the substance holds - downstream copy should use the page's wording, not '2024 core rules'.",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the '2024 core rules' attribution — the verifier's note states 'the page attributes the removal to the 5.5e core rules and never says 2024'",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-regrade-r10.json"
  },
  "1026": {
   "index": 1026,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "English SRD v5.2.1 (Published: May 01, 2025)",
   "note": "English Downloads list item, rendered contiguously (the link text plus the parenthetical in the same <li>). Previous SRD Releases section lists 'SRD v5.2.0 (Published: April 22, 2025)', supporting the second limb. Same list gives the two PDF URLs used for claims 1022 and 1027-1029.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1022-1036.json"
  },
  "1027": {
   "index": 1027,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Many more game terms are capitalized in SRD 5.2.1",
   "note": "Conversion guide p. 1, 'Using This Guide', step 3 'Update Capitalization'; the sentence continues 'than in 5.1; update their capitalization in your content to match', so the 'than in SRD 5.1' limb holds. PDF fetched with browser UA (2.47 MB, 15 pp.), pdftotext -enc UTF-8.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1022-1036.json"
  },
  "1028": {
   "index": 1028,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Game objects are more rigorously capitalized.",
   "note": "Opening sentence of the 'Capitalization' section, conversion guide p. 15, immediately before the bulleted list. Verbatim including the period.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1022-1036.json"
  },
  "1029": {
   "index": 1029,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "The following terms are capitalized:",
   "note": "CONFIRMED by independent count: 75 bullets exactly, from 'Abilities {Strength, Intelligence}' to 'Named parts of actions, class features, magic items, spells, etc.', spanning pp. 15-16 of the guide (grep -c '^•' over the section = 75). Quote verbatim (line-wrapped as 'The / following terms are capitalized:').",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1022-1036.json"
  },
  "1030": {
   "index": 1030,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "'Difficult Terrain' occurs 54 times, not 52",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that the capitalized form appears 52 times in SRD 5.2.1",
   "note": "Two limbs reproduce exactly: lower-case 'difficult terrain' 48 times in SRD 5.1 and 0 times in SRD 5.2.1 (identical under default and -raw extraction). The third does not: capitalized 'Difficult Terrain' counts 54 (default), 49 (-raw), 42 (-layout, which mis-joins the two columns); 52 reproduces under no route. Since the finder's 5.1 figures match default/raw exactly, 54 is the like-for-like number. All 54 hits are body text spread over 38 pages - the PDF has no index, and no hit sits in the Contents.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1022-1036.json"
  },
  "1031": {
   "index": 1031,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "'Bright Light' occurs 53 times, not 50",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that the capitalized form appears 50 times in SRD 5.2.1",
   "note": "Lower-case limbs reproduce exactly: 'bright light' 54 times in SRD 5.1, 0 times in SRD 5.2.1 (default and -raw). Capitalized 'Bright Light' counts 53 under the matching route (53 under -raw, 50 only under -layout, which mis-joins columns and also undercounts the 5.1 figures to 46, so it cannot be the finder's route); one further sentence-initial 'Bright light' exists in 5.2.1, giving 54 any-case. 53 hits across 34 pages, all body text.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1022-1036.json"
  },
  "1032": {
   "index": 1032,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "trueWording": "'Temporary Hit Points' occurs 76 times, not 72",
   "unsupportedLimb": "that the capitalized form appears 72 times in SRD 5.2.1",
   "note": "Lower-case limbs reproduce exactly: 'temporary hit points' 27 times in SRD 5.1, 0 times in SRD 5.2.1. Capitalized 'Temporary Hit Points' counts 76 (default), 74 (-raw), 58 (-layout); 72 reproduces under no route. One of the 76 is the Contents entry on p. 2, so the body figure is 75; 14 of the rest cluster on p. 18 (the Hit Points rules). Discrepancy is the same direction and size as claims 1030-1031.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1022-1036.json"
  },
  "1033": {
   "index": 1033,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Exploration involves delving into places that are dangerous and full of mystery.",
   "note": "CONFIRMED on both sides. SRD 5.2.1 p. 11 (footer '11'), under the heading 'Exploration'; the sentence counts 12 words. SRD 5.1 p. 86 (footer '86'), under 'The Environment', reads 'By its nature, adventuring involves delving into places that are dark, dangerous, and full of mysteries to be explored' - 19 words, with 'dark' dropped and 'mysteries to be explored' reduced to 'mystery'. Both cited page numbers check out.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1022-1036.json"
  },
  "1034": {
   "index": 1034,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "detail some of the ways adventurers interact with the environment",
   "note": "CONFIRMED. SRD 5.2.1 p. 11 second sentence counts 18 words; the SRD 5.1 p. 86 counterpart, which carries 'cover some of the most important ways in which' verbatim, counts 22. Both replacement fragments quoted in the claim are exact.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1022-1036.json"
  },
  "1035": {
   "index": 1035,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "transports you and up to 199 other willing creatures",
   "note": "Quote verbatim in the SRD 5.2.1 Rod of Security entry, which continues 'you can see to a demiplane' and repeats 'demiplane' three more times. The SRD 5.1 counterpart (SRD_CC_v5.1.pdf, media.wizards.com, same browser-UA + pdftotext route) reads 'to a paradise that exists in an extraplanar space' verbatim. Note the claim's url field cites only the 5.2.1 PDF; the 5.1 half was verified against the 5.1 PDF.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1022-1036.json"
  },
  "1036": {
   "index": 1036,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Dungeon Master (DM): After passing through the craggy peaks",
   "note": "CONFIRMED. Front matter reads 'D&D Basic Rules, Version 1.0, Released November 2018'. The DM speech from 'After passing through the craggy peaks' to 'spilling into the courtyard.' counts exactly 127 words (130 with the speaker label). Negative limb also holds: neither SRD 5.1 nor SRD 5.2.1 contains a single 'DM:' or 'GM:' dialogue turn, the phrase 'craggy peaks', or this passage (0 hits each). One citation slip: the passage is on printed page 3 (PDF page 3, footer '3'), not p. 2 as the claim's page field says - p. 2 is the Contents.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1022-1036.json"
  },
  "1037": {
   "index": 1037,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "Nine sentences; mean 14.11 words, population SD 4.43, range 5-21.",
   "note": "Recomputed from the live PDF (HTTP 200, browser UA, pdftotext -enc UTF-8). The DM read-aloud box in the Introduction gives exactly 9 sentences, 127 words, mean 14.1111, population SD 4.4333, shortest 5, longest 21 - every figure reproduces. No quote supplied, so graded on substance not verbatim. LOCATOR CORRECTION: the passage is on printed p. 3 (PDF page 3, footer '3'), not p. 2; p. 2 is the Contents page.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1037-1051.json"
  },
  "1038": {
   "index": 1038,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "127 words, zero digit characters in the passage.",
   "note": "Recomputed from the live PDF. Word total 127 matches exactly and a digit scan over the passage returns an empty set. No quote supplied, so graded on substance. Same locator correction as 1037: printed p. 3, not p. 2.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1037-1051.json"
  },
  "1039": {
   "index": 1039,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "Zero em dashes; zero contractions; one hyphen only.",
   "note": "Recomputed from the live PDF: em-dash count 0, en-dash count 0, and a contraction scan returns an empty set. The passage's only intraword punctuation is the single hyphen in the compound modifier before 'iron'. No quote supplied, so graded on substance. Locator: printed p. 3, not p. 2.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1037-1051.json"
  },
  "1040": {
   "index": 1040,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "the verb enumeration presented as complete - it omits the finite verb 'takes' in sentence 1, so the passage carries eleven finite verbs, not the ten listed",
   "trueWording": "No finite 'to be'; but sentence one also inflects 'takes'.",
   "note": "The quotation appears word for word on printed p. 3. The primary limb is CONFIRMED: a scan for is/are/was/were/be/been/being/am over the passage returns an empty set. The enumeration limb fails. Sentence 1 is compound - one clause has the road as subject with the finite verb 'takes', the other has Castle Ravenloft with 'towers'. The claim's list keeps only the second. Every other listed verb checks out (keep, look, gapes, spans, creak, stare, grin, hangs, stand), and the participles passing/disappearing/leading/straining/spilling are correctly excluded. Locator: printed p. 3, not p. 2.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1037-1051.json"
  },
  "1041": {
   "index": 1041,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Castle Ravenloft towers before you",
   "note": "Quotation appears word for word in the DM read-aloud box, and every limb holds. Two second-person tokens across 127 words = 15.7480 per 1,000, which rounds to the claimed 15.75; they fall in sentence 1 and sentence 7, i.e. 2 of 9 sentences. Locator: printed p. 3 (PDF page 3), not p. 2.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1037-1051.json"
  },
  "1042": {
   "index": 1042,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'describes environments only as numeric thresholds' - the section's own figures refute 'only', since 55.56 percent of its sentences carry no digit and two of its ten subsections (Heavy Precipitation and Strong Wind) contain no numeral at all",
   "trueWording": "When the temperature is 0 degrees Fahrenheit or lower",
   "note": "Quotation verbatim on p. 195 (PDF page 195, footer matches). Both measurements reproduce EXACTLY: the section body with subheadings stripped is 512 words in 27 sentences; 12 of those 27 sentences contain a digit = 44.44 percent; and a digit-run count returns 23 numerals = 44.9219 per 1,000 words, matching 44.92 to the stated precision. I recovered the finder's tokenizer: plain digit runs, which split 10,000 into two and 3d10 into two. The characterisation limb is what fails - qualitative sentences such as the ones defining Difficult Terrain and the effect on open flames describe environments with no numeric threshold whatever.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1037-1051.json"
  },
  "1043": {
   "index": 1043,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "Planes appendix 0.00; Environmental Effects 44.92 numerals per 1,000 words.",
   "note": "Both endpoints independently confirmed from the two live PDFs. SRD 5.1 Appendix PH-C (The Planes of Existence, pp. 363-365) contains ZERO digits across 1,530 words of body prose once running heads and folios are stripped, giving 0.00 per 1,000. SRD 5.2.1 Environmental Effects gives 44.92 per 1,000 as at index 1042. 'Deleted' also holds: SRD 5.2.1 has no Planes of Existence appendix (string count 0) and no pantheon lore whatever (Egyptian, Norse, Celtic, Olympus all count 0); the plane names survive only inside spell and rules text. One wording caveat, not a limb failure: a 'factor' is undefined against a zero denominator, so the separation is absolute rather than multiplicative. No quote supplied, so graded on substance.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1037-1051.json"
  },
  "1044": {
   "index": 1044,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "No second-person pronoun, no em dash, across 27 sentences.",
   "note": "Recomputed from the live PDF. A you/your/yours/yourself/yourselves scan over the p. 195 body returns an empty set; em-dash count 0 and en-dash count 0; the sentence count is 27, matching. Note the contrast this sets up with the Basic Rules read-aloud box at 1041, which is second-person throughout. No quote supplied, so graded on substance.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1037-1051.json"
  },
  "1045": {
   "index": 1045,
   "verdict": "PARTIAL",
   "unsupportedLimb": "'in 5 paragraphs' - the prose sets 8 paragraphs, not 5; 5 counts blank-line blocks (the intro plus one per pantheon), but three of those blocks open a second paragraph with the SRD's first-line indent (the Egyptian, Celtic and Norse continuations)",
   "trueWording": "Appendix PH-B: Fantasy-Historical Pantheons",
   "note": "Heading verbatim on p. 360 (PDF page 360, footer matches); the extracted text carries soft-hyphen artifacts inside 'Fantasy-Historical' that render as the claimed string. The other limbs hold exactly: 550 words on a whitespace tokenizer, 22 sentences, four pantheons (Celtic, Egyptian, Greek, Norse). 'The only institutional prose in either SRD' survives adversarial checking - SRD 5.1's other two appendices are PH-A (Conditions, pure rules) and PH-C (Planes, places rather than institutions), and SRD 5.2.1 carries no lore prose at all, its two lowercase 'pantheon' hits being spell text for Divine Intervention and the spiritual guardian. The paragraph count is the one limb that fails. I found the indent markers directly in the extraction: three continuation paragraphs beyond the five blocks.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1037-1051.json"
  },
  "1046": {
   "index": 1046,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "Mean 25.00 words, population SD 12.00, longest sentence 54.",
   "note": "Recomputed from the live PDF and matching to four decimals: 550 words over 22 sentences gives mean 25.0000, population SD 12.0038, longest 54. Reproducing this required recovering the finder's tokenizer - whitespace splitting, which keeps em-dash-joined pairs and the non-breaking-hyphen compounds as single tokens. An alphanumeric tokenizer instead yields 556 words, mean 25.27, SD 12.22, longest 55; the claim is exact under the stated pdftotext route. No quote supplied, so graded on substance.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1037-1051.json"
  },
  "1047": {
   "index": 1047,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "27.27 percent against 11.11 percent of sentences over thirty words.",
   "note": "Both halves reproduce exactly. Pantheon prose (p. 360): 6 of 22 sentences exceed 30 words = 27.27 percent. Planes appendix PH-C (pp. 363-365): 8 of 72 sentences exceed 30 words = 11.1111 percent. The 72 is reached by excluding the two bold run-in heads that a naive splitter counts as one-word sentences; counting them gives 74 and 10.81 percent, so the claim's figure identifies the correct basis. No quote supplied, so graded on substance.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1037-1051.json"
  },
  "1048": {
   "index": 1048,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_SUBSTANCE",
   "trueWording": "550 words carrying no second-person pronoun and no digit.",
   "note": "Recomputed from the live PDF: word total 550 on the whitespace tokenizer, a you/your/yours/yourself/yourselves scan returns an empty set, and a digit scan returns an empty set. Consistent with 1043's finding that the SRD's lore prose is numeral-free throughout. No quote supplied, so graded on substance.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1037-1051.json"
  },
  "1049": {
   "index": 1049,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "Where the land plummets from the snowy hills",
   "note": "Quotation appears word for word as the opening of The Norse Pantheon on p. 360, and both limbs hold. The entry opens on three stacked place clauses - the land and hills, the longboats and beach, the glaciers - before it reaches its main clause naming the land of the Vikings and the pantheon's home; no deity is named anywhere in that opening paragraph. The people is introduced through the climate that shapes it, which is the 'describes a people by its landscape' limb.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1037-1051.json"
  },
  "1050": {
   "index": 1050,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "It will not, however, include lore references.",
   "note": "Fetched live at HTTP 200 with a browser UA; the body is present in the served HTML, no summarizer involved. Quotation verbatim, byline 'by DND Staff May 6, 2024' matches the source attribution. The interval limb holds: the post is dated 6 May 2024 and SRD 5.2 was released 22 April 2025, an interval of 11 months 16 days, which 'a year before release' rounds fairly. Worth recording for the merger that the page itself forecasts release only as being within weeks of the Feb 2025 Monster Manual, so the page alone dates the lead at roughly nine to ten months; the full year comes from the actual release date.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1037-1051.json"
  },
  "1051": {
   "index": 1051,
   "verdict": "VERIFIED_VERBATIM",
   "trueWording": "DMsGuild is the place for you",
   "note": "Fetched live at HTTP 200 with a browser UA; body present in the served HTML. The claim's quoted fragment appears word for word and continues to name DMsGuild. The 'rather than to the SRD' limb is supported by immediate context, not inference: the DMsGuild sentence follows directly on the sentence excluding lore references from SRD 5.2, in the same answer to the question of what SRD 5.2 will contain, so the post is explicitly routing setting-based creation away from the SRD.",
   "fromFile": "verdicts-dnd-i1037-1051.json"
  },
